 it heads into its final days.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poll Watch: Track the Polls in the Race’s Final Days', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T00:48:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4897c1d0-6653-58b0-877c-fe70ee17a24a', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4897c1d0-6653-58b0-877c-fe70ee17a24a'}, {'abstract': 'Could surge barriers be a bargain against future eruptions of nature?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/01/opinion/worrying-beyond-hurricane-sandy.html', 'snippet': 'Could surge barriers be a bargain against future eruptions of nature?', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is small comfort to sodden and stranded New Yorkers that Hurricane Sandy’s flooding of the city’s infrastructure, from power lines to subways to low-lying communities, was predicted in grimly precise detail by scientists in the latest state and city climate studies. Deeper and more frequent flooding from Rockaway to Lower Manhattan and the city’s transit tunnels has been a repeated warning that largely went unnoticed by the public and most politicians. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Worrying Beyond Hurricane Sandy', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Worrying Beyond Hurricane Sandy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': '