essages to news about the storm.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ADVERTISING, marketing and public relations executives are asking a familiar question after Hurricane Sandy: What is the difference between appropriate efforts that are perceived as public service and inappropriate efforts that are demonized as capitalizing on bad news?', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/01/business/ADCO/ADCO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 778, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/01/business/ADCO/ADCO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1557, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/01/business/ADCO/ADCO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/01/business/ADCO/ADCO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/01/business/ADCO/ADCO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Capitalizing on Disaster, Sometimes With Results That Match', 'kicker': 'Advertising', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Capitalizing on Disaster, Sometimes With Results That Match', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Foursquare', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T00:52:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1af35697-cc79-5fbb-90f2-591bb6c4ba09', 'word_count': 1006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1af35697-cc79-5fbb-90f2-591bb6c4ba09'}, {'abstract': 'The MF Global bankruptcy prompted federal authorities to immediately bear down on the brokerage firm and the broader futures trading industry.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/10/31/a-year-after-mf-globals-collapse-brokerage-firms-feel-less-pressure-for-change/', 'snippet': 'The MF Global bankruptcy prompted federal authorities to immediately bear down on the brokerage firm and the broader futures trading industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When MF Global toppled a year ago, chaos engulfed a Chicago trading floor. Customers were locked out of their accounts, later discovering that about $1 billion of their money had disappeared.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Year After MF Global’s Collapse, Brokerage Firms Feel Less Pressure for Change', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Year After MF Global’s Collapse, Brokerage Firms Feel Less Pressure for Change', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Commodities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Futures and Options Trading', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commodity Futures Trading Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MF Global', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T00:55:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e08a44a-f480-51a7-8753-19ad3d301578', 'word_count': 870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e08a44a-f480-51a7-8753-19ad3d301578'}, {'abstract': 'For both the state and national polls to be accurate, and the math to work, something needs to give.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/01/us/election-news/when-state-polls-differ-from-national-polls.html', 'snippet': 'For both the state and national polls to be accurate, and the math to work, something needs to give.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney and President Obama remain roughly tied in national polls, while state polls suggest a lead for Mr. Obama in the Electoral College. Many people take this to mean that there is a fairly good chance of a split outcome between the Electoral College and the popular vote. But the story may not be so simple.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When State Polls Differ From National Polls', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When State Polls Differ From National Polls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T00:56:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b97352d-fa07-51d8-baca-0b0fc5652a81', 'word_count': 860, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b97352d-fa07-51d8-baca-0b0fc5652a81'}, {'abstract': 'Climate change has virtually been ignored in this presidential campaign, but Sandy brought it front and center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/01/opinion/kristof-will-climate-get-some-respect-now.html', 'snippet': 'Climate change has virtually been ignored in this presidential campaign, but Sandy brought it front and center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama and Mitt Romney seemed determined not to discuss climate change in this campaign. So thanks to Hurricane Sandy for forcing the issue: Isn’t it time to talk not only about weather, but also about climate?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-articleLarge-v2.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-articleLarge-v2.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/nicholas-kristof/nicholas-kristof-jumbo-v2.png', 'height'