Hurricane Sandy, the public quickly rendered verdicts on which ads or promotions were appropriate, and which crossed a line, Stuart Elliott reports. The perceived line-crossers included the retailer Jonathan Adler, he writes, which used Twitter and e-mails to invite people to “storm our site” and created a code “Sandy” for free shipping; the company has since apologized. More successful in enlisting the storm and its damage was Duracell, whose Duracell Rapid Responder truck offered free batteries and access to charging lockers for mobile devices and computers in the New York area.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: The Marketing Uses of a Hurricane, and Apple’s Aesthetic Battles', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Duracell International Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NBC Universal', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T14:07:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/204836af-f350-5395-ba8f-4864b00436c7', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/204836af-f350-5395-ba8f-4864b00436c7'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/01/what-were-reading-557/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Times: Hurricane Sandy has given new life to the term “global warming” — and “glamping.” That is, the glamorous brand of camping now being practiced by the upper crust of Lower Manhattan, whose idea of roughing it involves fondue pots and foraging for restaurant reservations. …', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T14:09:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/621bff95-93ac-5c93-8b9f-8c262a134b87', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/621bff95-93ac-5c93-8b9f-8c262a134b87'}, {'abstract': 'A video from Disney Parks imagines what might happen if the Dark Lord of the Sith paid a visit to the self-styled Happiest Place on Earth.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/01/darth-vader-goes-to-disneyland-literally/', 'snippet': 'A video from Disney Parks imagines what might happen if the Dark Lord of the Sith paid a visit to the self-styled Happiest Place on Earth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the pop-cultural galaxy continues to freak out about the recent announcement that the Walt Disney Company was buying Lucasfilm, an incalculable number of Disney-“Star Wars” mash-ups have made their way across the Internet. But perhaps none quite as adorable as the above video made by the company’s own Disney Parks division (and which we were pointed to by The Daily What), which imagines what might happen if Darth Vader, the Dark Lord of the Sith himself, paid a visit to the professed Happiest Place on Earth.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Darth Vader Goes to Disneyland (Literally)', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Disneyland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lucasfilm Ltd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walt Disney Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T14:10:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/567ba3d6-0ad6-5e8c-94a4-5f8de946a4bc', 'word_count': 87, 'uri': 'nyt://article/567ba3d6-0ad6-5e8c-94a4-5f8de946a4bc'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama continues to hold the lead in the vast majority of polls in Ohio, Wisconsin, Nevada and Iowa, the states that represent his path of least resistance toward winning the Electoral College. This was particularly apparent on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/01/oct-31-obamas-electoral-college-firewall-holding-in-polls/', 'snippet': 'President Obama continues to hold the lead in the vast majority of polls in Ohio, Wisconsin, Nevada and Iowa, the states that represent his path of least resistance toward winning the Electoral College. This was particularly apparent on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Oct. 11, this blog posed the question of whether President Obama’s “firewall” in battleground states was all that it was cracked up to be. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oct. 31: Obama’s Electoral College ‘Firewall’ Holding in Polls', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Electio