ain': 'Five Senate Races to Watch', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T16:04:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6d5d24c-8c1d-52c8-bd4e-14b3dd80ff83', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6d5d24c-8c1d-52c8-bd4e-14b3dd80ff83'}, {'abstract': 'Millions of dollars have been injected into Maine’s Senate race, giving fresh hope to the Republican nominee, despite the independent candidate’s persistent lead in the polls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/us/rove-versus-bloomberg-it-just-seems-that-way-in-maine.html', 'snippet': 'Millions of dollars have been injected into Maine’s Senate race, giving fresh hope to the Republican nominee, despite the independent candidate’s persistent lead in the polls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BANGOR, Me. — The Maine Senate race has become so convoluted that at times it has seemed as if Karl Rove and Michael R. Bloomberg were running against each other.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/us/politics/02maine-senate-span/02maine-senate-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 374, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/02/us/politics/02maine-senate-span/02maine-senate-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 374}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/0