re than I will ever cost the government.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Part D, Part 2', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T19:52:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Gross', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gross', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/162045ea-e08e-5b57-b484-0ce8982724e3', 'word_count': 1032, 'uri': 'nyt://article/162045ea-e08e-5b57-b484-0ce8982724e3'}, {'abstract': 'Chinua Achebe’s memoir about the moment when Nigeria split apart at a cost of more than a million lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/there-was-a-country-by-chinua-achebe.html', 'snippet': 'Chinua Achebe’s memoir about the moment when Nigeria split apart at a cost of more than a million lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rumors of Nigeria’s demise have been somewhat exaggerated. This turbulent and magnetic African megastate endures despite its intense regional, religious and other divisions (the country has an estimated 250 ethnic groups and more than 500 languages).', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Nossiter-Hochschild/1104-Nossiter-Hochschild-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/re