rd-quarter net income of $24.4 million, or 29 cents a share, up about 24 percent from $19.7 million, or 23 cents per share, in the year-earlier quarter, the company said on Thursday. Revenue was $186.3 million, up about 16 percent from $160.8 million in the year-earlier quarter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Strong Quarterly Results for DreamWorks Animation, as Profit Rises 24%', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DreamWorks Animation SKG Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T21:28:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/92112a6e-0846-5e94-81c2-f2c40be5bbf4', 'word_count': 196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/92112a6e-0846-5e94-81c2-f2c40be5bbf4'}, {'abstract': 'This season a fresh batch of stationery and leather-bound books make room for reviving pen-to-paper romances among the busy, stylish set.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/01/a-preference-for-paper/', 'snippet': 'This season a fresh batch of stationery and leather-bound books make room for reviving pen-to-paper romances among the busy, stylish set.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the fast-moving, fast-talking digital age, handwritten thank-you notes have surrendered to e-mails, text messages and tweets. Agendas and planners have been pushed aside for smartphone calendars that sync with desktops. But this season a fresh batch of stationery and leather-bound books make room for reviving pen-to-paper romances among the busy, stylish set.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Preference for Paper', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T21:30:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corbin Chamberlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corbin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chamberlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65ac89ba-3915-5be7-96db-ae62697261bd', 'word_count': 368, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65ac89ba-3915-5be7-96db-ae62697261bd'}, {'abstract': 'In “Egon Schiele’s Women” Galerie St. Etienne presents that Austrian artist’s views of women, as more than simply respectably chaste or seductively erotic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/arts/design/egon-schieles-women-at-galerie-st-etienne.html', 'snippet': 'In “Egon Schiele’s Women” Galerie St. Etienne presents that Austrian artist’s views of women, as more than simply respectably chaste or seductively erotic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Austrian artist Egon Schiele (1890-1918) was not what you would call a feminist. His drawings and watercolors show women, including teenage prostitutes, in poses both awkward and erotic. He slept with some of his models, impregnating one of them, and went to jail on charges (later dropped) of abducting and molesting a 14-year-old girl. At the very least he was a product of a patriarchal Austrian culture that found female sexuality mysterious and threatening — one that insisted upon the purity of upper-class brides but made Vienna the streetwalking capital of Europe.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/arts/02SCHIELE1_SPAN/02SCHIELE1_SPAN-art