ns, has come out with its list of “systemically important” big banks. Of the 28 firms on the list, eight are from the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world’s too-big-to-fail list is out, and nearly a third of the banks on it are from the United States.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The List That Big Banks Don’t Wish to Be On', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T23:08:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Eavis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eavis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2be60682-cbca-53d9-8edc-e7c072a2f11e', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2be60682-cbca-53d9-8edc-e7c072a2f11e'}, {'abstract': '“Festival of Lights” concerns a mother and daughter who have fled Guyana to seek a better life in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/movies/festival-of-lights-on-guyanese-immigrants-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': '“Festival of Lights” concerns a mother and daughter who have fled Guyana to seek a better life in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Festival of Lights” seeks to illuminate an immigrant world we don’t hear much about: people who have fled the South American country of Guyana seeking a better life in New York. But the film, by Shundell Prasad, seems reluctant to spend much time in or on Guyana, and so it ends up being largely just another story about a rebellious American teenager.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grown Up and Going Back to Guyana', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Festival of Lights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Festival of Lights (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prasad, Shundell', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T23:10:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3be205d0-f6d6-5b9e-8aa1-e0af0057adf9', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3be205d0-f6d6-5b9e-8aa1-e0af0057adf9'}, {'abstract': 'Why has there been so little notice paid to this long-suffering group? Maybe they have become so isolated and politically powerless that most voters don’t really care about them.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/01/the-forgotten-long-term-unemployed/', 'snippet': 'Why has there been so little notice paid to this long-suffering group? Maybe they have become so isolated and politically powerless that most voters don’t really care about them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Annie Lowrey and I have an article in Friday’s paper about the gigantic, overlooked underclass of long-term unemployed workers, whose safety net has now frayed bare and whose troubles are probably holding back the overall jobs picture as they become less and less employable. This 4.8-million-strong group has gotten very little attention during the presidential campaign, despite the candidates’ purported focus on the economy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Forgotten Long-Term Unemployed', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-01T23:14:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b32b8b0a-ccf2-56c6-9ccf-7ec74b57a62d', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b32b8b0a-ccf2-56c6-9ccf-7ec74b57a62d'}, {'abstract': 'In “Luv Shuv Tey Chicken Khurana,” a young man returns to Punjab and finds love, family and his grandfather’s famous Khurana chicken.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/movies/luv-shuv-tey-chicken-khurana-with-kunal-kapoor.html', 'snippet': 'In “Luv Shuv Tey Chicken Khurana,” a young man returns to Punjab and finds love, family and his grandfather’s famous Khurana chicken.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You could call the Hindi movie “Luv Shuv Tey Chicken Khurana” a comedy of reintegration. Instead of sending its hero abroad, as so many Indian films do, it brings him home.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/arts/02RDP_LUV_SPAN/02RDP_LUV_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/02/arts/02RDP_LUV_SPAN/02RDP_LUV_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/arts/02RDP_LUV_SPAN/02RDP_LUV_SPAN-jumb