2-11-02T14:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Sablich and Justin Sablich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sablich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99aa3b31-3e98-55db-8768-309a2ceaf28e', 'word_count': 1779, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99aa3b31-3e98-55db-8768-309a2ceaf28e'}, {'abstract': 'A roundup of reaction to the decision to run the New York City Marathon in the aftermath of Hurricane Sandy.', 'web_url': 'https://marathon.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/decision-to-run-marathon-divides-a-city/', 'snippet': 'A roundup of reaction to the decision to run the New York City Marathon in the aftermath of Hurricane Sandy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Emotions on the question of whether the New York City Marathon should go on Sunday in the aftermath of Hurricane Sandy continued to run high, as runners, fans and New Yorkers voiced their opinion on the polarizing issue.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Decision to Run Marathon Divides a City', 'kicker': 'On the Run', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York City Marathon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Running', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Road Runners Club', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:14:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Pilon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pilon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5adca27-c947-5418-8e63-1350107057b4', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5adca27-c947-5418-8e63-1350107057b4'}, {'abstract': 'The head of Americans United for Life isn’t a moderate, but she plays one on TV.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/magazine/charmaine-yoests-cheerful-war-on-abortion.html', 'snippet': 'The head of Americans United for Life isn’t a moderate, but she plays one on TV.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One day in the spring, I went with Charmaine Yoest, head of Americans United for Life, a pro-life advocacy group, to meet two of her five kids at a Barnes & Noble near her office in Washington. We sat down in the Starbucks corner of the bookstore, and James and Sarah, who are 8 and 11, told me about the March for Life on the National Mall. They go every year, scouting out heating vents to stand on when it’s cold and competing over who can hand out the most Life Counts posters. “We start up chants,” Sarah volunteered, looking up from her Frappuccino with whipped cream. “Like ‘Fight Planned Parenthood.’ ”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04yoest1/04yoest1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04yoest1/04yoest1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04yoest1/04yoest1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_