devastation wrought along the Atlantic coast by this week’s superstorm. Visitors can view a map of the region at NOAA’s National Geodetic Survey Web site and click on an icon to view a thumbnail or high-definition image of a specific area. Images are available for some of the Northeast’s hardest-hit areas, including Atlantic City, Seaside Heights, N.J., Ocean City, Md., and parts of Delaware.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Aerial Snapshots of the Storm Damage', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:35:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65870bb7-87f0-5a88-b687-113b0b60a721', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65870bb7-87f0-5a88-b687-113b0b60a721'}, {'abstract': 'A father and son made pieces that appealed to royalty and a pope.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/garden/metropolitan-museum-of-art-displays-furniture-of-the-roentgens.html', 'snippet': 'A father and son made pieces that appealed to royalty and a pope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art will exhibit one of the most comprehensive collections of the furniture of Abraham Roentgen and his son David, German cabinetmakers of the 18th century. The Roentgens created dressing tables, clocks and writing desks for Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette, Pope Pius VI, Catherine the Great and other nobles of Europe. They used Caribbean mahogany, cherry or walnut, and decorated their pieces with embedded bits of wood or ivory to create chinoiserie or allegorical scenes. But mostly they were renowned for their use of\xa0secret drawers, moving parts and hidden music boxes, sometimes revealed by the push of a button.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/garden/02showspic/02showspic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 948, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/garden/02showspic/02showspic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1852, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/garden/02showspic/02showspic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/02/garden/02showspic/02showspic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/garden/02showspic/02showspic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At the Met, Décor With All the Trappings', 'kicker': 'Currents | Shows', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At the Met, D&eacute;cor With All the Trappings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Furniture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:36:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0baa5369-7bf9-5431-b0b3-206cef13f874', 'word_count': 240, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0baa5369-7bf9-5431-b0b3-206cef13f874'}, {'abstract': 'In this volume of sweeping reflections, the playwright and activist Wole Soyinka takes Africa’s woes to heart.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/of-africa-by-wole-soyinka.html', 'snippet': 'In this volume of sweeping reflections, the playwright and activist Wole Soyinka takes Africa’s woes to heart.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the Africans who deserve some kind of secular sainthood is Wole So\xadyinka. Although best known as a playwright, he has also written poetry, novels and nonfiction; the luminous world evoked in his memoir “Aké: The Years of Childhood” sticks with me decades after I read it. Moreover, Soyinka has always been a passionate defender of human rights. For trying to negotiate peace during the Nigerian civil war of the late 1960s, he spent two years in prison, much of it in solitary confinement; some 30 years later, as an opponent of a later generation of military dictatorship, he had to escape Nigeria on a motorcycle and was sentenced to death in absentia.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104HOCHSCHILD/1104HOCHSCHILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104HOCHSCHILD/1104HOCHSCHILD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104HOCHSCHILD/1104HOCHSCHILD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 626, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104HOCHSCHILD/1104HOCHSCHILD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1222, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104HOCHSCHILD/1104HOCHSCHILD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104HOCHSCHILD/1104HOCHSCHILD-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104HOCHSCHILD/1104HOCHSCHILD-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Assessing Africa', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Elders Speak', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soyinka, Wole', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:46:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Hochschild', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hochschild', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/00112105-169c-5cb2-83c8-d516e6348128', 'word_count': 1135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00112105-169c-5cb2-83c8-d516e6348128'}, {'abstract': 'Webster’s Third New International was scorned for being less judgmental than its predecessor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/the-story-of-aint-by-david-skinner.html', 'snippet': 'Webster’s Third New International was scorned for being less judgmental than its predecessor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dictionary making has always been a much misunderstood art. The lexicographer’s job is to capture the language as people use it and understand it. But most people who go to a dictionary don’t care about common usage. They want to know what’s right.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Oconner-Sub/1104-Oconner-Sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Oconner-Sub/1104-Oconner-Sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Oconner-Sub/1104-Oconner-Sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 723, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Oconner-Sub/1104-Oconner-Sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1446, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Oconner-Sub/1104-Oconner-Sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Oconner-Sub/1104-Oconner-Sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Oconner-Sub/1104-Oconner-Sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What Everyone Was Saying', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Everyone Was Saying', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Skinner, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Merriam-Webster Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dictionaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:49:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': "By Patricia T. O'Conner", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': "O'Conner", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/37ad4e8c-86ee-5e36-aebd-bc4ddbd945db', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37ad4e8c-86ee-5e36-aebd-bc4ddbd945db'}, {'abstract': 'Applying statistical analysis to the past, Nate Silver evaluates the claims of sports pundits, political handicappers and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/the-signal-and-the-noise-by-nate-silver.html', 'snippet': 'Applying statistical analysis to the past, Nate Silver evaluates the claims of sports pundits, political handicappers and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nate Silver has lived a preposterously interesting life. In 2002, while toiling away as a lowly consultant for the accounting firm KPMG, he hatched a revolutionary method for predicting the performance of baseball players, which the Web site Baseball Prospectus subsequently acquired. The following year, he took up poker in his spare time and quit his job after winning $15,000 in six months. (His annual poker winnings soon ran into the six-figures.) Then, in early 2008, Silver noticed that most political prognostication was bunk. Silver promptly reinvented that field, too. His predictive powers were such that at one point the Obama campaign turned to him for guidance.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Scheiber/1104-Scheiber-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Scheiber/1104-Scheiber-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1408, 'width': 1408, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Scheiber/1104-Scheiber-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Scheiber/1104-Scheiber-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Scheiber/1104-Scheiber-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Known Unknowns', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Known Unknowns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "The Signal and the Noise: Why So Many Predictions Fail-but Some Don't (Book)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silver, Nate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Statistics', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:50:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Scheiber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scheiber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cfdb8f5-4dfb-5791-a578-95cea1faa422', 'word_count': 1219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cfdb8f5-4dfb-5791-a578-95cea1faa422'}, {'abstract': 'Ian Frazier’s hard-pressed Cursing Mommy moves from New Yorker columns to a novel of her own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/the-cursing-mommys-book-of-days-by-ian-frazier.html', 'snippet': 'Ian Frazier’s hard-pressed Cursing Mommy moves from New Yorker columns to a novel of her own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Welcome, my wonderful invisible friends. It’s that bountiful season when the air is tangy with the fragrance of cinnamon and glue sticks, and the leaves are crunching underfoot. Because they’re dead. Yes, death and decay are in the air — and cinnamon! Inhale the pure oxygen of gratitude. Particularly today, for the chance to review “The Cursing Mommy’s Book of Days,” even if this newspaper does pay freelancers terribly, because Ian Frazier is funny and clever and a wonderful distraction from an otherwise Boschian existence, what with one’s child already delivering homework-incomplete notes from the school of little geniuses the reviewer stupidly prayed he’d get into. Rejoice! For one day this man-child will find himself interested in something that doesn’t involve Tim Tebow, and when that day comes, the reviewer will cease being so bitter that every bloody parent she meets at that bloody school (note: cheapskate newspaper won’t let her curse), a school where other children speak Mandarin for fun, has an advanced degree in physics or computer science, something real and important and not some rinky-dink literature degree like hers. And when that day comes, my friends, she will no longer care that her Sad Inferior dna Does Not Stand a Chance Against These Shiny Blond Upper East Side Übermenschen.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Newman/1104-Newman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Newman/1104-Newman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1756, 'width': 1756, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Newman/1104-Newman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Newman/1104-Newman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Newman/1104-Newman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The No Good, Very Bad Year', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The No Good, Very Bad Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frazier, Ian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:50:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cde7bd7f-ed9e-52c6-8162-1a4003d743d0', 'word_count': 1254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cde7bd7f-ed9e-52c6-8162-1a4003d743d0'}, {'abstract': 'In a collection spanning 15 years, Nicholson Baker ponders political controversies, personal intimacies and fascinating obscurities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/the-way-the-world-works-by-nicholson-baker.html', 'snippet': 'In a collection spanning 15 years, Nicholson Baker ponders political controversies, personal intimacies and fascinating obscurities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an interview that appeared in The Paris Review last year, Nicholson \xadBaker was asked about the importance of realism in his work. “It becomes interesting,” he observed, “to come up with words that wrap around reality’s utility pole.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Sullivan/1104-Sullivan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 789, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Sullivan/1104-Sullivan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1577, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Sullivan/1104-Sullivan-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbna