is formed by the place you grow up, which way will its \xadneedle swing when you’re far from home?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104ALLEN/1104ALLEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 693, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104ALLEN/1104ALLEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1196, 'width': 809, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104ALLEN/1104ALLEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104ALLEN/1104ALLEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104ALLEN/1104ALLEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Far From Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Far From Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Donoghue, Emma', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:51:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Allen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Allen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a49e52d5-566e-5483-8290-ad084728bb75', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a49e52d5-566e-5483-8290-ad084728bb75'}, {'abstract': 'A. M. Homes’s dark satire of 21st-century domestic life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/may-we-be-forgiven-by-a-m-homes.html', 'snippet': 'A. M. Homes’s dark satire of 21st-century domestic life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a while there, in the middle of the last century, New York’s northern suburbs were something like the literary capital of the country. The novels of Richard Yates and the stories of John Cheever reimagined Westchester County as a mythic landscape to rival Yoknapatawpha, and the rider of the Metro-North as a prism for American yearning and unease. Then the ’60s came along and scrambled the cultural map. But the Westchester mystique persists, in “Mad Men” and men’s wear — and in much of A. M. Homes’s best fiction.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Hallberg/1104-Hallberg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 959, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Hallberg/1104-Hallberg-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1331, 'width': 1421, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Hallberg/1104-Hallberg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Hallberg/1104-Hallberg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Hallberg/1104-Hallberg-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 149, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Man of the House', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Man of the House', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Homes, A M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:52:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Garth Risk Hallberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Garth', 'middlename': 'Risk', 'lastname': 'Hallberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b4b2035-e3b6-55ee-906e-1f6655555a9f', 'word_count': 1400, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b4b2035-e3b6-55ee-906e-1f6655555a9f'}, {'abstract': 'In Michael Robotham’s “Say You’re Sorry,” two teenage girls are held captive in a cellar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/michael-robothams-say-youre-sorry-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Michael Robotham’s “Say You’re Sorry,” two teenage girls are held captive in a cellar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Robotham is one adult author you can really trust around young girls. In SAY YOU’RE SORRY (Mulholland/Little, Brown, $24.99), this Australian crime novelist assigns half the narration to Piper Hadley, who tells her story with the urgency of a latter-day Scheherazade. As we learn from her candid diary entries, Piper was abducted along with her best friend, Natasha McBain, when both were 15, and for the past three years, they’ve been held captive in a cellar by their kidnapper, a sexual predator they call George. Piper tries not to think too hard about the periodic visits her friend must make to George’s quarters, but after Tash comes back bloodied from their latest encounter, she finds a way to escape from the cellar, promising to return with help to rescue Piper.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Crime/1104-Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 837, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Crime/1104-Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1449, 'width': 1185, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Crime/1104-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Crime/1104-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Crime/1104-Crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Given Up for Dead', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Given Up for Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cornwell, Patricia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mayor, Archer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friis, Agnete', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robotham, Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaaberbol, Lene', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:53:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/349fde17-c67f-5e3d-9f31-7ae8137d7613', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/349fde17-c67f-5e3d-9f31-7ae8137d7613'}, {'abstract': 'A biographical novel of the notorious bank robber Willie Sutton.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/sutton-by-j-r-moehringer.html', 'snippet': 'A biographical novel of the notorious bank robber Willie Sutton.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Historical fiction might disappoint for lots of recondite literary reasons, but some imaginative re-\xadcreations just can’t live up to their real-life originals. Paradoxical and elusive, the bank robber Willie Sutton would appear to be a ready-made candidate for a smart, kaleidoscopic novel along the lines of T. Coraghessan Boyle’s “Road to Wellville,” Don DeLillo’s “Libra” and James Ellroy’s “L.A. Confidential,” or even for a crafty bio\xadpic with the chameleonlike spirit of Todd Haynes’s “I’m Not There,” in which six actors play aspects of Bob Dylan. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104POLITO/1104POLITO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 784, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/20