or an American journalist than Africa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before taking over as The New York Times’s West Africa bureau chief, Adam Nossiter was steeped in the American South. As a New Orleans-based national correspondent, he spent nearly 20 years reporting on droughts in Alabama and the long legacy of segregation in Arkansas. He’s switched over to covering water shortages in Niger and the long legacy of colonialism in Senegal — and he says he was primed to make the move.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Upfront/1104-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 630, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/books/review/1104-Upfront/1104-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2011, 'width': 1237, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nossiter, Adam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:53:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26fd3628-3565-58a5-93e9-944cfcda1fb2', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26fd3628-3565-58a5-93e9-944cfcda1fb2'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BACK TO BLOOD,  by Tom Wolfe. (Little, Brown, $30.) This sweeping novel, which aims to do for Miami what Wolfe did for New York and Atlanta, is full of exuberance, italics, status and sartorial details. Its appealing hero is a well-meaning Cuban-American cop.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books Of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T14:54:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea44e28c-9e7f-5689-bc0b-f523a31745da', 'word_count': 266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea44e28c-9e7f-5689-bc0b-f523a31745da'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LUMINOUS AIRPLANES,  by Paul La Farge. (Picador, $15.) After the dot-com crash, the unnamed narrator of La Farge’s touching novel — a computer programmer unmoored by the crash and also by the death of his grand\xadfather — returns to the upstate New York town where he spent childhood summers with his grandparents. Ostensibly, he’s there to sort out family possessions (among them a treasured 1894 book, “Progress in Flying Machines”); in reality, he’s sorting out his own past.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None