nt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carr, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, A O', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T17:12:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4b89d49-1324-5636-b7c5-d9bc4ee2afcc', 'word_count': 19, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4b89d49-1324-5636-b7c5-d9bc4ee2afcc'}, {'abstract': 'A colored candy primer to the electoral college from the Abstract Sunday blog.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/from-the-abstract-sunday-blog-an-electoral-college-primer/', 'snippet': 'A colored candy primer to the electoral college from the Abstract Sunday blog.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From the Abstract Sunday Blog: An Electoral-College Primer', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T17:17:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89a957d8-05c2-5e27-83aa-8ba3e6b9f63b', 'word_count': 11, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89a957d8-05c2-5e27-83aa-8ba3e6b9f63b'}, {'abstract': 'Movies have long loved the tale of Leo Tolstoy’s impassioned heroine Anna Karenina, but the faithfulness of adaptations have greatly varied.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/movies/keira-knightley-in-joe-wrights-anna-karenina.html', 'snippet': 'Movies have long loved the tale of Leo Tolstoy’s impassioned heroine Anna Karenina, but the faithfulness of adaptations have greatly varied.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT isn’t easy to be faithful. Anna Karenina, for example, the title heroine of the magisterial 1877 novel by Leo Tolstoy, thinks herself happy enough with her distinguished husband, her sweet-tempered young son and her impeccable standing in the highest levels of St. Petersburg society, until she meets a dashing cavalry officer and gives it all up for him. Her infidelity is not casual or discreet, as it is for other respectable women in her set, but helpless, urgent, terrifyingly intense; she doesn’t know what hit her. The infidelity of the many filmmakers who have tried to tell Anna’s story on the screen is not, generally, of that kind. A novel of the length (over 900 pages) and complexity of “Anna Karenina” and a feature film of, say, two hours’ duration do not make an ideal match; dissatisfaction is inevitable, outright betrayal highly likely. ', 'print_section': 'MT', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04RAFFERTY1_SPAN/04RAFFERTY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04RAFFERTY1_SPAN/04RAFFERTY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04RAFFERTY1_SPAN/04RAFFERTY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04RAFFERTY1_SPAN/04RAFFERTY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04RAFFERTY1_SPAN/04RAFFERTY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04RAFFERTY1_SPAN/04RAFFERTY1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04RAFFERTY1_SPAN/04RAFFERTY1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Degrees of Infidelity to Tolstoy’s Heroine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Degrees of Infidelity to Tolstoy’s Heroine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knightley, Keira', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Anna Karenina (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Law, Jude', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wright, Joe', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tolstoy, Leo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T17:21:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terrence Rafferty', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rafferty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e09b90d-af6e-5c3d-be32-5e47283e877b', 'word_count': 1730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e09b90d-af6e-5c3d-be32-5e47283e877b'}, {'abstract': 'In the wake of Hurricane Sandy, perhaps we need a discussion about the outsize place sports has cleared for itself in our society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/sports/no-back-seat-for-sports-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'In the wake of Hurricane Sandy, perhaps we need a discussion about the outsize place sports has cleared for itself in our society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While Hurricane Sandy might someday be looked upon as the turning point in our national discussion (or at least a regional one) about infrastructure and climate change, it could also be a pivotal point in a discussion about the outsize place sports has cleared for itself in our society. Maybe the whole firestorm over whether the New York City Marathon should be run Sunday, through a city freshly devastated by a giant storm, will end up being this city’s squabble, quickly forgotten. But in a twist of fate, it will also be the first New York City Marathon televised live nationally, so the country will have a front-row seat for the race, which is being run despite much opposition and with the sentiment among many New Yorkers that this is an insane use of city resources in the middle of a disaster.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Back Seat for Sports', 'kicker': 'Leading Off', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York City Marathon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Steelers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Diego Chargers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Knicks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harden, James', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bryant, Kobe', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T17:31:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df932d4a-a2bc-5b3b-bcc4-714d04cd5899', 'word_count': 744, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df932d4a-a2bc-5b3b-bcc4-714d04cd5899'}, {'abstract': 'Maybe we’ve misunderstood him all along.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/karl-roves-mission-accomplished/', 'snippet': 'Maybe we’ve misunderstood him all along.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The estimable Rick Perlstein has a fascinating essay about the seamless continuum from direct-mail marketing scams to direct-mail right-wing fundraising, and from there to the whole character of modern movement conservatism. Go read. I didn’t know, for example, that heroes of direct-mail fundraising like Richard Viguerie ended up delivering hardly any of the money to political causes; somehow it ended up swallowed by overhead, otherwise known as the fundraisers themselves.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Karl Rove’s Mission Accomplished', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T17:34:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/628ea8ab-93f0-5b2d-8250-eb2815085e09', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/628ea8ab-93f0-5b2d-8250-eb2815085e09'}, {'abstract': 'Making “Chasing Ice,” about a photographer documenting receding glaciers, Jeff Orlowski learned what cold really meant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/movies/talking-with-jeff-orlowski-about-chasing-ice.html', 'snippet': 'Making “Chasing Ice,” about a photographer documenting receding glaciers, Jeff Orlowski learned what cold really meant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WITH its concise title, “Chasing Ice” lets audiences know up front the kind of environmental adventure film they are in for. The documentary, which opens on Nov. 9 in New York, serves double duty as a profile of a photographer and a closer look inside one of his most ambitious projects.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04SNAPSHOT/04SNAPSHOT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 462, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04SNAPSHOT/04SNAPSHOT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1502, 'width': 677, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04SNAPSHOT/04SNAPSHOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04SNAPSHOT/04SNAPSHOT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/arts/04SNAPSHOT/04SNAPSHOT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What to Expect When You’re Shooting Glaciers', 'kicker': 'Snapshot | Jeff Orlowski', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What to Expect When You’re Shooting Glaciers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Balog, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Glaciers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T17:34:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/f546c14f-94d8-5537-bc98-9dffb4f5aa34', 'word_count': 647, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f546c14f-94d8-5537-bc98-9dffb4f5aa34'}, {'abstract': 'Massive open online courses are the educational happening of the moment. Everyone wants in. No one is quite sure what they’re getting into.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/education/edlife/massive-open-online-courses-are-multiplying-at-a-rapid-pace.html', 'snippet': 'Massive open online courses are the educational happening of the moment. Everyone wants in. No one is quite sure what they’re getting into.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN late September, as workers applied joint compound to new office walls, hoodie-clad colleagues who had just met were working together on deadline. Film editors, code-writing interns and “edX fellows” — grad students and postdocs versed in online education — were translating videotaped lectures into MOOCs, or massive open online courses. As if anyone needed reminding, a row of aqua Post-its gave the dates the courses would “go live.”', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/education/edl-04mooc-t_CA0/edl-04mooc-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'h