:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bradley Berman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bradley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f868df82-4a81-52e0-96ff-30afbcc8bd61', 'word_count': 596, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f868df82-4a81-52e0-96ff-30afbcc8bd61'}, {'abstract': 'Many of the investment products that helped lead to the financial crisis contained ample disclosures of their risks, but the sophisticated investors who lost billions failed to heed warning signs that should have led them to further investigation.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/reading-the-fine-print-in-abacus-and-other-soured-deals/', 'snippet': 'Many of the investment products that helped lead to the financial crisis contained ample disclosures of their risks, but the sophisticated investors who lost billions failed to heed warning signs that should have led them to further investigation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A common refrain from the financial crisis is that poor disclosure was a big contributor, if not the cause, of the financial crisis. Buyers of even the most complicated financial instruments were misled or were not provided full information concerning their investments. The results were catastrophic when the mortgage market crashed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reading the Fine Print in Abacus and Other Soured Deals', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collateralized Debt Obligations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conflicts of Interest', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hedge Funds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgage-Backed Securities', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Citigroup Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Credit Suisse Group AG', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T18:13:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3366be0c-70d1-512f-a9f3-87f277a25a44', 'word_count': 1422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3366be0c-70d1-512f-a9f3-87f277a25a44'}, {'abstract': 'The indie-pop quartet Alt-J won the Barclayscard Mercury Prize, which honors the best album by a British or Irish band.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/mercury-prize-goes-to-alt-j/', 'snippet': 'The indie-pop quartet Alt-J won the Barclayscard Mercury Prize, which honors the best album by a British or Irish band.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year’s prestigious Barclaycard Mercury Prize went to the indie-pop quartet Alt-J, whose debut album “An Awesome Wave” (Infectious Music) won praise from critics for the idiosyncratic structure of its songs, the BBC reported.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mercury Prize Goes to Alt-J', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain’s Mercury Prize Is Awarded to Alt-J', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and