'2012-11-02T19:20:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Garrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8686d1fa-1890-55d0-8859-b89913f9a31b', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8686d1fa-1890-55d0-8859-b89913f9a31b'}, {'abstract': 'For a true picture of the United States economy, ride the train from New York to Washington and take a good, hard look out the window.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/magazine/100000001879397/off-the-rails.html', 'snippet': 'For a true picture of the United States economy, ride the train from New York to Washington and take a good, hard look out the window.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a true picture of the United States economy, ride the train from New York to Washington and take a good, hard look out the window.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/04corridor1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/04corridor1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/mag-04corridor-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/mag-04corridor-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1127, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/04corridor1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/04corridor1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/04corridor1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Rails', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMTRAK', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T19:21:38+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Jacob Krupnick for The New York Times. Photographs by Pieter Hugo for The New York Times', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/05caeb96-e39e-5e40-baad-e5501570700a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/05caeb96-e39e-5e40-baad-e5501570700a'}, {'abstract': 'The death and life of the industrial corridor linking New York and Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/magazine/amtrak-industrial-corridor.html', 'snippet': 'The death and life of the industrial corridor linking New York and Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As anyone who rides Amtrak between New York and Washington knows, the trip can be a dissonant experience. Inside the train, it’s all tidy and digital, everybody absorbed in laptops and iPhones, while outside the windows an entirely different world glides by. Traveling south is like moving through a curated exhibit of urban and industrial decay. There’s Newark and Trenton and the heroic wreckage in parts of Philadelphia, block after block of hulking edifices covered in graffiti, the boarded-up ghost neighborhoods of Baltimore made familiar by “The Wire” — all on the line that connects America’s financial center and its booming capital city.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/04corridor1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 335, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/04corridor1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 335}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/mag-04corridor-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 769, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/mag-04corridor-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1127, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/04corridor1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/04corridor1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04corridor1/04corridor1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Empire of the In-Between', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Empire of the In-Between', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Delaware', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hugo, Pieter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wilmington (Del)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMTRAK', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Railroads', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maryland', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T19:22:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Davidson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davidson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8780d8e3-f3e8-53c4-8a8f-27553a0fefd6', 'word_count': 1612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8780d8e3-f3e8-53c4-8a8f-27553a0fefd6'}, {'abstract': 'The conversation has continued about Nate Silver, the statistics wizard, and his political predictions.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/the-timess-washington-bureau-chief-and-legions-of-others-in-defense-of-nate-silver/', 'snippet': 'The conversation has continued about Nate Silver, the statistics wizard, and his political predictions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nate Silver, author of the FiveThirtyEight blog on NYTimes.com, may be under attack from some people, as I noted in a post on Thursday, but he also has many defenders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Times’s Washington Bureau Chief, and Legions of Others, in Defense of Nate Silver', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T19:22:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6be9dd18-983b-57b1-b8ca-046372439500', 'word_count': 913, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6be9dd18-983b-57b1-b8ca-046372439500'}, {'abstract': 'The photos in Sunday’s magazine of the stretch between New York and Washington that got whacked by Hurricane Sandy don’t reveal anything about the hardship and heroics of the last week. But they do tell a powerful story about how the American economy has evolved over the last 50 years.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/this-sunday-man-made-devastation-vs-natural/', 'snippet': 'The photos in Sunday’s magazine of the stretch between New York and Washington that got whacked by Hurricane Sandy don’t reveal anything about the hardship and heroics of the last week. But they do tell a powerful story about how the American econ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes in journalism your work is overtaken by unpredictable events, and this has been one of those times at the magazine. The cover photo essay in this week’s issue is about an area of the United States, the stretch between New York and Washington, that got whacked by Hurricane Sandy. But because we put\xa0this package\xa0together and sent it to our printer in the week before the storm, you won’t see any evidence of Sandy in the pictures. Some readers may find that\xa0distracting\xa0initially, but these pictures deserve a long, close look. Although they don’t reveal anything about the hardship and heroics of the last week, they do tell a powerful story about how the American economy has evolved over the last 50 years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/magazine/02-pieter-hugo-grid/02-pieter-hugo-grid-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/magazine/02-pieter-hugo-grid/02-pieter-hugo-grid-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/magazine/02-pieter-hugo-grid/02-pieter-hugo-grid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/02/magazine/02-pieter-hugo-grid/02-pieter-hugo-grid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/02/magazine/02-pieter-hugo-grid/02-pieter-hugo-grid-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 111, 'width': 111, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'This Sunday: Man-Made Devastation vs. Natural', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T19:23:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0721f909-fc0c-59df-86dd-44e9e0c28b16', 'word_count': 466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0721f909-fc0c-59df-86dd-44e9e0c28b16'}, {'abstract': 'The cancellation of the league’s signature event, which was to be held on Jan. 1, was the latest and most significant result of the nearly seven-week lockout.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/sports/hockey/nhl-cancels-the-winter-classic.html', 'snippet': 'The cancellation of the league’s signature event, which was to be held on Jan. 1, was the latest and most significant result of the nearly seven-week lockout.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The N.H.L. on Friday canceled the Winter Classic, the outdoor game that is its signature regular-season event, two months before it was scheduled to be played, on Jan. 1. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citing Logistics, N.H.L. Cancels Winter Classic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Citing Logistics, N.H.L. Cancels Winter Classic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T19:24:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/656d071f-fca9-5628-adeb-c0aba0132d6f', 'word_count': 839, 'uri': 'nyt://article/656d071f-fca9-5628-adeb-c0aba0132d6f'}, {'abstract': 'How the cheat for variety-show hosts and soap-opera stars entered politics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/magazine/who-made-that-teleprompter.html', 'snippet': 'How the cheat for variety-show hosts and soap-opera stars entered politics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1952, Hubert Schlafly fielded a strange phone call: “The chief would like to have you come to the Waldorf Tower and show him that gadget,” said the voice on the other end. Schlafly, an engineer and a budding inventor, packed up his new machine and headed to the Waldorf-Astoria. He found himself face to face with Herbert Hoover, Schlafly would recall years later. The ex-president was scheduled to deliver a speech at the Republican convention that summer, and he worried about fumbling in front of the TV cameras. He needed a secret weapon. Schlafly had one: a “suitcase” with a window in its side, what he called a teleprompter. He created the device (with his two business partners) as a cheat for variety-show hosts and soap-opera stars who needed help remembering their lines.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04wmt/04wmt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04wmt/04wmt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04wmt/04wmt-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04wmt/04wmt-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1351, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04wmt/04wmt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04wmt/04wmt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04wmt/04wmt-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Teleprompter?', 'kicker': 'Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Teleprompter)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoover, Herbert Clark', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T19:26:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pagan Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pagan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d44639b2-5975-5d8d-99fc-aeebe0c8a9a1', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d44639b2-5975-5d8d-99fc-aeebe0c8a9a1'}, {'abstract': 'Voting for love and money.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/magazine/politics-or-pandering.html', 'snippet': 'Voting for love and money.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While the presidential candidates talked about the long-term benefits of their economic “plans” in the first debate, they also said that they won’t change programs that affect current (or near) retirees. Is it unethical for politicians to shift burdens to future generations, often ignoring the long-term-debt implications, while they hold harmless the older demographic (that is, likely voters) whose generation of politicians created the underfunded programs they currently benefit from? STEVEN BUTER, TACOMA, WASH.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/magazine/04ethicist/04ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_na