5 came up with the right answer. The first person to figure out the diagnosis was Heather Chambers, a third-year medical student at Dalhousie Medical School in Halifax, Nova Scotia. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/29/health/29well_diagnosis/29well_diagnosis-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 280, 'width': 400, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/29/health/29well_diagnosis/29well_diagnosis-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 280, 'width': 400, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/29/health/29well_diagnosis/29well_diagnosis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/02/29/health/29well_diagnosis/29well_diagnosis-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/02/29/health/29well_diagnosis/29well_diagnosis-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Think Like a Doctor: A Terrible Leg Wound Solved', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIBIOTICS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bacteria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hepatitis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Microbiology', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T20:36:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Sanders, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sanders', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fae27d5-4d62-58fa-9470-0fbf9e20b412', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fae27d5-4d62-58fa-9470-0fbf9e20b412'}, {'abstract': 'New federal regulations make it more difficult to conceal that a car was in a flood, but consumer advocates urge buyers to be cautious.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/buyer-beware-cars-flooded-by-hurricane-could-turn-up-on-the-market/', 'snippet': 'New federal regulations make it more difficult to conceal that a car was in a flood, but consumer advocates urge buyers to be cautious.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many cars submerged by the floods caused by Hurricane Sandy could eventually turn up on the automotive market. Consumer advocates warn that buyers need to beware of vehicles that have suffered water damage, only to be dried out and marketed by sellers who conceal their histories. Nevertheless, new federal regulations could help protect car buyers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Buyer Beware: Cars Flooded by Hurricane Could Turn Up on the Market', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Auto Reliability and Safety', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carfax', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Geico Corp', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T20:36:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marcia Biederman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marcia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Biederman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60b4c539-2bc4-58a3-ad8a-a1bd5efde9a5', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60b4c539-2bc4-58a3-ad8a-a1bd5efde9a5'}, {'abstract': 'The 2013 Ford Fusion Hybrid has one of the most robust, transparent and enjoyable hybrid systems, and it sells for a compelling $28,000.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/automobiles/autoreviews/a-hybrid-done-right-but-not-without-glitches.html', 'snippet': 'The 2013 Ford Fusion Hybrid has one of the most robust, transparent and enjoyable hybrid systems, and it sells for a compelling $28,000.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ELECTRONIC technology can be a double-edge sword, as Ford might attest after recent close shaves. The automaker’s glitchy Sync and MyFord Touch infotainment systems — co-developed, unsurprisingly, with Microsoft — have provoked jeremiads from consumers and critics and have contributed to the brand’s steep drop in consumer reliability ratings.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/automobiles/04HYBR/04HYBR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 262, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/04/automobiles/04HYBR/04HYBR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 262}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/automobiles/04HYBR/04HYBR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 10