ng Food, Quickly', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food Contamination and Poisoning', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Health and Mental Hygiene Department (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York Metropolitan Area', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T20:37:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d444e485-3687-5cfa-a2cd-0ecd6d777bc5', 'word_count': 480, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d444e485-3687-5cfa-a2cd-0ecd6d777bc5'}, {'abstract': 'Better markets are bad for Wall Street.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/dewey-cheatham-and-howe-for-romney/', 'snippet': 'Better markets are bad for Wall Street.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mike Konczal has an important post on the reasons for Wall Street’s hard turn away from Obama toward Romney. Many people — myself included — have emphasized the thin skins of the super-rich, who want admiration as well as wealth beyond imagining, and feel that Obama doesn’t give them the deference they’re entitled to. I’ve called this the “Ma! He’s looking at me funny!” effect. And surely it’s real.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dewey, Cheatham and Howe for Romney', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T20:50:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pa