es-the-new-generation-of-college-students.html', 'snippet': 'Arthur Levine takes a scholarly look at today’s college students.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR more than four decades, Arthur Levine has been exploring the psyche of college students, a quest that has led to three books on different generations’ behaviors and beliefs. The latest, “Generation on a Tightrope: A Portrait of Today’s College Student,” written with Diane R. Dean, covers 2006 to 2011, distilling information from surveys and interviews with both undergraduates and student-affairs officials at 31 campuses nationwide.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/education/edl-04QA-t_CA0/edl-04QA-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 475, 'width': 350, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/education/edl-04QA-t_CA0/edl-04QA-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 475, 'width': 350, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/education/edl-04QA-t_CA0/edl-04QA-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Digital Natives and Their Customs', 'kicker': 'Talking Head | Arthur Levine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Digital Natives and Their Customs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Youth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levine, Arthur E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-02T22:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'EdLife', 'section_name': 'Education', 'subsection_name': 'Education Life', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Tamar Lewin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Tamar', 'lastname': 'Lewin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/157a482d-0fd8-50d9-90eb-7950d238178b', 'word_count': 1060, 'uri': 'nyt://article/157a482d-0fd8-50d9-90eb-7950d238178b'}, {'abstract': 'Six steps to writing a term paper, from someone who should know.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/education/edlife/term-paper-tips-from-dave-tomar-professional-cheater.html', 'snippet': 'Six steps to writing a term paper, from someone who should know.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DAVE TOMAR spent the aughts writing nearly 4,000 papers for undergraduates and graduate students. In his most lucrative year, 2010, he made just over $50,000 — $100 to $120 a paper — but that was being tethered to the computer every single day for the college-paper mill he worked for. No subject was too big, not even a 170-page dissertation on international financial reporting standards. “That was a miserable four days,” says Mr. Tomar, 32, who was a communications major at Rutgers.', 'print_section': 'ED', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tips From a Professional Cheat', 'kicker': 'Strategy | Term Papers', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tips From a Professional Cheat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cheating', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name':