ced the temporary closing of New York Harbor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As if a spigot had been turned on again, gas began flowing through the New York City region on Friday as state and federal officials stepped up their efforts to bring relief to frustrated drivers.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/03run-gas/03run-gas-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/03run-gas/03run-gas-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/03run-gas/03run-gas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/03run-gas/03run-gas-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/03run-gas/03run-gas-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuomo Waives a Tax to Allow Docking Tankers to Unload Their Fuel More Quickly', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cuomo Waives a Tax to Allow Docking Tankers to Unload Their Fuel More Quickly', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York Metropolitan Area', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-03T00:14:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Winnie Hu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6eee1ea-3b64-58fc-9d6e-b34cacfa6951', 'word_count': 671, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6eee1ea-3b64-58fc-9d6e-b34cacfa6951'}, {'abstract': 'A 15-room duplex in a Rosario Candela-designed co-op building includes a wine cellar, extensive mahogany detailing and a suite for the staff.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/big-ticket-sold-for-24000000/', 'snippet': 'A 15-room duplex in a Rosario Candela-designed co-op building includes a wine cellar, extensive mahogany detailing and a suite for the staff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An ornate 15-room duplex at 834 Fifth Avenue, the Rosario Candela-designed bastion of international billionaires, sold for $24 million, the most expensive sale of the week, according to city records.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Ticket | Sold for $24,000,000', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-03T00:21:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Finn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf09f88d-b66f-5677-aff5-7cc6b2331696', 'word_count': 295, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf09f88d-b66f-5677-aff5-7cc6b2331696'}, {'abstract': 'College football message boards have become incubators for wildly absurd rumor and humor that increasingly find their way into the mainstream.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/sports/ncaafootball/college-football-message-boards-feed-fans-passion.html', 'snippet': 'College football message boards have become incubators for wildly absurd rumor and humor that increasingly find their way into the mainstream.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It started innocently. In August 2011, a Louisiana State fan who went by the online nickname Mike Linebacker decided that the dynamic Tigers cornerback Tyrann Mathieu deserved a nickname more befitting his fearless style of play than Little Ball of Hate — the one he had temporarily been saddled with. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/BOARD/BOARD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/BOARD/BOARD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Anonymous and Humorous, Message Boards Feed Fans’ Passion', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Anonymous and Humorous, Message Boards Feed Fans’ Passion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football (College)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louisiana State University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-03T00:27:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brett Michael Dykes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brett', 'middlename': 'Michael', 'lastname': 'Dykes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a57e4a9b-fbc6-5d9e-b0c7-f91f0064b3bf', 'word_count': 1091, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a57e4a9b-fbc6-5d9e-b0c7-f91f0064b3bf'}, {'abstract': 'For several years, a correspondent-turned-photographer documented the buildings, the neighborhoods and the way of life that disappeared to make way for a landscape of high-rise buildings that is the new Shanghai. There was no room for these communities in the image that the city envisioned for itself.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/a-shanghai-that-didnt-last/', 'snippet': 'For several years, a correspondent-turned-photographer documented the buildings, the neighborhoods and the way of life that disappeared to make way for a landscape of high-rise buildings that is the new Shanghai. There was no room for these commun...', 'lead_paragraph': 'By the time I moved to Shanghai, in 2003, the most famous features of the city were so well known that even first-time visitors could arrive enjoying a feeling of familiarity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Shanghai That Didn’t Last', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Qiu Xiaolong', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Shanghai (China)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-03T00:27:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Howard W. French', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Howard', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'French', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e09b93a-7ffb-5ca1-af49-2bba1179fa13', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e09b93a-7ffb-5ca1-af49-2bba1179fa13'}, {'abstract': 'The American Red Cross struggled on Friday to reassure beleaguered New York City residents that its disaster-relief efforts were at last getting up to speed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/nyregion/anger-grows-at-the-red-cross-response-to-the-storm.html', 'snippet': 'The American Red Cross struggled on Friday to reassure beleaguered New York City residents that its disaster-relief efforts were at last getting up to speed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The American Red Cross struggled on Friday to reassure beleaguered New York City residents that its disaster-relief efforts were at last getting up to speed, after the agency’s delayed arrival in devastated areas of Staten Island, Brooklyn and Queens drew intense criticism.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Anger Grows at Response by Red Cross', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Anger Grows At Response By Red Cross', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Queens (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Molinaro, James P', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Staten Island (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN RED CROSS', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-03T00:27:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Halbfinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f75ace9d-4ea3-53fc-bb5c-10a0f2b9df5a', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f75ace9d-4ea3-53fc-bb5c-10a0f2b9df5a'}, {'abstract': 'With mass transit still being restored and a number of uncertainties surrounding New York and New Jersey after Hurricane Sandy, the Nets may not sell out their opener in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/sports/basketball/nets-uncertain-of-what-delayed-home-opener-will-bring.html', 'snippet': 'With mass transit still being restored and a number of uncertainties surrounding New York and New Jersey after Hurricane Sandy, the Nets may not sell out their opener in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a native of New Orleans, Nets Coach Avery Johnson is familiar with hurricanes and the destruction they leave. So it was not surprising that at the team’s final practice before its official Brooklyn beginning on Saturday, Johnson talked about his history of storm-watching and how he would try to navigate through the unusual circumstances the team is now confronting. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Nets Uncertain of What Delayed Home Opener Will Bring', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nets Unsure Of What Opener Will Bring', 'name': None, 'seo': None,