zation': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5464adbf-d520-54d6-b7cd-6b6f22b39679', 'word_count': 828, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5464adbf-d520-54d6-b7cd-6b6f22b39679'}, {'abstract': 'Havana Central is designed to evoke pre-revolutionary Cuba, with loud Latin music, dark ceiling fans and Cuban cuisine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/nyregion/a-review-of-havana-central-in-yonkers.html', 'snippet': 'Havana Central is designed to evoke pre-revolutionary Cuba, with loud Latin music, dark ceiling fans and Cuban cuisine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OVER two visits to Havana Central, the Cuban restaurant that opened in April in the new Ridge Hill Shopping Center in Yonkers, I was surprised by the number of times waiters gathered to sing “Happy Birthday.” Bearing candlelit desserts, they repeatedly grouped around diners and gave spirited versions of the song with clapping and a loud “woo, woo” at the end of each line.', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/nyregion/04DINEWE3/04DINEWE3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/nyregion/04DINEWE3/04DINEWE3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/nyregion/04DINEWE3/04DINEWE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/nyregion/04DINEWE3/04DINEWE3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/nyregion/04DINEWE3/04DINEWE3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Evocation of Cuba, Before the Revolution', 'kicker': 'Westchester Dining | Yonkers', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Evocation of Cuba, Before the Revolution', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yonkers (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Havana Central (Yonkers, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-03T01:26:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily DeNitto', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeNitto', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/db096336-5f86-516e-a12e-2bc5a75182b9', 'word_count': 1004, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db096336-5f86-516e-a12e-2bc5a75182b9'}, {'abstract': 'Perhaps more so than in any other place in the city, the loss of power for people living in public housing projects has forced a return to a basic existence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/nyregion/in-public-housing-after-hurricane-sandy-fear-misery-and-heroism.html', 'snippet': 'Perhaps more so than in any other place in the city, the loss of power for people living in public housing projects has forced a return to a basic existence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It would be dark soon at the Coney Island Houses, the fourth night without power, elevators and water. Another night of trips up and down pitch-black staircases, lighted by shaky flashlights and candles. Another night of retreating from the dark. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In New York’s Public Housing, Fear Creeps In With the Dark', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In City’s Public Housing Projects, Fear Creeps In With the Dark', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public and Subsidized Housing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing Authority (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Power Outages and Blackouts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-03T01:27:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cara Buckley and Michael Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55988b70-9a1b-5365-a9c4-3414605bba47', 'word_count': 1230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55988b70-9a1b-5365-a9c4-3414605bba47'}, {'abstract': 'Thousands of runners learned Friday that the New York City Marathon had been canceled after a public backlash had mounted against putting on the race in the wake of Hurricane Sandy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/sports/runners-react-to-the-cancellation-of-the-new-york-city-marathon.html', 'snippet': 'Thousands of runners learned Friday that the New York City Marathon had been canceled after a public backlash had mounted against putting on the race in the wake of Hurricane Sandy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It did not seem too much of an inconvenience on Friday, a two-hour delay at Malpensa Airport in Milan. Nor was the nine-hour flight too uncomfortable. Raffaella Romazzotti was excited, headed to New York to run her first marathon. Shortly after she landed, she received a startling text message.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/JPRUNNERS/JPRUNNERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/JPRUNNERS/JPRUNNERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/JPRUNNERS/JPRUNNERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/JPRUNNERS/JPRUNNERS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/JPRUNNERS/JPRUNNERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/JPRUNNERS/JPRUNNERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/JPRUNNERS/JPRUNNERS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Runners Express Shock, Anger and Relief After Months of Training', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reversal Of Course, Burst Of Emotions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York City Marathon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-03T01:28:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeré Longman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeré', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Longman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8e91672-7bdd-5a29-aa0e-57c7ccedd157', 'word_count': 886, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8e91672-7bdd-5a29-aa0e-57c7ccedd157'}, {'abstract': 'Washington should be more responsive to legitimate concerns raised by residents of this Japanese island.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/opinion/outrage-in-okinawa.html', 'snippet': 'Washington should be more responsive to legitimate concerns raised by residents of this Japanese island.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The accusation against two American sailors for the rape of a woman in Okinawa in October has worsened tensions on the island, already inflamed by the recent deployment of the troubled MV-22 Osprey aircraft to the Marine Corps base in the city of Ginowan. Okinawans may be running out of adjectives to express their resentment and anger at being unwilling hosts for more than half of the 50,000 American military personnel stationed in Japan. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Outrage in Okinawa', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outrage in Okinawa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Bases and Installations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Status of Forces Agreements', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nakaima, Hirokazu', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Browning, Christopher', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dozierwalker, Skyler', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Okinawa and Other Ryukyu Islands (Japan)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-03T01:28:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/4dbd8fb7-821e-526f-bcbb-4a45810f2645', 'word_count': 329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4dbd8fb7-821e-526f-bcbb-4a45810f2645'}, {'abstract': 'How a penniless Philadelphia widow revolutionized naval signalling technology.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/02/a-woman-with-flare/', 'snippet': 'How a penniless Philadelphia widow revolutionized naval signalling technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beginning in the spring of 1862, the Union Navy operated extensively on the Mississippi River, seeking to open that great “father of waters” to military operations and commerce. The problem was: how to coordinate all those ships in mid-stream, not only against the enemy but the vagaries of the river itself? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Woman With Flare', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Navy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi River', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-03T01:30:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By C Kay Larson', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/919c16d0-267b-5bd7-82a3-a6685a80a945', 'word_count': 1263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/919c16d0-267b-5bd7-82a3-a6685a80a945'}, {'abstract': 'The Steelers plan to arrive in buses hours before Sunday’s game against the Giants instead of staying in hotel rooms in New Jersey that may be needed for displaced residents after Hurricane Sandy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/sports/football/without-hotel-in-new-jersey-steelers-forgo-away-game-ritual-against-giants.html', 'snippet': 'The Steelers plan to arrive in buses hours before Sunday’s game against the Giants instead of staying in hotel rooms in New Jersey that may be needed for displaced residents after Hurricane Sandy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Pittsburgh Steelers were laughing in their locker room Friday, recalling their high school days, when they would wear their uniforms on the school bus. When they got off the bus, they would put on their shoulder pads and go straight to the opponent’s field for the game.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/YSTEELERS/YSTEELERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/03/sports/YSTEELERS/YSTEELERS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1392, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'sup