t to go to a nearby diner, just because — but now both are confined to their home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the first six years of his marriage, Pat Marino — fueled more by spontaneity than hunger — would rouse his wife, Theresa, in the middle of the night and propose a jaunt to a nearby diner.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/nyregion/bull-NEEDIEST/bull-NEEDIEST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/nyregion/bull-NEEDIEST/bull-NEEDIEST-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1322, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/nyregion/bull-NEEDIEST/bull-NEEDIEST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/04/nyregion/bull-NEEDIEST/bull-NEEDIEST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/nyregion/bull-NEEDIEST/bull-NEEDIEST-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Flair for Spontaneity Is Dimmed by a Painful Disease', 'kicker': 'The Neediest Cases', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Flair for Spontaneity Is Dimmed by a Painful Disease', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times Neediest Cases Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Catholic Charities', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-04T02:05:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Otis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1841028-c781-5d09-a462-4ff114346954', 'word_count': 925, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1841028-c781-5d09-a462-4ff114346954'}, {'abstract': 'A storied flag that Mitt Romney discusses on the campaign trail, and the scoutmaster who purchased it, both showed up Saturday for the candidate in Colorado.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/03/a-character-in-a-romney-campaign-anecdote-appears-on-the-trail/', 'snippet': 'A storied flag that Mitt Romney discusses on the campaign trail, and the scoutmaster who purchased it, both showed up Saturday for the candidate in Colorado.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ENGLEWOOD, Colo. — Experienced watchers of Mitt Romney are familiar with an anecdote that he first told earlier this year, about a Boy Scout troop in Monument, Colo., that bought an American flag and then sent it around the country. First they had it flown above the Capitol. Then, they asked NASA if they would send it up into space. The flag went up in the space shuttle Challenger in January 1986, Mr. Romney always tells the crowd, and the Scouts were so excited watching from their classrooms — until the shuttle exploded after liftoff, killing all seven astronauts aboard, including a teacher, Christa McAuliffe.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Character in a Romney Campaign Anecdote Appears on the Trail', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flags, Emblems and Insignia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Boy Scouts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-04T02:37:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af17112b-9ea0-5721-94eb-a3edb928592f', 'word_count': 663, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af17112b-9ea0-5721-94eb-a3edb928592f'}, {'abstract': 'Having spent the off-season investing heavily in their roster, the Nets hung on to beat the Toronto Raptors to inaugurate their new arena in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/04/sports/basketball/brooklyn-nets-beat-raptors-in-season-opener.html', 'snippet': 'Having spent the off-season investing heavily in their roster, the Nets hung on to beat the Toronto Raptors to inaugurate their new arena in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the first time since 1957, a major professional sports franchise was able to call itself the home team in Brooklyn. After a wait of 55 years, a delay of a few days as New York recovered from Hurricane Sandy seemed hardly too much to ask.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/04/sports/jp-Nets/jp-Nets-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/04/sports/jp-Nets/jp-Nets-articleLar