d', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/05/magazine/05-fearandloathing/05-fearandloathing-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/05/magazine/05-fearandloathing/05-fearandloathing-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Read Like a Political Reporter', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bai, Matt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Draper, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leibovich, Mark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-05T19:22:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a2de2f5-c876-57ce-be13-0ba7a892c43a', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a2de2f5-c876-57ce-be13-0ba7a892c43a'}, {'abstract': 'In which I predict that the president wins the narrowest of narrow re-elections.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/05/prediction-time-obama-survives/', 'snippet': 'In which I predict that the president wins the narrowest of narrow re-elections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As of this writing, my colleague Nate Silver’s election forecast has President Obama’s odds of re-election at 86 percent. The details of his famous forecasting method may be proprietary, but the argument, at this point, is straightforward: The President leads Mitt Romney by a small but solid margin in the state-level polling; that lead has been robust for weeks; state polling has historically been somewhat more accurate and currently provides a richer data set than the national polls where Romney’s numbers are slightly better; and the tight national polling could be consistent with an Obama electoral college win in any case. To pull the election out, Romney needs to win several states where the polls show him basically tied with the president (or even slightly behind, in some of the latest Virginia polling) and then win a state or two from a list (Ohio, Wisconsin, Nevada, longer shots like Pennsylvania) where the polling has never really shown him doing anything but trailing. This is possible but unlikely; it has grown more unlikely with every passing day; and now “time is up”\xa0and Obama’s victory is nearly certain.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prediction Time: Obama Survives', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-05T19:25:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5491df8-74b4-55f0-a87f-026048614149', 'word_count': 1292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5491df8-74b4-55f0-a87f-026048614149'}, {'abstract': 'Amid corruption scandals and the appearance of excessive lobbying, the European Parliament has become a symbol of how institutions designed to build a united Europe have faltered.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/world/europe/europeans-losing-faith-in-their-parliament.html', 'snippet': 'Amid corruption scandals and the appearance of excessive lobbying, the European Parliament has become a symbol of how institutions designed to build a united Europe have faltered.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRUSSELS — When cracks recently appeared in beams of the European Parliament’s main chamber, forcing its closing, one member, Nigel Farage of the U.K. Independence Party, proclaimed that he would “work for the day that the whole democratic facade of the European Parliament is shut as well.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/business/global/06union/06union-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/business/global/06union/06union-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/business/global/06union/06union-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/06/business/global/06union/06union-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/business/global/06union/06union-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Europeans Losing Faith in Their Parliament', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Europeans Losing Faith in Their Parliament', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Parliament', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legislatures and Parliaments', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bribery and Kickbacks', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-05T19:30:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Kanter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kanter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da948823-e918-52f5-a36a-f95ae2abccac', 'word_count': 1217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da948823-e918-52f5-a36a-f95ae2abccac'}, {'abstract': 'What happens to paintings that are deemed forgeries? Some end up in the government’s hands, but others reappear on the walls of unsuspecting buyers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/arts/design/murky-laws-give-fake-artworks-a-future-as-real-ones.html', 'snippet': 'What happens to paintings that are deemed forgeries? Some end up in the government’s hands, but others reappear on the walls of unsuspecting buyers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As soon as Richard Grant, executive director of the Diebenkorn Foundation, glimpsed the three drawings in an Upper East Side apartment several years ago, he knew there was a problem. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/arts/FAKES/FAKES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 313, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/06/arts/FAKES/FAKES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 313}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/arts/FAKES/FAKES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 534, 'width': 1024, 'subType