est trees and should be reclassified, a study says, a move that may help save them from overharvesting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Australia’s giant eucalyptus trees, the tallest flowering plants on earth, should be classified as a species of rain forest tree, a new study reports.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/science/06OBTREE/06OBTREE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/science/06OBTREE/06OBTREE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/science/06OBTREE/06OBTREE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/06/science/06OBTREE/06OBTREE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/science/06OBTREE/06OBTREE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Giant Eucalyptus Trees Earn Rain Forest Stripes', 'kicker': 'Observatory', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Giant Eucalyptus Trees Earn Rain Forest Stripes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-05T20:26:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sindya N. Bhanoo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sindya', 'middlename': 'N.', 'lastname': 'Bhanoo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcea1386-cae5-539a-87bb-f8c6d81259b9', 'word_count': 276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcea1386-cae5-539a-87bb-f8c6d81259b9'}, {'abstract': 'The founder of the Australian cosmetics label Aesop is branching out with a new men’s accessories shop.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/05/worldly-goods/', 'snippet': 'The founder of the Australian cosmetics label Aesop is branching out with a new men’s accessories shop.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Australian cosmetics label Aesop is celebrating its 25th anniversary this year by opening a crop of design-centric stores around the globe. Its founder, Dennis Pahitis, has also branched out with Épatant (33 Peel Street; 011-61-3-9417-7739), a new men’s accessories shop with Aesop’s utilitarian-chic ethos, in the up-and-coming Collingwood district in Melbourne. He and a co-founder, Lachlan Smeeton, refurbished an old brick bicycle warehouse, giving it whitewashed walls, polished concrete floors and a dash of whimsy courtesy of the graphic artist Anne-Laure Cavigneaux, who adorned its bright blue door with illustrations inspired by the work of the French filmmaker Jacques Tati. The space includes a cafe run by the husband-and-wife team behind Cibi, a restaurant that serves Japanese-style home cooking. But mainly it’s a showcase for items that strike the owners’ fancy, like supersoft calfskin weekend bags commissioned from the Paris-based leather-goods maker Isaac Reina; bikes from the Swedish company Pilen; and a pair of indigo jogging shorts designed by Paphitis himself — what he calls “a sort of 1950s Italian gent’s summer look.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Worldly Goods', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Worldly Goods', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-05T20:28:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Ajudua', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ajudua', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c693f11-b530-56e1-8fb8-afd7767718c3', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c693f11-b530-56e1-8fb8-afd7767718c3'}, {'abstract': 'By Thursday, the playoffs in Major League Soccer will be reduced from eight teams to the final four, leaving some only a few days’ break between the end of the semifinals and the conference championships.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/05/m-l-s-playoffs-soon-to-be-four/', 'snippet': 'By Thursday, the playoffs in Major League Soccer will be reduced from eight teams to the final four, leaving some only a few days’ break between the end of the semifinals and the conference championships.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By late Thursday night, the playoffs in Major League Soccer will be reduced from eight to the final four. After a hectic schedule that has seen some teams (D.C. United and Houston, for example) play as many as four or five league games in two weeks, there will be only a few days’ break between the end of the conference semifinals and the conference championships. The final four will get an intermission between Games 1 and 2 because of FIFA’s international break for games on Nov. 14 (when the U.S. plays at Russia) and then a two-week rest before the final on Dec. 1 at the home of the finalist with the best regular-season record.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'M.L.S. Playoffs: Soon to Be Four', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Playoff Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'DC United', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Soccer', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Red Bulls (Soccer Team)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Sounders', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sporting Kansas City', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-05T20:28:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf44f483-70e2-559f-8cbd-55f413fba978', 'word_count': 1280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf44f483-70e2-559f-8cbd-55f413fba978'}, {'abstract': "In a surprising move reflecting the rivalry between two French luxury titans, PPR announced on Monday the departure from Balenciaga of the fashion house's star designer, Nicolas Ghesquière.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/business/global/top-designer-for-balenciaga-to-leave.html', 'snippet': "In a surprising move reflecting the rivalry between two French luxury titans, PPR announced on Monday the departure from Balenciaga of the fashion house's star designer, Nicolas Ghesquière.", 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — In a surprising move reflecting th