'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/015fcbf8-1dd3-5736-9e4c-3fe9e16e315e', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/015fcbf8-1dd3-5736-9e4c-3fe9e16e315e'}, {'abstract': 'The Pacific Northwest Ballet presented four world premieres, three ballets by dancers in the company and one by the Seattle-born Mark Morris.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/arts/dance/pacific-northwest-ballet-in-seattle-mark-morris-premiere.html', 'snippet': 'The Pacific Northwest Ballet presented four world premieres, three ballets by dancers in the company and one by the Seattle-born Mark Morris.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEATTLE — Few ballet companies anywhere can afford the risk of presenting a quadruple bill of world premieres. But Pacific Northwest Ballet, in its 40th-anniversary season, has gone further. A current “All Premiere” program presents not only four new works on the troupe’s main stage, at the Marion Oliver McCaw Hall, but three are by dancers in the company (Andrew Bartee, Margaret Mullin, Kiyon Gaines) and one is by the Seattle-born Mark Morris. There’s cause for local pride. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/arts/PACIFIC/PACIFIC-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/arts/PACIFIC/PACIFIC-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1302, 'width': 1799, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/arts/PACIFIC/PACIFIC-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name'