', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/7e4d07e4-b2a1-5c5d-a652-8551d9cdbefb', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/7e4d07e4-b2a1-5c5d-a652-8551d9cdbefb'}, {'abstract': 'More than 23,500 flights were canceled because of Hurricane Sandy, but the air travel system is already drawing near to relatively normal operations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/business/jump-starting-air-travel-after-hurricane-sandy.html', 'snippet': 'More than 23,500 flights were canceled because of Hurricane Sandy, but the air travel system is already drawing near to relatively normal operations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IMAGINE a resourceful young travel agent hunkered down at a crowded, noisy Starbucks, juggling a cellphone, a laptop and a yellow legal pad while struggling to rearrange a business traveler’s trip home from Beijing to Washington, after Hurricane Sandy knocked out power at both home and office. You now have an idea of how operations were going last week at the national headquarters of Ovation Corporate Travel on Fifth Avenue in Lower Manhattan.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/business/Road/Road-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 508, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/business/Road/Road-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 508, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/business/Road/Road-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/06/business/Road/Road-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/business/Road/Road-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jump-Starting Air Travel After Everything Went Dark', 'kicker': 'On the Road', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jump-Starting Air Travel After Everything Went Dark', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRLINES AND AIRPLANES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Business Travel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-05T23:55:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Sharkey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sharkey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eb8693e7-d6ac-52ea-92f5-75259a49beaa', 'word_count': 869, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eb8693e7-d6ac-52ea-92f5-75259a49beaa'}, {'abstract': 'The company said it earned just less than $2 billion in revenue in the third quarter, up 10 percent from the same period last year.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/05/live-nation-reports-strong-quarterly-earnings/', 'snippet': 'The company said it earned just less than $2 billion in revenue in the third quarter, up 10 percent from the same period last year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two years ago, the concert industry was shaken by the thought that it was not, as it had long believed, “recession-proof.” After a 15-year run of steadily growing returns, ticket sales in 2010 plunged 15 percent and caused the cancellations of a number of prominent tours.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Live Nation Reports Strong Quarterly Earnings', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rapino, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Live Nation Entertainment Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ticketmaster', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-05T23:59:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc6c910b-0e7d-5367-bb98-38ebaa07c596', 'word_count': 390, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc6c910b-0e7d-5367-bb98-38ebaa07c596'}, {'abstract': 'The reality show “Start-Ups: Silicon Valley” on Bravo was uniformly panned by critics, who thought it offered few insights into how the Valley really worked, and was not quite grotesque enough to be entertaining.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/05/review-roundup-the-silicon-valley-reality-show/', 'snippet': 'The reality show “Start-Ups: Silicon Valley” on Bravo was uniformly panned by critics, who thought it offered few insights into how the Valley really worked, and was not quite grotesque enough to be entertaining.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Poor Randi Zuckerberg. The reality show that the sister of Mark Zuckerberg is executive-producing, “Start-Ups: Silicon Valley” on Bravo, was overwhelmingly panned by critics. Wow, was it panned. Here are a few examples.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Review Roundup: The Silicon Valley Reality Show', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reality Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bravo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Silicon Valley (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-06T00:23:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damon Darlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Darlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77cf009f-dba1-561b-99ee-29b54c563186', 'word_count': 481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77cf009f-dba1-561b-99ee-29b54c563186'}, {'abstract': '“I’m a Socialist, but I’m just like everybody else,” a United States Senate candidate says.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/05/socialist-party-candidate-in-new-jersey-calls-his-platform-not-so-radical/', 'snippet': '“I’m a Socialist, but I’m just like everybody else,” a United States Senate candidate says.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’m a Socialist, but I’m just like everybody else,” the Senate candidate said. “The only difference is I believe in universal health care, a more fair public education system and in a living-wage amendment. I wouldn’t exactly call those ideas crazy or nuanced.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Socialist Party Candidate Calls His Platform Not So Radical', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pason, Greg', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Socialist Party USA', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-06T00:23:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel A. Medina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Medina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d51de94f-2efc-5759-a084-26642e079c97', 'word_count': 583, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d51de94f-2efc-5759-a084-26642e079c97'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Dukakis spent his 79th birthday on Saturday chasing a grown man down the street. The reason? To capture one more vote for Elizabeth Warren, the Democratic candidate for the Senate.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/05/in-the-final-days-of-the-campaign-dukakis-79-knocks-on-doors-for-democrats/', 'snippet': 'Michael Dukakis spent his 79th birthday on Saturday chasing a grown man down the street. The reason? To capture one more vote for Elizabeth Warren, the Democratic candidate for the Senate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'QUINCY, Mass. — Michael Dukakis spent his 79th birthday on Saturday chasing a grown man down the street. The reason? To capture one more vote for Elizabeth Warren, the Democratic candidate for the Senate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Final Days of the Campaign, Dukakis, 79, Knocks on Doors for Democrats', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Senate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, State Legislature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value