_date': '2012-11-06T18:09:31+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/8174abf4-6d53-5582-9084-9425d4cae5f4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/8174abf4-6d53-5582-9084-9425d4cae5f4'}, {'abstract': 'By dramatically slowing the game down, combined with the devastating efficiency they’ve displayed on offense, the Knicks are creating the equivalent of a ball-control, run-first football team.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/the-knicks-new-mantra-take-it-slow/', 'snippet': 'By dramatically slowing the game down, combined with the devastating efficiency they’ve displayed on offense, the Knicks are creating the equivalent of a ball-control, run-first football team.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In stark contrast to the 2011-12 season — a roller coaster ride of dizzying highs and gut-wrenching lows — and an off-season featuring shocking departures and the nostalgia-laced return of old faces, the Knicks’ first two games of 2012 have been a showcase for on-court dominance, led by a bruising defense and an unselfish, pass-happy offense (including everyone’s favorite former ball-stopper, Carmelo Anthony). An early key to this brief era of good feeling is that, in a dramatic reversal from seasons past, the Knicks are the slowest team in the league.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/sports/basketball/06woodson/06woodson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/06/sports/basketball/06woodson/06woodson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1679, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'le