ooding a big hurricane surge could cause in New York City long before Hurricane Sandy hit, reflects in this video on the impacts on the region — including on his own storm-flooded home in Piermont, N.Y., a tiny town along the Hudson River a few miles north of the George Washington Bridge. (The video was shot by the university.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Scientist Who Foresaw New York Storm Surge Reflects from His Flooded Home', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disasters and Emergencies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricanes and Tropical Storms', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insurance', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Safina, Carl', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Piermont (NY)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-06T21:52:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b5a9cb4-cf36-56ab-b181-42577c9c5dd1', 'word_count': 462, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b5a9cb4-cf36-56ab-b181-42577c9c5dd1'}, {'abstract': '“Take Note” was a conference at Harvard that centered on the different ways people have jotted down bits of information through the centuries.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/books/note-taking-seminars-at-radcliffe-institute-for-advanced-study.html', 'snippet': '“Take Note” was a conference at Harvard that centered on the different ways people have jotted down bits of information through the centuries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAMBRIDGE, Mass. — Every day in lecture halls across the country students sit quietly taking notes on laptops and in old-fashioned notebooks, or at least pretending to. But last Friday some 250 academics and civilians gathered at Harvard for a more self-conscious exercise: a chance to take notes on note-taking. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/07NOTE/07NOTE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/07NOTE/07NOTE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/07NOTE/07NOTE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 652, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/07NOTE/07NOTE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1304, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/07NOTE/07NOTE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/07NOTE/07NOTE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/07NOTE/07NOTE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Note-Taking’s Past, Deciphered Today', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Note-Taking’s Past, Deciphered Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cambridge (Mass)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-06T21:57:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6e36a80-4b89-5ba3-95eb-19235f7331b6', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6e36a80-4b89-5ba3-95eb-19235f7331b6'}, {'abstract': 'As other parts of Manhattan took steps to return to normal operations, normalcy in Chelsea remained an abstraction. There was too much salvage work to be done – and fast.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/artifacts-chelseas-new-normal/', 'snippet': 'As other parts of Manhattan took steps to return to normal operations, normalcy in Chelsea remained an abstraction. There was too much salvage work to be done – and fast.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Friday, when lights in downtown Manhattan began powering on and subway service was partly restored, the West Chelsea gallery district was still a disaster area. During the night of Hurricane Sandy, a veritable tsunami from the Hudson River literally drowned galleries located nearest to 11th Avenue, between West 19th and West 27th Street. The rush of water was so violent that it warped iron gates as if they were cardboard, tore off walls and flooded basements and back rooms where many galleries stored their inventories.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artifacts | Chelsea’s New Normal', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-06T22:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Yablonsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yablonsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f6f8377-c2c6-573e-99c3-a439c7a919b6', 'word_count': 1558, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f6f8377-c2c6-573e-99c3-a439c7a919b6'}, {'abstract': 'After Hurricane Sandy knocked out power in the Northeast, a New York start-up came up with a good publicity stunt: light a fire so people could charge their dead cellphones.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/biolite-stove/', 'snippet': 'After Hurricane Sandy knocked out power in the Northeast, a New York start-up came up with a good publicity stunt: light a fire so people could charge their dead cellphones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After Hurricane Sandy knocked out power in the Northeast, a New York start-up came up with a good publicity stunt: light a fire so people could charge their dead cellphones.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0,