/8b8ff128-bc01-5f47-ba02-6fe58a01bdea', 'word_count': 333, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b8ff128-bc01-5f47-ba02-6fe58a01bdea'}, {'abstract': 'In case you hadn’t noticed, we’re in an unusual period of our political history.', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/the-80s-want-their-politics-back-the-1880s/', 'snippet': 'In case you hadn’t noticed, we’re in an unusual period of our political history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many Americans may feel that on their lifetime the country has never been more closely divided. And they would be right, at least on the national electoral front. Our politics are now going back to the ’80s — the 1880s.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The ’80s Want Their Politics Back – the 1880s', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-06T22:14:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By JOSHUA SPIVAK', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SPIVAK', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad992468-89cf-52a1-bb15-207ef77a3ded', 'word_count': 718, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad992468-89cf-52a1-bb15-207ef77a3ded'}, {'abstract': 'The winner of the presidential election will claim the White House, but the loser could find a lucrative job in high finance by providing advice on deals or arranging speaking engagements.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/wall-street-offers-a-second-career-for-former-politicians/', 'snippet': 'The winner of the presidential election will claim the White House, but the loser could find a lucrative job in high finance by providing advice on deals or arranging speaking engagements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you wake up on Wednesday morning and find out whether Mitt Romney or Barack Obama will be in the White House next year, don’t pity the loser. He can always have a future career in high finance.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wall Street Offers a Second Career for Former Politicians', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Wall Street, a Lucrative Second Career Awaits Former Politicians', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Public Speaking', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blair, Tony', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Bill', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gore, Al', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Palin, Sarah', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quayle, Dan', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-06T22:23:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Davidoff Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': 'Davidoff', 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b3700fd-ff1f-57fd-841b-69a64d1a1123', 'word_count': 1202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b3700fd-ff1f-57fd-841b-69a64d1a1123'}, {'abstract': 'On Monday, the Internet radio service sued Ascap, one of the United States’ major performing rights organizations, to lower royalty rates.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/pandora-opens-a-new-front-in-its-royalty-war/', 'snippet': 'On Monday, the Internet radio service sued Ascap, one of the United States’ major performing rights organizations, to lower royalty rates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pandora Media is already engaged in one tense war with the music industry over its royalty rates. With a new lawsuit, it has expanded that war to two fronts, underscoring how critical royalties are to Pandora’s business — as well as to the artists and music groups that accuse the company of asking for a break at their expense.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pandora Opens a New Front in Its Royalty War', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Royalties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN SOCIETY OF COMPOSERS, AUTHORS AND PUBLISHERS', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pandora Media Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-06T22:25:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/123dd3af-bad1-5029-b9c4-cfbe2a90c45e', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/123dd3af-bad1-5029-b9c4-cfbe2a90c45e'}, {'abstract': 'A piano recital by Murray Perahia, displaced by the hurricane, comes to life at Avery Fisher Hall in Lincoln Center, far from the dangling crane boom that canceled his Carnegie Hall performance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/arts/music/murray-perahia-plays-at-avery-fisher-hall.html', 'snippet': 'A piano recital by Murray Perahia, displaced by the hurricane, comes to life at Avery Fisher Hall in Lincoln Center, far from the dangling crane boom that canceled his Carnegie Hall performance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Admirers of the pianist Murray Perahia in the New York area owe him thanks for sticking around the city during the difficult days after Hurricane Sandy. His recital at Carnegie Hall scheduled for last Friday night was canceled because of the dangling crane boom on the construction site of a new high-rise on West 57th Street, which forced Carnegie Hall to relocate, reschedule or simply cancel its offerings. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/MURRAY/MURRAY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/MURRAY/MURRAY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1509, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/MURRAY/MURRAY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/MURRAY/MURRAY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/MURRAY/MURRAY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Duets for Piano and Crane Boom', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Duets for Piano and Crane Boom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perahia, Murray', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-06T22:27:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/025741ee-9c0d-53f9-87fa-90f8fa7b9dd1', 'word_count': 500, 'uri': 'nyt://article/025741ee-9c0d-53f9-87fa-90f8fa7b9dd1'}, {'abstract': 'Things only a nerd could care about.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/06/trivial-stakes/', 'snippet': 'Things only a nerd could care about.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No, not the election as a whole — the stakes are huge: whether near-universal health care finally comes to America, whether we get any kind of tightening of financial regulation after the bankers destroyed the world economy. But there are a couple of trivial things I probably shouldn’t care about given the really important stuff, but can’t help thinking about.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Trivial Stakes', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-06T22:29:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organizatio