son? Can Tony Romo turn the Cowboys around? Judy Battista, the N.F.L. reporter for The Times, will answer questions from readers this week. Use the comment field below for your questions. Answers will appear Friday on The Fifth Down.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Have N.F.L. Questions? Judy Battista Has Answers', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-07T19:09:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/708e5407-4830-5310-9cf1-ed49fc1f78ab', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/708e5407-4830-5310-9cf1-ed49fc1f78ab'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans search for an electoral path forward. | Changes in store for Obama’s cabinet in the second term. | Will Republicans and Democrats be able to compromise?', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/timescast-politics-attention-shifts-to-washington/', 'snippet': 'Republicans search for an electoral path forward. | Changes in store for Obama’s cabinet in the second term. | Will Republicans and Democrats be able to compromise?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Associated Press', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TimesCast Politics: Attention Shifts to Washington', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-07T19:13:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e90ea42e-c53a-593c-8780-b7f059e68de7', 'word_count': 47, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e90ea42e-c53a-593c-8780-b7f059e68de7'}, {'abstract': 'What did G.O.P. donors get for their munificence? Nothing. Barack Obama is still president. The Democrats still control the Senate.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/saving-wealthy-donors-from-themselves/', 'snippet': 'What did G.O.P. donors get for their munificence? Nothing. Barack Obama is still president. The Democrats still control the Senate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American Crossroads and Restore Our Future, the largest pro-G.O.P. super PACS, together spent more than $400 million on federal races. Sheldon Adelson and his wife, Miriam, spent at least $53 million backing first Newt Gingrich, then Mitt Romney, as well as several Republican Senate and Congressional candidates, nearly all of whom lost. It’s not clear how much Charles and David Koch spent on the election, since it’s not known how much the brothers gave to so-called dark-money groups, but they reportedly pledged $60 million to defeat President Obama.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Saving Wealthy Donors From Themselves', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gingrich, Newt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch, Charles G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koch, David H', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-07T19:17:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39a7883c-9e7c-56c5-a286-65de6b61e534', 'word_count': 347, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39a7883c-9e7c-56c5-a286-65de6b61e534'}, {'abstract': 'There are not likely to be any new, sweeping investigations involving white-collar crime in President Obama’s second term, but prominent cases involving the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act and the fallout from the 2008 financial crisis loom.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/investigations-to-watch-in-a-second-term/', 'snippet': 'There are not likely to be any new, sweeping investigations involving white-collar crime in President Obama’s second term, but prominent cases involving the Foreign Corrupt Practices Act and the fallout from the 2008 financial crisis loom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In any election, new politics and policies are often debated in much detail. The impact of the presidential election on law enforcement will be more subtle, however, especially after there was little discussion about policing the markets during the campaign.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inquiries to Watch in a Second Term', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Corrupt Practices Act (1977)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-07T19:20:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c544132-c845-55a5-9954-f5f06a734dd6', 'word_count': 1128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c544132-c845-55a5-9954-f5f06a734dd6'}, {'abstract': 'The ArtFutura festival, beginning today in Montevideo, Uruguay, and 12 other Spanish-speaking cities from Barcelona to Buenos Aires, reflects the belief that unlike other art movements throughout history, digital art is uniquely global.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/07/seurat-painted-dots-these-artists-use-bits/', 'snippet': 'The ArtFutura festival, beginning today in Montevideo, Uruguay, and 12 other Spanish-speaking cities from Barcelona to Buenos Aires, reflects the belief that unlike other art movements throughout history, digital art is uniquely global.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It has become a trope over the past 20 years that we are turning into cyborgs. Ever reliant on smartphones and social networks, much of human existence has become inseparable from and defined by digital technology. If this is true, why should art be any different? In 2012, has art too become cyborg? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/7rdv-futuraa/7rdv-futuraa-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/arts/7rdv-futuraa/7rdv-futuraa-superJumbo.jpg', 'heigh