m/2012/11/08/test-yourself-english-nov-8-2012/', 'snippet': 'Can you choose which of the two homophones correctly completes the sentence?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question was written by Shannon Doyne. It comes from the article “Earliest Americans Arrived in Waves, DNA Study Finds.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | English, Nov. 8, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T09:31:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff7bf67c-450b-56a5-872e-17bc60d9f76a', 'word_count': 48, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff7bf67c-450b-56a5-872e-17bc60d9f76a'}, {'abstract': 'Friendly ties between Israel and Turkey soured after a botched raid on a ship trying to break the blockade of Gaza in 2010. Will a trial now spoil them for good?', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/turkish-israeli-relations-on-trial/', 'snippet': 'Friendly ties between Israel and Turkey soured after a botched raid on a ship trying to break the blockade of Gaza in 2010. Will a trial now spoil them for good?', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISTANBUL — In the early hours of May 31, 2010, Israeli commandos rappelled onto the deck of a Turkish ship, the Mavi Marmara, as it was about to run a blockade of the Gaza Strip. The result was a tragic mess. Nine Turkish passengers died in the fray. Turkey’s pragmatic friendship with Israel fell apart, endangering one of the few relationships in the Middle East that could help to stabilize the region.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkish-Israeli Relations on Trial', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T09:34:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Finkel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea4594d8-5f9b-5b3d-a570-94d4009617c0', 'word_count': 832, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea4594d8-5f9b-5b3d-a570-94d4009617c0'}, {'abstract': 'In Elizabeth Warren, Massachusetts has sent to the Senate a forceful advocate for consumers who is willing to stand up to the financial sector, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/the-importance-of-elizabeth-warren/', 'snippet': 'In Elizabeth Warren, Massachusetts has sent to the Senate a forceful advocate for consumers who is willing to stand up to the financial sector, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Johnson is the Ronald A. Kurtz Professor of Entrepreneurship at the M.I.T. Sloan School of Management and co-author of “White House Burning: The Founding Fathers, Our National Debt, and Why It Matters to You.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Importance of Elizabeth Warren', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Senate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T10:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4dc24e35-614b-5e42-a18c-5144898a4c41', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4dc24e35-614b-5e42-a18c-5144898a4c41'}, {'abstract': 'Mentoring and workshops have helped give local photographers, in Egypt and around the world, the time and resources to document their society’s issues with the delicacy and insight that might go unnoticed by foreigners.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/an-inside-view-on-documentary-stories/', 'snippet': 'Mentoring and workshops have helped give local photographers, in Egypt and around the world, the time and resources to document their society’s issues with the delicacy and insight that might go unnoticed by foreigners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was plenty of spot news for photographers to cover in Cairo in early 2011, when a popular uprising challenged decades of authoritarian rule. For Jessica Murray, the changes sweeping over politics and society were as much an opportunity to challenge another entrenched notion — how local photographers documented the world around them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Inside View on Documentary Stories', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berman, Nina (1960- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yenkin, Amy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Coptic Orthodox Church of Alexandria', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cairo (Egypt)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tahrir Square (Cairo)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T10:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7eb51c76-cdfd-5865-a383-9ea1bd93d4e4', 'word_count': 1193, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7eb51c76-cdfd-5865-a383-9ea1bd93d4e4'}, {'abstract': 'Is your bedroom your kingdom? How clean or messy is it? Does your mess make your parents crazy?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/is-your-bedroom-a-nightmare/', 'snippet': 'Is your bedroom your kingdom? How clean or messy is it? Does your mess make your parents crazy?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is your bedroom “an archaeological site of dirty socks, sports-drink bottles, ripped papers and empty boxes of Cheez-Its” like that of one of the teenagers described in this article? If so, does it make your parents crazy? How often do you clean it up? Does the mess bother you? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/01/garden/01MESSY_SPAN-LN/1JP-MESSY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/01/garden/01MESSY_SPAN-LN/1JP-MESSY1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/01/garden/01MESSY_SPAN-LN/1JP-MESSY1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/01/garden/01MESSY_SPAN-LN/1JP-MESSY1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/01/garden/01MESSY_SPAN-LN/01MESSY_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/01/garden/01MESSY_SPAN-LN/01MESSY_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/01/garden/01MESSY_SPAN-LN/01MESSY_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Your Bedroom a Nightmare?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T10:06:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d697acff-8d75-5577-91b6-7351ef24b778', 'word_count': 408, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d697acff-8d75-5577-91b6-7351ef24b778'}, {'abstract': 'The latest James Bond movie opens in American movie theaters Thursday, having already made a lap or two around the world. Have you seen it yet? Where did you see it, and what did you think?', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/skyfall-lands-finally-in-the-u-s/', 'snippet': 'The latest James Bond movie opens in American movie theaters Thursday, having already made a lap or two around the world. Have you seen it yet? Where did you see it, and what did you think?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beyond the festival circuit, moviegoers in Europe are often playing catchup with Americans, waiting weeks and sometimes months for films to arrive at the local cinema. Not so with the latest James Bond film “Skyfall,” which began its sweep — a victorious one — with a premiere in London on Oct. 23 and opened in dozens of countries around the world over the course of the next several days. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/08/arts/SKYFALL/SKYFALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/08/arts/SKYFALL/SKYFALL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/08/arts/SKYFALL/SKYFALL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/08/arts/SKYFALL/SKYFALL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1482, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/08/arts/SKYFALL/SKYFALL-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/08/arts/SKYFALL/SKYFALL-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/08/arts/SKYFALL/SKYFALL-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Skyfall’ Lands, Finally, in the U.S.', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bond, James (Fictional Character)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Craig, Daniel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dench, Judi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mendes, Sam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Skyfall (Movie)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T10:50:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jan Benzel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benzel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19153c86-4d3f-5576-9714-d7ba80aac1fc', 'word_count': 533, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19153c86-4d3f-5576-9714-d7ba80aac1fc'}, {'abstract': 'The confidence we can get when modeling relatively simple things, like an N.F.L. game, can lead to overconfidence when applying the same techniques beyond sports.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/week-10-n-f-l-game-probabilities-dont-be-so-sure/', 'snippet': 'The confidence we can get when modeling relatively simple things, like an N.F.L. game, can lead to overconfidence when applying the same techniques beyond sports.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Analytics have become a major part of political campaigns. President Obama’s re-election owes much to a small army of analysts who targeted potential voters and donors in ever narrowing slices, matching messages containing certain styles and emphasis with particular demographic audiences.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Week 10 N.F.L. Game Probabilities: Don’t Be So Sure', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T11:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fe75898-635b-5986-9a23-c0dd717be806', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fe75898-635b-5986-9a23-c0dd717be806'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/the-early-word-moving-along/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times, plus a look at what’s happening in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Today’s Times:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: Moving Along', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T11:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Southall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Southall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c34718a-d668-597e-8718-47cfbcc16360', 'word_count': 406, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c34718a-d668-597e-8718-47cfbcc16360'}, {'abstract': 'That the demands of gay rights activists in Argentina are now so broad and vague is proof of how far they’ve come.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/gays-in-argentina-revel-in-their-gains/', 'snippet': 'That the demands of gay rights activists in Argentina are now so broad and vague is proof of how far they’ve come.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BUENOS AIRES — While Argentina was getting ready for a big protest against the government on Thursday, I found myself thinking about the more cheerful march that will fill the streets of Buenos Aires on Saturday. Tens of thousands of people are expected to get together for Argentina’s 21st annual gay pride march down the Avenida de Mayo, and though again this year they will make demands, the gathering should also be a celebration of just how much things have improved for sexual minorities here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gay Rights Done Right', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parades', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transgender and Transsexual', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirchner, Cristina Fernandez de', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Buenos Aires (Argentina)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T11:58:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Politi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Politi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e7f9173-9d07-5dfb-a351-1fca0eba1628', 'word_count': 510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e7f9173-9d07-5dfb-a351-1fca0eba1628'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Crispin Miller of N.Y.U. argues that relying on electronic voting machines operated by private corporations invites fraud and poses a danger to democracy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/nyregion/professor-skeptical-about-integrity-of-electoral-process.html', 'snippet': 'Mark Crispin Miller of N.Y.U. argues that relying on electronic voting machines operated by private corporations invites fraud and poses a danger to democracy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Monday afternoon, just half a day before the polls opened, Prof. Mark Crispin Miller was feeling pessimistic about the electoral process. Actually, he had been feeling pessimistic for years, certainly since Senator John Kerry lost — or as Professor Miller tells it, since the credulous citizens of the United States were told that he lost, and the lily-livered news media swallowed that story hook, line and sinker. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Day for a Professor Suspicious of Voting Machines', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Long Day for a Professor Suspicious of Voting Machines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York University', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting Machines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Mark Crispin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T12:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariel Kaminer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaminer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/474bba44-1f5d-5c55-8acb-1425d99abf28', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/474bba44-1f5d-5c55-8acb-1425d99abf28'}, {'abstract': 'It’s apple-drinking season.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/magazine/turn-your-autumn-apples-into-cocktails.html', 'snippet': 'It’s apple-drinking season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Late autumn always feels folksy to me, if not downright pagan. Halloween has something to do with it, sure, but it goes deeper than that: it’s all dead leaves, fast-darkening skies, surges of wind and lashings of rain. The season sounds like Sandy Denny singing spooky old ballads about changelings, betrayal and revenge. It smells like damp firewood slowly drying. (No wonder I want to stay under the blankets reading Frost and Yeats.) And late fall definitely tastes like apples.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11drink1/11drink1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 468, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11drink1/11drink1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 468}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11drink1/11drink1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 999, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11drink1/11drink1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1014, 'width': 999, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11drink1/11drink1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11drink1/11drink1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11drink1/11drink1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Enough With the Pumpkins', 'kicker': 'Drink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Enough With The Pumpkins', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Seasons and Months', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'APPLES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T12:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rosie Schaap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rosie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71ace281-fa64-51ff-8462-600d573ba17e', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71ace281-fa64-51ff-8462-600d573ba17e'}, {'abstract': 'Here’s to Thanksgiving dressing, the potent outside voice of stuffing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/magazine/thanksgiving-dressing-the-outside-voice-of-stuffing.html', 'snippet': 'Here’s to Thanksgiving dressing, the potent outside voice of stuffing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question has dogged Thanksgiving cooks practically since Norman Rockwell painted “Freedom From Want” in 1942 and transformed the harvest feast into an American ideal: Is it stuffing or dressing?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '56', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11eat1/11eat1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11eat1/11eat1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 449}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11eat1/11eat1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11eat1/11eat1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1123, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11eat1/11eat1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11eat1/11eat1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11eat1/11eat1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Best Dressed', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Best Dressed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thanksgiving Day', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T12:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Sifton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sifton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee0db713-af96-5cfe-8ce2-63913d55d8ab', 'word_count': 501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee0db713-af96-5cfe-8ce2-63913d55d8ab'}, {'abstract': 'The uplifting story of the Oklahoma City Thunder may have suffered an agonizing plot twist, but just try to find someone in town who doesn’t still believe in a happy ending.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/magazine/the-oklahoma-city-thunders-fairy-tale-rise.html', 'snippet': 'The uplifting story of the Oklahoma City Thunder may have suffered an agonizing plot twist, but just try to find someone in town who doesn’t still believe in a happy ending.', 'lead_paragraph': 'N.B.A. scoring champions are, as a rule, weirdos and reprobates and in some cases diagnosable sociopaths. Something about dominating your opponent, publicly, more or less every day of your life, in the most visible aspect of your sport, tends to either warp your spirit or to be possible only to those whose spirits are already warped. Michael Jordan, when he wasn’t busy scoring, was busy punching a teammate in the face and gambling away small fortunes. Allen Iverson, in his spare time, recorded an aesthetically and morally terrible rap album and gave an iconic speech denigrating the very notion of practice. Kobe Bryant is and shall forever be Kobe Bryant. Wilt, Shaq, Pistol Pete, Dominique, McGrady, McAdoo, Rick Barry — it’s a near-solid roster of dysfunction: sadists, narcissists, malcontents, knuckleheads, misanthropes, womanizers, addicts and villains. While it’s true that plain old N.B.A. superstars do occasionally manage to be model citizens (cf. Tim Duncan, Grant Hill, Steve Nash), there is something irredeemable about a scoring champion.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/okc_game-slide-Q1J2/okc_game-slide-Q1J2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/okc_game-slide-Q1J2/okc_game-slide-Q1J2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/okc_game-slide-Q1J2/okc_game-slide-Q1J2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/okc_game-slide-Q1J2/okc_game-slide-Q1J2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/okc_game-slide-Q1J2/okc_game-slide-Q1J2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/okc_game-slide-Q1J2/okc_game-slide-Q1J2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/okc_game-slide-Q1J2/okc_game-slide-Q1J2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Basketball Fairy Tale in Middle America', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Oklahoma City Game', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Westbrook, Russell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City (Okla)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Presti, Sam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Durant, Kevin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coyne, Wayne', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brooks, Scott', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City Thunder', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T12:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/734efa4c-5e1b-5125-883c-4eae4d6f2bca', 'word_count': 7699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/734efa4c-5e1b-5125-883c-4eae4d6f2bca'}, {'abstract': 'Every month that business owners spend hunting for money instead of developing and marketing their product or service, they are wasting valuable time.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/the-genius-of-starting-a-company-without-outside-capital/', 'snippet': 'Every month that business owners spend hunting for money instead of developing and marketing their product or service, they are wasting valuable time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I wonder how many millions of small-business owners and entrepreneurs are sitting out there at this very moment, thinking about the money they need to finance their companies and ideas. The approach an owner chooses can make or break a business. It’s a decision that can change your life.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Genius of Starting a Company Without Outside Capital', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T12:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ami Kassar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ami', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kassar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ed6ed0d-22d4-51c2-b35b-4be5d20c53dd', 'word_count': 703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ed6ed0d-22d4-51c2-b35b-4be5d20c53dd'}, {'abstract': 'The French and Belgian governments said on Thursday that they would inject an additional $7 billion into the troubled bank Dexia.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/dexia-gets-new-5-5-billion-bailout/', 'snippet': 'The French and Belgian governm