ite-working-class-angst.html', 'snippet': 'John Updike’s 1971 novel “Rabbit Redux” remains the most illuminating and prophetic of modern political novels, though on the surface it seems not about politics at all.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the long election night began to take shape, all eyes remained fixed on the swing states. Some were on the Eastern Seaboard (Florida, Virginia), others in the West (Colorado, Nevada). In most cases the outcome hinged on the marshaling of dependable blocs of voters.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/18tanenhaus-img/18tanenhaus-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 351, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/18tanenhaus-img/18tanenhaus-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 351}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/18tanenhaus-img/18tanenhaus-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 984, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/18tanenhaus-img/18tanenhaus-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 984, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/18tanenhaus-img/18tanenhaus-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/18tanenhaus-img/18tanenhaus-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/18tanenhaus-img/18tanenhaus-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 135, 'width': 136, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Man in the Middle', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Updike, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T13:59:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Tanenhaus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanenhaus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58e54ac1-4663-5cf6-801e-ee157e588ce7', 'word_count': 1293, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58e54ac1-4663-5cf6-801e-ee157e588ce7'}, {'abstract': 'The Japanese-made Wave dripper from Kalita has a delicate heft, an elegant balance, and makes great coffee.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/ristretto-catch-the-wave/', 'snippet': 'The Japanese-made Wave dripper from Kalita has a delicate heft, an elegant balance, and makes great coffee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A recent addition to the no-fly list of overused phrases is “feels good in the hand,” a cogent collection of words worried into a cliché by tech bloggers writing about the tactile allure of their new favorite tablets and iPhones. If you go by the breathless accounts, everything made by Apple since 2008 feels really good. I have used the phrase in the past, and while I reserve the right to use it again in the future I’m not going to work the line into this column. I’ll find other ways to explain why I like brewing coffee with gear from Kalita, a Japanese manufacturer that opened shop in 1958 but whose products are only just starting to appear on these shores.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ristretto | Catch the Wave', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coffee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T14:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Oliver Strand', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Oliver', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strand', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f3112a8-6c24-59a4-8547-250a2049b30c', 'word_count': 781, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f3112a8-6c24-59a4-8547-250a2049b30c'}, {'abstract': 'The opposition was locked in extended bartering over the creation of a more diverse yet unified umbrella organization, as President Bashar al-Assad warned against foreign intervention in a rare interview.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/world/middleeast/syria-war-developments.html', 'snippet': 'The opposition was locked in extended bartering over the creation of a more diverse yet unified umbrella organization, as President Bashar al-Assad warned against foreign intervention in a rare interview.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DOHA, Qatar — The quarrelsome Syrian opposition was locked in extended bartering here in Doha on Thursday over the creation of a more diverse yet unified umbrella organization that its foreign backers hope will become a credible alternative to the Damascus government.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/world/syria/syria-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 796, 'width': 1024, '