ra, “La Clemenza di Tito,” being revived at the Met, has been subjected to harsh scrutiny. It turns out to be stranger and deeper than we have been told.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR much of its history Mozart’s final opera, “La Clemenza di Tito,” which was mostly composed after “Die Zauberflöte” but performed before it, has been thought to be, frankly, not so good.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11INTO_SPAN/11INTO_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11INTO_SPAN/11INTO_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11INTO_SPAN/11INTO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 636, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11INTO_SPAN/11INTO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1272, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11INTO_SPAN/11INTO_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11INTO_SPAN/11INTO_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11INTO_SPAN/11INTO_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Enduring Appeal of Mozart’s Oddball Opera', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Enduring Appeal of Mozart’s Oddball Opera', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'La Clemenza di Tito (Opera)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mozart, Wolfgang Amadeus', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T14:49:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aab535a3-8dc6-50ac-b806-3cc5bc4fd817', 'word_count': 1009, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aab535a3-8dc6-50ac-b806-3cc5bc4fd817'}, {'abstract': 'When Trey McIntyre Project performs at the Brooklyn Academy of Music this week, it will feature the culmination of a cultural diplomacy program with members of the Korea National Contemporary Dance Company.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/arts/dance/trey-mcintyre-project-collaborates-with-korean-dancers.html', 'snippet': 'When Trey McIntyre Project performs at the Brooklyn Academy of Music this week, it will feature the culmination of a cultural diplomacy program with members of the Korea National Contemporary Dance Company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN three South Korean dancers arrived in Boise, Idaho, a few weeks ago, they, along with a few other lucky airport visitors, were met with an unusual sight. As the women descended the escalator from baggage claim, the electropop sounds of “Gangnam Style,” the virally popular song-and-dance craze by the South Korean rapper Psy, greeted them, along with a group exuberantly performing its accompanying dance steps. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11TRE1_SPAN/11TRE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11TRE1_SPAN/11TRE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11TRE1_SPAN/11TRE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11TRE1_SPAN/11TRE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11TRE1_SPAN/11TRE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11TRE1_SPAN/11TRE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11TRE1_SPAN/11TRE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pacific Crossings And Other Choreography', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pacific Crossings And Other Choreography', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'McIntyre, Trey, Project', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T14:50:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Milzoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Milzoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b03d88e2-0039-5952-adfe-64a8fcc6d171', 'word_count': 1159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b03d88e2-0039-5952-adfe-64a8fcc6d171'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of cultural events.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/arts/nov-11-17.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of cultural events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I HAVE argued that the plays of Anton Chekhov are not the wistful, static, tasteful affairs that many modern productions would lead you to believe. Along with being a perceptive observer of the human condition, he was a theatrical showman. Still, I confess that I never imagined a circus about the author of “The Seagull.” Yet “DONKA: A LETTER TO CHEKHOV,” which opens Wednesday at the Brooklyn Academy of Music, features acrobatics, jugglers, clowning and ribbons falling from the sky.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11GUIDE5_SPAN/11GUIDE5_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11GUIDE5_SPAN/11GUIDE5_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11GUIDE5_SPAN/11GUIDE5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, '