2012/11/08/clinton-seeks-intelligence-reports-on-wildlife-traffickers/', 'snippet': 'Hillary Clinton describes new tactics, including the use of intelligence agencies, in the fight against global wildlife trafficking.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Obama administration, and the State Department in particular, face a long list of urgent issues, from Iran’s nuclear weapons program to trade disputes with China. That makes it hard to maintain a focus on the environment, whether the issue is new approaches to climate diplomacy or endangered species.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Clinton Seeks Intelligence Help in War on Wildlife Traffickers', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T22:18:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc5ebbb1-ce4d-5891-ae4c-61d4a771ffbe', 'word_count': 982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc5ebbb1-ce4d-5891-ae4c-61d4a771ffbe'}, {'abstract': 'The Salon: Art + Design is an art fair at the Park Avenue Armory with works from 53 exhibitors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/arts/design/the-salon-art-design-at-the-park-avenue-armory.html', 'snippet': 'The Salon: Art + Design is an art fair at the Park Avenue Armory with works from 53 exhibitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How do artworks and furniture from the 16th century to the present get into museums? Most international survey museums were built upon the existing collections of aristocrats or the donations of wealthy patrons. But new acquisitions often go through art dealers, and an art fair like The Salon: Art + Design is the kind of place where you might see a few before they find institutional homes. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09SALON2/09SALON2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09SALON2/09SALON2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09SALON2/SALON2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09SALON2/SALON2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09SALON2/09SALON2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09SALON2/09SALON2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09SALON2/09SALON2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Museum in the Making at the Park Avenue Armory', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Museum in the Making at the Park Avenue Armory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Park Avenue Armory (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T22:20:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Schwendener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwendener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd0a7f90-ea1a-5b64-bbdd-d12f595401c1', 'word_count': 775, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd0a7f90-ea1a-5b64-bbdd-d12f595401c1'}, {'abstract': 'Studies that gauge one treatment against another have received a lot of attention and federal support, but doctors have been slow to incorporate the findings, a new analysis shows.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/08/why-studies-that-compare-treatments-lack-impact/', 'snippet': 'Studies that gauge one treatment against another have received a lot of attention and federal support, but doctors have been slow to incorporate the findings, a new analysis shows.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Comparative effectiveness” studies, which compare one treatment for a particular illness against another to determine which works better, have received a lot of attention and billions of dollars in federal support in the last few years. But when I mentioned comparative effectiveness research recently to a colleague who I know is particularly interested in treatments and the clinical trials behind them, he let out a loud snort and guffaw before I even finished saying the words.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190-copy/chen_190-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190-copy/chen_190-copy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190-copy/chen_190-copy-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190-copy/chen_190-copy-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190-copy/chen_190-copy-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190-copy/chen_190-copy-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2008/09/15/health/chen_190-copy/chen_190-copy-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Studies That Compare Treatments Lack Impact', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T22:21:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pauline W. Chen, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pauline', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15339e62-08f0-5111-ae23-797ca8974099', 'word_count': 920, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15339e62-08f0-5111-ae23-797ca8974099'}, {'abstract': 'Dance and film both play roles in “Within (Labyrinth Within),” part of the Morphoses company’s run at the Joyce Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/arts/dance/morphoses-at-the-joyce-theater.html', 'snippet': 'Dance and film both play roles in “Within (Labyrinth Within),” part of the Morphoses company’s run at the Joyce Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Half of “Within (Labyrinth Within),” the hourlong dance that the Morphoses company brought to the Joyce Theater on Wednesday, is a film. It comes second (its title nested within the parentheses of the dance’s title) but chronologically was made first, choreographed and directed by Pontus Lidberg in 2010. Lourdes Lopez, who directs Morphoses, saw the film and asked Mr. Lidberg to add a live component. Considering how seldom marriages of film and live dance work, it’s remarkable how well this one does. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09MORPHOSES_SPAN/MORPHOSES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09MORPHOSES_SPAN/MORPHOSES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09MORPHOSES_SPAN/MORPHOSES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 714, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09MORPHOSES_SPAN/MORPHOSES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1429, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09MORPHOSES_SPAN/MORPHOSES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09MORPHOSES_SPAN/MORPHOSES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09MORPHOSES_SPAN/MORPHOSES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Be It Live or Filmed, Movement Has a Partner In Dreamlike Settings', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Be It Live or Filmed, Movement Has a Partner In Dreamlike Settings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Joyce Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morphoses/The Wheeldon Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-08T22:23:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a78e4a0e-4937-5194-a1a0-3a46614b0b4b', 'word_count': 561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a78e4a0e-4937-5194-a1a0-3a46614b0b4b'}, {'abstract': 'A dramatic account, directed by Nikolaj Arcel, of the affair between the 18th-century physician Johann Friedrich Struensee and the Danish queen Caroline Mathilda.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/movies/a-royal-affair-with-mads-mikkelsen.html', 'snippet': 'A dramatic account, directed by Nikolaj Arcel, of the affair between the 18th-century physician Johann Friedrich Struensee and the Danish queen Caroline Mathilda.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Johann Friedrich Struensee, a German-born 18th-century physician and martyr for liberty, is well known to schoolchildren in Denmark. Analogies are imperfect — this critic’s mastery of Danish history even more so — but Struensee, who died a few years before the start of the American Revolution, might be compared to Thomas Jefferson or Benjamin Franklin. Like other eminent figures of the Age of Reason he was both a politician and a man of science, a philosopher and what we might nowadays call a player.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09ROYAL_SPAN/09ROYAL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09ROYAL_SPAN/09ROYAL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09ROYAL_SPAN/ROYAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 724, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09ROYAL_SPAN/ROYAL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1448, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09ROYAL_SPAN/09ROYAL_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09ROYAL_SPAN/09ROYAL_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/arts/09ROYAL_SPAN/09ROYAL_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'wid