ction_name': 'Giving', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phyllis Korkki', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phyllis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Korkki', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/def94945-73bf-5573-bd48-62ffa92d9c49', 'word_count': 952, 'uri': 'nyt://article/def94945-73bf-5573-bd48-62ffa92d9c49'}, {'abstract': 'People of means are scrambling to take advantage of the significant gift tax exemption which, for now, will expire on Dec. 31 with the rest of the Bush tax cuts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/giving/a-soon-to-end-gift-tax-break-spurs-a-flurry-of-activity.html', 'snippet': 'People of means are scrambling to take advantage of the significant gift tax exemption which, for now, will expire on Dec. 31 with the rest of the Bush tax cuts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN most years, the decision about how much to give to children and grandchildren is not that difficult. The amount that a person can give annually to anyone without having to pay gift tax is $13,000. That is a fairly simple calculation. ', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tax Break on Gifts Soon to End', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tax Break On Gifts Soon to End', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gifts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inheritance and Estate Taxes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Net Worth Individuals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T01:14:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Giving', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af1c527d-6033-50d5-a578-21c67e702e6d', 'word_count': 908, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af1c527d-6033-50d5-a578-21c67e702e6d'}, {'abstract': 'Government agencies, travel-related companies, hotels and other groups are trying to train people to identify and report potential trafficking incidents.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/giving/the-travel-industry-takes-on-human-trafficking.html', 'snippet': 'Government agencies, travel-related companies, hotels and other groups are trying to train people to identify and report potential trafficking incidents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE travel industry — long an unwitting participant in human trafficking at hotels and on airplanes, trains and buses — lately has been increasing efforts to combat the problem, working with private advocacy groups and the federal government in long-term, coordinated initiatives that go beyond its normal philanthropic activities. ', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/giving/TRAVEL/TRAVEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/09/giving/TRAVEL/TRAVEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/09/giving/TRAVEL/TRAVEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 379, 'width': 652, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'