irstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64ecce7d-76fc-5ddb-a06b-eef8bf53f15f', 'word_count': 93, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64ecce7d-76fc-5ddb-a06b-eef8bf53f15f'}, {'abstract': 'The South African township of Soweto — famed for its resistance to apartheid — captured the imagination of Per-Anders Pettersson when he covered South Africa’s first democratic elections in 1994. Nearly twenty years later, its bustle still appeals.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/a-microcosm-of-the-new-south-africa/', 'snippet': 'The South African township of Soweto — famed for its resistance to apartheid — captured the imagination of Per-Anders Pettersson when he covered South Africa’s first democratic elections in 1994. Nearly twenty years later, its bustle still appeals...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Few place names are as burdened with history as Soweto. Nelson Mandela once called it home. The infamous center of resistance to apartheid — South Africa’s entrenched system of white supremacy — the township was a symbol of poverty and oppression on the one hand and heroism and sacrifice on the other. Fittingly, it gave its name to a 1976 uprising of young activists that forever changed the country’s political landscape.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Microcosm of the New South Africa', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Apartheid (Policy)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank':