aunting. I imagine, for instance, stopping a young Ron Hansen on a street corner in his native Omaha sometime in the early 1950s and pulling a modified Frank Capra maneuver. “You will grow up to write books about many things,” I’d tell him, “about Jesse James and Adolf Hitler and a nun named Mariette and the Jesuit poet Gerard Manley Hopkins. You will yourself be ordained as a deacon of the Roman Catholic Church. Now run along and play.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Birkerts/1111-Birkerts-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 893, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Birkerts/1111-Birkerts-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1235, 'width': 1416, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Birkerts/1111-Birkerts-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Birkerts/1111-Birkerts-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Birkerts/1111-Birkerts-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Odes to Omaha', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Odes to Omaha', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hansen, Ron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:35:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sven Birkerts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Birkerts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1eb51f4b-aa35-506b-b1bb-a4f4736d1a32', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1eb51f4b-aa35-506b-b1bb-a4f4736d1a32'}, {'abstract': 'John Banville’s hero stars in a movie version of events long past.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/ancient-light-by-john-banville.html', 'snippet': 'John Banville’s hero stars in a movie version of events long past.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the trompe l’oeil universe of the versatile Irish novelist John Banville, things rarely happen just once. The actual event in a particular intersection of space and time — the secret tryst, the trusted friend betrayed — is filtered through a kaleidoscope of memory and dream, lies and subterfuge, hallucinations and ghosts. In Banville’s latest novel, “Ancient Light,” there is the added distorting mirror of a movie version of events long past. The biopic in which the narrator, an aging actor named Alexander Cleave, is unexpectedly invited to star — landing “this plum part without applying for it, without even an audition” — is called, to hammer home the point, “The Invention of the Past.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Benfey/1111-Benfey-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 891, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',