on_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone and Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9752477-ab45-55bb-900e-bfa09d1bb96d', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9752477-ab45-55bb-900e-bfa09d1bb96d'}, {'abstract': 'A journalist combines the story of his son’s struggle to embrace his sexuality with a report about research on LGBT teenagers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/oddly-normal-by-john-schwartz.html', 'snippet': 'A journalist combines the story of his son’s struggle to embrace his sexuality with a report about research on LGBT teenagers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeanne Mixon, the wife of the New York Times national correspondent John Schwartz, arrived home one afternoon to find their 13-year-old son Joe incoherent, “goggle-eyed” and naked in the bathroom. Pill bottles were strewed on the floor, and a paring knife was sitting in the bathtub. Joe had tried to kill himself. This event — every parent’s nightmare — opens John Schwartz’s memoir, “Oddly Normal,” a deeply affecting account of Joe’s learning to embrace his sexuality, as well as his parents’ efforts to shield him from homophobia and help him endure a school system that continues to marginalize (and even pathologize) kids who need special understanding.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11Sheff-SUB/11Sheff-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11Sheff-SUB/11Sheff-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11Sheff-SUB/11Sheff-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11Sheff-SUB/11Sheff-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11Sheff-SUB/11Sheff-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Something to Tell You', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Something to Tell You', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schwartz, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:38:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Sheff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sheff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6494f198-e132-5de4-9aa0-6f2c96822d6a', 'word_count': 1228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6494f198-e132-5de4-9aa0-6f2c96822d6a'}, {'abstract': 'An unnamed colonial country is threatened by civil war in Katie Kitamura’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/gone-to-the-forest-by-katie-kitamura.html', 'snippet': 'An unnamed colonial country is threatened by civil war in Katie Kitamura’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': "Katie Kitamura's fiction is filled with caged men — men trapped in waning bodies, fighting for professional and psychic survival, fighting for dignity, or at least the appearance of it. In her startling first novel, “The Longshot,” the cage was literal, as an aging mixed martial arts fighter trained to re-enter the ring for a comeback, goaded by his gruff trainer. Kitamura’s new novel, “Gone to the Forest,” also crackles with an aura of tense masculine confinement, but here the cage is figurative, with two white farmers, one young, one old, in an unnamed colonial country battling to come to grips with what will soon be their postcolonial fate.", 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11NIXON/11NIXON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11NIXON/11NIXON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1738, 'width': 1086, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11NIXON/11NIXON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11NIXON/11NIXON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11NIXON/11NIXON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unsettled', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unsettled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kitamura, Katie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:38:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rob Nixon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nixon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbf7eaa0-bf5b-5b30-a0cd-af091fd1c3b8', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbf7eaa0-bf5b-5b30-a0cd-af091fd1c3b8'}, {'abstract': 'Jackson Cannon, a Boston bar director, went to the Netherlands to buy his own cask of so-called Holland gin.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/ordering-a-genever-by-the-barrel/', 'snippet': 'Jackson Cannon, a Boston bar director, went to the Netherlands to buy his own cask of so-called Holland gin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cocktail bars have become so focused on authenticity of ingredients that it has become fairly commonplace for owners or bartenders to travel to American whiskey distilleries to buy their own private barrel. Jackson Cannon, bar director of three Boston boîtes — Eastern Standard, The Hawthorne (where he is also co-owner), and Island Creek Oyster Bar — has done so for years. But no tavern, to Mr. Cannon’s knowledge, has anything like his latest acquisition: a full cask of genever, straight from Holland. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ordering a Genever, by the Barrel', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:38:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Simonson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simonson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf99287d-f782-5261-b84d-87b43431cb60', 'word_count': 361, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf99287d-f782-5261-b84d-87b43431cb60'}, {'abstract': 'Colm Toibin’s novella portrays a Mary we do not recognize from the Gospels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-testament-of-mary-by-colm-toibin.html', 'snippet': 'Colm Toibin’s novella portrays a Mary we do not recognize from the Gospels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mary, the mother of Jesus, has given Christianity a good name. None of the negatives that have made Christianity a byword for tyranny, cruelty and licensed hatred have attached to her. She has been free for centuries of the “blame Mom” syndrome, representing endless patience, loving kindness, mercy, succor, recourse.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11GORDON/11GORDON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11GORDON/11GORDON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1552, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11GORDON/11GORDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 