: 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/10/28/magazine/28cover/28cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 718, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/10/28/magazine/28cover/28cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1052, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/10/28/magazine/28cover/28cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/10/28/magazine/28cover/28cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/10/28/magazine/28cover/28cover-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The 10.28.12 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 10.28.12 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:38:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6cbad18-9ada-522b-9d0c-c6fadf9c3dd4', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6cbad18-9ada-522b-9d0c-c6fadf9c3dd4'}, {'abstract': 'A man-made interpretation of nature’s glue that never wears out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/magazine/who-made-velcro.html', 'snippet': 'A man-made interpretation of nature’s glue that never wears out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1941, a Swiss engineer named George de Mestral returned from a hunting trip with burs clinging to his pants and tangled in his dog’s coat. When de Mestral examined the seedpods under a microscope, he marveled at how they bristled with hooks ingeniously shaped to grasp at animal fur. “Most people stop at the ‘Oh, that’s cool, that’s what nature does,’ ” says Janine Benyus, a pioneer in the field of biomimicry, the science of studying natural models — anthills and lizard feet, say — to solve human problems. “He probably had to go back a lot of times,” she adds, “and really look” at those hooks. A bur, of course, can clamp onto wool socks with surprising force, and — even more amazing — once you pry it off, it can stick again and again, like glue that never wears out. But how to imitate this trick with human-made stuff? Eventually de Mestral learned to mold nylon into a fabric studded with tiny hooks or loops that acted like artificial burs.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11wmt/11wmt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11wmt/11wmt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11wmt/mag-11WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11wmt/mag-11WMT-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1125, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11wmt/11wmt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11wmt/11wmt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11wmt/11wmt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made Velcro?', 'kicker': 'Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Velcro)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Mestral, George', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Velcro Industries', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:38:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pagan Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pagan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86842c5a-b530-5e95-9050-c41d6fb67fb6', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86842c5a-b530-5e95-9050-c41d6fb67fb6'}, {'abstract': 'Does the end justify the means?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/magazine/the-lance-armstrong-conundrum.html', 'snippet': 'Does the end justify the means?', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was recently demonstrated by the U.S. Anti-Doping Agency that Lance Armstrong used performance-enhancing drugs during the seven years when he won the Tour de France. During the same period, Armstrong started Livestrong, a cancer-support organization known for its ubiquitous yellow bracelets. Is the unethical nature of Lance’s doping offset by the fact that his Livestrong organization has touched many lives in a positive way? Is it even right to consider Livestrong in our ethical analysis of Armstrong’s doping? MYRIAH JAWORSKI, WASHINGTON', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11ethicist/11ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11ethicist/11ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11ethicist/11ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11ethicist/11ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11ethicist/11ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Lance Armstrong Conundrum', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Lance Armstrong Conundrum', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMSTRONG, LANCE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tour de France (Bicycle Race)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Steroids', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Armstrong, Lance, Foundation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:38:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/5634cc86-a950-512b-ae96-13973372a75a', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5634cc86-a950-512b-ae96-13973372a75a'}, {'abstract': 'Every modern ruin tells a story — and it’s not always the one you want to hear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/magazine/how-detroit-became-the-world-capital-of-staring-at-abandoned-old-buildings.html', 'snippet': 'Every modern ruin tells a story — and it’s not always the one you want to hear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For decades, a succession of city officials has struggled mightily to rebrand Detroit’s battered image. Their ideas have included casino gambling, an ’80s festival mall, new ballparks, hosting a Formula One grand prix, hosting a Super Bowl, even commissioning (this was Mayor Coleman Young, in 1984) Berry Gordy (who fled Detroit for Los Angeles by the early 1970s, taking the entire Motown operation with him) to write a city theme modeled after Frank Sinatra’s “Theme from New York, New York.” Another member of the Rat Pack, Sammy Davis Jr., was conscripted to handle the vocals, but sadly, Gordy’s song, “Hello, Detroit,” failed to burn up the charts.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11Riff/mag-11Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 83, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11Riff/mag-11Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 83}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11Riff/mag-11Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 141, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11Riff/mag-11Riff-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 282, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11Riff/11Riff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11Riff/11Riff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11Riff/11Riff-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 151, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Detroit Became the World Capital of Staring at Abandoned Old Buildings', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘I Came to See the End of the World!’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Detroit (Mich)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:38:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Binelli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Binelli', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4eb8e3da-59f1-5663-9612-12ed92c7bd85', 'word_count': 1791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4eb8e3da-59f1-5663-9612-12ed92c7bd85'}, {'abstract': 'Did the people sitting with me at the police precinct know my attackers?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/magazine/crime-family.html', 'snippet': 'Did the people sitting with me at the police precinct know my attackers?', 'lead_paragraph': 'So I’m 19 and climbing into the back seat of a cop car in the middle of the night. Despite the absence of handcuffs around my wrists, I am crying and asking myself, over and over again, what I could have done differently. If I had crossed the street as those three men were approaching, would that have stopped them from mugging me? Or would pseudo-profiling have set in motion a more violent turn of events? For a moment, after being shoved to the ground, I even imagined getting up and giving chase until I was reunited with my prized possessions: $70 from baby-sitting gigs, an underused PalmPilot from my first corporate boss, most of my teeny-bopper CD collection and, irreplaceably, a college semester’s worth of notes. At least the ordeal was over.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '66', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11lives/mag-11Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11lives/mag-11Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11lives/11lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11lives/11lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11lives/11lives-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Crime Family', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Crime Family', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muggings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:38:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisa Mala', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mala', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d05cef5-7898-56c0-8d7c-5185c80b725e', 'word_count': 747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d05cef5-7898-56c0-8d7c-5185c80b725e'}, {'abstract': 'At the Punchestown Racecourse in Dublin, Ireland, a real slop-in-the-face affair.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/11/11/magazine/irish-jockeys.html', 'snippet': 'At the Punchestown Racecourse in Dublin, Ireland, a real slop-in-the-face affair.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the Punchestown Racecourse in Dublin, Ireland, a real slop-in-the-face affair.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Muddy Business', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ireland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jockeys, Drivers and Trainers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:38:31+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/38ee64d9-19c7-5dd4-a0ab-e8e5cf18cdee', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/38ee64d9-19c7-5dd4-a0ab-e8e5cf18cdee'}, {'abstract': 'And he wants you to wear your seat belt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/magazine/christopher-walken-isnt-as-weird-as-you-think.html', 'snippet': 'And he wants you to wear your seat belt.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Tell me about working on your new film, “A Late Quartet.”  String instruments are particularly difficult to simulate. It’s not like playing a trumpet, or sitting at a piano, when they can fake it. Both hands are involved, and body movements. We had to take lessons — a lot of lessons — and I had a cello that I practiced every day. Philip Seymour Hoffman and Catherine Keener became quite good, but I had a more difficult time. I didn’t get very good.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11talk/mag-11Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 524, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11talk/mag-11Talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1391, 'width': 712, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11talk/11talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11talk/11talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/magazine/11talk/11talk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 151, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Christopher Walken Isn’t as Weird as You Think', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Walkenized', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'A Late Quartet (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zilberman, Yaron', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Walken, Christopher', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:38:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Jessica Gross', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Jessica', 'lastname': 'Gross', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f841630-c592-5e37-87b9-593eb7b10411', 'word_count': 774, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f841630-c592-5e37-87b9-593eb7b10411'}, {'abstract': 'Buses, taxis and livery cabs, emergency vehicles, commercial vehicles and cars with doctors’ plates are all exempt.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/on-our-radar-gasoline-rationing-in-new-york/', 'snippet': 'Buses, taxis and livery cabs, emergency vehicles, commercial vehicles and cars with doctors’ plates are all exempt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly a week after New Jersey announced one, a gasoline rationing system is imposed in New York City and on Long Island. Drivers with plates ending in odd and even numbers will buy gas on alternate days. (A letter will count as an odd number.) Supplies have been disrupted by Hurricane Sandy, although the problem is easing. [ABC News]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Gasoline Rationing in New York', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:40:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95fd1adb-c3b6-516d-846d-33bbf4fe2204', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95fd1adb-c3b6-516d-846d-33bbf4fe2204'}, {'abstract': 'Eric Nuzum’s memoir opens a door to the emotional lives of America’s young.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/giving-up-the-ghost-by-eric-nuzum.html', 'snippet': 'Eric Nuzum’s memoir opens a door to the emotional lives of America’s young.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the weightier riddles orbiting the memoir, these days, is the reliability of the narrator. Since the advent of James Frey, at least, it has been the habit not only to greet a memoir with a torrent of legal inquiries but also to doubt the very accuracy of the voice telling us the story. Eric Nuzum’s “Giving Up the Ghost,” which is much concerned with the supernatural, is no exception. Nuzum’s account raises a clutch of questions about realism and memory. Because of this, I confess, and because I am schooled in memoirs involving psychiatric hospitals, I almost immediately began trying to come up with a diagnosis for the apparent unreliability of this narrator: borderline personality disorder, narcissistic personality disorder, bipolar type II, addictive personality disorder, mixed anxiety-\xaddepressive disorder and so on. Like sufferers of all these illnesses, Nuzum seems by turns lovable, charismatic, exhausting, irritating, self-destructive, impossible and sometimes plain dumb. And yet, by the end of the book, it’s hard to overlook just how effectively he has described the emotional lives of the American young.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Moody/1111-Moody-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Moody/1111-Moody-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1604, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Moody/1111-Moody-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Moody/1111-Moody-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Moody/1111-Moody-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Girl in the Blue Dress', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Girl in the Blue Dress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nuzum, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:41:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Moody', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Moody', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/be2575d7-11a1-51f1-9926-154d8fd940b7', 'word_count': 1242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be2575d7-11a1-51f1-9926-154d8fd940b7'}, {'abstract': 'Tyler Hamilton, who rode with Lance Armstrong, tells of drug use in cycling.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-secret-race-by-tyler-hamilton-and-daniel-coyle.html', 'snippet': 'Tyler Hamilton, who rode with Lance Armstrong, tells of drug use in cycling.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Hey dude, you got any Poe I can borrow?” The scene was Lance Armstrong’s villa in Nice, in 1999, the year that he first won the Tour de France, and the speaker was his teammate Tyler Hamilton. Or again: “Gonna visit Edgar. My old buddy Edgar.” These seemingly obscure allusions to the author of “The Raven” are explained as the plot — in more than one sense — unfolds in “The Secret Race,” Hamilton’s confessional memoir, written with Daniel Coyle. With its faults, the book is riveting enough, although today, some weeks after its publication, it reads like a trailer to the 200-page report from the United States Anti-Doping Agency released last month. This devastating indictment of the worst doping scandal — and the greatest conspiracy — yet seen in competitive sport led to the decision by the International Cycling Union, the cycling governing body, to strip Armstrong of his seven Tour de France titles and bar him from the sport for life. These are very dark times for bike racing.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '45', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11SUBWHEATCROFT/11SUBWHEATCROFT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11SUBWHEATCROFT/11SUBWHEATCROFT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 375}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11SUBWHEATCROFT/11SUBWHEATCROFT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 575, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11SUBWHEATCROFT/11SUBWHEATCROFT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 363, 'width': 575, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11SUBWHEATCROFT/11SUBWHEATCROFT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11SUBWHEATCROFT/11SUBWHEATCROFT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11SUBWHEATCROFT/11SUBWHEATCROFT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tour de Farce', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tour de Farce', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamilton, Tyler', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARMSTRONG, LANCE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tour de France (Bicycle Race)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coyle, Daniel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bicycles and Bicycling', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:41:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Geoffrey Wheatcroft', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wheatcroft', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfc4af00-2579-58f8-a534-b605888b49bb', 'word_count': 1267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfc4af00-2579-58f8-a534-b605888b49bb'}, {'abstract': 'A rejected book, an affair, a shattered marriage, a mountain of debt. Benjamin Anastas tells his story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/too-good-to-be-true-by-benjamin-anastas.html', 'snippet': 'A rejected book, an affair, a shattered marriage, a mountain of debt. Benjamin Anastas tells his story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Benjamin Anastas has seen better days. He’s pinned under an anvil of debt. His literary career is in tatters. His pregnant wife ran off with another writer, leaving Anastas a part-time dad before his son was even born. “Too Good to Be True” is a Job-style lament, as much the story of Anastas as that of 21st-\xadcentury America (which also turned out to be too good to be true), with its great promises and subsequent disappointments: the bountiful credit that became mountainous debt; the ultra-innovative technology that now enslaves us; broken families; two-household children. “Most of us,” Anastas reflects, “open our eyes at some point in our lives and find ourselves in a place we never would have chosen if we’d been paying more attention along the way.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11UNFIRTH/11UNFIRTH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11UNFIRTH/11UNFIRTH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1604, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11UNFIRTH/11UNFIRTH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11UNFIRTH/11UNFIRTH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11UNFIRTH/11UNFIRTH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lamentations', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lamentations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anastas, Benjamin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:41:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Olin Unferth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': 'Olin', 'lastname': 'Unferth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/77cccae1-6e88-50a6-b148-f717f578bd76', 'word_count': 1257, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77cccae1-6e88-50a6-b148-f717f578bd76'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THERE WAS A COUNTRY: A Personal History of Biafra,  by Chinua Achebe. (Penguin Press, $27.95.) The author of “Things Fall Apart” recalls with some nostalgia the Republic of Biafra, whose secession from Nigeria in 1967 led to a bloody civil war.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books Of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:42:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8560a47-5f70-569e-93e9-26337c8e2962', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8560a47-5f70-569e-93e9-26337c8e2962'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'INFERNO: The World at War, 1939-1945,  by Max Hastings. (Vintage, $17.95.) Hastings, who witnessed armed conflicts in Vietnam and the Falkland Islands as a war correspondent, has written extensively about key campaigns and personalities of World War II. This fluent, authoritative survey concentrates on the lived experiences of the men and women who took part in the war; as the book ranges from Dunkirk to Iwo Jima, Stalingrad to Guadalcanal, there is arresting material from interviews, diaries, letters and memoirs.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '52', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:42:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/f88fca1d-69cf-52fe-82a5-96ed1ecb0434', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f88fca1d-69cf-52fe-82a5-96ed1ecb0434'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Rabbit, Toad and the Boxcar Children are cast in sequels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-further-tale-of-peter-rabbit-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Peter Rabbit, Toad and the Boxcar Children are cast in sequels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once content to trumpet the classics already on their backlists, children’s book publishers have upped the ante, commissioning sequels and prequels to extend time-tested favorites in either direction. A business model that once pertained only to fun fiction series like the Hardy Boys and Goosebumps has gone decidedly upmarket, and so we have a spate of classics-plus.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Marcus02/1111-Marcus02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 931, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Marcus02/1111-Marcus02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1863, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Marcus02/1111-Marcus02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Marcus02/1111-Marcus02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Marcus02/1111-Marcus02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Carry On', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Carry On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Eleanor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Jacqueline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacLachlan, Patricia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Emma', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young, Clint', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:43:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leonard S. Marcus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leonard', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Marcus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d477ad68-a137-5318-bea2-1e78ac71feda', 'word_count': 993, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d477ad68-a137-5318-bea2-1e78ac71feda'}, {'abstract': 'Three books highlight the struggle for racial equality in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/hand-in-hand-by-andrea-davis-pinkney-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Three books highlight the struggle for racial equality in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before I picked up “Hand in Hand: Ten Black Men Who Changed America,” I moderated a panel called “When Work Disappears,” comprising an interracial group of lawyers, economists and a journalist turned television producer. And on the whole, the opinions and evidence we shared left the audience, as well as myself, in a “slough of despond.” Every aspect painted a bleak picture of African-Americans, including the tragic status of children living in poverty (38 percent); schools ill-equipped to meet students’ most basic needs; the disproportionate numbers in prison, most on relatively minor charges; and a rate of unemployment double that of whites. In short: The American dream for African-Americans — especially young men — is for now and the foreseeable future an American nightmare. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Hunter-Gault02/1111-Hunter-Gault02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 317, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Hunter-Gault02/1111-Hunter-Gault02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 317}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Hunter-Gault02/1111-Hunter-Gault02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 540, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Hunter-Gault02/1111-Hunter-Gault02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Hunter-Gault02/1111-Hunter-Gault02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Hunter-Gault02/1111-Hunter-Gault02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Hunter-Gault02/1111-Hunter-Gault02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Keeping Hope Alive', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Keeping Hope Alive', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinkney, Andrea Davis', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pinkney, Brian', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nelson, Kadir', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Martin Luther Jr', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cole, Henry (Illustrator)', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:44:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlayne Hunter-Gault', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlayne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hunter-Gault', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4a8959f-9e99-5647-9a7d-f70c80f7703d', 'word_count': 1301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4a8959f-9e99-5647-9a7d-f70c80f7703d'}, {'abstract': 'Jon Agee and Oliver Jeffers embrace the absurd in two new picture books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-other-side-of-town-and-this-moose-belongs-to-me.html', 'snippet': 'Jon Agee and Oliver Jeffers embrace the absurd in two new picture books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There exists in children’s literature a tradition of stories best described as “absurd” or “nonsense.” Such books derive their legitimacy and longevity from the open-mindedness of the people for whom they are written. Young children are not, after all, as insistent as their elders that a story adhere to the rules of logic or the nature of reality. This acceptance of the absurd reflects their willingness, eagerness even, to suspend disbelief. A feeling of liberation lies behind this attraction: the antic, anything-goes quality of the deeply nonsensical story is fundamentally appealing to a child’s anarchic spirit.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-VanAllsberg02/1111-VanAllsberg02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-VanAllsberg02/1111-VanAllsberg02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-VanAllsberg02/1111-VanAllsberg02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-VanAllsberg02/1111-VanAllsberg02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1359, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-VanAllsberg02/1111-VanAllsberg02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-VanAllsberg02/1111-VanAllsberg02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-VanAllsberg02/1111-VanAllsberg02-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Topsy-Turvy', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Topsy-Turvy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jeffers, Oliver', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AGEE, JON', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:44:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Van Allsburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': 'Van', 'lastname': 'Allsburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1dbca5e-f7d8-5523-ba10-8fee1255552b', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1dbca5e-f7d8-5523-ba10-8fee1255552b'}, {'abstract': 'An industrious tugboat and a mischievous fish discover that life is full of surprises.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/little-tug-and-this-is-not-my-hat.html', 'snippet': 'An industrious tugboat and a mischievous fish discover that life is full of surprises.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jon Klassen’s “I Want My Hat Back” and Stephen Savage’s “Where’s Walrus?,” though two of the most widely anticipated Caldecott Medal contenders of 2012, didn’t, in the end, get much love from the American Library Association committee that chose the winning picture book, “A Ball for Daisy,” by Chris Raschka, along with three runners-\xadup. The deliberations are secret, so we cannot know the Caldecott committee’s thinking, but the single (if expert) joke of “Walrus” and the Raymond Carveresque minimalist murder plot of “Hat” were perhaps not pluses. Still, both books were strong sellers and brought deserved attention to their creators.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Sutton/1111-Sutton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 297, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Sutton/1111-Sutton-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 297}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Sutton/1111-Sutton-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Sutton/1111-Sutton-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1013, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Sutton/1111-Sutton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Sutton/1111-Sutton-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Sutton/1111-Sutton-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Swimmies', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Swimmies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klassen, Jon', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Savage, Stephen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roger Sutton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roger', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sutton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/260a5ac7-63dc-5b4f-bcc3-77876ca58227', 'word_count': 721, 'uri': 'nyt://article/260a5ac7-63dc-5b4f-bcc3-77876ca58227'}, {'abstract': 'An elite school is part of a centuries-old battle against evil forces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-paladin-prophecy-by-mark-frost.html', 'snippet': 'An elite school is part of a centuries-old battle against evil forces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mark Frost, a co-creator, with David Lynch, of the seminal 1990s series “Twin Peaks,” is no stranger to the art of suspense. It’s unsurprising, then, that “The Paladin Prophecy,” his first venture into young adult literature (he’s written nine books for adults), contains thrills and plot twists in spades.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11LU/11LU-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 668, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11LU/11LU-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1493, 'width': 974, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11LU/11LU-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11LU/11LU-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11LU/11LU-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wonder Kid, Activated', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wonder Kid, Activated', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frost, Mark', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:45:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marie Lu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6082ceaa-864b-524c-9e32-dab6ebb24e8e', 'word_count': 610, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6082ceaa-864b-524c-9e32-dab6ebb24e8e'}, {'abstract': 'Amid an African coup, a girl and a baby ape are on the run.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/endangered-by-eliot-schrefer.html', 'snippet': 'Amid an African coup, a girl and a baby ape are on the run.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes during an act of heroism, it’s the savior who ends up being saved. Such is the case in Eliot Schrefer’s dazzling, big-hearted novel “Endangered,” which has been named a National Book Award finalist in the category of young people’s literature.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11CROKE/11CROKE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11CROKE/11CROKE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1745, 'width': 1323, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11CROKE/11CROKE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11CROKE/11CROKE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11CROKE/11CROKE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Adapt and Survive', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Adapt and Survive', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schrefer, Eliot', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:45:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vicki Constantine Croke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vicki', 'middlename': 'Constantine', 'lastname': 'Croke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d07209ab-2eba-57c4-b372-63d03afda3d9', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d07209ab-2eba-57c4-b372-63d03afda3d9'}, {'abstract': 'A teenage heroine uncovers mysteries at an all-male academy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-raven-boys-by-maggie-stiefvater.html', 'snippet': 'A teenage heroine uncovers mysteries at an all-male academy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Much of the drama of adolescence — the thrill of it, the pain — is about disengaging from family and finding that connection again, often elsewhere and in a completely different form. This simultaneous rejection of and yearning for human bonds is at the heart of Maggie Stiefvater’s latest novel, “The Raven Boys,” a well-paced neo-Gothic thriller about a teenage fivesome’s search for the supernatural . . . and themselves. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11HOLMES/11HOLMES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1022, 'width': 779, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11HOLMES/11HOLMES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1022, 'width': 779, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11HOLMES/11HOLMES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11HOLMES/11HOLMES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11HOLMES/11HOLMES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sleeping King', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Sleeping King', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stiefvater, Maggie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:46:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anna Holmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d4b846e-620d-504f-afe4-f0c2ec763fd5', 'word_count': 655, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d4b846e-620d-504f-afe4-f0c2ec763fd5'}, {'abstract': 'Ned Vizzini’s teenage hero faces challenges in two realms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-other-normals-by-ned-vizzini.html', 'snippet': 'Ned Vizzini’s teenage hero faces challenges in two realms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ned Vizzini has been a professional writer, and a good one, since the 1990s, when he was 15 years old and emerged with a series of vibrant columns for The New York Press about teenage life. Later, one of his novels, “It’s Kind of a Funny Story,” became a critical and commercial hit, as well as a 2010 movie starring Zach Galifianakis. Vizzini was then, and continues to be now, in full possession of a clever, seductively deadpan voice, as the first few sentences of his new young adult novel, “The Other Normals,” bear out: “This is a story about becoming a man, so naturally it starts with me alone in a room playing with myself. Not that way — playing Creatures & Caverns, the popular role-playing game. Popular being a relative term. I guess if Creatures & Caverns were really popular, I would have other people to play with.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Avatars', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Avatars', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vizzini, Ned', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:46:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Lipsyte', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lipsyte', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d6b3bca-cd0f-5a5b-8997-376ba180f625', 'word_count': 708, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d6b3bca-cd0f-5a5b-8997-376ba180f625'}, {'abstract': 'Two dystopian young adult novels challenge readers’ conceptions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/beta-and-whats-left-of-me.html', 'snippet': 'Two dystopian young adult novels challenge readers’ conceptions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the many pleasures of reading dystopian novels is encountering a different but recognizable world, and then uncovering clues to how things ended up this way and relishing the sense of discovery. Two new dystopian novels not only explore such alternative realms and the societies that populate them, but also probe the smaller worlds of human bodies and the souls that inhabit them. Both Rachel Cohn’s “Beta” and Kat Zhang’s “What’s Left of Me” offer meticulously crafted settings and unusual characters while posing fascinating questions about the nature of body and soul.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11CONDI/11CONDI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 668, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11CONDI/11CONDI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1335, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11CONDI/11CONDI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11CONDI/11CONDI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11CONDI/11CONDI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Whose Body Is It Anyway?', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Whose Body Is It Anyway?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zhang, Kat', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohn, Rachel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:46:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ally Condie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ally', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Condie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/73cac314-5ce5-5c25-b66c-f0711cc818af', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73cac314-5ce5-5c25-b66c-f0711cc818af'}, {'abstract': 'In three books, enemies are vanquished, European style.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/valentine-and-his-violin-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In three books, enemies are vanquished, European style.', 'lead_paragraph': 'European children’s books have a lot in common with the TV shows we Americans import from the British: either rarefied and arty (“Downton Abbey”) or full of poop jokes and low humor (um, “Benny Hill”). These three picture books from abroad offer a little bit of both the high and low ends of the artistic spectrum.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Brown02/1111-Brown02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 341, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Brown02/1111-Brown02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 341}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Brown02/1111-Brown02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 584, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Brown02/1111-Brown02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1169, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Brown02/1111-Brown02-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Brown02/1111-Brown02-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Brown02/1111-Brown02-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tastes of Victory', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tastes of Victory', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hopman, Philip', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Krause, Ute', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fardell, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:47:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a095bee-1391-585f-9493-168fc131bda7', 'word_count': 1019, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a095bee-1391-585f-9493-168fc131bda7'}, {'abstract': 'Two young adult novels imagine worlds affected by alarming technology.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/crewel-and-eve-adam.html', 'snippet': 'Two young adult novels imagine worlds affected by alarming technology.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Books with plots that center on technology are rarely about that technology; rather, they are about the impact of that technology. In two new young adult novels, “Crewel” and “Eve & Adam,” we face two potentially alarming worlds: one in which technology has fundamentally altered the human body, and in the other, where it’s changed the very fabric of reality. And it’s up to a couple of teenage heroines to deal with it.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tech Trouble', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tech Trouble', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Applegate, Katherine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Albin, Gennifer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grant, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:49:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maureen Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maureen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c598b6c5-f0ea-522a-9dde-28230e5173d1', 'word_count': 878, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c598b6c5-f0ea-522a-9dde-28230e5173d1'}, {'abstract': 'The race to build the bomb and Jewish resistance in World War II are the subjects of two nonfiction books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/bomb-and-beyond-courage.html', 'snippet': 'The race to build the bomb and Jewish resistance in World War II are the subjects of two nonfiction books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Earlier this year, a British publisher told me something that made me especially proud to write nonfiction. Nowhere else in the world, he said, is nonfiction for young people written as it is in the United States, where authors often select their own subjects rather than have them handed down by a publisher; find their own images rather than farm the job out to a photo researcher; and work closely with designers to lay out each page. He is right: American nonfiction for children and teenage readers is often a labor of love. Two new books about war, Steve Sheinkin’s “Bomb” and Doreen Rappaport’s “Beyond Courage,” offer particular excellence — vivid writing and original research inspired by personal passion — in a field that all too often \xadis invisible.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Aronson/1111-Aronson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 688, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Aronson/1111-Aronson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 571, 'width': 688, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Aronson/1111-Aronson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Aronson/1111-Aronson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Aronson/1111-Aronson-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 149, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Histories of War', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Histories of War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rappaport, Doreen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheinkin, Steve', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:49:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marc Aronson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Aronson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f3d8879-797e-520e-90a9-ed409811a201', 'word_count': 1055, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f3d8879-797e-520e-90a9-ed409811a201'}, {'abstract': 'These plucky heroines are determined to preserve the power of enchantment.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-last-dragonslayer-and-iron-hearted-violet.html', 'snippet': 'These plucky heroines are determined to preserve the power of enchantment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is it about dragons that fascinates us so? From the medieval tales of St. George and the Dragon to Anne McCaffrey’s telepathic dragons of Pern to the alien dragons populating the most recent young adult literature sensation, “Seraphina,” we seem to be endlessly drawn to these ancient mythic beasts.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spitfires', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spitfires', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fforde, Jasper', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barnhill, Kelly', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:49:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Von Drasek', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'Von', 'lastname': 'Drasek', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca7982b6-30df-5847-8013-698f1c3b8836', 'word_count': 815, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca7982b6-30df-5847-8013-698f1c3b8836'}, {'abstract': 'Girls who have lost their parents wonder where they fit in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-great-unexpected-by-sharon-creech.html', 'snippet': 'Girls who have lost their parents wonder where they fit in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How can life be so painful yet so beautiful? How can we make sense of what we feel? Many of the most beloved children’s books ask these questions by treading lightly on reality, allowing young readers to wade ankle-deep in the joys and agonies of being human without sinking into existential muck.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11WEILL/11WEILL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1003, 'width': 761, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11WEILL/11WEILL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1003, 'width': 761, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11WEILL/11WEILL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11WEILL/11WEILL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/11WEILL/11WEILL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Somebody to Care', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Somebody to Care', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Creech, Sharon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:49:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Weil', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weil', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f84b6186-128c-55b1-839a-cac22978414c', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f84b6186-128c-55b1-839a-cac22978414c'}, {'abstract': 'Three orphans learn to get by without their grandfather.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/keeping-safe-the-stars-by-sheila-oconnor.html', 'snippet': 'Three orphans learn to get by without their grandfather.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Harry Potter, Oliver Twist, Dorothy Gale, Huck Finn, Pippi Longstocking, Anne Shirley: \xadorphaned protagonists in children’s books are legion. They’re also the stuff undergraduate English lit classes are made of. Whatever our age, parental death, abandonment and neglect immediately tug at our sympathies, showing us the fearsome realities of the adult world, rendered starker when seen through a child’s eyes. There is even greater satisfaction when the young protagonists find the strength and resilience to persevere on their own.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Their Own', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Their Own', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Connor, Sheila", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:49:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Whitney Joiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Whitney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Joiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb8b079d-d0ac-501d-a17b-588a761d1612', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb8b079d-d0ac-501d-a17b-588a761d1612'}, {'abstract': 'This young adult novel’s hero succeeds because of his character.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/jepp-who-defied-the-stars-by-katherine-marsh.html', 'snippet': 'This young adult novel’s hero succeeds because of his character.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Self-conscious about your body? Unsure where — or if — you’ll ever fit in? Wondering when people will start treating you like an adult? Katherine Marsh’s “Jepp, Who \xadDefied the Stars” delves into these perennial adolescent challenges while transporting her readers to a tiny island in Denmark in the late 16th century — and without the help of either time machine or wizard. Instead Marsh turns to one of history’s \xadreal-life magicians, the brilliant, eccentric astronomer Tycho Brahe, to welcome her readers to his castle-cum-\xadobservatory, Uraniborg. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jester and Magician', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jester and Magician', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marsh, Katherine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:50:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Harrison', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harrison', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8277acb-a52e-51ff-b028-a1dd8c231a8d', 'word_count': 660, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8277acb-a52e-51ff-b028-a1dd8c231a8d'}, {'abstract': 'A world run by children is upended in this environmental tale.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-story-of-the-blue-planet-by-andri-snaer-magnason.html', 'snippet': 'A world run by children is upended in this environmental tale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In most children’s books, from “Little Bear” to “The Jungle Book,” nature exists as a kind of utopia, a lush playground with anthropomorphized animals. It’s rare to find stories that realistically depict the natural world — as a beautiful but fragile realm under siege. Is it because Dr. Seuss so brilliantly impugned the human race’s ecological crimes in “The Lorax” that another masterpiece in the genre has seemed beyond reach? Because our children are so screen-\xadaddicted and nature-starved, we fear \xadfurther exiling them from the outdoors with tales of environmental peril? Or maybe it’s because such stories would expose any grown-up who travels by aircraft or uses Ziploc baggies as an environmental offender. Whatever the reason, eco-lit needs more attention, and devotees will be pleased to discover a new addition from the Icelandic author Andri Snaer Magnason, who writes with a Seussian mix of wonder, wit and gravitas. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stealing the Sunlight', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stealing the Sunlight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Magnason, Andri Snaer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "D'Arcy, Julian Meldon", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:51:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Little', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Little', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e4c2bab-a7a4-5fea-ba43-dc4d27cf7a7e', 'word_count': 730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e4c2bab-a7a4-5fea-ba43-dc4d27cf7a7e'}, {'abstract': 'It seems Romneyworld, along with Fox News, was unable to receive messages from reality.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/the-republican-bubble/', 'snippet': 'It seems Romneyworld, along with Fox News, was unable to receive messages from reality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is it possible that the numbers-crunchers high up in the Romney campaign really had no idea President Obama might win? A report Thursday by CBS News says that senior Romney aides were utterly “shellshocked” Tuesday night when they lost one swing state after another.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Republican Bubble', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rove, Karl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silver, Nate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T15:53:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Firestone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Firestone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7cb7807-9f23-5fe5-8ae7-18e2d564f23a', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7cb7807-9f23-5fe5-8ae7-18e2d564f23a'}, {'abstract': 'Peter Mayle on his new novel, expat greed and why travel writing just isn’t for everyone.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/09/bon-mots/', 'snippet': 'Peter Mayle on his new novel, expat greed and why travel writing just isn’t for everyone.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Peter Mayle on his new novel, expat greed and why travel writing just isn’t for everyone.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bons Mots', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bon Mots', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-09T16:00:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e64dcd3-e5eb-5b00-91af-a2f18e1fc392', 'word_count': 528, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e64dcd3-e5eb-5b00-91af-a2f18e1fc392'}, {'abstract': 'The Colts are 6-3 and a great story, but there are significant obstacles ahead: The next game is at New England.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/10/sports/colts-on-a-roll-its-unreal-leading-off.html', 'snippet': 'The Colts are 6-3 and a great story, but there are significant obstacles ahead: The next game is at New England.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the hyperbole-a-minute world of the N.F.L., the Indianapolis Colts’ victory Thursday night over Jacksonville makes quarterback Andrew Luck the shoo-in for rookie of the year and proves the Colts are totally going to the playoffs, maybe the Super Bowl, and no one in Indianapolis remembers that Peyton Manning used to be their quarterback. Keep following this bubbling optimism and Coach Chuck Pagano will not only beat his own cancer in inspirational fashion, but everyone will always beat cancer and every day will be sunny and 70 degrees.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/10/sports/football/10leadingpic/10leadingpic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop