ection_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marcus Mabry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marcus', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mabry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/33a7a483-6dc9-5a4a-8482-09a323ca2853', 'word_count': 642, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33a7a483-6dc9-5a4a-8482-09a323ca2853'}, {'abstract': 'A Rhodes scholar and Iraq war veteran urges us to find “a better way to walk in the world.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/books/review/the-warriors-heart-by-eric-greitens.html', 'snippet': 'A Rhodes scholar and Iraq war veteran urges us to find “a better way to walk in the world.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eric Greitens is an astonishingly accomplished person. He has been a Rhodes scholar, a decorated member of the Navy SEALs and a humanitarian volunteer among populations in need throughout the world. So it is something of a surprise that his memoir “The Warrior’s Heart,” a young adult adaptation of his best-selling book, “The Heart and the Fist,” begins with the word “you.” What follows is a direct address that places readers in a moment of moral indecision and ends with the question: “What do you do?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Powers/1111-Powers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 804, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Powers/1111-Powers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1418, 'width': 1114, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/books/review/1111-Power