E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/191521f9-c887-5f73-a485-d5c40489322f', 'word_count': 1094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/191521f9-c887-5f73-a485-d5c40489322f'}, {'abstract': '“Fritz Lang: The Early Works,” a new DVD set, reveals early efforts in the years before film noir.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/movies/new-on-dvd-fritz-lang-the-early-works.html', 'snippet': '“Fritz Lang: The Early Works,” a new DVD set, reveals early efforts in the years before film noir.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE single most important figure in the development of the motion picture as an art form was probably D. W. Griffith, but from his debut as a director in 1908 to the premature end of his career in 1931, he remained a child of the Victorian era. For all of his innovations in film form, his view of the world, as embedded in the sort of stories he chose to tell and the kind of characters with which he populated them, belonged to the 19th century. There isn’t much in his work that would have surprised Balzac or Dickens.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11KEHR1_SPAN/11KEHR1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11KEHR1_SPAN/11KEHR1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/arts/11KEHR1_SPAN/11KEHR1-jumbo.jpg', 'h