: 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manny Fernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42ec5467-0660-57b4-ac4e-e1dcf13f3fa2', 'word_count': 886, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42ec5467-0660-57b4-ac4e-e1dcf13f3fa2'}, {'abstract': 'Josmery Batista, who received a diagnosis of Stage 4 breast cancer last year, is so enfeebled by the effects of chemotherapy that sometimes she is unable to hold her infant son.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/nyregion/single-mother-battling-breast-cancer-takes-strength-from-her-children.html', 'snippet': 'Josmery Batista, who received a diagnosis of Stage 4 breast cancer last year, is so enfeebled by the effects of chemotherapy that sometimes she is unable to hold her infant son.', 'lead_paragraph': 'She had not expected her fingernails to feel as if they had been hollowed out. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/nyregion/bull-NEEDIEST/bull-NEEDIEST-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 717, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/nyregion/bull-NEEDIEST/bull-NEEDIEST-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1435, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/nyregion/bull-NEEDIEST/bull-NEEDIEST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/nyregion/bull-NEEDIEST/bull-NEEDIEST-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/nyregion/bull-NEEDIEST/bull-NEEDIEST-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Battling Breast Cancer, Single Mother Takes Strength From Her Children', 'kicker': 'The Neediest Cases', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Battling Breast Cancer, Single Mother Takes Strength From Her Children', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times Neediest Cases Fund', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Catholic Charities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Breast Cancer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-11T01:10:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Otis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Otis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/faa4bb2e-95eb-595f-84c4-c0e64cfd390b', 'word_count': 800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/faa4bb2e-95eb-595f-84c4-c0e64cfd390b'}, {'abstract': 'A number of polling firms that conduct their surveys online had strong results. Some telephone polls also did well. But others, especially those that called only landlines or took other methodological shortcuts, performed poorly.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/10/which-polls-fared-best-and-worst-in-the-2012-presidential-race/', 'snippet': 'A number of polling firms that conduct their surveys online had strong results. Some telephone polls also did well. But others, especially those that called only landlines or took other methodological shortcuts, performed poorly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Americans’ modes of communication change, the techniques that produce the most accurate polls seems to be changing as well. In last Tuesday’s presidential election, a number of polling firms that conduct their surveys online had strong results. Some telephone polls also performed well. But others, especially those that called only landlines or took other methodological shortcuts, performed poorly and showed a more Republican-leaning electorate than the one that actually turned out.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Which Polls Fared Best (and Worst) in the 2012 Presidential Race', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-11T01:38:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1caf6658-4956-55d3-9f42-db28df77972a', 'word_count': 1578, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1caf6658-4956-55d3-9f42-db28df77972a'}, {'abstract': 'Texas A&M quarterback Johnny Manziel, a redshirt freshman, did not ruin No. 1 Alabama’s hopes of repeating as national champion by himself, but he was a big part of the Aggies’ upset win Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/sports/ncaafootball/error-prone-alabama-falls-to-texas-am.html', 'snippet': 'Texas A&M quarterback Johnny Manziel, a redshirt freshman, did not ruin No. 1 Alabama’s hopes of repeating as national champion by himself, but he was a big part of the Aggies’ upset win Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUSCALOOSA, Ala. — At some point Saturday, the glare was supposed to be too much for the Texas A&M Aggies, the new arrivals in the Southeastern Conference, who were not supposed to be ready for the ferociousness of this kind of SEC road game. It was Alabama’s top-rated defense against this phenomenon called Johnny Football, and surely the Aggies would compete for a quarter or two and then go away once their redshirt freshman quarterback, Johnny Manziel, had his face mask shoved into the turf at Bryant-Denny Stadium. ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/sports/YJPALABAMA/YJPALABAMA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/sports/YJPALABAMA/YJPALABAMA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/sports/YJPALABAMA/YJPALABAMA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 635, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/sports/YJPALABAMA/YJPALABAMA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1270, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/sports/YJPALABAMA/YJPALABAMA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/sports/YJPALABAMA/YJPALABAMA-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/sports/YJPALABAMA/YJPALABAMA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Error-Prone Alabama Seals Its Loss and, Maybe, Its Fate', 'kicker': 'Texas A&M 29, Alabama 24', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Error-Prone Alabama Seals Its Loss and, Maybe, Its Fate', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football (College)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manziel, Johnny', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Texas A&M University', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Alabama', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-11T01:50:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ray Glier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ray', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78232320-3e79-5214-8a43-bce322dcaa75', 'word_count': 865, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78232320-3e79-5214-8a43-bce322dcaa75'}, {'abstract': 'Kaskaskia, Ill., a former state capital settled in 1703, has shrunk after devastating floods to 14 full-time residents, who have to take a detour to Missouri to get there.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/us/kaskaskia-journal-living-in-the-american-atlantis.html', 'snippet': 'Kaskaskia, Ill., a former state capital settled in 1703, has shrunk after devastating floods to 14 full-time residents, who have to take a detour to Missouri to get there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KASKASKIA, Ill. — When Manny and Dorothy Brown stand atop the stairs rising to their screen door, they look at the overgrown field across Grand Avenue and still see Kaskaskia’s buzzing general store. Fathers are planning turkey shoots; mothers, bake sales. Schwinns clatter past rows of homes while little cowboys and Indians shriek down by the church.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/us/KASKASKIA-1/KASKASKIA-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/11/us/KASKASKIA-1/KASKASKIA-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/us/KASKASKIA-1/KASKASKIA-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/us/KASKASKIA-1/KASKASKIA-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/11/us/KASKASKIA-1/KASKASKIA-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/11/us/KASKASKIA-1/KASKASKIA-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Living in the American Atlantis (Population 14)', 'kicker': 'Kaskaskia Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Living in the American Atlantis (Population 14)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Illinois', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Floods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historic Buildings and Sites', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kaskaskia (Ill)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restoration and Renovation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-11T01:51:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Schwarz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwarz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f6feedf-7c36-5611-8426-d76a8a164152', 'word_count': 898, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f6feedf-7c36-5611-8426-d76a8a164152'}, {'abstract': 'Eight deaths in a Staten Island enclave during Hurricane Sandy have raised questions about why the victims were in their homes when the storm hit and whether the city bore some responsibility for their failure to evacuate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/nyregion/how-a-staten-island-community-became-a-deathtrap.html', 'snippet': 'Eight deaths in a Staten Island enclave during Hurricane Sandy have raised questions about why the victims were in their homes when the storm hit and whether the city bore some responsibility for their failure to evacuate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eugene Contrubis heard the many warnings about Hurricane Sandy but decided to ride it out in his drafty, one-story bungalow at 162 Kiswick Street, near the beach on Staten Island. Soft-spoken and frail, he was a retired Police Department clerk who wrote poetry, enjoyed chess and adored his nieces. When they were children, he hung a swing from a tree in his yard for them to play on. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How a Beach Community Became a Deathtrap', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker