lishing companies, newspapers and cultural groups while promoting industries that can spread China’s influence abroad — all firmly under party control.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/12/world/1112CHINA/1112CHINA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 685, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/12/world/1112CHINA/1112CHINA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1990, 'width': 1332, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/12/world/1112CHINA/1112CHINA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/12/world/1112CHINA/1112CHINA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/12/world/1112CHINA/1112CHINA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'China, at Party Congress, Lauds Its Cultural Advances', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China, at Party Congress, Lauds Its Cultural Advances', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privatization', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-11T13:01:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9e972e4-c135-50a7-98cf-4452d265fcb9', 'word_count': 563, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9e972e4-c135-50a7-98cf-4452d265fcb9'}, {'abstract': 'Today’s generation of veterans return home to perhaps the most pro-veteran environment in decades — a marked difference from the reception Vietnam vets received decades ago.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/11/did-vietnam-change-the-way-we-welcome-veterans-home/', 'snippet': 'Today’s generation of veterans return home to perhaps the most pro-veteran environment in decades — a marked difference from the reception Vietnam vets received decades ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s generation of veterans return home to perhaps the most pro-veteran environment in decades. Many large companies actively recruit and employ veterans, and the Post-9/11 G.I. Bill provides benefits for higher education and vocational training to help veterans make the transition. Organizations such as the Iraq and Afghanistan Veterans of America, Veterans of Foreign Wars and American Legion are entirely focused on veteran issues, and many other nonprofit organizations are assisting veterans in their transition to the civilian life.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Did Vietnam Change the Way We Welcome Veterans Home?', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCain, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shinseki, Eric K', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Army', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-11T13:15:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Hsia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hsia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16a15524-451a-5514-a400-610284644cbd', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16a15524-451a-5514-a400-610284644cbd'}, {'abstract': 'Prosecutors say Staff Sgt. Robert Bales acted alone when he killed 16 Afghan civilians earlier this year, but defense lawyers have tried to raise some doubt.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/12/us/lawyers-for-robert-bales-question-lone-gunman-claim.html', 'snippet': 'Prosecutors say Staff Sgt. Robert Bales acted alone when he killed 16 Afghan civilians earlier this year, but defense lawyers have tried to raise some doubt.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JOINT BASE LEWIS-McCHORD, Wash. — A defense lawyer for Army Staff Sgt. Robert Bales pressed witnesses on Saturday about whether more than one soldier might have been involved in the killings of 16 Afghan civilians earlier this year. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Soldier’s Defense Questions Lone Gunman Claim', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Soldier’s Defense Questions Lone Gunman Claim', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bales, Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defe