e': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sherwin-Williams Company', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-12T14:25:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dealbook', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dealbook', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e6a40f7-c407-5fa3-bba3-b8cc15ddc904', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e6a40f7-c407-5fa3-bba3-b8cc15ddc904'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/12/what-were-reading-565/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NPR: Jacob and Wilhelm Grimm, a k a the Brothers Grimm, were not all about fairy cakes, magic apples and candy houses. Turns out there was a startling amount of culinary gruesomeness: women, men and children chopped up, cooked and eaten. – Glenn Collins ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-12T14:44:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19526936-ea26-58ee-abd2-381547d2010c', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19526936-ea26-58ee-abd2-381547d2010c'}, {'abstract': 'Also, the National Book Awards has aspirations to become more glamorous and influential; and Valerie Eliot, widow and literary executor of T.S.Eliot, dies at 86.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/12/the-breakfast-meeting-msnbc-on-the-rise-and-judgments-on-martha-conan-and-bond/', 'snippet': 'Also, the National Book Awards has aspirations to become more glamorous and influential; and Valerie Eliot, widow and literary executor of T.S.Eliot, dies at 86.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cable news network MSNBC has redefined itself during the four years of the Obama presidency, Brian Stelter writes, “from CNN also-ran to the anti-Fox.” As it became widely known as the nation’s liberal television network, it has passed CNN in the ratings. In its 2008 election coverage, the network still paired the NBC News host David Gregory with its outspoken anchor at the time, Keith Olbermann; in 2012, the liberal host Rachel Maddow anchored the coverage, surrounded by four other liberal hosts and a moderate conservative, Steve Schmidt. In many ways, Mr. Stelter writes, the network, which until 2005 was partly owned by Microsoft, is where Fox was a decade ago — trying to maximize profit from its popularity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: MSNBC on the Rise, and Judgments on Martha, Conan and Bond', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bond, James (Fictional Character)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Book Awards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Eliot, Thomas Stearns', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Entwistle, George E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maddow, Rachel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Broadcasting Corp', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Martha Stewart Living Omnimedia Inc', 'rank': 8