one, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57d0cd65-6665-5140-9878-4191435fdf65', 'word_count': 1745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57d0cd65-6665-5140-9878-4191435fdf65'}, {'abstract': 'Cider seems to go down better to a soundtrack of eerie ballads and tragic old love songs.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/12/what-to-listen-to-when-sipping-a-november-drink/', 'snippet': 'Cider seems to go down better to a soundtrack of eerie ballads and tragic old love songs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Like drinking, music\xa0is seasonal, I think. And as I explain in my November Drink column, late autumn is definitely folk-music prime time for me. Cider seems to go down better to a soundtrack of eerie ballads and tragic old love songs, many of them dating back hundreds of years. I favor British folk and folk-rock artists like Shirley Collins, Ewan MacColl and Martin Carthy — and above all Sandy Denny, who died in 1978 at 31. (By then she’d made her mark with the legendary folk-rock outfit Fairport Convention, but she might forever be best known for her ethereal vocals on Led Zeppelin’s “Battle of Evermore.”) Her voice, at once primal and refined, is like a solitary walk through the woods in late autumn. As for younger British musicians, I’m smitten with the Unthanks, a Northumbrian band fronted by the sisters Rachel and Becky Unthank, whose rich, haunting harmonies are just right. But I can’t resist some of the greatest traditional American singers either — especially Jean Ritchie and Ralph Stanley.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What to Listen to When Sipping a November Drink', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Autumn (Season)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-12T18:01:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rosie Schaap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rosie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d706961-37a9-57a6-bec5-d38bb58aa80d', 'word_count': 284, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d706961-37a9-57a6-bec5-d38bb58aa80d'}, {'abstract': 'A series of articles and multimedia examining the China’s pollution crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2007/08/26/world/asia/choking_on_growth.html', 'snippet': 'A series of articles and multimedia examining the China’s pollution crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A series of articles and multimedia examining the China’s pollution crisis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China - Pollution - Environment - Economy - Choking on Growth Part I', 'kicker': 'SPECIAL SERIES', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'FACTORIES AND INDUSTRIAL PLANTS', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-12T18:03:36+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'World', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/4d32ba49-fb7d-5d22-9c4b-bc3218bf4e1d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/4d32ba49-fb7d-5d22-9c4b-bc3218bf4e1d'}, {'abstract': 'The government hopes to avoid the large and increasingly violent environmental protests of the last year, which halted several industrial projects.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/world/asia/china-mandates-social-risk-reviews-for-big-projects.html', 'snippet': 'The government hopes to avoid the large and increasingly violent environmental protests of the last year, which halted several industrial projects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — The cabinet of China has ordered that all major industrial projects must pass a “social risk assessment” before they begin, a move aimed at curtailing the large and increasingly violent environmental protests of the last year, which forced the suspension or cancellation of chemical plants, coal-fired power plants and a giant copper smelter.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/world/CHINA-1/CHINA-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/13/world/CHINA-1/CHINA-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/world/CHINA-1/CHINA-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/world/CHINA-1/CHINA-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/world/CHINA-1/CHINA-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/13/world/CHINA-1/CHINA-1