ml', 'snippet': 'This week: predicting election outcomes with data analysis, and raising a gay child.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week: predicting election outcomes with data analysis, and raising a gay child.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Science Times Podcast', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-12T20:06:41+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/cea3a90f-12b9-5b15-845c-e84ae48e252e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/cea3a90f-12b9-5b15-845c-e84ae48e252e'}, {'abstract': 'A new study suggests that more frequent storms could help recharge aquifers.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/12/water-supply-in-a-warming-world/', 'snippet': 'A new study suggests that more frequent storms could help recharge aquifers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than anything else, climate change is a water problem. Scientists expect more coastal flooding and possibly more inland flooding. They expect higher temperatures and greater evaporation to deplete water resources, creating risks for the food supply. They believe sea-level rise will eventually render some regions uninhabitable.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Water Supply in a Warming World', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-12T20:07:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Gillis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0b195434-4cef-55a0-b2ba-e650859c4bdf', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0b195434-4cef-55a0-b2ba-e650859c4bdf'}, {'abstract': 'International observers issued preliminary findings on the November 6 general elections.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/12/the-election-vibrant-complex/', 'snippet': 'International observers issued preliminary findings on the November 6 general elections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prior to the election, Iowa and Texas threatened international election observers with criminal sanctions if they came too close to polling locations (within 300 feet in Iowa, 100 feet in Texas). Alabama, Florida, Michigan, Mississippi, Ohio, Pennsylvania and Tennessee also tried to impede monitors from the Organization of Security and Cooperation in Europe.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Election: ‘Vibrant,’ ‘Complex’', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voter Registration and Requirements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-12T20:15:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f492c76b-d2b9-569a-ba77-e2e58dde99b2', 'word_count': 317, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f492c76b-d2b9-569a-ba77-e2e58dde99b2'}, {'abstract': 'Primer, a product said to fill in fine lines and enlarged pores on the skin before using foundation makeup, is getting more popular, and increasingly specialized.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/fashion/use-of-primers-increases.html', 'snippet': 'Primer, a product said to fill in fine lines and enlarged pores on the skin before using foundation makeup, is getting more popular, and increasingly specialized.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANY appearance-conscious women have grown accustomed to applying primer, a product said to fill in fine lines and enlarged pores on the skin, before using foundation makeup. ', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/fashion/15SKINSPAN/15SKINSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 520, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/13/fashion/15SKINSPAN/15SKINSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 520}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/fashion/15SKINSPAN/15SKINSPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 899, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/fashion/15SKINSPAN/15SKINSPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1199, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/fashion/15SKINSPAN/15SKINSPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/13/fashion/15SKINSPAN/15SKINSPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/fashion/15SKINSPAN/15SKINSPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width