icle/b2d6cbce-6d0e-5006-9341-6dcb20ce3588', 'word_count': 238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2d6cbce-6d0e-5006-9341-6dcb20ce3588'}, {'abstract': 'For some time, there has been anecdotal evidence that troops who had severe nerve, muscle or bone damage to an arm or leg were better off undergoing amputations rather than trying to keep the limb. Now there is hard data that seems to confirm that suspicion.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/12/study-finds-amputating-damaged-limbs-improves-quality-of-life/', 'snippet': 'For some time, there has been anecdotal evidence that troops who had severe nerve, muscle or bone damage to an arm or leg were better off undergoing amputations rather than trying to keep the limb. Now there is hard data that seems to confirm that...', 'lead_paragraph': 'For some time, there has been anecdotal evidence that troops who had severe nerve, muscle or bone damage to an arm or a leg were better off undergoing amputations than trying to keep the limb. Now there is hard data that seems to confirm that suspicion. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Study Finds Amputating Damaged Limbs Improves Quality of Life', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMPUTATION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Physical Therapy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostheses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooke Army Medical Center', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Reed, Walter, National Military Medical Center', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-12T20:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Dao', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dao', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e92d61d3-956c-533f-bad9-e15fd9652203', 'word_count': 885, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e92d61d3-956c-533f-bad9-e15fd9652203'}, {'abstract': 'Was Romney really the best the Republicans could do?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/12/the-romney-candidacy-in-retrospect/', 'snippet': 'Was Romney really the best the Republicans could do?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In one of my last pre-election posts, I remarked that the closeness of the election and Mitt Romney’s impressive final month of campaigning meant that he would probably enjoy more respect\xa0in defeat than is usual for losing presidential candidates. A week later, that prediction looks more than a little premature, mostly because I thought the final outcome would be closer than it was (closer to 50-49 than the ’04-esque 51-48 it looks like we’ll end up with), and I didn’t realize how completely, wildly off the mark the Romney’s campaign’s theory of the electorate (a theory that I myself found relatively persuasive, I should note)\xa0would turn out to be. Right now, between his pollsters’ overconfidence and his consultants’ failure to deliver an effective turnout operation, the epitaph for Romney’s campaign looks more cruel than I anticipated: To borrow from Slate’s John Dickerson, he’ll be remembered as the numbers guy whose numbers were all wrong.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Romney Candidacy in Retrospect', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-12T20:55:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news