}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5c99797-35da-5d29-8e4e-8594e8473b53', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5c99797-35da-5d29-8e4e-8594e8473b53'}, {'abstract': 'Senator John Kerry and Susan E. Rice, the United Nations ambassador, are considered front-runners to succeed Hillary Rodham Clinton as secretary of state.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/us/politics/top-candidates-to-replace-clinton-at-state-dept-may-face-hurdles.html', 'snippet': 'Senator John Kerry and Susan E. Rice, the United Nations ambassador, are considered front-runners to succeed Hillary Rodham Clinton as secretary of state.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — For months, the Beltway parlor game about who will succeed Hillary Rodham Clinton as secretary of state has revolved around two names: Susan E. Rice, the American ambassador to the United Nations, and Senator John Kerry of Massachusetts.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/us/KERRY-1/KERRY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/13/us/KERRY-1/KERRY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/us/KERRY-1/KERRY-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/us/KERRY-1/KERRY-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1323, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/us/KERRY-1/KERRY-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/13/us/KERRY-1/KERRY-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/us/KERRY-1/KERRY-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Top Candidates for State Dept. Are Both Facing Possible Hurdles', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Top Candidates For State Dept. Are Both Facing Possible Hurdles', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Susan E', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kerry, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T01:06:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Landler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Landler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1b794db-8115-50b7-884d-02c26230c346', 'word_count': 1148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1b794db-8115-50b7-884d-02c26230c346'}, {'abstract': 'Novak Djokovic defeated No. 2 Roger Federer, 7-6 (6), 7-5, on Monday in the final match of the ATP World Tour Finals in London.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/sports/tennis/novak-djokovic-downs-roger-federer-to-win-atp-finals.html', 'snippet': 'Novak Djokovic defeated No. 2 Roger Federer, 7-6 (6), 7-5, on Monday in the final match of the ATP World Tour Finals in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With a backhand winner down the line and a staggering roar, top-ranked Novak Djokovic announced his claim as the best player of the year for a second year in a row.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Djokovic Downs Federer to Win ATP Finals', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Tennis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Djokovic Wins ATP Finals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tennis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Federer, Roger', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Djokovic, Novak', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T01:06:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c42a891-0c26-54eb-a149-f33c1759e78d', 'word_count': 77, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c42a891-0c26-54eb-a149-f33c1759e78d'}, {'abstract': 'The parents of Austin Tice, a 31-year-old American freelance reporter, said that the Syrian government told them that it had no information about his whereabouts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/world/middleeast/syria-missing-journalist-austin-tices-parents-ask-for-help.html', 'snippet': 'The parents of Austin Tice, a 31-year-old American freelance reporter, said that the Syrian government told them that it had no information about his whereabouts.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The parents of Austin Tice, a 31-year-old American freelance journalist who has been missing in Syria for three months, said Monday that the Syrian government told them that it had no information about his whereabouts. The parents, Marc and Debra Tice, who traveled from Houston to Beirut, Lebanon, said at a news conference that they would travel to Syria if necessary to be reunited with their son. The missing journalist, a former Marine whose articles on the Syria conflict and the insurgent Free Syrian Army have been published by several news organizations, was last heard from on Aug. 13. A video that showed a person said to be Mr. Tice, apparently in the custody of Islamist militants in Syria, surfaced on Oct. 1, but its authenticity was never confirmed. American officials initially said they believed that Syria’s government had detained Mr. Tice, who entered Syria without official permission. Last week, a State Department spokeswoman, Victoria Nuland, said she had been urging the Syrian government “to be more forthcoming” with any information it might have about Mr. Tice. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Syria: Parents of Missing Journalist Appeal for Help', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Syria: Parents of Missing Journalist Appeal for Help', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tice, Austin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Missing Persons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T01:08:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f14f0400-d64a-504c-ba67-65aa168e6b4e', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f14f0400-d64a-504c-ba67-65aa168e6b4e'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo’s $30 billion request comes at a politically difficult time, as leaders prepare for negotiations aimed at averting a fiscal crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/nyregion/federal-disaster-aid-for-new-york-faces-hurdle-in-congress.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo’s $30 billion request comes at a politically difficult time, as leaders prepare for negotiations aimed at averting a fiscal crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo’s request for $30 billion in federal disaster aid for the State of New York in the wake of Hurricane Sandy faces significant hurdles in Congress, and it may take weeks, if not months, before action is taken on it, Congressional officials said on Monday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Federal Aid for New York Faces Hurdle in Congress', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Federal Aid For New York Faces Hurdle In Congress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Aid (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T01:10:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Raymond Hernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raymond', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ac1ce2a-a335-51ab-b2ef-fd95fa853aa8', 'word_count': 604, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ac1ce2a-a335-51ab-b2ef-fd95fa853aa8'}, {'abstract': 'An immigration appeals tribunal ruled that sending a militant Islamic preacher to Jordan to face terrorism charges would violate his right to a fair trial under European human rights law.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/world/europe/britain-radical-cleric-wins-deportation-appeal.html', 'snippet': 'An immigration appeals tribunal ruled that sending a militant Islamic preacher to Jordan to face terrorism charges would violate his right to a fair trial under European human rights law.', 'lead_paragraph': ' An immigration appeals tribunal ruled Monday that sending a militant Islamic preacher known as Abu Qatada to Jordan to face terrorism charges would violate his right to a fair trial under European human rights law. Judge John Mitting ordered Abu Qatada, 52, whose real name is Omar Mahmoud Mohammed Othman, to be released from prison on Tuesday under tight bail conditions. The ruling was a blow to the British government, which wants to rid the country of foreign-born radical clerics it says are fomenting Islamic militancy. British security officials say Mr. Othman was one of Al Qaeda’s top operatives in Europe. Previous attempts to deport him were blocked by the European Court of Human Rights over concerns that evidence obtained from others under torture might be used against him in Jordan. The preacher, who is of Palestinian descent, was convicted in absentia of involvement in two bombing plots in Jordan in 1999 and 2000, and faces a retrial if returned home. Home Secretary Theresa May said the government would appeal Monday’s ruling. “Qatada is a dangerous man,” she said. “The government has been doing everything it can to get rid of Abu Qatada, and we will continue to do so.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Britain: Radical Cleric Wins Deportation Appeal', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Britain: Radical Cleric Wins Deportation Appeal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Othman, Omar Mahmoud Mohammed', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deportation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Jordan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Court of Human Rights', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T01:10:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/618675bc-365a-5470-ae32-d5592e98f9d4', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/618675bc-365a-5470-ae32-d5592e98f9d4'}, {'abstract': 'Why the traditional version of the just war theory must be rejected.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/12/rethinking-the-just-war-part-2/', 'snippet': 'Why the traditional version of the just war theory must be rejected.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the second of two parts. The first part can be found here.', 'source': 'The New