hority official said Tuesday, four months after a television investigation raised new suspicions that the Palestinian leader had been poisoned. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/world/13arafat_cnd/13arafat_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/13/world/13arafat_cnd/13arafat_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/world/13arafat_cnd/13arafat_cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/world/13arafat_cnd/13arafat_cnd-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Palestinians Prepare to Exhume Arafat to Check for Poison', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Palestinians Prepare to Exhume Arafat to Check for Poison', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ARAFAT, YASIR', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poisoning and Poisons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T13:40:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Isabel Kershner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Isabel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kershner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0f42fa0-8348-544b-b8f4-d5bb02d0bf1d', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0f42fa0-8348-544b-b8f4-d5bb02d0bf1d'}, {'abstract': 'The father-and-son team behind a money market fund that nearly took down the economy can breathe a sigh of relief. | Goldman Sachs is set to announce its new partners. | Another insider trading trial gets under way. | Comparing J.C. Penney to Apple makes little sense, Andrew Ross Sorkin writes.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/money-market-pioneer-cleared-of-fraud/', 'snippet': 'The father-and-son team behind a money market fund that nearly took down the economy can breathe a sigh of relief. | Goldman Sachs is set to announce its new partners. | Another insider trading trial gets under way. | Comparing J.C. Penney to Appl...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bruce Bent and his son, the team behind the money market fund that nearly took down the economy, can breathe a sigh of relief. A federal jury rejected charges by the Securities and Exchange Commission that they defrauded investors when their fund, the Reserve Primary Fund, collapsed in September 2008. Nathaniel Popper and Jessica Silver-Greenberg report in The New York Times: “The S.E.C. did convince the jury that the younger Mr. Bent’s statements were negligent, and that the parent company had made fraudulent statements. But the decision clearing the Bents of fraud accusations underscored the difficulty prosecutors and regulators have had in holding financiers accountable for precipitating the financial crisis.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Money Market Pioneer Cleared of Fraud', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T13:42:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ff93bd7-e99f-53e7-a4ed-b524df0352de', 'word_count': 1764, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ff93bd7-e99f-53e7-a4ed-b524df0352de'}, {'abstract': 'Companies wishing to tap into shale gas resources in Europe are treading lightly for fear of arousing the ire of environmental groups.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/business/energy-environment/fracking-still-controversial-in-europe.html', 'snippet': 'Companies wishing to tap into shale gas resources in Europe are treading lightly for fear of arousing the ire of environmental groups.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. ANNES, ENGLAND — Just outside Blackpool, a town of faded cabarets and amusement rides on the Irish Sea, a drilling rig sits in a muddy farm field. The big white and yellow machine represents the latest attempt by Cuadrilla Resources to see if it can bring Northwest England the sort of shale gas revolution that has transformed the U.S. energy picture.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/business/SHALE/SHALE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/14/business/SHALE/SHALE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/business/SHALE/SHALE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 573, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/business/SHALE/SHALE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1147, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 's