Behind With Sponsorship Deal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Time Warner and Garden Put Past Behind With Sponsorship Deal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Time Warner Cable Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Madison Square Garden', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T15:23:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Sandomir', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sandomir', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16dbf0e3-1f2a-5fef-b34c-37774283fc56', 'word_count': 607, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16dbf0e3-1f2a-5fef-b34c-37774283fc56'}, {'abstract': 'Some incoming Congressional freshmen, and even some incumbents, seem to be backing away from the anti-tax “pledge” sponsored by Grover Norquist’s group.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/support-wavers-for-anti-tax-pledge/', 'snippet': 'Some incoming Congressional freshmen, and even some incumbents, seem to be backing away from the anti-tax “pledge” sponsored by Grover Norquist’s group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is known simply as “the pledge.” And if you are a Republican member of Congress, it has been considered heresy to do anything short of fully devoting yourself to its low-tax principles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Support Wavers for Anti-Tax ‘Pledge’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Norquist, Grover G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T15:28:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeremy W. Peters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeremy', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Peters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a29ac9d-c3e0-593c-8d76-41dd03ce522c', 'word_count': 210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a29ac9d-c3e0-593c-8d76-41dd03ce522c'}, {'abstract': 'The flagship Greenmarket will return; disaster crews have displaced it since Nov 3.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/union-square-market-to-reopen-this-week/', 'snippet': 'The flagship Greenmarket will return; disaster crews have displaced it since Nov 3.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Call it the ghost market: for 16 days, the bustling four-day-a-week Union Square Greenmarket has been displaced by emergency workers from Con Edison and utility crews from Alabama, Georgia and Pennsylvania who have used the park as a staging area.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Union Square Market to Reopen This Week', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Local Food', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Greenmarket (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Union Square Greenmarket', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Union Square (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T15:29:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13757b8f-7006-528b-a27b-bad0f8a4d6b5', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13757b8f-7006-528b-a27b-bad0f8a4d6b5'}, {'abstract': 'The Egyptian government wants to ease the mess of Cairo life by shortening the workday. That’s putting the cart before the horse.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/in-cairo-a-curfew-against-chaos/', 'snippet': 'The Egyptian government wants to ease the mess of Cairo life by shortening the workday. That’s putting the cart before the horse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — There aren’t many advantages to living in this city. It is falling apart under the weight of overpopulation, traffic is gridlocked, the pollution is toxic, and there are few parks or other green areas in which to seek respite. But residents will proudly say that there are few cities that offer Cairo’s oddly laid-back buzz. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Cairo, a Curfew Against Chaos', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Curfews', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Power Outages and Blackouts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cairo (Egypt)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-13T15:35:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Issandr El Amrani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Issandr', 'middlename': 'El', 'lastname': 'Amrani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'ra