n Tuesday ordered the parole of a physicist convicted of selling China secret information about satellites, in a case that rights activists called part of a crackdown on scientists working with foreigners. Valentin V. Danilov, left, sentenced in 2004 to 14 years in prison for treason, will be released next week because of health considerations and good behavior, a court spokeswoman said in statements carried by the Interfax news service. Mr. Danilov has denied that the information he sold to a Chinese company was secret and said his trial was politically motivated. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/world/BRIEFS/BRIEFS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 725, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/world/BRIEFS/BRIEFS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1355, 'width': 960, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/world/BRIEFS/BRIEFS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/14/world/BRIEFS/BRIEFS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/world/BRIEFS/BRIEFS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: Physicist Accused of Selling Secrets Is Paroled', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: Physicist Accused of Selling Secrets Is Paroled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T04:44:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Roth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4dc54789-52c5-5e4f-b3e0-188592939a77', 'word_count': 95, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4dc54789-52c5-5e4f-b3e0-188592939a77'}, {'abstract': 'An internal review of how the United Nations handled the bloody final months of Sri Lanka’s civil war in 2009 has concluded that the response was “a grave failure of the U.N.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/world/asia/report-says-un-failed-in-sri-lanka.html', 'snippet': 'An internal review of how the United Nations handled the bloody final months of Sri Lanka’s civil war in 2009 has concluded that the response was “a grave failure of the U.N.”', 'lead_paragraph': ' An internal review of how the United Nations handled the bloody final months of Sri Lanka’s civil war in 2009, when as many as 40,000 civilians were killed, has concluded that the response was “a grave failure of the U.N.,” according to a leaked draft report. The panel, led by Charles Petrie, a former U.N. official, criticized what it called “a sustained and institutionalized reluctance” by staff members in Sri Lanka at the time “to stand up for the rights of the people they were mandated to assist.” The report’s executive summary says “many senior U.N. staff simply did not perceive the prevention of killing of civilians as their responsibility.” The report also found fault with the way senior officials in New York dealt with the crisis. Officials chose “not to speak up” about “broken commitments and violations of international law” by both the Sri Lankan government and Tamil Tiger rebels because that was seen as a way to increase access to victims of the conflict. A spokesman for Secretary General Ban Ki-moon refused to comment on the draft but said the final report would be made public soon. Lyse Doucet, the chief international correspondent for BBC News who obtained the draft, reported Tuesday that the executive summary, “which sets out the panel’s conclusions in stark terms,” was removed from the final report. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Report Says U.N. Failed in Sri Lanka', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | United Nations', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Report Says U.N. Failed in Sri Lanka', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Liberation Tigers of Tamil Eelam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sri Lanka', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T04:45:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d2e8c8f-7e7f-5883-a7d2-0a724ed145f9', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d2e8c8f-7e7f-5883-a7d2-0a724ed145f9'}, {'abstract': 'It was a night of great relief for collectors and dealers after last week’s disappointing auctions of Impressionist and modern art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/arts/design/contemporary-art-sale-is-sothebys-record.html', 'snippet': 'It was a night of great relief for collectors and dealers after last week’s disappointing auctions of Impressionist and modern art.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I have all the time in the world,” Tobias Meyer, Sotheby’s principal auctioneer, said on Tuesday night as he leaned over the lectern and flashed a smile at Lisa Dennison, the auction house’s chairwoman. She was on the telephone, trying to draw a higher bid from a client. Two tenacious collectors were competing to take home “No. 1 (Royal Red and Blue),” a classic Mark Rothko painting from 1954 that is also a conspicuous example of what dealers like to call “wall power,” and the bidding was already at $61 million. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/nyregion/AUCTION/AUCTION-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 595, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/nyregion/AUCTION/AUCTION-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1191, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '$375.1 Million Art Sale Is Sotheby’s Record', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '$375.1 Million Art Sale Is Sotheby’s Record', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rothko, Mark', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pollock, Jackson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warhol, Andy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sotheby's", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T04:48:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cb2adec-2830-5380-afb8-0f2f76aae82a', 'word_count': 682, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cb2adec-2830-5380-afb8-0f2f76aae82a'}, {'abstract': 'What Republicans should learn from the Obama campaign. Hint: it’s not all about the Latino vote.', 'web_url': 'https://campaignstops.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/13/notes-for-next-time/', 'snippet': 'What Republicans should learn from the Obama campaign. Hint: it’s not all about the Latino vote.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thanks to David Petraeus’s adultery and the so-called “fiscal cliff,” it’s almost possible to forget that there was a presidential election just a week ago. (And God knows the Republicans would like to!) But before the campaign disappears into the haze of social life in Tampa and tax reform proposals in Washington, here are four under-the-radar lessons from President Obama’s successful re-election bid.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes for Next Time', 'kicker': 'Campaign Stops', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T04:57:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60101974-2895-5887-9163-2bea0b88c2ab', 'word_count': 1184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60101974-2895-5887-9163-2bea0b88c2ab'}, {'abstract': 'Active people typically live longer than those who are sedentary, but precisely what types or amounts of exercise most affect life span has not been clear. Several new studies, though, are beginning to provide some clarity, suggesting that certain activities may be better than others.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/can-housework-help-you-live-longer/', 'snippet': 'Active people typically live longer than those who are sedentary, but precisely what types or amounts of exercise most affect life span has not been clear. Several new studies, though, are beginning to provide some clarity, suggesting that certain...', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s well known by now that active people typically live longer than those who are sedentary. But precisely what types or amounts of exercise most affect life span has not been clear. Several new studies, though, are beginning to provide some clarity, suggesting that certain activities may be better than others in terms of affecting mortality risk. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/health/14physed/14physed-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/health/14physed/14physed-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/health/14physed/14physed-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'w