’s nomination to be commander of American forces in Europe had been postponed until the facts were known.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/us/13pentagon_cnd/13pentagon_cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 723, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/13/us/13pentagon_cnd/13pentagon_cnd-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1445, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Panetta Praises General Linked to Petraeus Scandal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Panetta Praises General Linked to Petraeus Scandal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Panetta, Leon E', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Allen, John R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Petraeus, David H', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelley, Jill', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Broadwell, Paula', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADULTERY', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T07:36:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Bumiller and Eric Schmitt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bumiller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmitt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3688d918-8d9e-5cd2-8dca-404e972f26c0', 'word_count': 648, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3688d918-8d9e-5cd2-8dca-404e972f26c0'}, {'abstract': 'Evaluating the prospects for India’s cricket team, ahead of the test match that begins this week.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/outlook-grim-as-india-prepares-to-face-england/', 'snippet': 'Evaluating the prospects for India’s cricket team, ahead of the test match that begins this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As India prepares to take on England in what promises to be an exciting series of four tests at home starting Thursday, its frontline batsmen’s brittle batting and lackluster form is a cause for concern, particularly after the retirement of Rahul Dravid and V.V.S. Laxman in quick succession this year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Outlook Grim as India Prepares to Face England', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cricket (Game)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dravid, Rahul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pujara, Cheteshwar', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tendulkar, Sachin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T07:47:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Haresh Pandya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Haresh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pandya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0b317f3-eef6-5a22-b566-2e649972a264', 'word_count': 895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0b317f3-eef6-5a22-b566-2e649972a264'}, {'abstract': 'Caught between the Kurds’ lucrative regional contracts and the Iraqi government’s ire, Exxon Mobil bows out of a big field, Iraq’s Deputy Prime Minister announced at the Oil and Money Conference.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/oil-fight-between-baghdad-and-kurds-comes-to-london/', 'snippet': 'Caught between the Kurds’ lucrative regional contracts and the Iraqi government’s ire, Exxon Mobil bows out of a big field, Iraq’s Deputy Prime Minister announced at the Oil and Money Conference.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — During the regime of Saddam Hussein, Iraq’s Kurds were subjected to a brutal campaign of killings and disappearances. While participating in the new Iraq’s politics, they have guarded their autonomy and have been determined to develop their own oil resources, which lay undiscovered under Saddam.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/blogs/14rdv-oil-and-money-west-qurna-1/14rdv-oil-and-money-west-qurna-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/blogs/14rdv-oil-and-money-west-qurna-1/14rdv-oil-and-money-west-qurna-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1338, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Oil Fight Between Baghdad and Kurds Comes to London', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kurds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Exxon Mobil Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T08:37:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Reed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Reed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8992a539-6ff9-5d1f-a9fa-9abdc223be8c', 'word_count': 245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8992a539-6ff9-5d1f-a9fa-9abdc223be8c'}, {'abstract': 'Can you calculate the total value of the college tuition donated to 500 seniors graduating from high schools in Kalamazoo, Mich., if you know the cost per year of their college educations?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/test-yourself-math-nov-14-2012/', 'snippet': 'Can you calculate the total value of the college tuition donated to 500 seniors graduating from high schools in Kalamazoo, Mich., if you know the cost per year of their college educations?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Test your math skills with this question created by Patrick Honner at Math for America from the Sunday Magazine article “Why These Kids Get a Free Ride to College.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Yourself | Math, Nov. 14, 2012', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T09:33:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b82b7ad-05b1-5f2e-ba14-f76cfe7f0c79', 'word_count': 57, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b82b7ad-05b1-5f2e-ba14-f76cfe7f0c79'}, {'abstract': 'When have you overcome obstacles and tried something truly challenging?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/kayaking-blind/', 'snippet': 'When have you overcome obstacles and tried something truly challenging?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Erik Olsen reports on the blind athlete Erik Weihenmayer’s preparations for kayaking the Colorado River in “Blind Adventurer Trains for His Scariest Challenge Yet.” Watch the above video and read the article, then answer the following questions:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kayaking Blind', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T09:45:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ariha Setalvad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ariha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Setalvad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1f1e515-5080-5cfb-b434-5e4b2e34d2f1', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1f1e515-5080-5cfb-b434-5e4b2e34d2f1'}, {'abstract': 'In recent months, deadly clashes between police and striking miners have revived fears that state security forces have returned to being forces of repression.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/miners-strike-at-fear/', 'snippet': 'In recent months, deadly clashes between police and striking miners have revived fears that state security forces have returned to being forces of repression.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Greg Marinovich is an award-winning photojournalist and filmmaker from South Africa. As a member of the famed “Bang-Bang Club” — which included Kevin Carter, Ken Oosterbroek and Joao Silva — he chronicled his homeland’s rocky transition from apartheid to democracy. His photo of a suspected spy being hacked and burned alive by supporters of the African National Congress earned Mr. Marinovich the Pulitzer Prize for spot news photography in 1991. He recently spent several weeks investigating a brutal clash in August when security forces killed 34 miners who had gone out on a wildcat strike.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South African Miners Strike at Fear', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Torture', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuma, Jacob G', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AFRICAN NATIONAL CONGRESS', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lonmin Plc', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Marikana (South Africa)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T10:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Marinovich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marinovich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d9add3a-2161-5c7b-ab17-b4f500c607c0', 'word_count': 1300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d9add3a-2161-5c7b-ab17-b4f500c607c0'}, {'abstract': 'In a bad storm or flood, what would you try to save in your home?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/with-your-home-in-danger-what-would-you-try-to-save/', 'snippet': 'In a bad storm or flood, what would you try to save in your home?', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s 9 at night, the electricity is out and the floodwaters are pouring into the first floor. You only have time to make split-second decisions about what to save from your home. What do you save and what do you toss?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/12/booming/12winerip-booming-LN/12winerip-booming-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 724, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/12/booming/12winerip-booming-LN/12winerip-booming-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1442, 'width': 2040, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/12/booming/12winerip-booming-LN/12winerip-booming-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/12/booming/12winerip-booming-LN/12winerip-booming-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/12/booming/12winerip-booming-LN/12winerip-booming-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With Your Home in Danger, What Would You Try to Save?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T10:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce018145-00b7-5581-a8bb-e30304a99ee7', 'word_count': 239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce018145-00b7-5581-a8bb-e30304a99ee7'}, {'abstract': 'With the ever-rising need for security abroad, what can American diplomacy accomplish in the fragile nations that need it most?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/magazine/christopher-stevens-and-the-problem-of-american-diplomacy.html', 'snippet': 'With the ever-rising need for security abroad, what can American diplomacy accomplish in the fragile nations that need it most?', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Ronald Neumann began his Foreign Service career in the early 1970s, he sometimes carried a pistol to protect himself. It was a reasonable precaution. American diplomats in those days lived without benefit of blast walls or security advisers, even in volatile countries, and consulates were at times housed on the ground floors of apartment buildings, with local families living on the upper stories. Neumann worked with a freedom that is scarcely imaginable for many diplomats today; he could go anywhere, by himself, and talk to anyone. In the early ’80s, when he was the deputy mission chief in Yemen, Neumann got wind of a threat to burn down the embassy building in the capital, Sana. The Arab world was in turmoil at the time, after an Israeli invasion of Lebanon and months of mounting violence. Much of the anger was directed at Americans. The embassy was easily accessible to any passer-by, an ordinary house in a residential neighborhood with no police protection. But Neumann — whose boss was out of the country at the time — did not close it down. Then things became more serious: there were rumors that angry Palestinians in Sana were planning to attack Neumann’s house. Neumann, a taciturn Vietnam veteran, took it in stride. “I brought a shotgun home from the embassy and locked the front gate,” Neumann told me. “My wife asked me if there was anything else we could do. I told her no. So she said, ‘In that case I’ve got some curtains I’ve been meaning to wash; I might as well do it now.’ I remember thinking, This is probably how they handled it when the Indian raids went down in the old West; just stay inside and mend the saddles.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18stevens1/18stevens1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18stevens1/18stevens1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18stevens1/18stevens1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18stevens1/18stevens1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18stevens1/18stevens1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18stevens1/18stevens1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18stevens1/18stevens1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can American Diplomacy Ever Come Out of Its Bunker?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Walling Off the World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Benghazi (Libya)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diplomatic Service, Embassies and Consulates', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stevens, J Christopher', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Security and Warning Systems', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Benghazi Attack (2012)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T10:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert F. Worth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Worth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3beaacb5-5989-5314-8fc2-fa808704b1d4', 'word_count': 3622, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3beaacb5-5989-5314-8fc2-fa808704b1d4'}, {'abstract': 'The British soft drink makers Britvic and A.G. Barr agreed on Wednesday to an all-share merger in a deal worth around $2.3 billion.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/british-soft-drink-companies-agree-2-3-billion-merger/', 'snippet': 'The British soft drink makers Britvic and A.G. Barr agreed on Wednesday to an all-share merger in a deal worth around $2.3 billion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — The British soft drink makers Britvic and A.G. Barr agreed on Wednesday to an all-share merger in a deal worth around $2.3 billion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Soft Drink Makers Agree to $2.3 Billion Merger', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print