 Brainy Battery', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Luggage With a Battery That Charges Devices on the Go', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T16:52:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7ab21e0-011c-5b5e-99af-29e120a05791', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7ab21e0-011c-5b5e-99af-29e120a05791'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Addison is the only prisoner on death row in the state, which has not executed anyone since 1939.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/end-the-death-penalty-in-new-hampshire/', 'snippet': 'Michael Addison is the only prisoner on death row in the state, which has not executed anyone since 1939.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New Hampshire Supreme Court is devoting Wednesday to hearing the appeal of Michael Addison, who was sentenced to death in 2008 for fatally shooting a police officer. Mr. Addison seeks a new trial or at least a new sentencing hearing and he deserves both. His were riddled with unfairness. But in illustrating how hard it is to administer capital punishment even-handedly, his case provides an opportunity for the state to take a larger step toward justice by abolishing the death penalty.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'End the Death Penalty in New Hampshire', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Addison, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-14T16:54:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lincoln Caplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lincoln', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5013a3df-6b0e-58d7-9b2d-fd44604e0ede', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5013a3df-6b0e-58d7-9b2d-fd44604e0ede'}, {'abstract': 'Dr. P. Murali Doraiswamy of Duke University addresses reader questions about the cause and diagnosis of Alzheimer’s disease and other memory problems.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/14/booming/answers-about-alzheimers-part-1.html', 'snippet': 'Dr. P. Murali Doraiswamy of Duke University addresses reader questions about the cause and diagnosis of Alzheimer’s disease and other memory problems.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Readers recently submitted more than 100 questions about Alzheimer’s and memory loss to this week’s expert, Dr. P. Murali Doraiswamy, a psychiatry professor at Duke University Medical Center and an author of “The Alzheimer’s Action Plan.” Dr. Doraiswamy has also been an adviser to government agencies, advocacy groups and businesses.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/booming/07expertplea-booming-memory/07expertplea-booming-memory-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/07/booming/07expertplea-booming-memory/07expertplea-booming-memory-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':