15', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T02:48:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/afe2b2c3-9e3a-5f70-a217-ef7b50a25c56', 'word_count': 41, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afe2b2c3-9e3a-5f70-a217-ef7b50a25c56'}, {'abstract': 'In an era of over-the-top Heisman Trophy campaigns, the Aggies are taking a different approach with the freshman quarterback sensation Johnny Manziel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/sports/ncaafootball/texas-am-hushes-the-heisman-hype-over-johnny-manziel.html', 'snippet': 'In an era of over-the-top Heisman Trophy campaigns, the Aggies are taking a different approach with the freshman quarterback sensation Johnny Manziel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the years, as Heisman Trophy campaigns came to resemble political campaigns, universities took a more active role in extolling the virtues of their contenders. Tactics included mailing neckties to voters (Brigham Young for Ty Detmer), paying for a towering billboard in Times Square (Oregon for Joey Harrington) and sending binoculars (Rutgers for Ray Rice) that urged voters to “See Ray Run.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/sports/HEISMAN/HEISMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 791, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/sports/HEISMAN/HEISMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1581, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': Non