ut it’s the taste that counts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/magazine/the-2-d-thanksgiving.html', 'snippet': 'Sure, a flattened turkey looks a little strange. But it’s the taste that counts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Believe it or not, there is more than one way to roast a turkey. First, you must ask yourself what you really want. I’ll offer you three options: A fast, crisp-skinned bird, moist and not overcooked, served with roasted vegetables; a leisurely braised bird, also with veggies; or the classic stand-up roast, presented beautifully in all its glory, prepared in a straightforward manner.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18eat1/18eat1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18eat1/18eat1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18eat1/18eat1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 739, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18eat1/18eat1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 984, 'width': 1363, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18eat1/18eat1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18eat1/18eat1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18eat1/18eat1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The 2-D Thanksgiving', 'kicker': 'Eat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 2-D Thanksgiving', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Turkeys', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thanksgiving Day', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T12:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba4831c8-cdb7-5dba-8026-eea0d487dae1', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba4831c8-cdb7-5dba-8026-eea0d487dae1'}, {'abstract': 'I imagined that, if I grew my vending business, life would become more exciting. Little did I know how exciting it would become', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/the-big-decision-stay-small-or-try-to-grow/', 'snippet': 'I imagined that, if I grew my vending business, life would become more exciting. Little did I know how exciting it would become', 'lead_paragraph': 'A traditional small business usually has five to 25 employees and does $1 million to $5 million in sales. I spent four years as an owner of a traditional small business, a vending company, and for the first three years I thought it was a great place to be. We had profits, it was an easy business to control, our customers loved us and I was able to go to work at 7 a.m. instead of 4 a.m., which is what I had been doing when the business was smaller. What’s not to like?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Big Decision: Stay Small or Try to Grow', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Family Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T12:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josh Patrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Patrick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/daa7a7c7-a366-5d60-8113-ac7669f7d2a4', 'word_count': 834, 'uri': 'nyt://article/daa7a7c7-a366-5d60-8113-ac7669f7d2a4'}, {'abstract': 'President Kirchner of Argentina is ignoring the largest collective anti-government demonstration in a decade at her peril.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/the-president-of-argentina-ignores-discontent-at-her-peril/', 'snippet': 'President Kirchner of Argentina is ignoring the largest collective anti-government demonstration in a decade at her peril.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BUENOS AIRES — For a split second last Friday, it seemed that President Cristina Fernández de Kirchner might mention the several hundred thousand people who took to the streets the previous night to demonstrate against her government. But at the end of a 49-minute speech in which she discussed the re-election of Barack Obama, the “other major event” she evoked was the 18th Congress of the Chinese Communist Party.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Divide and Be Conquered', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value'