jor': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Clear Channel Communications Inc', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Madison Square Garden', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Weinstein Co', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Who, The', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T16:25:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By James C. McKinley Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'McKinley', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2aebd49-1db8-5715-ac86-316f632d2bb2', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2aebd49-1db8-5715-ac86-316f632d2bb2'}, {'abstract': 'An Istanbul eatery’s decision to stop serving alcohol evokes fear of Kulturkampf.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/of-turkish-wine-and-backward-logic/', 'snippet': 'An Istanbul eatery’s decision to stop serving alcohol evokes fear of Kulturkampf.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IZMIR, Turkey — I suffered a peculiarly Turkish strain of NIMBY the other day while trying to make a reservation at an upmarket kebab restaurant in my nook of Istanbul. The person at the other end of the line informed me that the restaurant no longer served alcohol. I put down the phone in a mild state of shock. I had always pooh-poohed the Cassandras who believe Turkey is hopelessly divided between secular and Islamic-oriented elites. But now Kulturkampf was at my dining table.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Of Turkish Wine and Backward Logic', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T16:26:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Finkel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67182c26-9c11-593b-b655-ea69a9f93c78', 'word_count': 575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67182c26-9c11-593b-b655-ea69a9f93c78'}, {'abstract': 'Xi Jinping gave his first speech as general secretary of the Communist Party, impressing many Chinese by avoiding slogans but giving no indication as to whether he favors fundamental reforms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/world/asia/new-chinese-leader-offers-few-hints-of-a-shift-in-direction.html', 'snippet': 'Xi Jinping gave his first speech as general secretary of the Communist Party, impressing many Chinese by avoiding slogans but giving no indication as to whether he favors fundamental reforms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — China’s new leader, Xi Jinping, impressed many people with a plain-spoken promise to address problems in the country’s ruling party on Thursday, but his new leadership team offered few clues as to a clear shift in direction.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/world/China-refer/China-refer-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/16/world/China-refer/China-refer-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight