cal,” taking turns in the title role.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The casting for the four girls who will take turns in the title role in “Matilda the Musical” on Broadway was announced Thursday by the show’s producers, the Royal Shakespeare Company and the Dodgers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/arts/15artsbeat-matilda/15artsbeat-matilda-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/arts/15artsbeat-matilda/15artsbeat-matilda-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/arts/15artsbeat-matilda/15artsbeat-matilda-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/15/arts/15artsbeat-matilda/15artsbeat-matilda-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/arts/15artsbeat-matilda/15artsbeat-matilda-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Matilda’ Musical Names Four Stars', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dahl, Roald', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Shakespeare Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Shubert Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T18:59:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicia R. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicia', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/94cda7a0-6871-5965-b2da-20cd2a947c52', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/94cda7a0-6871-5965-b2da-20cd2a947c52'}, {'abstract': 'Whether they live near the coast or not, state taxpayers and insurance ratepayers are likely to be on the hook when damage from hurricanes exceeds insurance policy reserves.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/taxpayers-at-risk-as-storm-bills-come-due/', 'snippet': 'Whether they live near the coast or not, state taxpayers and insurance ratepayers are likely to be on the hook when damage from hurricanes exceeds insurance policy reserves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The states along the Gulf of Mexico and the southeastern Atlantic coast have plenty of experience when it comes to rebuilding after hurricanes. As Eric Lipton, Mary Williams Walsh and I reported this week, those residents are accustomed to turning to the National Flood Insurance Program for the money needed to rebuild — so much so that the program is nearly $18 billion in debt. And the program is likely to exceed its ability to borrow from the federal Treasury when the bills for Hurricane Sandy come due.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taxpayers at Risk As Storm Bills Come Due', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coastal Areas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Katrina (2005)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Insurance Information Institute', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Flood Insurance Program', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rand Corp', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wharton School of the University of Pennsylvania', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T19:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96e0d699-3d9a-5219-ac55-7e0c1450ef07', 'word_count': 675, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96e0d699-3d9a-5219-ac55-7e0c1450ef07'}, {'abstract': 'The public editor plans to listen and respond to readers in a new way, holding office hours Monday and Tuesday, on Twitter, at @sulliview and at the #OpenPublic hashtag.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/an-experiment-by-the-public-editor-on-twitter/', 'snippet': 'The public editor plans to listen and respond to readers in a new way, holding office hours Monday and Tuesday, on Twitter, at @sulliview and at the #OpenPublic hashtag.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since I began as The Times’s public editor in early September, I have taken up a lot of subjects – from the paper’s quote-approval policy to its coverage of Libya, from Oscar de la Renta’s attack on the fashion critic Cathy Horyn to the Chinese government’s blackout of The Times’s Chinese-language Web site in mainland China. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Experiment by the Public Editor on Twitter', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T19:04:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f14b99e8-6447-501d-b7fa-16c3c078bf19', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f14b99e8-6447-501d-b7fa-16c3c078bf19'}, {'abstract': 'Rides to the Thanksgiving Day parade and reserved seating are being provided to lift spirits.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/special-parade-views-for-5000-who-suffered-from-hurricane/', 'snippet': 'Rides to the Thanksgiving Day parade and reserved seating are being provided to lift spirits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They might have lost their homes or their possessions, but 5,000 New Yorkers whose lives were upended by Hurricane Sandy can enjoy a free ride to the Macy’s Thanksgiving Day Parade and watch the action from reserved bleachers along the route, Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg and Macy’s announced on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/nyregion/15macys2-cityroom/15macys2-cityroom-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 864, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/nyregion/15macys2-cityroom/15macys2-cityroom-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 864, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag