rs. | An interview with Biden’s former economic adviser. | Romney blames his loss on “gifts” Obama gave.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Luke Sharrett for The New York Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'TimesCast Politics: Assessing the Storm Damage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Susan E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T20:28:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e418bfa3-6e8a-5267-9acc-f3ab1788dd79', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e418bfa3-6e8a-5267-9acc-f3ab1788dd79'}, {'abstract': 'Chefs in the Argentine capital are drawing crowds by grabbing on to a trend that has become a mainstay in other foodie hot spots.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/travel/the-pop-up-surfaces-in-buenos-aires.html', 'snippet': 'Chefs in the Argentine capital are drawing crowds by grabbing on to a trend that has become a mainstay in other foodie hot spots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CASUAL parrillas, a glut of sushi and Peruvian restaurants and the occasional trendy boîte have dominated the Buenos Aires dining scene for much of the last decade. But these days, chefs are importing more than their cuisine. They have embraced a culinary trend that has become commonplace for up-and-comers in cities like London and New York: the pop-up.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18-headsup-pic/18-headsup-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18-headsup-pic/18-headsup-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18-headsup-pic/18-headsup-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18-headsup-pic/18-headsup-pic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18-headsup-pic/18-headsup-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18-headsup-pic/18-headsup-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18-headsup-pic/18-headsup-pic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pop-Up Pops Up in Buenos Aires', 'kicker': 'Heads Up', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Argentine Chefs and Pop-Ups', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARGENTINA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pop-Up Stores and Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Buenos Aires (Argentina)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T20:29:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shivani Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/293b4fd1-58bd-57d3-8816-eaf453c57dbd', 'word_count': 688, 'uri': 'nyt://article/293b4fd1-58bd-57d3-8816-eaf453c57dbd'}, {'abstract': 'Two issues where a more libertarian Republican Party would probably be a more successful party.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/republicans-in-a-changing-country/', 'snippet': 'Two issues where a more libertarian Republican Party would probably be a more successful party.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’ve had about a week’s worth of recriminations and arguments about where the Republican Party goes from here, and the one theme uniting all of them is this: Whatever a given writer believed before the election correlates marvelously with their favored explanation for why Mitt Romney went down to defeat. If you were happy with the ideological situation on the right, you’ve probably either emphasized the practical deficiencies of the Romney campaign (his weaknesses as a candidate, his flawed turnout operation, his pollster’s rose-colored glasses), or else looked for a supposed silver bullet like immigration reform to solve the Republican Party’s demographic issues without touching the rest of the party platform. If you’re socially conservative and populist but skeptical of Wall Street and big business, then you’ve probably argued the party’s biggest mistake was to nominate a plutocrat and run a campaign tilted too far toward economic individualism and the interests of the rich. If you’re fiscally conservative but libertarian on social issues, then you’ve probably made the case that Todd Akin, Richard Mourdock and anti-immigrant yahoos took the focus off the economy, and that cultural conservatives need to pipe down and let the party modernize on gay rights, immigration and abortion. If you’re David Frum, who has been arguing that the party needs to be more middle class friendly and more socially liberal, then you’ve been claiming vindication on both counts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Republicans in a Changing Country', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T20:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d7539f4-94d2-59d2-96ef-fb31f1e56dda', 'word_count': 1074, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d7539f4-94d2-59d2-96ef-fb31f1e56dda'}, {'abstract': 'At this spot, which opened in January, there’s a narrative behind the name and a philosophy behind the food.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/travel/restaurant-report-katz-orange-in-berlin.html', 'snippet': 'At this spot, which opened in January, there’s a narrative behind the name and a philosophy behind the food.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Berlin may be a capital of Europe’s art and clubbing scenes, but when it comes to food, it still lags well behind restaurant-obsessed cities like Paris and London. There are a few standout Michelin-starred restaurants and plenty of cheap kebabs and currywurst stands, but not much in between. In the last few years though, a few ambitious (mostly American) chefs have opened small, modern bistro-style spots that celebrate local organic ingredients. The success of farmer-driven places like Lokal and Little Otik (both overseen by American chefs) have proven that there is a growing appetite for upscale seasonal comfort food.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18BITES_SPAN/18BITES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18BITES_SPAN/18BITES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18BITES_SPAN/18BITES_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18BITES_SPAN/18BITES_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18BITES_SPAN/18BITES_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18BITES_SPAN/18BITES_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/travel/18BITES_SPAN/18BITES_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Restaurant Report: Katz Orange in Berlin', 'kicker': 'Bites ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Berlin: Katz Orange', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T20:31:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gisela Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gisela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2faca22f-5241-5401-91fc-160851962798', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2faca22f-5241-5401-91fc-160851962798'}, {'abstract': 'Questions and answers on $500 billion in tax increases and across-the-board spending cuts scheduled to take effect after Jan. 1.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/us/politics/the-fiscal-cliff-explained.html', 'snippet': 'Questions and answers on $500 billion in tax increases and across-the-board spending cuts scheduled to take effect after Jan. 1.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many Americans must be wondering: What is all this about a “fiscal cliff”? And why did it receive so little attention during the presidential campaign? ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Demystifying the Fiscal Impasse That Is Vexing Washington', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Demystifying the Fiscal Impasse That Is Vexing Washington', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T20:35:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/162219cb-1dc4-58e9-ac22-efdd7393ac61', 'word_count': 1303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/162219cb-1dc4-58e9-ac22-efdd7393ac61'}, {'abstract': 'A 250-foot observation tower will give race fans a view from the top.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/a-towering-landmark-for-formula-one-track/', 'snippet': 'A 250-foot observation tower will give race fans a view from the top.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fans of Formula One come to see the action on the track, not what’s around the track, said Juan Miró, an architect. But luckily for him, the owners of the Circuit of the Americas, the new Formula One site on the edge of Austin, Tex., wanted to create a memorable setting for events like the 2012 United States Grand Prix (which runs Friday through Sunday).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Towering Landmark for Formula One Track', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Racing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Formula One', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Austin (Tex)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_d