tor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Good Forecasts', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Good Forecasts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T21:57:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/40d6771b-5cb5-5fcb-9ab8-622fa019a129', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40d6771b-5cb5-5fcb-9ab8-622fa019a129'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Nicholson Baker’s collection of essays “The Way the World Works.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/reading-history-morally.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Nicholson Baker’s collection of essays “The Way the World Works.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reading History Morally?', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reading History Morally?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T21:57:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/6aedd262-abbb-5610-9ac5-4eb5bd4987a8', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6aedd262-abbb-5610-9ac5-4eb5bd4987a8'}, {'abstract': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Steven Rinella’s “Meat Eater.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/one-mans-meat.html', 'snippet': 'A reader responds to a recent review of Steven Rinella’s “Meat Eater.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Man’s Meat', 'kicker': 'Letter', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'One Man’s Meat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T21:58:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/64841766-85da-5891-98b4-019baced1cf2', 'word_count': 104, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64841766-85da-5891-98b4-019baced1cf2'}, {'abstract': 'The baseball commissioner said he would review Miami’s trade of top talent for Toronto prospects, but he noted that “clubs have to make their own decisions.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/sports/baseball/bud-selig-gives-no-indication-he-will-block-marlins-trade.html', 'snippet': 'The baseball commissioner said he would review Miami’s trade of top talent for Toronto prospects, but he noted that “clubs have to make their own decisions.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROSEMONT, Ill. — Two days after the Miami Marlins shocked their fans and the baseball world by announcing a trade that purged their roster of large salaries and much of its talent, Commissioner Bud Selig weighed in.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reviewing Marlins Deal, Selig Expresses Caution', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reviewing Marlins Deal, Selig Expresses Caution', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reyes, Jose', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Marlins', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toronto Blue Jays', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Josh', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Selig, Bud', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T21:59:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Strauss', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdb4bfec-cd9a-5e53-8b0c-0b4bc2f455d9', 'word_count': 588, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdb4bfec-cd9a-5e53-8b0c-0b4bc2f455d9'}, {'abstract': 'Rajon Rondo of the Celtics and Omer Asik of the Rockets have excelled. Ersan Ilyasova of the Bucks has not.', 'web_url': 'https://offthedribble.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/five-surprises-of-the-young-n-b-a-season/', 'snippet': 'Rajon Rondo of the Celtics and Omer Asik of the Rockets have excelled. Ersan Ilyasova of the Bucks has not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The excitement of a new N.B.A. season makes it easy to mine surprises from small sample sizes. From hot team starts (Knicks, Clippers and Spurs) to redeemed expectations (DeAndre Jordan and O.J. Mayo) to rookie leaps (Anthony Davis, Michael Kidd-Gilchrist and Damian Lillard), there are plenty of front-and-center narratives ripe for the double take. But what about the more under-the-radar story lines? Here are six developments to keep your eyes on.Rajon Rondo’s midrange shooting: It seems no discussion of Rondo’s place in the current N.B.A. is complete without a proper hand-wringing over the guard’s notoriously inconsistent jumper. So far at least, that qualm has been muted, as Rondo, Boston’s floor general, has quietly put up some of the best shooting numbers of his career: a true shooting percentage (which accounts for free throws and 3-point field goals) of 53 percent, including 50 percent from 16 to 24 feet, and 67 percent from 8 to 16 feet. It will be interesting to see whether Rondo can continue making teams pay for their sagging and hedging, but Boston will certainly pocket the found money as long as it can.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/sports/basketball/15asik/15asik-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/sports/basketball/15asik/15asik-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1518, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/sports/basketball/15asik/15asik-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/15/sports/basketball/15asik/15asik-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/sports/basketball/15asik/15asik-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Six Surprises of the Young N.B.A. Season', 'kicker': 'Off the Dribble', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Basketball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Adelman, Rick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Anthony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kirilenko, Andrei', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morey, Daryl', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Popovich, Gregg', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rondo, Rajon', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Clippers', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Miami Heat', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Milwaukee Bucks', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'San Antonio Spurs', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T22:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Cavan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cavan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a38d094e-2418-54df-902f-da33ea9e06f6', 'word_count': 978, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a38d094e-2418-54df-902f-da33ea9e06f6'}, {'abstract': 'The wages of austerity, again.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/oh-we-see-disaster/', 'snippet': 'The wages of austerity, again.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I got my first heads-up of the extent to which austerity fever had taken over the minds of Very Serious People when the OECD, which is conventional wisdom central, went all in for austerity now now now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Oh, We See Disaster', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T22:02:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2a35059-e119-5390-b583-ff751ecedbfe', 'word_count': 139, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2a35059-e119-5390-b583-ff751ecedbfe'}, {'abstract': 'The “Moby-Dick” marathon organizers assembled a list of “practice words” for readers to mull in advance of the marathon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/nyregion/how-to-speak-whale.html', 'snippet': 'The “Moby-Dick” marathon organizers assembled a list of “practice words” for readers to mull in advance of the marathon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “Moby-Dick” marathon organizers assembled a list of “practice words” for readers to mull in advance of the marathon. Stretch your tongue muscles and dust off your dictionaries, Melville fans. AMANDA PETRUSICH', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How to Speak Whale', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'How to Speak Whale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Moby-Dick (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T22:06:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Petrusich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Petrusich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b58d51a4-c0ed-5929-a11d-c11b493c1467', 'word_count': 63, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b58d51a4-c0ed-5929-a11d-c11b493c1467'}, {'abstract': 'A three-day reading of “Moby-Dick” will take place beginning Friday at three independent bookstores in Brooklyn and Manhattan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/nyregion/moby-dick-marathon-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'A three-day reading of “Moby-Dick” will take place beginning Friday at three independent bookstores in Brooklyn and Manhattan.', 'lead_paragraph': '“MOBY-DICK” intimidates. Its hundreds of pages of rich, sometimes inscrutable prose, assembled in service of a quest story that’s heartbreaking if not existentially ruinous, make up the kind of novel you’ll want to impale, attach to an oak plaque and hang over the mantel when you finish. If you finish. Much like its namesake, it is not easily vanquished.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '36', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/16MOBYBOOKCOMBO/16MOBYBOOKCOMBO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/16MOBYBOOKCOMBO/16MOBYBOOKCOMBO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/16MOBYBOOKCOMBO/16MOBYBOOKCOMBO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/16MOBYBOOKCOMBO/16MOBYBOOKCOMBO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/16MOBYBOOKCOMBO/16MOBYBOOKCOMBO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Call It a ‘Moby’ Marathon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Call It a ‘Moby’ Marathon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Melville, Herman', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Housing Works', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Moby-Dick (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T22:08:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Petrusich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Petrusich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/498f1820-8803-5fe1-ae41-9ea7124ae273', 'word_count': 1221, 'uri': 'nyt://article/498f1820-8803-5fe1-ae41-9ea7124ae273'}, {'abstract': 'Still uncertain about what kind of menu to plan for Thanksgiving? Use our handy Thanksgiving-erator and have a menu planned for you.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/11/15/dining/thanksgiving-menu-generator.html', 'snippet': 'Still uncertain about what kind of menu to plan for Thanksgiving? Use our handy Thanksgiving-erator and have a menu planned for you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Still uncertain about what kind of menu to plan for Thanksgiving? Use our handy Thanksgiving-erator and have a menu planned for you.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Thanksgiving-erator', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thanksgiving Day', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T22:11:00+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Food', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/736f2cad-6ba9-5e9c-a7e1-f182ce46fb60', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/736f2cad-6ba9-5e9c-a7e1-f182ce46fb60'}, {'abstract': 'As Bank of America and the insurer MBIA continue to battle over claims left over from the financial crisis, the lawyers are doing well, but those who suffered in the crisis are not.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/business/bank-of-america-and-mbia-revisit-the-mortgage-debacle.html', 'snippet': 'As Bank of America and the insurer MBIA continue to battle over claims left over from the financial crisis, the lawyers are doing well, but those who suffered in the crisis are not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two villains in the financial crisis — whose negligence, or worse, caused untold suffering when the property bubble burst — are fighting it out. They are battling in the courts, and now in the bond market, where lucky owners of one bond issued by a company that may yet go broke are being offered the chance to cash out at par value. Lawyers are cleaning up.The only people who don’t seem to be doing very well are those who are most deserving: the victims of the crisis. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/norris-75-standardinline/norris-75-standardinline-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/04/19/business/norris-75-standardinline/norris-75-standardinline-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As the Crisis Is Revisited, Lawyers Win', 'kicker': 'High & Low Finance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As the Crisis Is Revisited, Lawyers Win', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Countrywide Financial Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MBIA Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgages', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T22:12:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/046bcecc-64a0-5642-aba1-f99d901c963b', 'word_count': 1497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/046bcecc-64a0-5642-aba1-f99d901c963b'}, {'abstract': 'Barbara Keal’s felt hats will be on exhibit at Anthropologie’s gallery at Rockefeller Plaza beginning Nov. 15.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/fashion/fantastical-felt-hats-creature-comforts-for-the-head.html', 'snippet': 'Barbara Keal’s felt hats will be on exhibit at Anthropologie’s gallery at Rockefeller Plaza beginning Nov. 15.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Barbara Keal’s felt hats look like something out of a children’s story: they are large; they have ears, often with furry surfaces and felt hanging down from them. Some of her hats resemble sheep, others foxes. Still others look like creatures out of your imagination, or that of Ms. Keal, a British artist who says, “I experience myself still a child in some ways.”\xa0', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/fashion/hatsspan/hatsspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/16/fashion/hatsspan/hatsspan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/fashion/hatsspan/hatsspan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/fashion/hatsspan/hatsspan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/fashion/hatsspan/hatsspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/16/fashion/hatsspan/hatsspan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/fashion/hatsspan/hatsspan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fantastical Felt Hats: Creature Comforts for the Head', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hats and Caps', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wool and Woolen Goods', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ANTHROPOLOGIE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T22:12:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb602ba1-06d4-5ad3-a1d0-4920fd94a1c9', 'word_count': 262, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb602ba1-06d4-5ad3-a1d0-4920fd94a1c9'}, {'abstract': 'Kevin Young is working on a book of nonfiction — “a how and why of hoaxes” — called “Unoriginal Sin.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Kevin Young is working on a book of nonfiction — “a how and why of hoaxes” — called “Unoriginal Sin.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Kevin Young reviews Yuval Taylor and Jake Austen’s “Darkest America,” a history of minstrelsy performed by African-Americans. Young, the author of seven books of poetry, also writes nonfiction, and in “The Grey Album” he examines the centrality of African-American culture to American culture. “I use the idea of lying — or ‘storying’ — to trace both a hidden tradition and a tradition of hiding,” Young wrote in an e-mail. “I look at the imagination as a way of escape, from Negro spirituals to hip-hop, Phillis Wheatley to Public Enemy.” Did his research overlap much with the material covered by Taylor and Austen? “I suppose we read the same books,” Young said, “many of which they tried to contradict.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-upfront/1118-upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 630, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-upfront/1118-upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1261, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Young, Kevin (1970- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-15T22:14:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d84bfca-e98d-5360-8a75-793d721f22ed', 'word_count': 206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d84bfca-e98d-5360-8a75-793d721f22ed'}, {'abstract': 'A MoMA exhibition, “Tokyo 1955-1970: A New Avant-Garde,” shows how the city transformed itself from the capital of a war-torn nation into an international center for arts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/arts/design/tokyo-1955-1970-a-new-avant-garde-at-moma.html', 'snippet': 'A MoMA exhibition, “Tokyo 1955-1970: A New Avant-Garde,” shows how the city transformed itself from the capital of a war-torn nation into an international center for arts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Modernist art, insurgent and utopian, ignited like a firecracker cluster in cities across the globe in the early 20th century. Paris, New York, Shanghai, Bombay, Lagos and Tokyo each had its own version, which was also part of larger trend. Yet the new art’s progress was not on the same timetable everywhere. There were local interruptions.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/16TOKYO_SPAN/16TOKYO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 477, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/16TOKYO_SPAN/16TOKYO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlarg