ection_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/532f9741-e057-5dc8-abcd-c74ac0b148fb', 'word_count': 1050, 'uri': 'nyt://article/532f9741-e057-5dc8-abcd-c74ac0b148fb'}, {'abstract': 'The stations, in New York City, on Long Island and in Westchester County, raised their prices after Hurricane Sandy, charging $4.74 to $5.50 a gallon, the New York attorney general said.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/15/state-accuses-13-new-york-gas-stations-of-price-gouging/', 'snippet': 'The stations, in New York City, on Long Island and in Westchester County, raised their prices after Hurricane Sandy, charging $4.74 to $5.50 a gallon, the New York attorney general said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thirteen gas stations in New York City, on Long Island and in Westchester County are being hit with civil price-gouging charges in the wake of Hurricane Sandy, the state attorney general, Eric T. Schneiderman, said. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'State Accuses 13 New York Gas Stations of Price-Gouging', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schneiderman, Eric T', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T00:04:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f38d9fa-ef1e-5eea-83dd-51e989a931b8', 'word_count': 409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f38d9fa-ef1e-5eea-83dd-51e989a931b8'}, {'abstract': 'In “First Winter” a group of Brooklyn residents react in various ways to being stranded at a country farmhouse during a blackout.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/movies/first-winter-a-benjamin-dickinson-film.html', 'snippet': 'In “First Winter” a group of Brooklyn residents react in various ways to being stranded at a country farmhouse during a blackout.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What sophisticated urban dweller hasn’t entertained the uneasy fantasy of being stranded in the country during a catastrophe without power, communication or transportation? In Benjamin Dickinson’s dour, visually stunning “First Winter,” the visitors at an upstate New York yoga retreat find themselves cut off from the outside world during the dead of an unusually harsh winter. As it dawns on these coddled Brooklyn hipsters camped in a remote farmhouse that they’re running out of food, they are forced to muster whatever survival skills they have acquired. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/16FIRST_SPAN/16FIRST_SPAN-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 339, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/16FIRST_SPAN/16FIRST_SPAN-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 339}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/16FIRST_SPAN/16FIRST_SPAN-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 578, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': N