dle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T01:29:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Adams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/557d3a8b-bafd-5bda-af6c-a2426e6b8f29', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/557d3a8b-bafd-5bda-af6c-a2426e6b8f29'}, {'abstract': 'Raising the retirement age on Social Security and Medicare would be a harsh blow to Americans in the bottom half of the income distribution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/opinion/life-death-and-deficits.html', 'snippet': 'Raising the retirement age on Social Security and Medicare would be a harsh blow to Americans in the bottom half of the income distribution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'America’s political landscape is infested with many zombie ideas — beliefs about policy that have been repeatedly refuted with evidence and analysis but refuse to die. The most prominent zombie is the insistence that low taxes on rich people are the key to prosperity. But there are others.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/paul-krugman/paul-krugman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}},