, 'lead_paragraph': "A view from above of the Douglas family's stockpile, including solar panels, a vacuum sealer and a year's supply of a wheat.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/magazine/video-survivalist-vid/video-survivalist-vid-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/15/magazine/video-survivalist-vid/video-survivalist-vid-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/magazine/video-survivalist-vid/video-survivalist-vid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/15/magazine/video-survivalist-vid/video-survivalist-vid-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Survivalist Photo Shoot', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:34:57+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Dwight Eschliman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dwight', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eschliman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/defdc795-ecbc-5507-9b96-7278c8163b8a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/defdc795-ecbc-5507-9b96-7278c8163b8a'}, {'abstract': 'The eight portraits hanging in a residence for Jesuit priests in SoHo are a link to one of the most notorious episodes in the brutal civil war in El Salvador.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/in-priests-dining-room-a-reminder-of-brutality-in-el-salvador/', 'snippet': 'The eight portraits hanging in a residence for Jesuit priests in SoHo are a link to one of the most notorious episodes in the brutal civil war in El Salvador.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Inside a SoHo residence on Thompson Street, eight portraits hang in a corner of the dining room, arrayed above jars of sunflower and pumpkin seeds, boxes of cookies, and bottles of soy sauce and vinegar. The framed drawings – two women flanked by six men – seem utterly ordinary. But what happened to them 23 years ago this week was not.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Priests’ Dining Room, a Reminder of Brutality in El Salvador', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '8 Killed in El Salvador, Revered on Jesuits’ Wall', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Priests', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'El Salvador', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'SoHo (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:39:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ae7edc1-ed4e-551c-a62b-ffe5cc267529', 'word_count': 778, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ae7edc1-ed4e-551c-a62b-ffe5cc267529'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THOMAS JEFFERSON: The Art of Power,  by Jon Meacham. (Random House, $35.) This readable and well-\xadresearched life celebrates Jefferson’s skills as a practical politician, unafraid to wield power even when it conflicted with his small-government views.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:44:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/191f0adb-0c34-5ceb-a821-6bb14559fd03', 'word_count': 280, 'uri': 'nyt://article/191f0adb-0c34-5ceb-a821-6bb14559fd03'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE ANGEL ESMERALDA: Stories,  by Don DeLillo. (Scribner, $15.) DeLillo’s first collection of short fiction, written between 1979 and 2011, offers telling insights into his fascination with the uneasy margins of contemporary life. In the 1994 title story, an aging nun in the South Bronx is swept up in a crowd that gathers around a billboard, seeking holy significance in the meaningless murder of a homeless girl.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:44:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/7088f464-31a5-5309-bd07-8250c10f6414', 'word_count': 485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7088f464-31a5-5309-bd07-8250c10f6414'}, {'abstract': 'Tessa Hadley’s stories observe subtle differences and what they signify.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/married-love-stories-by-tessa-hadley.html', 'snippet': 'Tessa Hadley’s stories observe subtle differences and what they signify.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Class hurts. Its injuries — as small as a paper cut, or as large as the difference between the 99 and the 1 percent — carry with them an exponential degree of shame. One often hears that we in the United States are in denial about class, but it might be more accurate to say that we maintain a tight-lipped silence about differences we can all see perfectly well. We know the relative rankings of brands, schools, addresses, food and body mass indexes; we understand understatement. We even understand about understanding understatement and rejecting its clenched, single-strand-of-pearls aspirations. We did, after all, invent Target. In contemporary American fiction, class markers aren’t in short supply, nor are writers shy about using them. George Saunders and Deborah Eisenberg come to mind as American writers who are deeply aware of class — he the lower, she the upper middle — and who have each created an aesthetics founded in the sensibility of these ever-vexed class positions. We can pretty much tell where their characters are likely to shop. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Derasmo/1118-Derasmo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Derasmo/1118-Derasmo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Derasmo/1118-Derasmo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Derasmo/1118-Derasmo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1421, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Derasmo/1118-Derasmo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Derasmo/1118-Derasmo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Derasmo/1118-Derasmo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Class Notes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Class Notes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hadley, Tessa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:45:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': "By Stacey D'Erasmo", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stacey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "D'Erasmo", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7925efb0-01e3-5661-8dfb-a79bb2c64ea3', 'word_count': 970, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7925efb0-01e3-5661-8dfb-a79bb2c64ea3'}, {'abstract': 'New books about food by The Times’s writers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/new-books-about-food-by-the-timess-writers.html', 'snippet': 'New books about food by The Times’s writers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the holidays approaching, the population density of many home kitchens will be increasing — and so will the stress level, for novice cooks and for more experienced hosts and hostesses coping, as Sam Sifton puts it in THANKSGIVING: How to Cook It Well (Random House, $18), with a day of “crowded dinner tables, of people arriving late or needing to leave early, of burned yams and spouses who forgot to buy the one thing — the one thing! — you asked them not to forget to buy.” Now the national editor of The Times, Sifton was once the newspaper’s restaurant critic, and during that period served as “a one-man Thanksgiving help line.” This season, though, the help can come from a whole crew of The Times’s writers, all with new books. Mark Bittman’s HOW TO COOK EVERYTHING: The Basics (Wiley, $35) is full of colorful illustrations, and COOKFIGHT: 2 Cooks, 12 Challenges, 125 Recipes, an Epic Battle for Kitchen Dominance (Ecco/Harper\xadCollins, $29.99) allows Kim Severson and Julia Moskin to divert readers who might have come to blows over competing stuffing recipes. Others could settle in with Eric Asimov’s HOW TO LOVE WINE: A Memoir and Manifesto (Morrow/HarperCollins, $24.99). Or slink off with a nice bottle of Chablis and THE NEW YORK TIMES BOOK OF WINE: More Than 30 Years of Vintage Writing (Sterling Epicure, $24.95), edited by Howard G. Goldberg. With luck, no one will notice your absence until the bottle is empty.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cooking Times', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cooking Times', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:45:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/20f7f9c6-e52b-58fc-b9c6-afe303aa436c', 'word_count': 244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20f7f9c6-e52b-58fc-b9c6-afe303aa436c'}, {'abstract': 'Bee Wilson serves up a smorgasbord of information about food tools and techniques.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/consider-the-fork-by-bee-wilson.html', 'snippet': 'Bee Wilson serves up a smorgasbord of information about food tools and techniques.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Which comes first, the stir-fry or the wok?” It may sound like a bad joke, but the answer holds the key to one of the world’s great cuisines. Bee Wilson’s supple, sometimes playful style in “Consider the Fork,” a history of the tools and techniques humans have invented to feed themselves, cleverly disguises her erudition in fields from archaeology and anthropology to food science. Only when you find yourself rattling off statistics at the dinner table will you realize how much information you’ve effortlessly absorbed. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Drzal/1118-Drzal-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Drzal/1118-Drzal-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 150}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Drzal/1118-Drzal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 256, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Drzal/1118-Drzal-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Drzal/1118-Drzal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Drzal/1118-Drzal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Drzal/1118-Drzal-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Science of Sizzle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Science of Sizzle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Bee (1974- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:45:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dawn Drzal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dawn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drzal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/699d3847-bc3e-5082-8a77-49163b07d579', 'word_count': 856, 'uri': 'nyt://article/699d3847-bc3e-5082-8a77-49163b07d579'}, {'abstract': 'Times critics on “Anna Karenina,” “Silver Linings Playbook” and “The Twilight Saga: Breaking Dawn — Part 2.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/this-weeks-movies-nov-16/', 'snippet': 'Times critics on “Anna Karenina,” “Silver Linings Playbook” and “The Twilight Saga: Breaking Dawn — Part 2.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Times critics look at the latest film adaptation of Leo Tolstoy’s “Anna Karenina,” David O. Russell’s comedy “Silver Linings Playbook” and the final installment in the “Twilight” series, “Breaking Dawn — Part 2.” All of this week’s reviews can be found here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Movies: Nov. 16', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:45:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/82d40fe4-ed2f-5f93-a856-0f6ff3f1c288', 'word_count': 44, 'uri': 'nyt://article/82d40fe4-ed2f-5f93-a856-0f6ff3f1c288'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Charles McGrath talks about Alice Munro’s “Dear Life”; Leslie Kaufman has notes from the field; J. D. Biersdorfer discusses Sean Howe’s history of Marvel Comics; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. John Williams is the host, filling in for Sam Tanenhaus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2012/11/16/books/review/18books_pod.html', 'snippet': 'This week, Charles McGrath talks about Alice Munro’s “Dear Life”; Leslie Kaufman has notes from the field; J. D. Biersdorfer discusses Sean Howe’s history of Marvel Comics; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. John Williams is the host, fillin...', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Charles McGrath talks about Alice Munro’s “Dear Life”; Leslie Kaufman has notes from the field; J. D. Biersdorfer discusses Sean Howe’s history of Marvel Comics; and Gregory Cowles has best-seller news. John Williams is the host, filling in for Sam Tanenhaus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Review Podcast', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Podcasts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:47:25+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/820d696f-b9b4-5794-9174-37d963fb574b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/820d696f-b9b4-5794-9174-37d963fb574b'}, {'abstract': 'A longtime clean-air campaigner sees a valuable role for citizens as methane monitors.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/can-public-leak-patrols-stem-gas-emissions-at-a-profit/', 'snippet': 'A longtime clean-air campaigner sees a valuable role for citizens as methane monitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nov. 18, 8:54 a.m. | Updates Below |This is the second of a pair of “Your Dot” reactions to my post on\xa0citizens’ efforts to build mapped data bases\xa0as a tool for tracking the gas drilling surge in the United States.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Public Leak Patrols Stem Gas Emissions at a Profit?', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clean Air Act', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drilling and Boring', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Methane', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Lisa P', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:49:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e7abb1b-05dd-5aff-b0e2-0bd2f3a53e5f', 'word_count': 1323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e7abb1b-05dd-5aff-b0e2-0bd2f3a53e5f'}, {'abstract': 'A sudsy party in Watertown, N.Y.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/11/18/magazine/foam-party.html', 'snippet': 'A sudsy party in Watertown, N.Y.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A sudsy party in Watertown, N.Y.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'There Will Be Foam', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:50:05+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/87518fe6-5494-556b-98d3-4f8abbd46a93', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/87518fe6-5494-556b-98d3-4f8abbd46a93'}, {'abstract': 'Why do women squander so much of our youth worrying about being too old?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/magazine/girls-love-math-we-never-stop-doing-it.html', 'snippet': 'Why do women squander so much of our youth worrying about being too old?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I live in Los Angeles, where despite my best efforts, I occasionally hear a story like this: A friend knows an actress whose burglar alarm code — 2828 — serves as a reminder to her of the age she must never surpass. (The repetition adds a touch of hysteria, which I like.) Another friend lives next door to a model-actress who, in her late 20s, is considered to be so far out on the ledge of her prime that she was recently cast opposite a 40-something man as the mother of two teenage children. While the age distribution represented is biologically feasible (it’s possible that a woman could have her first child, with a 30-year-old husband, at age 12), child marriage is generally frowned upon even in California.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '52', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18riff/mag-18Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 89, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18riff/mag-18Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 89}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18riff/mag-18Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 153, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18riff/mag-18Riff-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 305, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18riff/18riff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18riff/18riff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18riff/18riff-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Girls Love Math. We Never Stop Doing It.’', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Girls Love Math.We Never Stop Doing It.’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Age, Chronological', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:50:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carina Chocano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chocano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7fc887c8-de8b-5983-9634-fc3dd6361b1a', 'word_count': 1950, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7fc887c8-de8b-5983-9634-fc3dd6361b1a'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs from a sudsy party in Watertown, N.Y.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/magazine/foam-party-photographs-by-alec-soth.html', 'snippet': 'Photographs from a sudsy party in Watertown, N.Y.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Crazy Legs Saloon, in Watertown, N.Y., probably wouldn’t have its summertime foam parties were it not for two neighbors: Slack Chemical and Fort Drum. Mike L’Huillier, the bar’s manager, says a lot of the military personnel in the area had foam parties back home, so he decided to throw one of his own. But foam machines aren’t cheap, so L’Huillier fashioned one with a barrel from Slack Chemical, a wet-dry vacuum, a garden hose and plenty of dish soap. He called it the Monster. The photographer Alec Soth happened to capture the scene earlier this year when he was in upstate New York working on The LBM Dispatch, a newspaper he publishes periodically. The best part about getting to soak 250 people, according to L’Huillier? “You walk in the next day and it’s like you took the bar through a carwash.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '45', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'There Will Be Foam', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'There Will be Foam', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Staley, Willy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Watertown (NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:50:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Willy Staley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Willy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Staley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c2a6804-25de-5884-8750-46e4b200f11a', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c2a6804-25de-5884-8750-46e4b200f11a'}, {'abstract': 'A subway Robin Hood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/magazine/mother-and-child-reunion.html', 'snippet': 'A subway Robin Hood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m a social worker in the adoption field. A woman who surrendered her infant decades ago asked me for information about the child, and I gave her as much as I legally could, which is not very much. (New York is a closed-records state.) Two days later, I picked up a magazine with an article about someone I recognized as this woman’s child; the article mentioned the birth name, which was given by the woman I spoke to. What can I do? The birth mother knows what she named her child but may never read this particular article. I would never reveal confidential identities, but does pointing her toward a published piece carry the same weight? M.D.P., NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18ethicist/18ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mother-and-Child Reunion', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mother-and-Child Reunion', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transit Systems', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADOPTIONS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:50:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a71af57-f665-5b8c-b654-86f509eec047', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a71af57-f665-5b8c-b654-86f509eec047'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/magazine/the-11-412-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EMPIRE OF THE IN-BETWEEN', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 11.4.12 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 11.4.12 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:50:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2c9e971-fb47-5bbd-9e91-b4101bca0edf', 'word_count': 567, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2c9e971-fb47-5bbd-9e91-b4101bca0edf'}, {'abstract': 'And how it became a psychedelic icon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/magazine/who-made-that-sugar-cube.html', 'snippet': 'And how it became a psychedelic icon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 1700s, Europeans bought their sugar in brown loafs that had to be hacked, pounded and smashed into smaller pieces. “People just accepted that in order to use sugar, you had to go through all this physical trouble,” says Elizabeth Abbott, the author of “Sugar: A Bittersweet History.” “The big surprise is that there wasn’t a sugar cube invented much earlier.” By the 1800s, stores sold sugar already broken up into random-size pieces. But these chunks could be inconvenient at teatime. They often had to be dunked doughnut-style, because they wouldn’t fit in the cup. When the tea was finished, you were left with a sticky clump to dry out for future use.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18WMT-t_CA0/18WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 320, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18WMT-t_CA0/18WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 320}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18WMT-t_CA0/mag-18WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 876, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18WMT-t_CA0/mag-18WMT-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 905, 'width': 1058, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18WMT-t_CA0/18WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18WMT-t_CA0/18WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Sugar Cube?', 'kicker': 'Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Sugar Cube)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Abbott, Elizabeth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sugar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:50:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pagan Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pagan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf7d8c62-784e-59aa-871c-ab5bbdc26ce9', 'word_count': 506, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf7d8c62-784e-59aa-871c-ab5bbdc26ce9'}, {'abstract': 'To become truly flexible, a yoga rookie faces his worst fear.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/magazine/no-one-told-me-learning-yoga-would-involve-snakes.html', 'snippet': 'To become truly flexible, a yoga rookie faces his worst fear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not long ago, I found myself in the back seat of a small car deep in the jungle of India, an arm’s length from a 40-pound python, which sat tightly coiled within a burlap sack. I hate snakes. I won’t step foot in the reptile house at the zoo, and I’d rather be recircumcised than watch the movie “Snakes on a Plane.” How I ended up in this particular car, with this particular snake, is a long story. It all got started because of my inability to touch my toes.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18lives/mag-18Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 373, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18lives/mag-18Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 355, 'width': 373, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18lives/18lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18lives/18lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18lives/18lives-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No One Told Me Learning Yoga Would Involve Snakes', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cobra Pose', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Yoga', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:50:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Halpern', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Halpern', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b7d6988a-4974-5f95-bcf9-a5d75ad69420', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b7d6988a-4974-5f95-bcf9-a5d75ad69420'}, {'abstract': 'The musician talks about ex-boyfriends, Dolly Parton and what makes a band cool.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/magazine/the-many-insecurities-of-taylor-swift.html', 'snippet': 'The musician talks about ex-boyfriends, Dolly Parton and what makes a band cool.', 'lead_paragraph': ' At the CMA awards earlier this month, Carrie Underwood and Brad Paisley cracked a joke about your reported breakup with Robert F. Kennedy Jr.’s son Conor Kennedy. But the camera never panned to you.  They don’t pan to you if you’re not laughing.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18talk/mag-18Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 750, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18talk/mag-18Talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1099, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18talk/18talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18talk/18talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/magazine/18talk/18talk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Many Insecurities of Taylor Swift', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Islands in the iPod Stream', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swift, Taylor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T15:50:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Susan Dominus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Susan', 'lastname': 'Dominus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/0577c5d6-e898-5e01-a0bd-b362d1d0b857', 'word_count': 737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0577c5d6-e898-5e01-a0bd-b362d1d0b857'}, {'abstract': 'Anthony Tommasini performs some of his favorite classical music moments on piano and invites readers to suggest their own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/arts/music/100000001907790/musical-moments.html', 'snippet': 'Anthony Tommasini performs some of his favorite classical music moments on piano and invites readers to suggest their own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anthony Tommasini performs some of his favorite classical music moments on piano and invites readers to suggest their own.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/video-moments-chopin/video-moments-chopin-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/video-moments-chopin/video-moments-chopin-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/video-moments-chopin/video-moments-chopin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/16/arts/video-moments-chopin/video-moments-chopin-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mu