//article/7746b49e-7887-53c0-8239-02d796f60d6e'}, {'abstract': 'There are many passages from all sorts of pieces that have always moved me. I want to share some of my favorites and hear about yours.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/musical-moments-what-moves-us/', 'snippet': 'There are many passages from all sorts of pieces that have always moved me. I want to share some of my favorites and hear about yours.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We all have our favorites in music. An opera or a symphony. Or, being a little more specific, a favorite aria in an opera, or movement in a symphony, or song in a song cycle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Musical Moments: What Moves Us?', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/21c1c494-752e-5811-b3e2-768cd2f9cc7e', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/21c1c494-752e-5811-b3e2-768cd2f9cc7e'}, {'abstract': 'Chrysler’s electric car, the Fiat 500e, will make its debut at the American Music Awards on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/wheelies-the-e-v-edition/', 'snippet': 'Chrysler’s electric car, the Fiat 500e, will make its debut at the American Music Awards on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In which we bring you motoring news from around the Web:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wheelies: The E.V. Edition', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMERICAN MUSIC AWARDS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electric and Hybrid Vehicles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ghosn, Carlos', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chrysler Group LLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fiat SpA', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nissan Motor Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Southwest Research Institute', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:03:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03d42eb2-0612-5757-9f1e-f5c0d2e6841c', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03d42eb2-0612-5757-9f1e-f5c0d2e6841c'}, {'abstract': 'In Tel Aviv, my five-year-old daughter is taking refuge from bombs.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/in-israel-a-night-in-a-bomb-shelter/', 'snippet': 'In Tel Aviv, my five-year-old daughter is taking refuge from bombs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SANTA MONICA, Calif. — My five-year-old daughter Yael just called from Tel Aviv to say that she’s doing fine. She’s a bit scared, but that’s only because I’m not there to watch her — if I were home, she would not be afraid. The brave soul: it was her first time in a bomb shelter. Until yesterday, for our family rocket fire was a troubling part of life but not a personal experience: the last time that Tel Aviv was under fire was during the first Gulf War, back in 1991.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Call From the Shelter', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tel Aviv (Israel)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:04:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shmuel Rosner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shmuel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46c8cebd-7b8c-5946-aec8-53d81b279dfc', 'word_count': 533, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46c8cebd-7b8c-5946-aec8-53d81b279dfc'}, {'abstract': 'Rather than propose a way to bridge the political divide on the issue, the president said he would be listening to experts and then conducting an “education process” about long-term steps to address a warming planet.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/obama-on-climate-policy-not-just-now-thanks/', 'snippet': 'Rather than propose a way to bridge the political divide on the issue, the president said he would be listening to experts and then conducting an “education process” about long-term steps to address a warming planet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Environmental advocates have expressed frustration with the lack of discussion of climate change in the presidential race this year, a reticence that persisted even after the devastation of Hurricane Sandy. On Wednesday, in his first post-election news conference, President Obama offered his most extensive remarks on climate change in months. They did not particularly thrill environmentalists.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama on Climate Policy: Not Just Now, Thanks', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fuel Efficiency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romm, Joe', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for American Progress', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Energy Department', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:07:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9cc2b59-bb2f-5493-bbcf-ffdf2fb1c749', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9cc2b59-bb2f-5493-bbcf-ffdf2fb1c749'}, {'abstract': 'The Danny Meyer restaurant has also started taking reservations for large parties.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/union-square-cafe-updates-its-cafe/', 'snippet': 'The Danny Meyer restaurant has also started taking reservations for large parties.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the first time in its 27 years, Union Square Cafe will accept reservations for parties of more than six. The Danny Meyer restaurant has also done some renovating in the front cafe area, adding a banquette and a communal table, and eliminating tablecloths. A new family-style menu will be served in this part of the restaurant.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Union Square Cafe Updates the Cafe', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Union Square Cafe', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Union Square (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:10:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04d783ce-077f-547a-962a-98b0b5e2e50b', 'word_count': 66, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04d783ce-077f-547a-962a-98b0b5e2e50b'}, {'abstract': 'Baudelaire stands at the center of this study of 19th-century art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/la-folie-baudelaire-by-roberto-calasso.html', 'snippet': 'Baudelaire stands at the center of this study of 19th-century art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Roberto Calasso is noted for being an intellectually challenging writer. He is supremely learned, wearing his gigantic erudition ostentatiously on both sleeves. He can prestidigitate with prestigious quotations from, and references to, everything from the Vedas to Hollywood. His new book to us (in \xadItaly it is already penultimate) is “La Folie Baudelaire,” with the folly perhaps as much Calasso’s as Charles Baudelaire’s, equaled only by that of the unsuspecting reader embarking on a hoped-for smooth sail. As Calasso himself might put it, “Caveat lector!”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118SIMON/1118SIMON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 981, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118SIMON/1118SIMON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1961, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118SIMON/1118SIMON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118SIMON/1118SIMON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118SIMON/1118SIMON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paris Review', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paris Review', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McEwen, Alastair', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baudelaire, Charles Pierre', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Calasso, Roberto', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:10:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Simon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6625904b-8f74-514a-a39e-a5d1afc00a4c', 'word_count': 1306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6625904b-8f74-514a-a39e-a5d1afc00a4c'}, {'abstract': 'Marvel’s story is full of characters, on and off the page.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/marvel-comics-the-untold-story-by-sean-howe.html', 'snippet': 'Marvel’s story is full of characters, on and off the page.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Even if you didn’t grow up making a beeline for the local emporium on New Comic Book Day — also known as Wednesday in most places — odds are Marvel Comics has entered your consciousness recently. This year alone, a film about one Marvel superhero team, “The Avengers,” smashed a box-office record and made more than $207 million on its opening weekend. The glossy August issue of Marvel’s “Astonishing X-Men” showcased the same-sex wedding of the hero Northstar and his boyfriend. And soon a free-to-play online video game, Marvel Heroes, will allow players to virtually stomp the bad guys as Spider-Man, Iron Man, Wolverine, Captain America and scores of other classic characters. That’s the modern Marvel.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Biersdorfer/1118-Biersdorfer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 962, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Biersdorfer/1118-Biersdorfer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Biersdorfer/1118-Biersdorfer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Biersdorfer/1118-Biersdorfer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Biersdorfer/1118-Biersdorfer-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Spinning Their Web', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Spinning Their Web', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Marvel Comics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Howe, Sean', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:10:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. D. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/163ce853-a9a0-592f-951e-d44b3cb37f0b', 'word_count': 1154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/163ce853-a9a0-592f-951e-d44b3cb37f0b'}, {'abstract': 'Yuval Taylor and Jake Austen examine the complex history of black performers and the minstrel tradition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/darkest-america-by-yuval-taylor-and-jake-austen.html', 'snippet': 'Yuval Taylor and Jake Austen examine the complex history of black performers and the minstrel tradition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few years ago, while I was teaching a graduate seminar, the subject turned to blackface minstrelsy, one of America’s first popular art forms. We were very likely in a discussion of the long poems of T. S. Eliot (“The Waste Land” had originally been called “He Do the Police in Different Voices,” and his “Sweeney Agonistes” includes blackface) or of John Berryman (who used minstrel show structures and dialect in “The Dream Songs” as late as 1969). One student, shocked by the persistence of minstrelsy, said another of her professors had insisted that blackface died with the 19th century. Only showing her a picture of Judy Garland in blackface convinced her otherwise.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118YOUNGSUB/1118YOUNGSUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118YOUNGSUB/1118YOUNGSUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1830, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118YOUNGSUB/1118YOUNGSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118YOUNGSUB/1118YOUNGSUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118YOUNGSUB/1118YOUNGSUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Wearing the Mask', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wearing the Mask', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Yuval', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Austen, Jake', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:12:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin Young', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Young', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/45604d0d-efd8-5841-a288-a877d8475c60', 'word_count': 1837, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45604d0d-efd8-5841-a288-a877d8475c60'}, {'abstract': 'Dan Josefson’s hero navigates the social world of a therapeutic boarding school in upstate New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/thats-not-a-feeling-by-dan-josefson.html', 'snippet': 'Dan Josefson’s hero navigates the social world of a therapeutic boarding school in upstate New York.', 'lead_paragraph': '“That’s Not a Feeling,” Dan Josefson’s mordant, cockeyed first novel about a boarding school in upstate New York, includes, as many fantasy novels also do, a hand-drawn map of his invented world. Downhill from the gym, we see, is the ski-lodge-like cafetorium, which sits just behind the converted mansion used as an administrative building, itself uphill from a swath of pine trees known as “the Enchanted Forest.” Josefson could have reasonably included a glossary as well, as his Roaring Orchards School for Troubled Teens comes equipped with its own abstruse vocabulary. Students are classified, almost like Grammy Award nominees, as Alternative, New or Regular. Teachers do not restrain students; they “wiggle” them. Yummies are forbidden, and fibs must be turned in; students who break these rules can be sheeted, skirted, cornered, ghosted or roomed, or have their furniture popped. That the school is coeducational has no relation to its bane of “intimacy blockers.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118MILES/1118MILES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 846, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118MILES/1118MILES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1691, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118MILES/1118MILES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118MILES/1118MILES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118MILES/1118MILES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Outsiders', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Outsiders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Josefson, Dan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:12:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Miles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/00b2d141-9d00-5b2b-9805-3f81344d6098', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00b2d141-9d00-5b2b-9805-3f81344d6098'}, {'abstract': 'You can make your own Hostess treats at home.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/making-your-own-ding-dongs/', 'snippet': 'You can make your own Hostess treats at home.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is really over? No more road trip Twinkies picked up at gas stations, or clandestine Ding Dongs hidden under teenager’s twin beds, now that Hostess Brands is packing up its chemically stabilized creams and calling it a day? Even if another baker doesn’t take them up, it doesn’t have to be that way nation, for I have been preparing!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Making Your Own Ding Dongs', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:12:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Steinhauer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steinhauer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b44a3db4-2303-51ef-9b33-bb0ab11aa6ec', 'word_count': 178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b44a3db4-2303-51ef-9b33-bb0ab11aa6ec'}, {'abstract': 'A statue of the Virgin Mary that still stands in Breezy Point, Queens, after Hurricane Sandy and fire destroyed the house beside it has become a much-visited, much-discussed symbol of survival.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/17/us/a-madonna-that-survived-the-storm-in-breezy-point.html', 'snippet': 'A statue of the Virgin Mary that still stands in Breezy Point, Queens, after Hurricane Sandy and fire destroyed the house beside it has become a much-visited, much-discussed symbol of survival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Where the McNulty home once stood on the corner of Oceanside and Gotham, a few blocks from the Atlantic Ocean on the spit of land in Queens called Breezy Point, there now remains a charred, twisted ruin. Flooding and fire have left behind nothing but the foundation. Within it are strewed a dislodged bathtub, an air-conditioner casing battered into a helix shape, a mailbox coated with ashes.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/17/us/17religion-span/17religion-span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/17/us/17religion-span/17religion-span-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/17/us/17religion-span/17religion-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/17/us/17religion-span/17religion-span-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/17/us/17religion-span/17religion-span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/17/us/17religion-span/17religion-span-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/17/us/17religion-span/17religion-span-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Amid the Ashes, a Statue of Mary Stands as a Symbol of Survival', 'kicker': 'On Religion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Amid the Ashes, A Statue of Mary Stands as a Symbol Of Survival', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franklin, Frank II', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mary, Mother of Jesus', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Breezy Point (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:12:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samuel G. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samuel', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63102e66-cf38-57ba-8e95-b02e732396ef', 'word_count': 921, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63102e66-cf38-57ba-8e95-b02e732396ef'}, {'abstract': 'Karen Elliott House draws on 30 years of research and reporting in this examination of Saudi society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/on-saudi-arabia-by-karen-elliott-house.html', 'snippet': 'Karen Elliott House draws on 30 years of research and reporting in this examination of Saudi society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Peter Berg’s whodunit “The Kingdom,” a young F.B.I. agent boarding a plane to Riyadh asks a seasoned colleague what Saudi Arabia is like. “A bit like Mars,” replies the more experienced man. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118TOTTEN/1118TOTTEN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 721, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118TOTTEN/1118TOTTEN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1442, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118TOTTEN/1118TOTTEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118TOTTEN/1118TOTTEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118TOTTEN/1118TOTTEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Closed Kingdom', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Closed Kingdom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'House, Karen E', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Saudi Arabia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:12:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. Totten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Totten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ac6a6d8-8254-5cb3-af85-0225ef421277', 'word_count': 1216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ac6a6d8-8254-5cb3-af85-0225ef421277'}, {'abstract': 'William Rehnquist frequently opposed the Supreme Court’s decisions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/the-partisan-the-life-of-william-rehnquist-by-john-a-jenkins.html', 'snippet': 'William Rehnquist frequently opposed the Supreme Court’s decisions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Jimmy Lee Gray was sent to the Mississippi gas chamber in 1983 the procedure went horribly wrong. He gasped for breath and convulsed wildly, slamming his head against a metal pole hard enough to shake the room. The episode caused widespread revulsion, and Mississippi eventually switched from gas to lethal injection.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118COHEN/1118COHEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 292, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118COHEN/1118COHEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 292}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118COHEN/1118COHEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 498, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118COHEN/1118COHEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 996, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118COHEN/1118COHEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118COHEN/1118COHEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118COHEN/1118COHEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Justice Dissents', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Justice Dissents', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jenkins, John A', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rehnquist, William H', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T16:13:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b7e2af3-794d-54ff-83ff-79e20f9fc127', 'word_count': 1255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b7e2af3-794d-54ff-83ff-79e20f9fc127'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Blackhouse,” by Peter May, a detective in Edinburgh is dispatched to his home village to investigate a gruesome murder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/books/review/the-blackhouse-by-peter-may-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Blackhouse,” by Peter May, a detective in Edinburgh is dispatched to his home village to investigate a gruesome murder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Peter May is a writer I’d follow to the ends of the earth — which is where he takes us in THE BLACKHOUSE (Silver Oak, $24.95), the first novel in a projected trilogy featuring Detective Sergeant Finlay (Fin) Macleod of the Edinburgh police force. The setting is the windswept terrain of the Isle of Lewis, the northernmost of the Outer Hebrides, “a brooding landscape that in a moment of sunlight could be unexpectedly transformed.” Not a pretty place to be romanticized, given its foul weather, stagnant economy, rampant alcoholism and high suicide rate, but a “godforsaken bloody place” that deserves respect and even awe.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Crime/1118-Crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 649, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Crime/1118-Crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1832, 'width': 1161, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/books/review/1118-Crime/1118-Crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thu