he Belle Harbor Manor nursing home in Far Rockaway, Queens, wrote about some of the storms she has seen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Miriam Eisenstein-Drachler is an evacuee from the Belle Harbor Manor nursing home in Far Rockaway, Queens, which was badly damaged by Hurricane Sandy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Evacuee’s Letter From the Park Slope Armory', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evacuations and Evacuees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing Homes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T20:17:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c12a5550-440f-5146-b0aa-c224ccf34ce3', 'word_count': 98, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c12a5550-440f-5146-b0aa-c224ccf34ce3'}, {'abstract': 'With news of the liquidation of Hostess Brands, some fans of its snacks found themselves suddenly yearning for a Twinkie.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/a-companys-demise-leaves-an-ache-in-the-sweet-tooth/', 'snippet': 'With news of the liquidation of Hostess Brands, some fans of its snacks found themselves suddenly yearning for a Twinkie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Consumers are suddenly nostalgic for Twinkies, whether they love or hate the cream-filled confections. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Company’s Demise Leaves an Ache in the Sweet Tooth', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shutdowns (Institutional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hostess Brands', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T20:29:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e565ffd9-84e4-58aa-977a-c93495733a1c', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e565ffd9-84e4-58aa-977a-c93495733a1c'}, {'abstract': 'Social events like book readings are taking place at buildings across Manhattan as part of an effort to draw in potential buyers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/realestate/selling-books-while-selling-buildings.html', 'snippet': 'Social events like book readings are taking place at buildings across Manhattan as part of an effort to draw in potential buyers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ABOUT 60 women gathered inside a co-op apartment on Liberty Street in the financial district on a Sunday evening not long ago to hear Dana Adam Shapiro read from his newly released book, “You Can Be Right (or You Can Be Married): Looking for Love in the Age of Divorce,” a journalistic investigation into why so many marriages fail. The women were also there to buy lingerie, get a psychic reading — and inspect the $2.68 million triplex co-op for sale.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/realestate/18BOOK_SPAN/book-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/realestate/18BOOK_SPAN/book-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2