yt://video/cbe6b18a-e78f-5a49-8692-5504e9be1187'}, {'abstract': 'A year after the repeal of “don’t ask, don’t tell,” many gay students at military academies enjoy a visibility that was once unimaginable.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/fashion/military-academies-adjusting-to-repeal-of-dont-ask-dont-tell.html', 'snippet': 'A year after the repeal of “don’t ask, don’t tell,” many gay students at military academies enjoy a visibility that was once unimaginable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR their club’s big debut this semester, the cadets at the United States Air Force Academy hammered out talking points, printed fliers and hung their logo, a rainbow-colored globe, in their booth in Arnold Hall. Then they held their breath.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/fashion/18DADT_SPAN/18DADT_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/fashion/18DADT_SPAN/18DADT_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/fashion/18DADT_SPAN/18SUBDADT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/fashion/18DADT_SPAN/18SUBDADT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1433, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/fashion/18DADT_SPAN/18DADT_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/fashion/18DADT_SPAN/18DADT_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/fashion/18DADT_SPAN/18DADT_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Out of the Closet and Into a Uniform', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out of the Closet and Still in Uniform', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T20:48:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel L. Swarns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Swarns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71520c4c-2ce4-57a9-aec4-404493d4ed8b', 'word_count': 1596, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71520c4c-2ce4-57a9-aec4-404493d4ed8b'}, {'abstract': 'Hurricane Sandy knocked the facade off a Lower Manhattan wall, revealing 1970s graffiti.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/hidden-until-the-storms-whirl-and-splash/', 'snippet': 'Hurricane Sandy knocked the facade off a Lower Manhattan wall, revealing 1970s graffiti.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the less calamitous pieces of damage done by Hurricane Sandy was this: It knocked the facade off a wall on South Street just north of Broad Street near the East River, revealing a time capsule of hand-carved 1970s graffiti that included ancient phone numbers and declarations of love. Daniel Barry, a freelance photographer for The Times, came across it on Oct. 31, after the storm water receded.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/nyregion/16wall2-cityroom/16wall2-cityroom-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/nyregion/16wall2-cityroom/16wall2-cityroom-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/nyregion/16wall2-cityroom/16wall2-cityroom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/16/nyregion/16wall2-cityroom/16wall2-cityroom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/nyregion/16wall2-cityroom/16wall2-cityroom-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hidden, Until the Storm’s Whirl and Splash', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T20:50:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df2efdbe-e951-5d86-b51d-b711dae4078e', 'word_count': 70, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df2efdbe-e951-5d86-b51d-b711dae4078e'}, {'abstract': 'The move means the network has now cut ties with half of its new entries, including the drama “Made in Jersey.” Its other new shows, “Vegas” and “Elementary,” have won full orders for the season.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/cbs-drops-a-2nd-new-show-partners/', 'snippet': 'The move means the network has now cut ties with half of its new entries, including the drama “Made in Jersey.” Its other new shows, “Vegas” and “Elementary,” have won full orders for the season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The signs of a disappointing fall season for new network series continued on Friday when CBS dropped the second of the four series introduced in the fall, the comedy “Partners,” which has been a weak spot on the network’s traditionally strong Monday comedy lineup.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'CBS Drops a 2nd New Show, ‘Partners’', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'CBS Cancels ‘Partners’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CBS Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T20:52:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/507e7e07-bd72-512a-89e7-b961c3952773', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/507e7e07-bd72-512a-89e7-b961c3952773'}, {'abstract': 'In an era of one-person laptop bands, disco is seeing a revival in New York, with more acts turning to big ensembles and live shows.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/arts/music/disco-revival-in-new-york-with-crystal-ark-and-escort.html', 'snippet': 'In an era of one-person laptop bands, disco is seeing a revival in New York, with more acts turning to big ensembles and live shows.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ON a Saturday afternoon last month a crowd packed into a studio on an industrial block in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, to see the electronic dance music ensemble the Crystal Ark Party Machine. As a D.J. played acid-house tracks, two female singers wearing purple-and-white tie-dyed robes and starfish tiaras shook gourds and spun around like dervishes, uttering Spanish incantations. A man with a shaved head and a woman with Cleopatra bangs, both wearing emerald-green, sequin-trimmed oversize jumpsuits, danced beside them. As they vogued to the tribal throb, it became apparent that the backs of their outfits were actually see-through mesh, the only part of the spectacle that left little to the imagination.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/arts/18DISCO1_SPAN/18DISCO1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/18/arts/18DISCO1_SPAN/18DISCO1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/arts/18DISCO1_SPAN/18DISCO1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/arts/18DISCO1_SPAN/18DISCO1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/arts/18DISCO1_SPAN/18DISCO1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/18/arts/18DISCO1_SPAN/18DISCO1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/18/arts/18DISCO1_SPAN/18DISCO1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Disco’s New Big Band Era', 'kicker': 'Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Disco’s New Big Band Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Midnight Magic (Music Group)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Crystal Ark Party Machine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Escort (Music Group)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T20:56:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Rubin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rubin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea3ec9ff-5313-5bfd-9933-6d064a9a978d', 'word_count': 1510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea3ec9ff-5313-5bfd-9933-6d064a9a978d'}, {'abstract': 'Generals given to preening aren’t so good at winning wars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/opinion/sunday/a-phony-hero-for-a-phony-war.html', 'snippet': 'Generals given to preening aren’t so good at winning wars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FASTIDIOUSNESS is never a good sign in a general officer. Though strutting military peacocks go back to Alexander’s time, our first was MacArthur, who seemed at times to care more about how much gold braid decorated the brim of his cap than he did about how many bodies he left on beachheads across the Pacific. Next came Westmoreland, with his starched fatigues in Vietnam. In our time, Gen. David H. Petraeus has set the bar high. Never has so much beribboned finery decorated a general’s uniform since Al Haig passed through the sally ports of West Point on his way to the White House.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/17/opinion/17generals-img/17generals-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 513, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/17/opinion/17generals-img/17generals-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 513}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/17/opinion/17generals-img/17generals-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 846, 'width': 988, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/17/opinion/17generals-img/17generals-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 846, 'width': 988, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/17/opinion/17generals-img/17generals-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/17/opinion/17generals-img/17generals-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/17/opinion/17generals-img/17generals-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Phony Hero for a Phony War', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Phony Hero for a Phony War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Petraeus, David H', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T21:03:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lucian K. Truscott Iv', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lucian', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Truscott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc5a6e5e-3aec-5262-8a96-727ca918f4c9', 'word_count': 949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc5a6e5e-3aec-5262-8a96-727ca918f4c9'}, {'abstract': 'It turns out there’s a lot to discover when you look back.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/16/t-winter-travel-2012-editors-letter/', 'snippet': 'It turns out there’s a lot to discover when you look back.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Editors have wandering eyes; it’s practically a job requirement. That’s especially true as we put together the travel editions of T — the guiding sentiment is that someplace new, some unturned corner, always awaits. We seldom look back. Our cover girl, Karlie Kloss, dangling her feet into the mouth of Santiago Crater in Nicaragua as if it were a country lake captures that spirit in a single jaw-dropping shot (apologies to her mom and her agent). She squeezed that trip with the photographer Ryan McGinley in between fashion shows in New York and Paris — amazing cities that must nonetheless seem old hat to her by now — because, well, Nicaragua is precisely not New York or Paris. (Though, as Julia Felsenthal discovered, it’s only a matter of time before the country winds up a hot spot.)', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'T Winter Travel 2012 | Editor’s Letter', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editor’s Letter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-16T21:08:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffries Blackerby', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffries', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Blackerby', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f3503cb-177e-5a49-9bef-3417cfdb9058', 'word_count': 373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f3503cb-177e-5a49-9bef-3417cfdb9058'}, {'abstract': 'A performance of a song that was originally written in the 1950s to urge America to return Okinawa to Japanese control, now a request to give Okinawa back to Okinawans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/t-magazine/100000001903516/shima-uta-okinawa-ni-kaese.html', 'snippet': 'A performance of a song that was originally written in the 1950s to urge America to return Okinawa to Japanese control, now a request to give Okinawa back to Okinawans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A performance of a song that was originally written in the 1950s to urge America to return Okinawa to Japanese control, now a request to give Okinawa back to Okinawans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/14/t-magazine/video-18tmag