ions', 'value': 'Cambodia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-18T10:36:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Baker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d84613e-0fa7-526b-9087-d6cf2801d9e4', 'word_count': 1088, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d84613e-0fa7-526b-9087-d6cf2801d9e4'}, {'abstract': 'We have scandal at the highest levels of the CIA, in part because technology has made spies of all of us, and all of us its victims.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/my-fathers-era-of-spying-was-a-much-simpler-and-safer-one/', 'snippet': 'We have scandal at the highest levels of the CIA, in part because technology has made spies of all of us, and all of us its victims.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was a kid in the late 1970s my father used to take me on walks with our dog. We were living in Belgrade at the time, where he was a diplomat based at the U.S Embassy. On these outings, during which we meandered into the verdant parks around our house, my father would often stop to admire the trees. Sometimes he’d ruffle around at the base of one of them looking for chestnuts. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Father’s Era of Spying Was a Much Simpler — and Safer — One', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Broadwell, Paula', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Petraeus, David H', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-18T12:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e6f7124-7150-527b-9602-c8d724a79821', 'word_count': 985, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e6f7124-7150-527b-9602-c8d724a79821'}, {'abstract': 'Chen Deming, who has led China’s policy on trade negotiations as commerce minister, failed to win a seat on the Central Committee and will be replaced.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/business/global/both-us-and-china-will-appoint-new-trade-negotiators.html', 'snippet': 'Chen Deming, who has led China’s policy on trade negotiations as commerce minister, failed to win a seat on the Central Committee and will be replaced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — The rare congruity of the political calendars in the United States and China, with both countries choosing leaders this month, is about to produce an equally rare replacement of most of each side’s senior trade negotiators, with unpredictable results for bilateral economic relations.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Change in Negotiators Shakes Up U.S.-China Trade Policy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Change in Negotiators Shakes Up U.S.-China Trade Policy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chen Deming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-18T12:16:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Keith Bradsher', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Keith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradsher', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/caf999ea-ba74-5e0e-9252-f9cc175ee2e3', 'word_count': 844, 'uri': 'nyt://article/caf999ea-ba74-5e0e-9252-f9cc175ee2e3'}, {'abstract': 'Our holiday cards are usually a slapdash affair, saying little about our family other than that I’ve somehow managed to purchase stamps. But this year, for those that don’t know us well, our Christmas photo reveals a bit more.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/coming-out-by-christmas-card/', 'snippet': 'Our holiday cards are usually a slapdash affair, saying little about our family other than that I’ve somehow managed to purchase stamps. But this year, for those that don’t know us well, our Christmas photo reveals a bit more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Holiday cards that really catch my eye, either for their brilliance or buffoonery, tend to be trying too hard. My sin, on the other hand, is not trying hard enough. I either don’t send out a card at all, producing a low-grade guilt that dogs me into the New Year, or slap together a family picture on Thanksgiving weekend, my kids’ grins matching the forced lineup. What does our card convey? Merely that I’ve gotten it done.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coming Out by Christmas Card', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christmas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lambda Legal Defense and Education Fund', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-18T13:30:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maureen Salamon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maureen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Salamon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8f2a07a-b683-57dc-a811-9ebe0fe5e452', 'word_count': 684, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8f2a07a-b683-57dc-a811-9ebe0fe5e452'}, {'abstract': 'Being in the Billion-Dollar Club limits how, and if, a start-up can get out. For one thing, when you’re the most expensive product on the shelf, very few companies can afford to buy you.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/disruptions-a-1-billion-start-up-might-not-be-so-fun/', 'snippet': 'Being in the Billion-Dollar Club limits how, and if, a start-up can get out. For one thing, when you’re the most expensive product on the shelf, very few companies can afford to buy you.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Congratulations! Your start-up is now valued at over $1 billion!', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/technology/19disrupt-pic/19disrupt-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/technology/19disrupt-pic/19disrupt-pic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1298, 'width': 204