light Saga: Breaking Dawn — Part 2” took in an estimated $141.3 million at North American theaters over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The final “Twilight” movie arrived in North America in time for the weekend — the gooey end of an era for some, not a moment too soon for others — and took in an estimated $141.3 million. Made for about $120 million by Lionsgate’s Summit Entertainment, “The Twilight Saga: Breaking Dawn — Part 2” was easily the No. 1 movie in the land (including overseas, where it took in an additional $199.6 million). Still, it did not smash opening-weekend records, as some analysts had hoped; adjusting for inflation, “The Twilight Saga: New Moon” sold $154 million in tickets in November 2009. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Final ‘Twilight’ Movie Tops Weekend Box Office', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Final ‘Twilight’ Tops Box Office Here and Abroad', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spielberg, Steven', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-18T17:52:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d41d4ca9-0215-5231-ac74-248eed21e30e', 'word_count': 242, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d41d4ca9-0215-5231-ac74-248eed21e30e'}, {'abstract': 'European governments are going after American companies, like Google and Amazon.com, that pay little or no taxes in Europe, despite generating billions of dollars in revenue on the continent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/technology/19iht-tax19.html', 'snippet': 'European governments are going after American companies, like Google and Amazon.com, that pay little or no taxes in Europe, despite generating billions of dollars in revenue on the continent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Google reported sales of more than $4 billion in Britain last year. It paid less than $10 million in taxes.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/business/sub-19Tax/sub-19Tax-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/19/business/sub-19Tax/sub-19Tax-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/business/sub-19Tax/sub-19Tax-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 561, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/business/sub-19Tax/sub-19Tax-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1121, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'European Countries Seek More Taxes From U.S. Multinational Companies', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'European Countries Seek More Taxes From U.S. Multinational Companies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Starbucks Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Europe', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-18T17:58:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Pfanner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pfanner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a5edc42-a318-528a-b57c-5ab590eb0d1e', 'word_count': 1478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a5edc42-a318-528a-b57c-5ab590eb0d1e'}, {'abstract': 'Dianne Feinstein, chairwoman of the Senate Intelligence Committee, said she planned to investigate why the C.I.A.’s quick determination of a terrorist role in the Sept. 11 attack in Benghazi, Libya, was not reflected in the “talking points” used days later on television by Susan E. Rice, the ambassador to the United Nations. But she defended the administration’s handling of the aftermath of the attack.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/18/feinstein-says-senate-will-investigate-benghazi-talking-points/', 'snippet': 'Dianne Feinstein, chairwoman of the Senate Intelligence Committee, said she planned to investigate why the C.I.A.’s quick determination of a terrorist role in the Sept. 11 attack in Benghazi, Libya, was not reflected in the “talking points” used d...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The chairwoman of the Senate Intelligence Committee said Sunday that she planned to investigate why the C.I.A.’s quick determination of a terrorist role in the Sept. 11 attack in Benghazi, Libya, was not reflected in the “talking points” used days later on television by Susan E. Rice, the ambassador to the United Nations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Feinstein Says Senate Will Investigate Benghazi ‘Talking Points’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-18T18:30:39+0000', 'docume