tion': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9f7ec9a-d717-5674-902a-2583b9ae56e5', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9f7ec9a-d717-5674-902a-2583b9ae56e5'}, {'abstract': 'The president and chief executive of Intel, Paul S. Otellini, is retiring in May after 40 years with the company. The company said it would prepare to transition to a new leader over the next six months.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/intel-chief-executive-to-retire-in-may/', 'snippet': 'The president and chief executive of Intel, Paul S. Otellini, is retiring in May after 40 years with the company. The company said it would prepare to transition to a new leader over the next six months.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — After almost 40 years at Intel, its chief executive, Paul S. Otellini, is retiring, just as the company struggles with a momentous challenge: computing’s big shift to mobile devices.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/technology/19bits-otellini/19bits-otellini-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/technology/19bits-otellini/19bits-otellini-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1475, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/technology/19bits-otellini/19bits-otellini-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/19/technology/19bits-otellini/19bits-otellini-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/technology/19bits-otellini/19bits-otellini-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Intel Chief Executive to Retire in May', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Surprise, Intel Chief Will Retire', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Otellini, Paul S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intel Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T14:57:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Quentin Hardy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Quentin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hardy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d354af7-6daa-5bb2-bab8-77b54aff7f6b', 'word_count': 870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d354af7-6daa-5bb2-bab8-77b54aff7f6b'}, {'abstract': 'Also, Rupert Murdoch apologizes for a Twitter message about the conflict in Gaza that critics labeled anti-Semitic.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/the-breakfast-meeting-washington-post-woes-and-no-more-words-from-roth/', 'snippet': 'Also, Rupert Murdoch apologizes for a Twitter message about the conflict in Gaza that critics labeled anti-Semitic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The clumsy handoff between editors at The Washington Post — with the announcement last week that Martin Baron of The Boston Globe would be taking over from Marcus Brauchli after months of speculation that Mr. Brauchli’s days were numbered — has also put the spotlight on Katharine Weymouth, the Graham family scion who is the newspaper’s publisher, David Carr writes. The newspaper is struggling to find its economic footing — circulation and advertising are down, and the profit cushion from endeavors like the Kaplan education division is disappearing. By dismissing her handpicked editor, Mr. Carr writes, Ms. Weymouth “seems to be upending the loyalty and accountability that has been a hallmark of her family’s ownership of the newspaper.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Washington Post Woes, and No More Words From Roth', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baron, Martin D', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roth, Philip', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weymouth, Katharine Bouchage', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Machinima Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Post', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T14:59:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3950bb5-08cc-5fae-9aa7-8b7d6dff40ac', 'word_count': 526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3950bb5-08cc-5fae-9aa7-8b7d6dff40ac'}, {'abstract': 'A documentary about a man-made eco-disaster offers a morality tale about the human relationship to the land.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/on-our-radar-the-dust-bowl/', 'snippet': 'A documentary about a man-made eco-disaster offers a morality tale about the human relationship to the land.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Dust Bowl,” a new documentary from Ken Burns, revisits the “great plow-up” for wheat crops and the crippling drought that followed in the Thirties. [PBS]A helicopter begins dropping poison bait in the Galápagos Islands as part of a broad effort to exterminate 180 million nonnative rats that are threatening unique bird and reptile species there. [Associated Press]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: ‘The Dust Bowl’', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T15:04:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c0d4596-b80b-51ae-be9a-df0db35d5f10', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c0d4596-b80b-51ae-be9a-df0db35d5f10'}, {'abstract': 'These are dark days for people who want to keep the white in the red, white and blue.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/racial-politics/', 'snippet': 'These are dark days for people who want to keep the white in the red, white and blue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What with President Obama’s re-election, the Puerto Rican plebiscite favoring \xa0statehood, and the calls by some Republican lawmakers, opinion leaders, and op-ed and radio and TV blowhards urging the party to surrender, already, on immigration reform, these are dark days for people who want to keep the white in the red, white and blue.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Racial Politics', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jindal, Bobby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'King, Steven A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Reilly, Bill", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T15:10:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lawrence Downes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lawrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Downes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9c56a91-5eb0-59ea-b7bf-c25436aef7ad', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9c56a91-5eb0-59ea-b7bf-c25436aef7ad'}, {'abstract': 'Yipes! Stripes! The Steelers’ throwback uniforms were just one reason Sunday’s game against the Ravens was tough to watch.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/leading-off-eyesores-of-all-kinds/', 'snippet': 'Yipes! Stripes! The Steelers’ throwback uniforms were just one reason Sunday’s game against the Ravens was tough to watch.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Football fans are a contentious lot, so it’s hard to get everyone to agree on much of anything (the N.F.L.’s ludicrous referee lockout being perhaps the most notable exception). But it’s fairly safe to say after several retina-searing experiences, that whatever you think of the Steelers and their bad-boy-turned-Mr.-Wonderful-now-injured quarterback and whatever side of the Steelers-Ravens rivalry you fall on, the Steelers’ throwback uniforms need to be thrown back. Immediately if not yesterday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/sports/football/19uniforms/19uniforms-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/sports/football/19uniforms/19uniforms-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/sports/football/19uniforms/19uniforms-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/19/sports/football/19uniforms/19uniforms-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/sports/football/19uniforms/19uniforms-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Leading Off: Eyesores of All Kinds', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dooley, Derek', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freeman, Josh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gronkowski, Rob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keselowski, Brad', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Luck, Andrew', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maroney, McKayla', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Matt', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tomlin, Mike', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Atlanta Falcons', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Baltimore Ravens', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indianapolis Colts', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Steelers', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T15:22:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a927c5f-3838-5991-b5e8-bb7c0e16b282', 'word_count': 693, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a927c5f-3838-5991-b5e8-bb7c0e16b282'}, {'abstract': 'After a huge Thanksgiving meal, a dessert without too much butter or sugar fits the bill. Today: a decadent-tasting pumpkin caramel mousse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/health/to-end-the-feast-something-sweet-but-light-recipes-for-health.html', 'snippet': 'After a huge Thanksgiving meal, a dessert without too much butter or sugar fits the bill. Today: a decadent-tasting pumpkin caramel mousse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s always the desserts that tip the balance at Thanksgiving. We’re pacing ourselves, full but not too full, until we get to those irresistible pies. I love them as much as anybody, but I thought I’d work on some pielike desserts this week that have the fillings we love without as much butter in the crust. Galettes, phyllo-wrapped strudel and clafoutis all fit the bill. I filled layers of phyllo with a mixture of caramelized chopped apples, pears, mixed dried fruit and slivered almonds and rolled it up into two beautiful strudels, one of which I froze and will pull out and bake on Thanksgiving. Galettes are more rustic than pies, but they’ll look beautiful on any Thanksgiving buffet, and judging from the way they went over in my house when I was testing the recipes, the apple and the pear and cranberry galettes will please a crowd. A clafoutis is one of the easiest impressive desserts that you can make; it looks a bit like a pie, but there’s no crust. Pears are the fruit to use at this time of year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/19recipehealth/19recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/20/science/19recipehealth/19recipehealth-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/19recipehealth/19recipehealth-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 668, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/19recipehealth/19recipehealth-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1336, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/19recipehealth/19recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/20/science/19recipehealth/19recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/19recipehealth/19recipehealth-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'To End the Feast, Something Sweet but Light', 'kicker': 'Recipes for Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desserts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T15:29:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e129f04-a880-58e7-9932-4ab7fc6ade9b', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e129f04-a880-58e7-9932-4ab7fc6ade9b'}, {'abstract': 'Ms. Gregory’s decision to step down came as a surprise after a reconceived version of the festival, which focused on small-scale experimental work.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/director-of-britains-dance-umbrella-to-step-down/', 'snippet': 'Ms. Gregory’s decision to step down came as a surprise after a reconceived version of the festival, which focused on small-scale experimental work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Betsy Gregory, director of the British Dance Umbrella, announced last week that she would leave after the 2013 edition of the festival. Dance Umbrella, the most important dance festival in Britain, began in 1978 and played a vital role in the 1980s and ’90s in popularizing the work of artists like Merce Cunningham, Trisha Brown, Stephen Petronio and Mark Morris, and bringing formerly little-known European artists to Britain. This year, however, the festival’s budget was drastically slashed, by 37 percent\xa0 (from $869,000 in 2011 to around $556,000 for the next three years) in a round of nationwide cuts made by the British Arts Council, the central source of government funding for all the arts. Ms. Gregory’s decision to step down nonetheless came as a surprise after a reconceived version of the festival, which focused on small-scale experimental work, was generally deemed a success, drawing packed houses and critical plaudits.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Director of Britain’s Dance Umbrella to Step Down', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Trisha', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Petronio, Stephen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dance Umbrella', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T15:31:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Sulcas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sulcas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48b70a69-66f1-55f4-befe-2dc101d82178', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48b70a69-66f1-55f4-befe-2dc101d82178'}, {'abstract': 'A radio commercial for The Contributor, a newspaper in Nashville, Tenn., that benefits the homeless.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2012/11/19/business/media/19adnewsletter-audio.html', 'snippet': 'A radio commercial for The Contributor, a newspaper in Nashville, Tenn., that benefits the homeless.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A radio commercial for The Contributor, a newspaper in Nashville, Tenn., that benefits the homeless.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Radio Ad for The Contributor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo