tista', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1c7ea26-fee8-58be-841e-3f9f0a17c9aa', 'word_count': 1418, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1c7ea26-fee8-58be-841e-3f9f0a17c9aa'}, {'abstract': 'In an essay, a doctor who served in the Army in Vietnam suggests that the American medical system begin asking adult patients whether they are veterans, because their military experience could have had profound effects on their health many years later.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/medical-history-should-include-military-history-doctor-says/', 'snippet': 'In an essay, a doctor who served in the Army in Vietnam suggests that the American medical system begin asking adult patients whether they are veterans, because their military experience could have had profound effects on their health many years l...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seven weeks after his induction into the Army in 1966, Dr. Jeffrey L. Brown was sent to Vietnam, where he spent a year treating front-line soldiers, sometimes under fire. He knew next to nothing about weapons when he went, but returned a battlefield doctor. Back home, he got married, started a family and opened a pediatric practice. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Medical History Should Include Military History, Doctor Says', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AGENT ORANGE (DEFOLIANT)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Journal of the American Medical Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Medical College', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veter