8c7cdfcc6e'}, {'abstract': '“Car Talk” is expanding to weekdays starting Friday on Sirius XM’s Public Radio channel.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/though-dead-car-talk-expands/', 'snippet': '“Car Talk” is expanding to weekdays starting Friday on Sirius XM’s Public Radio channel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Their retirement last month notwithstanding, the on-air presence of the “Car Talk” guys, Tom and Ray Magliozzi, known as Click and Clack, is expanding to weekdays starting Friday, on Sirius XM’s Public Radio channel.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Though Dead, ‘Car Talk’ Expands', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Magliozzi, Ray', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Public Radio', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'XM Satellite Radio Holdings Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T18:59:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Garrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cd2a082-731d-54d8-b221-a73cec5bedab', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cd2a082-731d-54d8-b221-a73cec5bedab'}, {'abstract': 'The men’s flagship store on Savile Row in London was designed in the spirit of McQueen’s major muses like the arts, nature and military pageantry.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/new-on-savile-row-the-alexander-mcqueen-mens-store/', 'snippet': 'The men’s flagship store on Savile Row in London was designed in the spirit of McQueen’s major muses like the arts, nature and military pageantry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not much about the new Alexander McQueen men’s flagship (the world’s first) on Savile Row in London suggests the dark, dour designs that the late artist was often earmarked for. Not even the colors. The designer’s posthumous homecoming to the street where, at age 16, he worked as an apprentice at the bespoke tailors Anderson and Sheppard and then at the neighboring Gieves and Hawkes, seems a fitting return to the company’s British tailoring roots. Working hard to elevate McQueen men’s wear to a higher level of detail and tailoring, the creative director, Sarah Burton, worked closely with David Collins Studio to outfit the store’s interiors in the spirit of McQueen’s major muses like the arts, nature and military pageantry. Inside, the shop’s walls recall the faded grandeur of stately English homes, clad as they are with white plaster panels and acanthus carvings that, up close, are compounded barnacles, feathers and foxgloves. This play on nature continues throughout the store; display cases and tables stand on clawed feet, and tall mirrors are propped up by what appear to be squirrels. A narrow strip of red carpet meant to invoke a military procession traces a line down the center of the store, atop which stands a colonnade of brass clothes railings carved to look like a vertebrate. Perhaps the biggest extravagance is the back-of-house glass inspiration cube curated by Sadie Coles. The first exhibited work is a Sarah Lucas sculpture, “Icon,” from her “Bunny” series, that portrays the British dancer Michael Clark. Of course the point and legal stipulation of keeping a store on Savile Row is that there will be in-house tailors busy at work and visible from the street in their basement workshop. An important detail, among many, that the new McQueen store doesn’t overlook.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New On Savile Row | The Alexander McQueen Men’s Store', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McQueen, Alexander', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T19:01:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rocky Casale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rocky', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Casale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f07df30b-8e78-541d-89d0-d828b02d579e', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f07df30b-8e78-541d-89d0-d828b02d579e'}, {'abstract': 'The Buntin Group produces a pro bono campaign for a nonprofit group in Nashville that publishes a so-called street newspaper, The Contributor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/business/media/pro-bono-ads-for-a-nashville-newspaper-that-aids-homeless.html', 'snippet': 'The Buntin Group produces a pro bono campaign for a nonprofit group in Nashville that publishes a so-called street newspaper, The Contributor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the time of year when many advertising agencies offer help to the homeless through efforts like creating pro bono campaigns and matching donations that employees make to charities. For an agency in Nashville, helping the homeless has been a year-round mission.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/business/media/19adnewsletter1/19adnewsletter1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 448, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/business/media/19adnewsletter1/19adnewsletter1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 896, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/business/media/19adnewsletter1/19adnewsletter1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/19/business/media/19adnewsletter1/19adnewsletter1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/business/media/19adnewsletter1/19adnewsletter1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ads Seek Support for 21st Century ‘Newsies’', 'kicker': 'Campaign Spotlight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ads Seek Support for 21st Century &#8216;Newsies&#8217;', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nonprofit Organizations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Buntin Group', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nashville (Tenn)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T19:02:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4c6918d-06ab-5acc-9033-122db4b7e15f', 'word_count': 1273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4c6918d-06ab-5acc-9033-122db4b7e15f'}, {'abstract': 'For fans of Charles Mingus, the Jackson 5, Heart and Amy Winehouse, among others, new gems and old.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/23/arts/music/boxed-sets-of-cds-and-dvds-for-2012-critics-picks.html', 'snippet': 'For fans of Charles Mingus, the Jackson 5, Heart and Amy Winehouse, among others, new gems and old.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AMY WINEHOUSE: ‘AT THE BBC’ This is a smart response to an awful reality. When Amy Winehouse died last year, it was unclear how much unreleased music remained. But there was still room to fill out her legacy, as this CD and DVD collection, which gathers recordings she made for various BBC productions over the course of her career, attests to. Musically, the performances are taut, all the better to steady Winehouse as she sang, which was necessary because even as far back as 2004, she could be as wobbly as she was ferocious. Live, she could play it straight, or indifferent, or aggressive, her voice an otherworldly mumble. When she roared, like on a 2006 “Tears Dry on Their Own” captured on the DVD here, it gleamed, and she brought real sass to standards like “Lullaby of Birdland.” The 2007 take on “You Know I’m No Good” is eerie, cutting and woozy. And some recordings open old wounds — is the barely-there “Love Is a Losing Game” from 2009 emaciated by aesthetic strategy or failing health? The DVD, an hourlong documentary about a 2006 performance backed only by guitar and bass in a tiny Irish church for about 100 people, is indulgent apart from the exquisite performance clips and snippets of an interview in which Winehouse recalls being flabbergasted by Thelonious Monk and Ray Charles in her youth. Performances like the ones in this set are too often considered detritus, less essential than an artist’s formal releases. But this collection reflects the breadth and power and, yes, wisdom of the BBC, the state-owned broadcaster that performs an outsize role in British cultural life, and with releases like this, may also be the nation’s official eulogizer as well. (Republic, one CD and one DVD, $19.99) JON CARAMANICA', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/arts/giftguide-music/giftguide-music-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/arts/giftguide-music/giftguide-music-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/arts/giftguide-music/giftguide-music-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/19/arts/giftguide-music/giftguide-music-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/arts/giftguide-music/giftguide-music-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Easy to Wrap, Ready to Rock ’n’ Roll', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Do You Have Michael Jackson in a Box?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T19:15:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/5587bf55-19c3-548c-af05-08137f8651b0', 'word_count': 4686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5587bf55-19c3-548c-af05-08137f8651b0'}, {'abstract': 'The High Water Women Foundation, which was founded in 2005 by senior women in the hedge fund industry, holds financial literacy classes, focusing on lower-income communities.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/lessons-in-credit-on-a-micro-level/', 'snippet': 'The High Water Women Foundation, which was founded in 2005 by senior women in the hedge fund industry, holds financial literacy classes, focusing on lower-income communities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stephanie Tupper spends her days in Citigroup‘s corporate treasury, monitoring the liquidity of the giant bank. But Thursday evening, her task was decidedly more micro.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lessons in Credit, on a Micro Level', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Siebert, Muriel F', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'High Water Women Foundation', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T19:24:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50ae5fbd-fc74-5f2d-aaf8-c16d01081a67', 'word_count': 419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50ae5fbd-fc74-5f2d-aaf8-c16d01081a67'}, {'abstract': 'Ramon Dominguez, with an unorthodox style on New York racetracks, will lead riders in the United States in earnings for a third year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/sports/an-unorthodox-jockey-and-predictable-winner.html', 'snippet': 'Ramon Dominguez, with an unorthodox style on New York racetracks, will lead riders in the United States in earnings for a third year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is easy to pick out Ramon Dominguez in a crowded field. To the untrained eye, most jockeys look the same in the saddle, but not Dominguez. Taller than your average rider, at 5 feet 6 inches, he sits high in the saddle, stretches his hands onto the reins in an unorthodox manner and flashes his whip in an almost exaggerated half-windmill arc. There is an easier way to spot him, too: look for who is in front at the finish.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/sports/19jockey2_cnd/19jockey2_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/19/sports/19jockey2_cnd/19jockey2_cnd-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/sports/19jockey2_cnd/19jockey2_cnd-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/sports/19jockey2_cnd/19jockey2_cnd-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/sports/19jockey2_cnd/19jockey2_cnd-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'lega