1fa2-e9db-5480-a5d0-aefac8460422', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/d2831fa2-e9db-5480-a5d0-aefac8460422'}, {'abstract': 'The snack food, with its unique properties, managed even to find a place in the world of science — science in the classroom, at least.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/honoring-a-spongy-subject-of-classroom-experiments/', 'snippet': 'The snack food, with its unique properties, managed even to find a place in the world of science — science in the classroom, at least.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The possible death of the Twinkie, announced on Friday, would be a loss not just to the world of junk food, but also to the world of science — science in the classroom, at least.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/20TWIN_SPAN/20TWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 324, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/20/science/20TWIN_SPAN/20TWIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 324}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/20TWIN_SPAN/20TWIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 543, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/20TWIN_SPAN/20TWIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 928, 'width': 1749, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/20TWIN_SPAN/20TWIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/20/science/20TWIN_SPAN/20TWIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/science/20TWIN_SPAN/20TWIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Honoring a Spongy Subject of Classroom Experiments', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Honoring a Spongy Subject Of Classroom Experiments', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hostess Brands', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T20:55:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Denise Grady', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Denise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grady', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d31d487-9271-5523-b391-f1e1579e435d', 'word_count': 421, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d31d487-9271-5523-b391-f1e1579e435d'}, {'abstract': '“Magic Fingers,” a new experimental art space, hosted an intimate inauguration last Saturday night with “Questionable Beliefs; Part 3,” an exhibition by the artist Danny McDonald.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/miracle-on-10th-street-magic-fingers-opens-at-last/', 'snippet': '“Magic Fingers,” a new experimental art space, hosted an intimate inauguration last Saturday night with “Questionable Beliefs; Part 3,” an exhibition by the artist Danny McDonald.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It made perfect sense that “Magic Fingers” — a new experimental art space in the East Village — would make its debut on Halloween given the theatrical cover this holiday provides, but the opening had been postponed twice because of Hurricane Sandy. Instead, the pop-up gallery hosted an intimate inauguration last Saturday night with “Questionable Beliefs; Part 3,” an exhibition by the artist Danny McDonald. The evening began with various set pieces trickling out from the diminutive storefront: first a fortune teller, then Turkish delights dispensed from an emergency rations box, black light manicures administered curbside, a purple question mark funerary wreath was brought forth. McDonald is a veteran on the downtown scene having worked under Colin de Land at his American Fine Arts Gallery in SoHo during its ’90s heyday. The artist intended his presentation at Magic Fingers to read as a “tableau vivant in the form of a theatricized political cartoon.” As darkness fell, the curator Alison Gingeras emerged sweeping a witch’s broom, presumably to clear the area for McDonald’s alter ego Mindy Vale, who masquerades as a Gypsy horror drag queen. Minutes later with a dead megaphone in hand, Vale popped out of the front window lamenting the loss of electricity and the kinds of hardships many New Yorkers have suffered of late and then, sitting on step stool, proceeded to cut up $100 bill doormats and toss sections into the crowd as a symbolic Robin Hood gesture. The event’s crescendo was Vale’s karaoke performance of the Oliver Twist song “Who Will Buy?” before she ran up the block with three pink balloons headed for the after-party at The Pyramid Club. As Gingeras obliquely summed up the performance, “Mindy is the perfect ambassador to preside over this return to the East Village.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Miracle on 10th Street | ‘Magic Fingers’ Opens at Last', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East Village (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T21:00:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Powers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Powers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5964694b-384b-5154-a817-1acaad2c7420', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5964694b-384b-5154-a817-1acaad2c7420'}, {'abstract': 'A high school senior has “little pity for a school with a $100 million endowment that wants to burrow its way into the forefront of my consciousness by bragging about its Frisbee golf team.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/education/100000001912602/the-envelope-please-candice-childress.html', 'snippet': 'A high school senior has “little pity for a school with a $100 million endowment that wants to burrow its way into the forefront of my consciousness by bragging about its Frisbee golf team.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A high school senior has “little pity for a school with a $100 million endowment that wants to burrow its way into the forefront of my consciousness by bragging about its Frisbee golf team.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/education/video-envpls-candice-3/video-envpls-candice-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/19/education/video-envpls-candice-3/video-envpls-candice-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/19/education/video-envpls-candice-3/video-envpls-candice-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/19/education/video-envpls-candice-3/video-envpls-candice-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Envelope, Please: Candice Childress', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T21:07:52+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Education', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/ca37c606-9ae2-5dd2-ab76-95de594d216c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/ca37c606-9ae2-5dd2-ab76-95de594d216c'}, {'abstract': 'In an interview with GQ, he said the age of the earth was “one of the great mysteries.”', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/marco-rubio-is-not-a-scientist/', 'snippet': 'In an interview with GQ, he said the age of the earth was “one of the great mysteries.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Marco Rubio is a Republican senator from Florida. He is sometimes called the “crown prince” of the Tea Party movement. He will likely run for president in 2016. He sits on the Commerce, Science and Transportation Committee. He is not a scientist.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Marco Rubio Is ‘Not a Scientist’', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rubio, Marco', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T21:08:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83b9900b-3d93-5d9b-be86-b3e58111f42b', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83b9900b-3d93-5d9b-be86-b3e58111f42b'}, {'abstract': 'To accompany Carina Chocano’s Riff this past Sunday on women and age, we bring you some examples of Hollywood’s portrayal of unrealistic couples.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/great-moments-in-age-inappropriate-casting/', 'snippet': 'To accompany Carina Chocano’s Riff this past Sunday on women and age, we bring you some examples of Hollywood’s portrayal of unrealistic couples.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this past Sunday’s magazine, Carina Chocano wrote a Riff column about why women waste their youth worrying about their age. If TV is any indication, we have reason to worry — we all but disappear once we reach age 40. Seventy-one percent of women on TV are in their teens, 20s and 30s, according to Martha M. Lauzen, the executive director of the Center for the Study of Women in Television and Film at San Diego State University, who appears in Jennifer Siebel Newsom’s documentary, “Miss Representation.” Relying so heavily on under-40 female leads can mess with women’s heads and create some pretty mismatched on-screen coupling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Great Moments in Age-Inappropriate Casting', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T21:10:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Lawson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'mid