': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Erlanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4c9d8e8-4769-5a69-9ea2-57b564d3b2f6', 'word_count': 1154, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4c9d8e8-4769-5a69-9ea2-57b564d3b2f6'}, {'abstract': 'Once, iTunes was considered a danger to the music business and many prominent bands stayed away. But with more than a quarter of all U.S. music sold there, iTunes has won over nearly all doubters and Spotify is the new concern.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/19/acdc-joins-itunes-as-spotify-emerges-as-musics-new-disrupter/', 'snippet': 'Once, iTunes was considered a danger to the music business and many prominent bands stayed away. But with more than a quarter of all U.S. music sold there, iTunes has won over nearly all doubters and Spotify is the new concern.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Apple iTunes store, once considered dangerous new territory for the music industry, won over a last holdout — AC/DC — on Monday. But the band’s decision to join iTunes has also revealed that the industry has found a new digital frenemy: Spotify, where AC/DC’s music is still nowhere to be found.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'AC/DC Joins iTunes, as Spotify Emerges as Music’s New Disrupter', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AC/DC', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'iTunes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Spotify', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T