None, 'lastname': 'Carey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0537b985-cf9a-5a38-be9f-0e4c843e6ddd', 'word_count': 1320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0537b985-cf9a-5a38-be9f-0e4c843e6ddd'}, {'abstract': 'In “Wild Man in Rome,” Matthew Maguire riffs on a range of places and characters in a 65-minute monologue at the Wild Project.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/theater/reviews/wild-man-in-rome-a-solo-show-by-matthew-maguire.html', 'snippet': 'In “Wild Man in Rome,” Matthew Maguire riffs on a range of places and characters in a 65-minute monologue at the Wild Project.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When last seen in 2010, the Wild Man, Matthew Maguire’s alter ego in a solo show of the same name, was musing on the taking of risks, the value of thrills. It didn’t matter if the stories Mr. Maguire shared from his own life seemed fairly mild — drugs, stolen cars and other signs of a misspent youth — his belief was unshakable: Each of us houses a need to be astonished, especially by our own choices. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Escaping in Rome, Do as the Wild Man Does', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Escaping in Rome, Do as the Wild Man Does', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maguire, Matthew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Creation Production Company', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-19T22:53:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel M. Gold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2baf31bc-6790-5061-a389-9609e50b3b14', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2baf31bc-6790-5061-a389-9609e50b3b14'}, {'abstract': 'The Monteverdi Choir and Orchestre Révolutionnaire et Romantique gave energetic renditions of Beethoven’s Ninth and his “Missa Solemnis” at Carnegie Hall over the weekend.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/arts/music/john-eliot-gardiner-leads-ensembles-at-carnegie-hall.html', 'snippet': 'The Monteverdi Choir and Orchestre Révolutionnaire et Romantique gave energetic renditions of Beethoven’s Ninth and his “Missa Solemnis” at Carnegie Hall over the weekend.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Too often we think of historically informed performances in terms of what is stripped away: less vibrato; fewer players; the muted brilliance of gut strings and natural horns. But as John Eliot Gardiner demonstrated in two red-blooded performances of Beethoven masterworks at Carnegie Hall this past weekend, the period-instrument movement is, at its best, an ambitious grab for big effects and heightened expressive power. On Friday he led his Monteverdi Choir and Orchestre Révolutionnaire et Romantique in a flame-drawn rendition of the Ninth; on Saturday he gave a glowing performance of the “Missa Solemnis.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/arts/20REV1/20REV1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/arts/20REV1/20REV1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None,