//article/9d53d1fb-9a0c-52e9-aef8-fd94ad1de017'}, {'abstract': 'Exempting foreign exchange derivatives from rules under the Dodd-Frank reform law could cause another crisis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/opinion/a-step-back-for-derivatives-regulation.html', 'snippet': 'Exempting foreign exchange derivatives from rules under the Dodd-Frank reform law could cause another crisis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the campaign trail, President Obama took credit for imposing tough rules on Wall Street. But, last week, the administration moved to weaken new rules on derivatives. Late Friday afternoon, the Treasury Department announced that it was exempting certain foreign exchange derivatives from rules under the Dodd-Frank reform law that are intended to reduce risk and increase transparency. The exempted derivatives — instruments known as foreign exchange swaps and forwards — represent a $4 trillion-a-day global market and are one of the big banks’ largest sources of derivatives trading revenue. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Step Back for Derivatives Regulation', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Step Back for Derivatives Regulation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Derivatives (Financial Instruments)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-20T01:53:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/750bea04-fae6-5179-86ec-d2b75e9e0d55', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/750bea04-fae6-5179-86ec-d2b75e9e0d55'}, {'abstract': 'Recent comments from Justice Samuel Alito Jr. of the Supreme Court again raises the role of the news media and how they are organized under American law.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/opinion/justice-alito-citizens-united-and-the-press.html', 'snippet': 'Recent comments from Justice Samuel Alito Jr. of the Supreme Court again raises the role of the news media and how they are organized under American law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, Justice Samuel Alito Jr. speciously defended the Supreme Court’s disastrous ruling in the 2010 Citizens United case by arguing that the ruling, which allowed unlimited independent campaign spending by corporations and unions, was not really groundbreaking at all. In fact, he said, all it did was reaffirm that corporations have free speech rights and that, without such rights, newspapers would have lost the major press freedom rulings that allowed the publication of the Pentagon Papers and made it easier for newspapers to defend themselves against libel suits in New York Times v. Sullivan. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Justice Alito, Citizens United and the Press', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Justice Alito, Citizens United and the Press', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Citizens United v Federal Election Commission (Supreme Court Decision)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALITO, SAMUEL A JR', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of the Press', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Newspapers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporations', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-20T01:53:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4ea6cae-fbe0-59e7-aaef-61ebd1133830', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4ea6cae-fbe0-59e7-aaef-61ebd1133830'}, {'abstract': 'Rescheduling dividend payments and selling stocks are among the strategies being employed to avoid higher tax rates set to take effect in 2013.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/business/economy/early-dividend-for-wal-mart-is-latest-move-in-tax-scramble.html', 'snippet': 'Rescheduling dividend payments and selling stocks are among the strategies being employed to avoid higher tax rates set to take effect in 2013.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What a difference six days makes. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/business/JP-DIVIDEND/JP-DIVIDEND-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 361, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/20/business/JP-DIVIDEND/JP-DIVIDEND-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 361}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/business/JP-DIVIDEND/JP-DIVIDEND-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 611, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/business/JP-DIVIDEND/JP-DIVIDEND-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1222, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Early Dividend for Wal-Mart Is Latest Move in Tax Tactics', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Early Dividend For Wal-Mart Is Latest Move In Tax Tactics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wal-Mart Stores Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corporate Taxes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-20T01:55:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nathaniel Popper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nathaniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Popper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea7706f5-4c82-5ea3-9284-58d18e65c860', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea7706f5-4c82-5ea3-9284-58d18e65c860'}, {'abstract': 'Israel is not the only party responsible for the current conflict in Gaza.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/opinion/hamass-illegitimacy.html', 'snippet': 'Israel is not the only party responsible for the current conflict in Gaza.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The death of more than 100 Palestinians and the wounding of hundreds of others in the six-day-old Gaza war were not enough for the top leader of Hamas, Khaled Meshal. Speaking in Cairo on Monday, he taunted Israel to begin a ground invasion, saying “if you wanted to launch it, you would have done it.” He ignored the fact that an invasion would kill many more Palestinians and further devastate the Gaza Strip, which, in August, before the current fighting, the United Nations predicted would be unlivable by 2020. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hamas’s Illegitimacy', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hamas&#8217;s Illegitimacy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gaza Strip', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hamas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meshal, Khaled', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-20T01:56:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/393f58ea-0c57-5b20-8a52-019056e1af13', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/393f58ea-0c57-5b20-8a52-019056e1af13'}, {'abstract': 'An unorthodox crop of  center-right free-thinkers defy    caricatures of the G.O.P.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/opinion/brooks-the-conservative-future.html', 'snippet': 'An unorthodox crop of  center-right free-thinkers defy    caricatures of the G.O.P.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you listened to the Republican candidates this year, you heard a conventional set of arguments. But if you go online, you can find a vibrant and increasingly influential center-right conversation. Most of the young writers and bloggers in this conversation intermingle, but they can be grouped, for clarity’s sake, around a few hot 