 '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/783c0007-62f0-519c-be64-206522f8607e', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/783c0007-62f0-519c-be64-206522f8607e'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/arts/television/whats-on-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (Sundance) ICONOCLASTS: KAREEM ABDUL-JABBAR + CHUCK D  Mr. Abdul-Jabbar, the N.B.A.’s career scoring leader and a Hall of Famer, and the rapper Chuck D (above right, with Mr. Abdul-Jabbar) collaborated last year on the documentary “On the Shoulders of Giants,” about the New York Renaissance basketball team. Popularly known as the Harlem Rens, this all-black basketball team was formed in 1923 during the Harlem Renaissance, and regularly beat white teams and won the inaugural World Professional Basketball Tournament in Chicago in 1939. In this season finale the men discuss jazz and basketball while delving into race relations, history and cultural influences.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-20T06:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_