me as if it were a formal title. So we don’t refer to “novelist Zadie Smith” or “cellist Yo-Yo ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'We try to avoid the unnatural journalistic mannerism of the “false title” — that is, using a description or job designation with someone’s name as if it were a formal title. So we don’t refer to “novelist Zadie Smith” or “cellist Yo-Yo Ma.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'False Titles', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-20T13:00:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/969271c8-0516-5a46-9be8-5412d65355fe', 'word_count': 1085, 'uri': 'nyt://article/969271c8-0516-5a46-9be8-5412d65355fe'}, {'abstract': 'Everyone knows how long a minute is, but just try counting the megabytes your phone is chewing through on a regular basis. Now an iPhone app promises to analyze phone bills automatically to determine which data and calling plans are the best and cheapest for customers.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/ratemizer-iphone-app-savings/', 'snippet': 'Everyone knows how long a minute is, but just try counting the megabytes your phone is chewing through on a regular basis. Now an iPhone app promises to analyze phone bills automatically to determine which data and calling plans are the best and c...', 'lead_paragraph': ' Everyone knows how long a minute is, but just try counting the number of megabytes your phone is chewing through on a regular basis without going insane. Now an iPhone app released over the weekend promises to analyze phone bills automatically to