orted', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b3712a5-9fff-5688-9736-d2de0928b6db', 'word_count': 1087, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b3712a5-9fff-5688-9736-d2de0928b6db'}, {'abstract': 'In her new album, “Unapologetic,” Rihanna’s icy smoothness prevails, and she maintains her cool, never once verging on the maudlin, even in the more vulnerable songs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/arts/music/rihannas-album-unapologetic-makes-most-of-her-talent.html', 'snippet': 'In her new album, “Unapologetic,” Rihanna’s icy smoothness prevails, and she maintains her cool, never once verging on the maudlin, even in the more vulnerable songs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 13th word of the first verse of the first song on “Unapologetic,” the seventh album by Rihanna, is a curse, and she relishes it, hitting the syllables hard, spitting them out sharply as if she hoped they might wound someone. The song, “Phresh Out the Runway,” is a chaotically dense spray of boasts over a muscular, scraping beat. Rihanna sounds indignant and impressed with herself, proclaiming, “Walk up in this bitch like I own the ho.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/arts/21RIHANNA/21RIHANNA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/21/arts/21RIHANNA/21RIHANNA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/arts/21RIHANNA/21RIHANNA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 751, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/arts/21RIHANNA/21RIHANNA