cle', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07273295-784a-549b-b93b-5ec53fb26b26', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07273295-784a-549b-b93b-5ec53fb26b26'}, {'abstract': 'If we all followed Calvin Trillin, we’d have pasta carbonara for Thanksgiving — a deli egg-bacon-and-cheese-on-a-roll that has been pasta-fied, fancified and fetishized.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/dining/an-unlikely-thanksgiving-stand-in-pasta-carbonara.html', 'snippet': 'If we all followed Calvin Trillin, we’d have pasta carbonara for Thanksgiving — a deli egg-bacon-and-cheese-on-a-roll that has been pasta-fied, fancified and fetishized.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CALVIN TRILLIN does not like turkey. He has called it “basically something college dormitories use to punish students for hanging around on Sunday.” In 1981, he suggested in The New Yorker that Americans gather around the Thanksgiving table for an unlikely substitute: pasta carbonara. He imagined that it was served at the first Thanksgiving, confounding both the Indians and the Pilgrims, who declared it “heretically tasty” and “the work of the devil.”', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/dining/21CARBONARA1_SPAN/21CARBONARA1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/21/dining/21CARBONARA1_SPAN/21CARBONARA1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/dini