y The Times.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Office Hours’ Experiment: Day 2', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-20T22:12:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a881fcd9-80d9-5ccf-acec-8a40644fbc64', 'word_count': 202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a881fcd9-80d9-5ccf-acec-8a40644fbc64'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Bewkes, who became chief executive in 2008, has led the effort to put emphasis on the company’s cable television assets while shedding other assets not related to the core entertainment business.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/bewkes-to-remain-in-time-warner-posts-through-2017/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Bewkes, who became chief executive in 2008, has led the effort to put emphasis on the company’s cable television assets while shedding other assets not related to the core entertainment business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeffrey L. Bewkes, chairman and chief executive of Time Warner, will remain in his posts for at least five more years, the media company said on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bewkes to Remain in Time Warner Posts Through 2017', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cable Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Compensation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bewkes, Jeffrey L', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Time Warner Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-20T22:14:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4fa4fff-e7f0-58f4-9d27-9f533c8bb12a', 'word_count': 290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4fa4fff-e7f0-58f4-9d27-9f533c8bb12a'}, {'abstract': 'An analyst forecast that the new BlackBerry phones would have a 20 to 30 percent chance of success — and the company’s stock got a bump.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/rim-stock-gets-a-lift/', 'snippet': 'An analyst forecast that the new BlackBerry phones would have a 20 to 30 percent chance of success — and the company’s stock got a bump.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A 20 to 30 percent chance of success is hardly a ringing endorsement. But that forecast for the coming line of BlackBerry 10 phones by Peter Misek, an analyst with Jeffries, was enough to raise the stock price of Research in Motion on Tuesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'RIM Stock Gets a Lift', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'BlackBerry (Handheld Device)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Research In Motion Ltd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-20T22:21:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'las