'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ffbd09c-fde3-5c7a-8058-39d74b191fa5', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ffbd09c-fde3-5c7a-8058-39d74b191fa5'}, {'abstract': 'Last year unemployment insurance kept 2.3 million people above the official poverty line, including 620,000 who were children living with a family member who received benefits.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/20/jobless-benefits-as-an-antipoverty-program/', 'snippet': 'Last year unemployment insurance kept 2.3 million people above the official poverty line, including 620,000 who were children living with a family member who received benefits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In addition to the tax increases and broad federal spending cuts (known as “sequestration”) scheduled to take effect at the end of this year, the emergency unemployment benefits system is also expected to come to an end.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jobless Benefits as an Antipoverty Program', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment Insurance', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T00:07:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4ea7c44-8d45-5fd4-98f2-60362f83974b', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4ea7c44-8d45-5fd4-98f2-60362f83974b'}, {'abstract': 'In “Red Dawn,” a remake of the 1984 film, young people band together to fight North Koreans who have invaded Spokane, Wash.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/movies/red-dawn-remake-trades-soviets-for-north-koreans.html', 'snippet': 'In “Red Dawn,” a remake of the 1984 film, young people band together to fight North Koreans who have invaded Spokane, Wash.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The ideal viewer for “Red Dawn,” a slicked-up redo of the 1984 John Milius war flick about a Soviet invasion (with Cuban and Nicaraguan support) of the United States, has to be Kim Jong-un, the leader of North Korea. That’s because by changing the attackers to North Koreans, the filmmakers have paid him a great compliment by making his country a Hollywood villain. Thinking adults will find a North Korean invasion the stuff of wing-nut fantasies, which — with kids who just want to see guys shoot stuff up — is probably what the distributor is banking on. Everyone else interested may want to go with the campy flow, like a colleague who snorted of the invaders, “What, did they paddle over in canoes?” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/arts/RED/RED-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/21/arts/RED/RED-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/arts/RED/RED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/arts/RED/RED-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'imag