name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lower East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Russ & Daughters (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T11:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joyce Wadler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joyce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wadler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b89fc05-1661-5562-acae-ae3f16d2473b', 'word_count': 1259, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b89fc05-1661-5562-acae-ae3f16d2473b'}, {'abstract': 'Parenting our children was good practice for the issues we face with our parents.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/patience-consciousness-and-white-lies/', 'snippet': 'Parenting our children was good practice for the issues we face with our parents.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My wife and I are blessed with having three “semi-independent” parents in their mid-80s living within a few blocks of us. Our children grew up knowing their grandparents as integral parts of our nuclear family, within walking distance for most of their childhoods. But now that our nest is empty, we find ourselves reliving many of the parenting issues we faced when our children were little — now in geriatric versions, at close range. As it turns out, parenting was good practice for the issues we face with our own parents. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Patience, Consciousness and White Lies', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elderly', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T11:39:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harley A. Rotbart, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harley', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Rotbart', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6af2e20e-e083-5d98-8f91-ac3434d006cb', 'word_count': 961, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6af2e20e-e083-5d98-8f91-ac3434d006cb'}, {'abstract': 'By partnering with governments to peacefully redistribute small plots of land, the nonprofit group Landesa is helping to bring food, income, stability and dignity to India’s poorest.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/what-a-little-land-can-do/', 'snippet': 'By partnering with governments to peacefully redistribute small plots of land, the nonprofit group Landesa is helping to bring food, income, stability and dignity to India’s poorest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The poorest people in the world are those who don’t have land. In India, landlessness is a better predictor of poverty than illiteracy or belonging to castes at the bottom of society.\xa0 At least 17 million rural households in India are completely landless, living on others’ land and working as sharecroppers or day laborers tending other peoples’ crops.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What a Little Land Can Do', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Land Use Policies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rural Areas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prosterman, Roy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Landesa', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Bengal (India)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T12:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/def5074b-b69b-5fe6-b9ee-831471043655', 'word_count': 1254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/def5074b-b69b-5fe6-b9ee-831471043655'}, {'abstract': "Six months after guiding Chelsea to an unlikely title, Roberto Di Matteo was fired Wednesday with the club on the brink of being eliminated from this season's Champions League.", 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/22/sports/soccer/22iht-soccer22.html', 'snippet': "Six months after guiding Chelsea to an unlikely title, Roberto Di Matteo was fired Wednesday with the club on the brink of being eliminated from this season's Champions League.", 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Barely six months after Roberto Di Matteo persuaded disruptive and failing Chelsea players to pull together and win both the English F.A. Cup and the Champions League, the coach has been fired.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/22/sports/22iht-soccer22a/22iht-soccer22a-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/22/sports/22iht-soccer22a/22iht-soccer22a-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/22/sports/22iht-soccer22a/22iht-soccer22a-jumbo.