ation': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70f220a7-dc64-59dc-a238-3c0ddee54c0c', 'word_count': 324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70f220a7-dc64-59dc-a238-3c0ddee54c0c'}, {'abstract': 'When that Microsoft Word document is finally complete and everyone who worked on it is happy, you can remove all the comments embedded in the file at once.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/qa-deleting-the-conversation-in-word/', 'snippet': 'When that Microsoft Word document is finally complete and everyone who worked on it is happy, you can remove all the comments embedded in the file at once.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Deleting the Conversation in Word', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deleting Comments\r\nIn Word Documents', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T12:40:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3113547-1012-5258-8cfe-c1f00ab354eb', 'word_count': 142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3113547-1012-5258-8cfe-c1f00ab354eb'}, {'abstract': 'The World Resources Institute says that 1,199 new coal plants are being planned worldwide.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/king-coal-alive-and-kicking/', 'snippet': 'The World Resources Institute says that 1,199 new coal plants are being planned worldwide.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some 1,200 new coal-fired power plants are being planned across the globe despite concerns about greenhouse gas emissions from such generating stations, the most polluting type, the World Resources Institute estimates. Two-thirds of them would operate in China and India, it says. [World Resources Institute] ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'King Coal, Alive and Kicking', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T12:41:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6fbfc88-b600-5951-a71b-23e9d0e8de5d', 'word_count': 180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6fbfc88-b600-5951-a71b-23e9d0e8de5d'}, {'abstract': 'Filmmakers are pushing hard against, and sometimes dispensing with, storytelling conventions, and audiences seem willing to follow them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/movies/films-dispense-with-storytelling-conventions.html', 'snippet': 'Filmmakers are pushing hard against, and sometimes dispensing with, storytelling conventions, and audiences seem willing to follow them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOOK past the award-season hype and the current bounty of decent, good, great movies, and one thing becomes clear: We live in interesting narrative times, cinematically. In “Cloud Atlas” characters jump across centuries, space and six separate stories into a larger tale about human interconnectedness. In “Anna Karenina” Tolstoy’s doomed heroine suffers against visibly artificial sets, a doll within an elaborate dollhouse, while in “Life of Pi” a boy and a tiger share a small boat in a very big sea amid long silences, hallucinatory visuals and no obvious story arc. In movies like these, as well as in “The Master” and “Holy Motors,” filmmakers are pushing hard against, and sometimes dispensing with, storytelling conventions, and audiences seem willing to follow them. The chief film critics of The New York Times, Manohla Dargis and A. O. Scott, consider this experimental turn.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/movies/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/25/movies/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/movies/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/movies/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/movies/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/movies/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/movies/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT/20121122_CRITICS-slide-O6CT-thumbLarge-v2.jp