': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T14:48:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb1c3544-b258-53c0-b004-8df9e5faf727', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb1c3544-b258-53c0-b004-8df9e5faf727'}, {'abstract': 'Faced with studies that found inorganic arsenic, a carcinogen, in rice, Lundberg Family Farms is struggling to reassure its customers, who greeted the news with panic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/22/business/smallbusiness/lundberg-family-farms-confronts-reports-of-arsenic-in-its-rice-case-study.html', 'snippet': 'Faced with studies that found inorganic arsenic, a carcinogen, in rice, Lundberg Family Farms is struggling to reassure its customers, who greeted the news with panic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lundberg Family Farms grows rice in Richvale, Calif., about 70 miles north of Sacramento. It has 225 employees and 5,000 acres of farmland, and it contracts with about 40 other family farms to grow rice on 12,000 acres throughout the Sacramento Valley. It sells 17 varieties of rice and more than 150 products made from rice, and it produces more than $50 million in annual revenue.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/22/business/smallbusiness/22sbiz/22sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/22/business/smallbusiness/22sbiz/22sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/22/business/smallbusiness/22sbiz/22sbiz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'ju