news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae734d06-6ea0-5187-9a50-5d2ce8caf04b', 'word_count': 976, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae734d06-6ea0-5187-9a50-5d2ce8caf04b'}, {'abstract': 'A case study of physical transformation through competitive Bikram yoga.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/hell-bent-by-benjamin-lorr.html', 'snippet': 'A case study of physical transformation through competitive Bikram yoga.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How many times have you found yourself clicking through a slide show about someone who has lost lots of weight? In the last one I saw, a woman who lost 160 pounds took a picture every 20 pounds or so. The images were riveting, even though it often looked as if she were standing in a closet under a fluorescent light, her hair stringy, her face un-made-up. The human body amazes — both in its grotesqueness and beauty. Today, physical transformation is the stuff of reality TV shows; a century ago, it was the foundation of Bernarr Macfadden’s media empire. His flagship magazine, True Story, published readers’ accounts of miraculous weight loss. And the miracles rarely stopped at the shedding of pounds: health and vitality were regained, marriages reinvigorated, purpose found. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125SYMAN/1125SYMAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 709, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125SYMAN/1125SYMAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1418, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125SYMAN/1125SYMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125SYMAN/1125SYMAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125SYMAN/1125SYMAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sublime Shapes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sublime Shapes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Yoga', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lorr, Benjamin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T16:50:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stefanie Syman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stefanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Syman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/04dd6fc9-129b-5f48-87ac-78d9d75e659b', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04dd6fc9-129b-5f48-87ac-78d9d75e659b'}, {'abstract': 'The man who seeks to ban Bigfoot hunting in New York tells City Room the state is “taking a safe position” with its finding that “no program or action in relation to mythical animals is warranted.”', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/state-inaction-leaves-bigfoot-population-vulnerable/', 'snippet': 'The man who seeks to ban Bigfoot hunting in New York tells City Room the state is “taking a safe position” with its finding that “no program or action in relation to mythical animals is warranted.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'What is with these small-minded state bureaucrats? The founder of the Chautauqua Lake Bigfoot Expo in the western corner of the state – the heart of New York’s Bigfoot country — asked the Department of Environmental Conservation to ban the hunting of the furry hominid. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/nyregion/21bigfoot-cityroom/21bigfoot-cityroom-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 691, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/nyregion/21bigfoot-cityroom/21bigfoot-cityroom-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1383, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/nyregion/21bigfoot-cityroom/21bigfoot-cityroom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/21/nyregion/21bigfoot-cityroom/21bigfoot-cityroom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/nyregion/21bigfoot-cityroom/21bigfoot-cityroom-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'State Inaction Leaves Bigfoot Population Vulnerable', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hunting and Trapping', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sasquatch (Bigfoot)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T16:54:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba8d62a9-99c2-5d3d-87b6-6d0873e11a26', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba8d62a9-99c2-5d3d-87b6-6d0873e11a26'}, {'abstract': 'This first novel centers on a race to make computers seem more human.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/a-working-theory-of-love-by-scott-hutchins.html', 'snippet': 'This first novel centers on a race to make computers seem more human.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You could argue that the fundamental question behind all literature is: “What does it mean to be human?” Some people have even argued that storytelling itself is what makes us more than just monkeys with iPhones — that Homer created the modern consciousness, or that Shakespeare (as Harold Bloom has it) invented the human identity. In recent years, however, literature has lost a lot of ground on that score to evolutionary psychology, neurobiology and computer science, and particularly to the efforts of artificial intelligence researchers. So as we wait for the Singularity, when our iPhones will become sentient and Siri will start telling us what we can do for her, many of the savvier fiction writers have begun to come to grips with the fact that the tutelary spirit of the quest for the human may not be Dante or Emily Dickinson or Virginia Woolf, but Alan Turing, the British mathematician who helped start the revolution in computing.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125HYNES/1125HYNES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125HYNES/1125HYNES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1552, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125HYNES/1125HYNES-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125HYNES/1125HYNES-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125HYNES/1125HYNES-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fooled You', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fooled You', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hutchins, Scott', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T16:56:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Hynes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hynes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/38897993-25e5-5691-9fcf-550533bc4158', 'word_count': 1119, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38897993-25e5-5691-9fcf-550533bc4158'}, {'abstract': 'Hikers searching for meaning find dead bodies instead.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/black-heart-on-the-appalachian-trail-by-t-j-forrester.html', 'snippet': 'Hikers searching for meaning find dead bodies instead.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re one of the many readers who recently devoured Cheryl Strayed’s hiking memoir, “Wild,” your interest may be piqued by the title of T. J. Forrester’s new novel, “Black Heart on the Appalachian Trail.” Trail books like “Wild” and “A Walk in the Woods,” Bill Bryson’s classic take, tend to mix in common ingredients: offbeat characters, funny anecdotes, well-chosen details, warm epiphanies.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Barcott/1125-Barcott-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 388, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Barcott/1125-Barcott-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 388}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Barcott/1125-Barcott-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Barcott/1125-Barcott-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1420, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Barcott/1125-Barcott-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legac