Saul Steinberg.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saul Steinberg, the pre-eminent cartoonist of the 20th century, specialized in high-I.Q. humor. In an age when cartoons were populated by henpecked husbands and conniving wives in hair curlers, he found his inspiration in the unlikely realm of philosophical inquiry. Long associated with The New Yorker, he once filled four pages of the magazine with a field of hand-drawn question marks.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125SOLOMON/1125SOLOMON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 729, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125SOLOMON/1125SOLOMON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1451, 'width': 1033, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125SOLOMON/1125SOLOMON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125SOLOMON/1125SOLOMON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125SOLOMON/1125SOLOMON-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drawing the Line, and Crossing It', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drawing the Line, and Crossing It', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Steinberg, Saul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bair, Deirdre', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:16:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deborah Solomon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deborah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Solomon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/185da754-e226-51c2-90e3-eec2936bec2b', 'word_count': 1440, 'uri': 'nyt://article/185da754-e226-51c2-90e3-eec2936bec2b'}, {'abstract': 'Anne Applebaum recounts the Soviet grab for Eastern Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/iron-curtain-by-anne-applebaum.html', 'snippet': 'Anne Applebaum recounts the Soviet grab for Eastern Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Having brilliantly documented the horror of Stalin’s Soviet terror machine in her Pulitzer Prize-\xadwinning “Gulag,” Anne Applebaum now offers a bulky sequel, “Iron Curtain,” about the brutal effort of that same machine to crush and colonize Eastern Europe in the first decade after World War II. Her evidence, once again drawn from archival research and some survivor interviews, is overwhelming and convincing. But the heart of her story is hardly news.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125FRANKEL/1125FRANKEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125FRANKEL/1125FRANKEL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1830, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125FRANKEL/1125FRANKEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125FRANKEL/1125FRANKEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125FRANKEL/1125FRANKEL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stalin’s Shadow', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stalin’s Shadow', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Eastern Europe', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Applebaum, Anne', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:16:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Max Frankel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Max', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frankel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b409a8ed-9b2c-5407-83f6-686f9a00f33a', 'word_count': 1203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b409a8ed-9b2c-5407-83f6-686f9a00f33a'}, {'abstract': 'The heroine of Ian McEwan’s novel goes to work for MI5.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/sweet-tooth-by-ian-mcewan.html', 'snippet': 'The heroine of Ian McEwan’s novel goes to work for MI5.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ian McEwan’s work falls into two distinct periods. His early stories and novels were all cool post-1960s perversity, a high-end parade of deadpan macabre and kink and sideshow eccentricity: ghastly death, corpses and butchery, bestiality, incest and pedophilia, insanity, dwarves. But since he turned 50, around the turn of the century, he’s published lovely historical fiction about the disastrous sexual misunderstandings of youth (“Atonement,” “On Chesil Beach”), and contemporary fiction about an \xadalternative-energy researcher (“Solar”) and a deeply sane, happily married surgeon (“Saturday”). It’s as if Johnny Rotten had changed into Bono. And in the same way I like both the Sex Pistols and U2, I’ve enjoyed the best of McEwan’s fiction in both modes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Andersen/1125-Andersen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 948, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Andersen/1125-Andersen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1289, 'width': 1392, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Andersen/1125-Andersen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Andersen/1125-Andersen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Andersen/1125-Andersen-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I Spy', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I Spy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McEwan, Ian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:16:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kurt Andersen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kurt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Andersen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a81b2a5-6b78-50fe-bd86-f57621d91973', 'word_count': 1469, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a81b2a5-6b78-50fe-bd86-f57621d91973'}, {'abstract': 'Sherman Alexie’s stories explore the dissonance and alienation of contemporary Native American life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/blasphemy-by-sherman-alexie.html', 'snippet': 'Sherman Alexie’s stories explore the dissonance and alienation of contemporary Native American life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his 1936 essay “The Storyteller,” Walter Benjamin drew a sharp distinction between prose fiction, meaning novels and short stories, and actual storytelling, meaning spoken narratives passed from one individual to another. The essential quality of storytelling, Benjamin wrote, is lived experience: “Every story contains, openly or covertly, something useful . . . a moral; some practical advice; a proverb or maxim. In every case the storyteller has counsel for his readers. But if today ‘having counsel’ has an old-fashioned ring, this is because the communicability of experience is decreasing. . . . We have no counsel either for ourselves or for others.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125ROW/1125ROW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 860, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125ROW/1125ROW-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1719, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125ROW/1125ROW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125ROW/1125ROW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125ROW/1125ROW-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Without Reservation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Without Reservation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Alexie, Sherman', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:17:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Row', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Row', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/13bff02e-a748-5e86-8bdf-3bba2f559b09', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13bff02e-a748-5e86-8bdf-3bba2f559b09'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Sullivan explores the setting of the Revolutionary War — and it’s not in Massachusetts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/my-american-revolution-by-robert-sullivan.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Sullivan explores the setting of the Revolutionary War — and it’s not in Massachusetts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine Herodotus on steroids, not rambling in a roughly straight line from Cyrus to Xerxes, but diverging onto untrodden paths that transmogrify into fluvial streams of consciousness. That sort of detour through history pretty much sums up the quixotic scenic route Robert Sullivan travels in his winsome book “My American Revolution.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125ROBERTS/1125ROBERTS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 714, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125ROBERTS/1125ROBERTS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1427, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125ROBERTS/1125ROBERTS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125ROBERTS/1125ROBERTS-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125ROBERTS/1125ROBERTS-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Over the River and Through the Woods', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Over the River and Through the Woods', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Revolutionary War, American (1775-83)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, Robert', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:17:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Roberts', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roberts', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/751f46f4-d5fc-5bc2-94e9-f3c42298c67c', 'word_count': 783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/751f46f4-d5fc-5bc2-94e9-f3c42298c67c'}, {'abstract': 'The Penn State wrestling team has won two consecutive N.C.A.A. titles. Iowa had won the previous three, and is trying to return to form.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/iowa-wrestlers-want-the-championship-back/', 'snippet': 'The Penn State wrestling team has won two consecutive N.C.A.A. titles. Iowa had won the previous three, and is trying to return to form.', 'lead_paragraph': '1:10 p.m. | Updated Matt McDonough doesn’t hide how he feels about two straight third-place N.C.A.A. finishes for the Iowa wrestling team. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/21/sports/21iowa/21iowa-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 633, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo',