me': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Hoffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d24a8dd0-f75b-5879-aa31-408b3a9f318d', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d24a8dd0-f75b-5879-aa31-408b3a9f318d'}, {'abstract': 'Republicans won’t give up on the idea that the White House covered up the truth.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/the-benghazi-conspiracy/', 'snippet': 'Republicans won’t give up on the idea that the White House covered up the truth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Republicans have the same basic attitude toward conspiracy theories as the Plains Indians had toward the buffalo – they are the basis of life, even religion, and no part, no matter how minor, should go unexploited. Hence Senator John McCain’s milking of the Benghazi attack. Or, rather, not the attack itself but the Obama administration’s response to it.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Benghazi Conspiracy', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Political Game Called Benghazi', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCain, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rice, Susan E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:21:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32a8d357-9aa0-5d54-91f5-f5395a632151', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32a8d357-9aa0-5d54-91f5-f5395a632151'}, {'abstract': 'Before we opened the doors at last week’s Open Source Science Fair, we spent a little time with the exhibitors to get summaries of their projects and some general thoughts on open source.', 'web_url': 'https://open.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/open-source-science-fair-exhibitor-experiences/', 'snippet': 'Before we opened the doors at last week’s Open Source Science Fair, we spent a little time with the exhibitors to get summaries of their projects and some general thoughts on open source.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before we opened the doors at last week’s Open Source Science Fair, we spent a little time with the exhibitors to learn more about their projects and get their general thoughts on open source. Here are some of their insights and comments.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Open Source Science Fair: Exhibitor Experiences', 'kicker': 'Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:24:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marci Windsheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marci', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Windsheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3ddcb31-21e3-55d1-ba60-bceb0ff0315b', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3ddcb31-21e3-55d1-ba60-bceb0ff0315b'}, {'abstract': 'As the Affordable Care Act takes hold, some employers will use their health insurance coverage to attract workers, while others will drop the coverage and use higher pay as the lure, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/the-future-of-employer-provided-health-insurance/', 'snippet': 'As the Affordable Care Act takes hold, some employers will use their health insurance coverage to attract workers, while others will drop the coverage and use higher pay as the lure, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Casey B. Mulligan is an economics professor at the University of Chicago. He is the author of “The Redistribution Recession: How Labor Market Distortions Contracted the Economy.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Employer-Provided Health Insurance and the Market', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Benefits', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:27:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Casey B. Mulligan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Casey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07ceae5b-3789-5827-99f4-cdeb7f3a2a76', 'word_count': 706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07ceae5b-3789-5827-99f4-cdeb7f3a2a76'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE PATRIARCH: The Remarkable Life and Turbulent Times of Joseph P. Kennedy,  by David Nasaw. (Penguin Press, $40.) This riveting history captures the sweep of Kennedy’s life — as Wall Street speculator, moviemaker, ambassador and dynastic founder.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:28:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed71400c-175a-5086-b2aa-84b32cfef763', 'word_count': 266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed71400c-175a-5086-b2aa-84b32cfef763'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CARRY THE ONE,  by Carol Anshaw. (Simon & Schuster, $15.) Beginning with a bohemian wedding in Wisconsin in 1983 and spanning some 25 years, this wry, humane novel follows a group of friends bound together by a fatal accident. With her characters taking refuge in art, drugs, social justice and love, Anshaw, as she did in her 1992 novel, “Aquamarine,” explores how one event or choice can irretrievably alter a life.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:28:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/b114035f-001f-583d-829d-fb4528c10ab2', 'word_count': 493, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b114035f-001f-583d-829d-fb4528c10ab2'}, {'abstract': 'Sonia Faleiro has always been compelled to write about marginalized communities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Sonia Faleiro has always been compelled to write about marginalized communities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Indian writing in the 1980s and ’90s was dominated by exuberance, and the novel. Think of the freewheeling language and plots of Salman Rushdie’s “Midnight’s Children” or Arundhati Roy’s “God of Small Things.” Writing of a very different sort is flourishing today. Magical realism has been supplanted by gritty, immersive reporting on poverty, sex trafficking and farmer suicides. Think of “The Beautiful and the Damned,” by Siddhartha Deb, and Sonia Faleiro’s “Beautiful Thing: Inside the Secret World of Bombay’s Dance Bars.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-upfront/1125-upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 652, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-upfront/1125-upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1304, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Faleiro, Sonia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:28:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90204bb5-e5fe-5f94-9ed3-5173b8508ff3', 'word_count': 258, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90204bb5-e5fe-5f94-9ed3-5173b8508ff3'}, {'abstract': 'Do you love the Oklahoma City Thunder? Whether you live in the Sooner state or elsewhere, tell us why you’re a fan in the comments section.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/21/thunder-nation-roars/', 'snippet': 'Do you love the Oklahoma City Thunder? Whether you live in the Sooner state or elsewhere, tell us why you’re a fan in the comments section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sam Anderson’s cover story on the Oklahoma City Thunder for the Nov. 11 issue inspired Thunder fans to write in from all over, not just the Sooner state. A fan in Pennsylvania said it’s his favorite team; another in Nevada praised the team for providing up-tempo, unselfish, team-oriented basketball “in abundance.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/magazine/20-analytics-thunder-nation/20-analytics-thunder-nation-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/magazine/20-analytics-thunder-nation/20-analytics-thunder-nation-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 480, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/magazine/20-analytics-thunder-nation/20-analytics-thunder-nation-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/20/magazine/20-analytics-thunder-nation/20-analytics-thunder-nation-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/20/magazine/20-analytics-thunder-nation/20-analytics-thunder-nation-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 140, 'width': 140, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Thunder Nation Roars', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, Sam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City Thunder', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oklahoma City (Okla)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:30:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Staff', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Staff'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2fcb795d-e773-51e8-a785-7940669a4ce1', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2fcb795d-e773-51e8-a785-7940669a4ce1'}, {'abstract': 'Despite potential pitfalls, parents and children remember their book collaborations as rewarding experiences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/parents-and-children-collaborate-on-books.html', 'snippet': 'Despite potential pitfalls, parents and children remember their book collaborations as rewarding experiences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The science writer Sandra Blakeslee has written seven books with eminent authors like the psychologist Judith Wallerstein and the neuro\xadscientist V. S. Ramachandran. But her favorite collaborator to date has been her son, Matthew. “The way we worked together was perfect,” she said about “The Body Has a Mind of Its Own,” the 2007 book they wrote together. “I said, ‘I’ll do Chapter 1, you do 2, I’ll do 3, you do 4.’ Then we started swapping them back and forth.” No anger, no fights, no stepping on toes, she insisted. Never. “Our different strengths would come to the fore. Matt is a beautiful writer, a better writer than I am, and I’m a little better on structure.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Henig/1125-Henig-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 646, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Henig/1125-Henig-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1423, 'width': 898, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Henig/1125-Henig-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Henig/1125-Henig-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125-Henig/1125-Henig-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Like Mother, Like Child', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Like Mother, Like Child', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kingsolver, Barbara', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Carol Higgins', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Mary Higgins', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T17:30:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Marantz Henig and Samantha Henig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': 'Marantz', 'lastname': 'Henig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Samantha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Henig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9da3647f-26c2-5e4e-a595-b39bb2f1c21f', 'word_count': 1249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9da3647f-26c2-5e4e-a595-b39bb2f1c21f'}, {'abstract': 'Evan Thomas argues that Dwight D. Eisenhower was a brilliantly canny peacekeeper.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/books/review/ikes-bluff-by-evan-thomas.html', 'snippet': 'Evan Thomas argues that Dwight D. Eisenhower was a brilliantly canny peacekeeper.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Evan Thomas’s “Ike’s Bluff: President Eisenhower’s Secret Battle to Save the World” is an examination of Dwight Eisenhower’s record that seeks to understand how he successfully kept the United States out of a major war during the eight years of his presidency. It is in keeping with the recent trend in complimentary revisionist histories of the administration, like “Eisenhower in War and Peace,” by Jean Edward Smith, and “Eisenhower: The White House Years,” by Jim Newton. Thomas tells us that once Eisenhower “extricated America from the Korean War in 1953,” his mission was to “avoid any war.” For that reason, Thomas, who teaches writing and journalism at Princeton and is the author of several books, concentrates on Eisenhower’s foreign policy and national security decisions to the exclusion of almost everything else. The president’s civil rights record, for example, is mentioned only briefly, and as a demonstration of his leadership talents.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125LARISON/1125LARISON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 332, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125LARISON/1125LARISON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 332}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125LARISON/1125LARISON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 566, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125LARISON/1125LARISON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1133, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125LARISON/1125LARISON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125LARISON/1125LARISON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/books/review/1125LARISON/1125LARISON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'l