 bears his names, shops for clever storage for children’s toys.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Aram, who owns the decorative accessories and tabletop company that bears his names, shops for clever storage for children’s toys.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shopping for Toy Storage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Toys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-21T23:45:13+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Home & Garden', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/c59f6e38-64eb-522e-81b9-97e488049f18', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/c59f6e38-64eb-522e-81b9-97e488049f18'}, {'abstract': 'The documentary “The Central Park Five” is equal measures criminal investigation, cultural exhumation and a consideration of race in a presumptively postracial America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/22/movies/the-documentary-the-central-park-five.html', 'snippet': 'The documentary “The Central Park Five” is equal measures criminal investigation, cultural exhumation and a consideration of race in a presumptively postracial America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The documentary “The Central Park Five” revisits two New York nightmares. The first and most famous was the rape and beating of a 28-year-old white woman who, very early on April 20, 1989, was found in Central Park bound, gagged, nearly naked and nearly dead, her head crushed and shirt soaked in her blood. For years she was known only as the Central Park jogger, and her assailants were widely thought to be the five black and Latino teenagers, 14 to 16, who were arrested in the attack. The directors Ken Burns, David McMahon and Sarah Burns argue that the convictions, and the years the defendants served for the crime they were later absolved of, were a second, racially motivated crime.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/22/arts/22CENTRAL/22CENTRAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 319, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/22/arts/22CENTRAL/22CENTRAL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 319}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/22/arts/22CENTRAL/22CENTRAL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 575, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/22/arts/22CENTRAL/22CENTRAL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 575, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/22/arts/22CENTRAL/22CENTRAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/22/arts/22CENTRAL/22CENTRAL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/22/arts/22CENTRAL/22CENTRAL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 117, 'width': 117, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Filmmakers Still Seek Lessons From a Case That Rocked a City', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Seeking Lessons From a Notorious Case', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 