'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-22T01:24:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07334795-2832-5036-9824-793b5f5b6d6a', 'word_count': 941, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07334795-2832-5036-9824-793b5f5b6d6a'}, {'abstract': 'We may be oblivious to the extraordinary. This is a reversible condition.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/22/opinion/on-being-not-dead.html', 'snippet': 'We may be oblivious to the extraordinary. This is a reversible condition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE night last year I called my friend Oliver and told him to meet me on the roof of our apartment building. He lives three flights down from me. I had pulled together a simple dinner — roast chicken, good bread, olives, cherries, wine. We ate at a picnic table. I’d forgotten wineglasses, so we traded swigs out of the bottle. It was summer. The sun was setting on the Hudson. Neighbors were enjoying themselves at nearby tables. The breeze was nice. The surrounding cityscape looked like a stage set for a musical. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Being Not Dead', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On Being Not Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Happiness', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thanksgiving Day', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-22T01:30:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Hayes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hayes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b892e7e-00e4-5df7-9d1b-7657bee42877', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b892e7e-00e4-5df7-9d1b-7657bee42877'}, {'abstract': 'Good news, people! In the middle of all the planning for Thanksgiving dinner, I took some incredibly important questions from dear readers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/22/opinion/collins-the-turkey-chronicles.html', 'snippet': 'Good news, people! In the middle of all the planning for Thanksgiving dinner, I took some incredibly important questions from dear readers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thanksgiving used to be the signal for the start of holiday shopping, but that was long ago and, of course, now the signal is Arbor Day. But Thanksgiving still retains an important role as the real end to the election season. This is it. No more talking about what happened in Ohio. Time to move forward. And, as a public service, I am willing to take questions.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/gail-collins/gail-collins-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'c