 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b86d3c29-3074-5ca7-a9c3-e113e5c68d6f', 'word_count': 422, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b86d3c29-3074-5ca7-a9c3-e113e5c68d6f'}, {'abstract': 'All that cooking grease can be diverted to avoid clogging pipes and sewers or even generate a little biofuel.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/22/did-you-save-some-turkey-fat-other-oils/', 'snippet': 'All that cooking grease can be diverted to avoid clogging pipes and sewers or even generate a little biofuel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What could be more heartwarming than the sight of a table groaning with glistening Thanksgiving turkey, candied yams and buttery brussel sprouts or green beans? Still, all of that succulence is balanced by chaos and mess in the kitchen, where towers of plates, pans, and pots also glisten with the day’s accumulation of oil and grease. After an afternoon sweating over turkey and plying argumentative relatives with food and drink, you find your sink strainer or your garbage disposal doing double duty. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Did You Save Some Turkey Fat? Other Oils?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biodiesel Fuel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biofuels', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sewers and Sewage', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thanksgiving Day', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Turkeys', 'ran