ni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sangha', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db24504e-567e-58b6-b3c9-cec0ecc03016', 'word_count': 1787, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db24504e-567e-58b6-b3c9-cec0ecc03016'}, {'abstract': 'FP Patisserie, a new cafe from François Payard in the Upper East Side, draws a steady crowd craving an experience of refined familiarity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/nyregion/at-fp-patisserie-out-for-a-decadent-treat.html', 'snippet': 'FP Patisserie, a new cafe from François Payard in the Upper East Side, draws a steady crowd craving an experience of refined familiarity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Loyalists, led by the ladies who lunch, have been flocking to François Payard’s gleaming new cafe since the pastry chef returned to the Upper East Side on Nov. 8. The front of FP Patisserie at 74th Street and Third Avenue features pyramids of macarons, shelves of dark chocolate creations and a long pastry case of elegant tarts, set against color-splashed oil paintings and under a David Rockwell glass chandelier. There’s a small wine/coffee bar at the back of the shop, which opens discreetly into a spare salon de thé with 30 seats for breakfast, lunch and early dinner. Payard devotees, thrilled to see the hot chocolate on the limited menu, will not yet find the onion soup they loved from the 74th and Lexington location, which closed in 2009. But despite competition from a bustling new boulangerie across the street, the patisserie draws a steady crowd craving an experience of refined familiarity. And an éclair or three.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/nyregion/25TABLE_SPAN/25TABLE_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/25/nyregion/25TABLE_SPAN/25TABLE_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/nyregion/25TABLE_SPAN/25TABLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/nyregion/25TABLE_SPAN/25TABLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/nyregion/25TABLE_SPAN/25TABLE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/nyregion/25TABLE_SPAN/25TABLE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/nyregion/25TABLE_SPAN/25TABLE_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Out for a Decadent Treat', 'kicker': 'At the Table | FP Patisserie', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out for a Decadent Treat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'FP Patisserie (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Payard, Francois', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bakeries and Baked Products', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Upper East Side (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-24T00:46:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Robbins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robbins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/295d8873-5af8-5eb0-8768-ecfc8ce5b07b', 'word_count': 520, 'uri': 'nyt://article/295d8873-5af8-5eb0-8768-ecfc8ce5b07b'}, {'abstract': '“Our Global Kitchen,” at the American Museum of Natural History, is a show about how cultures transform nature for food, and how, in recent years, those transformations may have gone awry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/24/arts/design/our-global-kitchen-at-american-museum-of-natural-history.html', 'snippet': '“Our Global Kitchen,” at the American Museum of Natural History, is a show about how cultures transform nature for food, and how, in recent years, those transformations may have gone awry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Thanksgiving weekend, the subject of food is slightly risky. Bringing it up may be like going to a supermarket after a gargantuan dinner and finding that everything on display is unappealing. But a visit to the new exhibition “Our Global Kitchen: Food, Nature, Culture” at the American Museum of Natural History should quickly revive the intellectual appetite. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/24/arts/24GLOBALjp2/24GLOBALjp2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 646, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/24/arts/24GLOBALjp2/24GLOBALjp2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1291, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thu