:23:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By James Atlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Atlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/06f9e470-5b17-5d00-8186-fd7c57683f73', 'word_count': 1419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06f9e470-5b17-5d00-8186-fd7c57683f73'}, {'abstract': 'In October 2004, the investment company Taihong was one of the largest shareholders of insurance giant Ping An, holding 135 million shares.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/11/25/business/ping-ans-hidden-shareholders-friends-and-family-of-wen-jiabao.html', 'snippet': 'In October 2004, the investment company Taihong was one of the largest shareholders of insurance giant Ping An, holding 135 million shares.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In October 2004, the investment company Taihong was one of the largest shareholders of insurance giant Ping An, holding 135 million shares.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ping An’s Hidden Shareholders: Friends and Family of Wen Jiabao', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-24T19:24:04+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/d43c94db-ad90-547f-a49b-a4b10dd53eff', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/d43c94db-ad90-547f-a49b-a4b10dd53eff'}, {'abstract': 'Hurricane Sandy offered a preview of the dangers to come as the planet warms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/opinion/sunday/rising-seas-vanishing-coastlines.html', 'snippet': 'Hurricane Sandy offered a preview of the dangers to come as the planet warms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE oceans have risen and fallen throughout Earth’s history, following the planet’s natural temperature cycles. Twenty thousand years ago, what is now New York City was at the edge of a giant ice sheet, and the sea was roughly 400 feet lower. But as the last ice age thawed, the sea rose to where it is today. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rising Seas, Vanishing Coastlines', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rising Seas, Vanishing Coastlines', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-24T19:24:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Strauss and Robert Kopp', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strauss', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kopp', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/602e0377-2612-5872-8aa5-60c47795ce9c', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/602e0377-2612-5872-8aa5-60c47795ce9c'}, {'abstract': 'Sibling rivalry plays out at the dinner — or lunch or breakfast — table.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/opinion/sunday/sibling-rivalry-one-long-food-fight.html', 'snippet': 'Sibling rivalry plays out at the dinner — or lunch or breakfast — table.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS one of four brothers, I grew up in a veritable petri dish for sibling rivalry. Harry, Ned, Mark and I rarely fought physically, but there was little we didn’t contest: baseball, checkers and Candy Land, definitely, but more sophisticated sports as well — the battle over the Sunday funnies, the race to take the first bath, the jousting for position on the sofa as our mother read to us before bed.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/sunday-review/25BROTHERS/25BROTHERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 430, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/25/sunday-review/25BROTHERS/25BROTHERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 430}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/sunday-review/25BROTHERS/25BROTHERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 733, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/sunday-review/25BROTHERS/25BROTHERS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1466, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/sunday-review/25BROTHERS/25BROTHERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/25/sunday-review/25BROTHERS/25BROTHERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/25/sunday-review/25BROTHERS/25BROTHERS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sibling Rivalry: One Long Food Fight', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sibling Rivalry: One Long Food Fight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-24T19:35:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Howe Colt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Howe', 'lastname': 'Colt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8a85e6f-69a0-5914-8b80-f66ee1372b3d', 'word_count': 1513, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8a85e6f-69a0-5914-8b80-f66ee1372b3d'}, {'abstract': 'In one realm you’re moving forward in ignorance. In the other you’re moving backward with something resembling omniscience.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/24/the-dual-lives-of-the-biographer/', 'snippet': 'In one realm you’re moving forward in ignorance. In the other you’re moving backward with something resembling omniscience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The biographer has two lives: The one she leads, and the one she ultimately understands. The first is a muddle of misgivings and misapprehensions, hesitations and half-chances, devoted to the baggage carousel or the Netflix queue or wherever the empty calories of existence are served.', 'print_section': 'SR',