t national currency — interest rates are near record lows:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Incredible Credibility', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-25T13:48:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8118664-4b02-5b8d-ae01-3708f29d833f', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8118664-4b02-5b8d-ae01-3708f29d833f'}, {'abstract': 'While kids often really do want to know about God, at other times they are seeking reassurance that their theory-building attempts to explain the world are acceptable.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/an-agnostic-talks-to-her-children-about-god/', 'snippet': 'While kids often really do want to know about God, at other times they are seeking reassurance that their theory-building attempts to explain the world are acceptable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am a religious Jew, married to a rabbi and active in my congregation. I am also a research psychologist of religion and an agnostic. I realize this may sound confusing (or confused), but after years of deliberations, I made my peace with God’s possible nonexistence.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Agnostic Talks to Her Children About God', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Religion and Belief', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-25T13:50:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nurit Novis Deutsch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nurit', 'middlename': 'Novis', 'lastname': 'Deutsch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fd3867b-b7e2-5ab5-8198-8735b6d3aa26', 'word_count': 770, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fd3867b-b7e2-5ab5-8198-8735b6d3aa26'}, {'abstract': 'What about wages?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/25/the-fake-skills-shortage/', 'snippet': 'What about wages?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kudos to Adam Davidson for some much-needed mythbusting about the supposed skills shortage holding the US economy back. Whenever you see some business person quoted complaining about how he or she can’t find workers with the necessary skills, ask what wage they’re offering. Almost always, it turns out that what said business person really wants is highly (and expensively) educated workers at a manual-labor wage. No wonder they come up short.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fake Skills Shortage', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-25T14:02:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1335554-6732-58d5-bef5-85d6d879b36c', 'word_count': 152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1335554-6732-58d5-bef5-85d6d879b36c'}, {'abstract': 'Drivers and implications of coastal losses in a warming world.', 'web_url': 'https