original': 'By Saul Zachary', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Saul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zachary', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de03ff3a-6540-5cdc-bf4b-a1ba5e3312d7', 'word_count': 98, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de03ff3a-6540-5cdc-bf4b-a1ba5e3312d7'}, {'abstract': 'On “MedTech Row,” entrepreneurs create new health care products and services for the poor.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/26/in-delhi-a-sand-hill-road-for-cheap-health-care/', 'snippet': 'On “MedTech Row,” entrepreneurs create new health care products and services for the poor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You’ve probably heard of Sand Hill Road, home to Silicon Valley’s venture capitalists, who changed the way the tech industry did business. You probably haven’t heard of New Delhi’s MedTech Row, but the innovators and entrepreneurs who work here are no less ambitious in attempting to transform the health care industry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Delhi, a ‘Sand Hill Road’ for Cheap Health Care', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dell, Susan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Omidyar, Pierre M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gates, Bill and Melinda, Foundation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard Medical School', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Massachusetts Institute of Technology', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Omidyar Network', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Program for Appropriate Technology in Health', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Stanford University', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-26T08:17:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jyoti Pande Lavakare', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jyoti', 'middlename': 'Pande', 'lastname': 'Lavakare', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca7f03cc-fa39-58f5-a04a-d73e6e376c2b', 'word_count': 1150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca7f03cc-fa39-58f5-a04a-d73e6e376c2b'}, {'abstract': 'In a clever satire of international aid, a Norwegian NGO calls for Africans to send radiators to its frostbitten people.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/26/radi-aid-spoofs-the-classic-call-for-aid-to-africa/', 'snippet': 'In a clever satire of international aid, a Norwegian NGO calls for Africans to send radiators to its frostbitten people.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO — “Africans unite to save Norwegians from dying of frostbite.” That’s the tagline of the fictional fundraising campaign “Radi-Aid.” The campaign, supposedly sponsored by concerned Africans, comes with all the trappings of the charity industry: snapshots of the suffering, branded T-shirts and an uplifting music video. The associated song — all vibrato and platitudes — asks Africans to collect and send radiators to the northern hemisphere. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Black Man’s Burden', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Aid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Humanitarian Aid', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 4, 'major': '