ageIf President Obama is serious about closing Guantanamo Bay, he needs to become more engaged with the issue.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opinion Report: Close Guantanamo Bay', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-26T15:21:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cac3eed9-b0b4-53ea-9a8a-dc280ebe019f', 'word_count': 41, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cac3eed9-b0b4-53ea-9a8a-dc280ebe019f'}, {'abstract': 'The Giants are inspiring. The Jets are so tiring even a longtime fan is hanging up his hat.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/26/leading-off-new-yorks-bipolar-state-of-mind/', 'snippet': 'The Giants are inspiring. The Jets are so tiring even a longtime fan is hanging up his hat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sometimes, the two professional football teams in the New York metropolitan area — and no, that does not include Rutgers, its Big Ten cash grab notwithstanding — seem to be slotted into roles, as if the N.F.L. season were a Broadway production. The Giants are the respectable franchise, with community pillars as owners, a spotlight-wary general manager, the sometimes grumpy but good-hearted coach and the aw-shucks quarterback. They win Super Bowls in inspiring fashion, usually beating the league’s most reviled team (the Patriots).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/sports/football/27leadingpic/27leadingpic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 658, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jum