 wage protections and other safeguards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nannies, caregivers and housecleaners earn a median wage of about $10 an hour, and few receive benefits like health insurance or paid sick days, according to the first-ever national statistical study of domestic workers, which is being released on Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/business/Workers/Workers-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 731, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/business/Workers/Workers-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1463, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/business/Workers/Workers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/27/business/Workers/Workers-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/business/Workers/Workers-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Study of Home Help Finds Low Worker Pay', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Study of Home Help Finds Low Worker Pay', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domestic Service', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T02:53:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6fcb1bbf-2d37-5dac-8f67-5270eec3b6d9', 'word_count': 1024, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6fcb1bbf-2d37-5dac-8f67-5270eec3b6d9'}, {'abstract': 'Subpoenas for call records of stolen cellphones have allowed the Police Department to store the information of thousands of victims a year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/nyregion/new-york-city-police-amassing-a-trove-of-cellphone-logs.html', 'snippet': 'Subpoenas for call records of stolen cellphones have allowed the Police Department to store the information of thousands of victims a year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a cellphone is reported stolen in New York, the Police Department routinely subpoenas the phone’s call records, from the day of the theft onward. The logic is simple: If a thief uses the phone, a list of incoming and outgoing calls could lead to the suspect. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'City Is Amassing Trove of Cellphone Logs', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'City Is Amassing Trove of Cellphone Logs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subpoenas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T02:55:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Goldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb39c1ce-f3f7-5e7c-ba1d-ca0104616784', 'word_count': 915, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb39c1ce-f3f7-5e7c-ba1d-ca0104616784'}, {'abstract': 'A brazen power grab by President Morsi of Egypt threatens a young democracy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/opinion/president-mohamed-morsis-power-grab-in-egypt.html', 'snippet': 'A brazen power grab by President Morsi of Egypt threatens a young democracy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Mohamed Morsi of Egypt appears to have made a course correction in his latest and most alarming power grab. During a meeting with the country’s top judges on Monday, he reportedly agreed to limit the sweeping authority he seized by unilateral decree last week. Instead of exempting all his decisions from judicial review, he would retain just the power to protect the constitutional assembly from being dissolved by the courts before it finishes its work early next year. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Crisis in Egypt', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Crisis in Egypt', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Muslim Brotherhood (Egypt)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T02:57:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/67af5bd0-fd27-5dd5-aa2e-5fa6508d4714', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67af5bd0-fd27-5dd5-aa2e-5fa6508d4714'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Edlinger and his 1988 ascent at a climbing competition in Utah helped popularize the sport, which he called “a form of yoga.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/sports/patrick-edlinger-a-trendsetter-for-sport-climbing-dies-at-52.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Edlinger and his 1988 ascent at a climbing competition in Utah helped popularize the sport, which he called “a form of yoga.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Patrick Edlinger, a versatile and charismatic French rock climber who helped popularize competitive sport climbing in the 1980s — “a form of yoga,” he called it — died on Nov. 16 at his home in La Palud-sur-Verdon, France. He was 52.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/sports/subEDLINGER-obit/subEDLINGER-obit-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/sports/subEDLINGER-obit/subEDLINGER-obit-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/sports/subEDLINGER-obit/subEDLINGER-obit-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/27/sports/subEDLINGER-obit/subEDLINGER-obit-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/sports/subEDLINGER-obit/subEDLINGER-obit-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Patrick Edlinger, a Trendsetter for Sport Climbing, Dies at 52', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Patrick Edlinger, 52, Sport Climber, Dies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Edlinger, Patrick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Climbing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T02:59:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Yardley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yardley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/3afce2da-abe9-5a0f-83c8-c12f3c46bc42', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3afce2da-abe9-5a0f-83c8-c12f3c46bc42'}, {'abstract': 'Combining unlimited spending on elections with issue advocacy is a dangerous escalation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/opinion/when-super-pacs-become-lobbyists.html', 'snippet': 'Combining unlimited spending on elections with issue advocacy is a dangerous escalation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “super PACs” and secret-money groups that polluted this year’s election with hundreds of millions of dollars’ worth of largely ineffective attack ads are not slinking away in shame. Many are regrouping and raising more money for a task that is no less pernicious: lobbying Congress and the White House on behalf of their special-interest donors, using the new, anything-goes rules of super PACs to make their advocacy more powerful.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When ‘Super PACs’ Become Lobbyists', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When ‘Super PACs’ Become Lobbyists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Action Committees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lobbying and Lobbyists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Americans for Prosperity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Club for Growth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Crossroads', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICANS FOR TAX REFORM', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T03:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b9905ab-5b5a-57ac-9054-ab0cbb7a16fb', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b9905ab-5b5a-57ac-9054-ab0cbb7a16fb'}, {'abstract': 'This Tuesday puzzle from Peter A. Collins will get stuck in your head.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/26/do-re-mi/', 'snippet': 'This Tuesday puzzle from Peter A. Collins will get stuck in your head.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TUESDAY’S PUZZLE — Get ready for some music. The theme in this Tuesday puzzle from Peter A. Collins focuses on a certain musician who was born 70 years ago today, with additional clues pertaining to five of this performer’s classic songs.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do-Re-Mi', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T03:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Gaffney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gaffney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4df757d5-8c18-5511-a1cf-b37dc153a48b', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4df757d5-8c18-5511-a1cf-b37dc153a48b'}, {'abstract': 'The family of Frank Olson, a scientist who unknowingly was given LSD by the Central Intelligence Agency, alleges the government covered up the truth about his death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/us/family-of-frank-olson-man-drugged-by-cia-plans-suit.html', 'snippet': 'The family of Frank Olson, a scientist who unknowingly was given LSD by the Central Intelligence Agency, alleges the government covered up the truth about his death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Nearly 60 years after the death of a government scientist who had been given LSD by the Central Intelligence Agency without his knowledge, his family says it plans to sue the government, alleging that he was murdered and did not commit suicide as the C.I.A. has long maintained.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/us/OLSON/OLSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/27/us/OLSON/OLSON-article