rts', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chase Stuart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chase', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stuart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7de86103-1ab1-5f75-88d9-e74203d46d83', 'word_count': 1618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7de86103-1ab1-5f75-88d9-e74203d46d83'}, {'abstract': 'The human toll of Mexico’s violent drug trade is portrayed in haunting, cinematic fashion by Eros Hoagland. His images — and how he works — reflect that struggle, as well as a more personal one.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/a-reckoning-at-the-frontier/', 'snippet': 'The human toll of Mexico’s violent drug trade is portrayed in haunting, cinematic fashion by Eros Hoagland. His images — and how he works — reflect that struggle, as well as a more personal one.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mexico’s northern borderlands have a dry, rugged feel in Eros Hoagland’s photographs — bathed in hot, pale light, with details emerging from the sepia-toned shadows. From quiet moments of mourning and foreboding to wide vistas of a landscape that has challenged generations, he purposely chose this arid palette to illustrate how the drug war has played out along the border.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Reckoning at the Frontier', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gonzalez, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kickstarter', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ciudad Juarez (Mexico)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tijuana (Mexico)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T10:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Gonzalez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bba6aedb-9aff-55aa-b0f7-48d5b8a20894', 'word_count': 1094, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bba6aedb-9aff-55aa-b0f7-48d5b8a20894'}, {'abstract': 'Why we should care about some guy’s lawn in Phoenix.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/magazine/the-wild-life-of-american-cities.html', 'snippet': 'Why we should care about some guy’s lawn in Phoenix.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of America’s hottest cities and one of its coldest may have more in common than you would guess. In places like Phoenix and Minneapolis, scientists think that cities are starting to look alike in ways that have nothing to do with the proliferation of Starbucks, WalMart or T.G.I Fridays. It has to do with the flowers we plant and the fertilizers we use and the choices we make every spring when we emerge from our apartments and homes and descend on local garden centers. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02eureka/02eureka-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 530, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02eureka/02eureka-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 530}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/1