cument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chandler Buckingham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chandler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckingham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5ad4221-d9e8-5d29-adf5-1bd68fd852d9', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5ad4221-d9e8-5d29-adf5-1bd68fd852d9'}, {'abstract': 'An obsession with fantasy sports is hardly different from other Web-based vices. Many online addictions stem from a desperate need to control an outcome, any outcome.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/when-fantasy-football-becomes-an-addiction/', 'snippet': 'An obsession with fantasy sports is hardly different from other Web-based vices. Many online addictions stem from a desperate need to control an outcome, any outcome.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve played fantasy football since 2006, when I was a 22-year-old cub reporter at The Gazette in Prince George’s County, Md., and the paper’s sports editor asked me to fill in the last spot in the office’s fantasy league. I thought it silly, and that I’d be a natural at the game. After all, I had watched 12 hours of football with my dad every Sunday since I was 10 years old. I knew which players were good. I knew which players weren’t. What else was there to know?', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Fantasy Football Becomes an Addiction', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Treating Fantasy Football Like the Addiction It Can Be', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T11:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By C.D.  Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 