including his mistakes. Alas, menschlichkeit is a rare virtue in modern America, certainly in the political sphere, where nobody ever admits being wrong about anything.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bruce Bartlett Is A Mensch', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T12:01:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcdc0ad7-dbbe-55c1-8b72-b4650ba08cb8', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcdc0ad7-dbbe-55c1-8b72-b4650ba08cb8'}, {'abstract': 'Syrian rebels accused the authorities of launching an airstrike outside the northern city of Idlib on Tuesday, killing at least 20 people as they waited to have their olives turned into oil.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/world/middleeast/civilians-killed-in-syrian-strike-on-olive-press-rebels-say.html', 'snippet': 'Syrian rebels accused the authorities of launching an airstrike outside the northern city of Idlib on Tuesday, killing at least 20 people as they waited to have their olives turned into oil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — Syrian rebels accused the authorities on Tuesday of launching an airstrike on an olive press “filled with people” in fields just outside the northern city of Idlib, killing at least 20 people and wounding 50 as they waited to have their olives turned into oil.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Civilians Killed in Syrian Strike on Olive Press, Rebels Say', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Syrian Rebels Report Many Dead in Airstrike on Olive Press', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T12:13:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hania Mourtada and Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hania', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mourtada', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aada4639-337d-59d1-bf2f-4406125653a3', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aada4639-337d-59d1-bf2f-4406125653a3'}, {'abstract': 'The 0.1 percent versus the 1 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/the-stiffs-and-the-players/', 'snippet': 'The 0.1 percent versus the 1 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The contortions Republicans are going through in an attempt to avoid raising tax rates are quite something, and they pose something of a puzzle: why are they making noises about raising revenue by limiting deductions, while still screaming bloody murder at any hint of a rise in tax rates?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stiffs and the Players', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T12:23:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f090527-6680-5abd-9469-77b8471efb22', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f090527-6680-5abd-9469-77b8471efb22'}, {'abstract': 'Subpoenas for call records of stolen cellphones have allowed the Police Department to store the information of thousands of victims a year.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/daily-report-police-are-amassing-trove-of-cellphone-logs/', 'snippet': 'Subpoenas for call records of stolen cellphones have allowed the Police Department to store the information of thousands of victims a year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When a cellphone is reported stolen in New York, the Police Department routinely subpoenas the phone’s call records, from the day of the theft onward. The logic is simple: if a thief uses the phone, a list of incoming and outgoing calls could lead to the suspect.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/