ws_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Val Haller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Val', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa73296e-cfd2-5b79-87ae-0b9c8f690f30', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa73296e-cfd2-5b79-87ae-0b9c8f690f30'}, {'abstract': 'The Chinese Communist Party’s newspaper reported, accurately, that The Onion has named North Korea’s Leader the Sexiest Man Alive. The word satire was notably absent from the report.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/something-missing-in-chinese-newspapers-entirely-accurate-summary-of-onion-report/', 'snippet': 'The Chinese Communist Party’s newspaper reported, accurately, that The Onion has named North Korea’s Leader the Sexiest Man Alive. The word satire was notably absent from the report.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated | Wednesday, 10:02 a.m. A state-run newspaper in China reported, accurately, that The Onion has named North Korea’s leader its Sexiest Man Alive for 2012. Left unsaid in the report, which was featured on the English-language home page of People’s Daily Online on Tuesday, is whether the editors of the Chinese Communist Party’s newspaper are in on the joke that the American publication is, well, kidding.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/world/asia/27lede_lil/27lede_lil-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 261, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/27/world/asia/27lede_lil/27lede_lil-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 261, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail',