'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/15/world/asia/20121115china-337-slide-dupe-LN/20121115china-337-slide-dupe-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ten Ways to Investigate Transition in China', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T19:58:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tom Marshall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marshall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/49c42096-4ed1-5603-a785-8f6029c24d91', 'word_count': 1541, 'uri': 'nyt://article/49c42096-4ed1-5603-a785-8f6029c24d91'}, {'abstract': 'Soft alligator slip-ons, now with polished-but-tough hardware.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/the-get-hoorsenbuhs-and-newbarks-slippers/', 'snippet': 'Soft alligator slip-ons, now with polished-but-tough hardware.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Those struggling with the decision between buying a Hoorsenbuhs diamond link bracelet and Newbark’s butter-soft alligator slip-ons for a loved one this holiday season can relax. The Los Angeles based jeweler and luxury slipper maker have just teamed up and are now offering six exquisite shoe styles featuring Hoorsenbuhs’s signature polished-but-tough hardware. “We’re big fans of the jewelry,” says Maryam Malakpour, who designs the travel-friendly Newbarks with her sister, Marjan. “It was only a matter of getting it onto our flats.” Made of American alligator — black, white or a combination of both — with the option of silver, yellow, white and rose gold buckles or chains, with diamonds or without, the slippers are meant to be customized. “It’s such a special concept,” says Robert Keith, Hoorsenbuhs’s creative director and founder. “We just want to see something amazing come of it.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Get | Hoorsenbuhs and Newbark’s Slippers', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'If It’s Too Hard to Choose', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T20:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dca01553-f269-5680-8421-4ab0ba16b591', 'word_count': 150, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dca01553-f269-5680-8421-4ab0ba16b591'}, {'abstract': 'When two rich and powerful companies go head to head trying to attract talent, the weapon of choice is usually money. Yet even as Apple and Google strive for dominance in apps, neither seems to be doing much to woo developers.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/apple-and-googles-tax-on-developers/', 'snippet': 'When two rich and powerful companies go head to head trying to attract talent, the weapon of choice is usually money. Yet even as Apple and Google strive for dominance in apps, neither seems to be doing much to woo developers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before Apple sent its iPhone out into the world, anyone with a great idea for a cellphone app had to deal with the carriers — a difficult and time-consuming proposition. Apple simplified the process so a bright high school student could bring an app to market.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apple and Google’s Tax on Developers', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Android (Operating System)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jobs, Steven P', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-27T20:04:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Streitfeld', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Streitfeld', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14cd169a-19b9-5cdc-823b-4543229663d0', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14cd169a-19b9-5cdc-823b-4543229663d0'}, {'abstract': 'It may have taken a year and a corporate spinoff, but ConAgra has succeeded in buying Ralcorp. And it couldn’t have come soon enough for ConAgra’s chief executive, Gary Rodkin, who sees Ralcorp’s strength in private-label brands as an important part of his own company’s future.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/conagras-chief-emphasizes-the-lure-of-private-label-brands/', 'snippet': 'It may have taken a year and a corporate spinoff, but ConAgra has succeeded in buying Ralcorp. And it couldn’t have come soon enough for ConAgra’s chief executive, Gary Rodkin, who sees Ralcorp’s strength in private-label brands as an important pa...', 'lead_paragraph': 'It may have taken a year and a corporate spinoff, but ConAgra has succeeded in buying Ralcorp, for about $5 billion. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'ConAgra’s Chief