'nyt://article/540d98f8-b6ca-5805-9990-a1a89c207968'}, {'abstract': 'Three victims of sexual abuse by a prominent priest filed a lawsuit for damages for what they claim was its negligent handling of their accusations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/world/americas/chile-3-abuse-victims-sue-church.html', 'snippet': 'Three victims of sexual abuse by a prominent priest filed a lawsuit for damages for what they claim was its negligent handling of their accusations.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Three victims of sexual abuse by a prominent priest filed a lawsuit for damages against the Catholic Church on Tuesday for what they claim was its negligent handling of their accusations. In 2004, the men accused the Rev. Fernando Karadima of having abused them as teenagers in the 1980s and 1990s. The archbishop of Santiago opened an investigation but “did not adopt any measures to prevent the abuses from continuing or take disciplinary measures against the priest, who remained in the priesthood for another five years,” the lawsuit says. The Vatican found Father Karadima guilty of abuse last year, and ordered him to a life of seclusion, prayer and penitence. A Chilean judge dismissed criminal charges because a statute of limitations had expired. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chile: 3 Abuse Victims Sue Church', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chile: 3 Abuse Victims Sue Church', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chile', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karadima, Fernando', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Priests', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Abuse and Neglect', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T04:21:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pascale Bonnefoy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pascale', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bonnefoy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff3e1d68-a29e-586c-9562-b1ce53111174', 'word_count': 123, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff3e1d68-a29e-586c-9562-b1ce53111174'}, {'abstract': 'The United States has revived an international effort to stop drug planes from using the tiny Central American country as a way station for cocaine smuggling to the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/world/americas/as-us-shares-intelligence-with-honduras-other-antidrug-aid-stays-frozen.html', 'snippet': 'The United States has revived an international effort to stop drug planes from using the tiny Central American country as a way station for cocaine smuggling to the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The United States has resumed sharing radar intelligence with Honduras after a four-month suspension, reviving an international effort to stop drug planes from using the tiny Central American country as a way station for cocaine smuggling to the United States. American officials said the suspension, which began in August after the Honduran Air Force shot down two suspected drug planes in violation of international law, ended on Nov. 19. Honduran officials revised their procedures, retrained their pilots and confirmed in writing that Honduran authorities would not use American intelligence to “damage, destroy, disable or threaten civilian aircraft,” according to State Department officials. Other forms of security cooperation between the United States and Honduras are still suspended. About $50 million due to be assigned to antidrug and security efforts is being withheld by Democrats in Congress over concerns about American involvement in deadly drug raids this year, and accusations of death squad involvement by senior Honduran police officials. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As U.S. Shares Intelligence with Honduras, Other Antidrug Aid Stays Frozen', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Honduras: As U.S. Shares Radar, Other Antidrug Aid Stays Frozen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Honduras', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T04:34:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Damien Cave', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Damien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cave', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd8f3666-9e81-50de-982b-f2975b9f242c', 'word_count': 157, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd8f3666-9e81-50de-982b-f2975b9f242c'}, {'abstract': 'Three car bombs struck outside Shiite mosques on Tuesday, killing at least 20 people and wounding more than 50.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/world/middleeast/baghdad-bombings-kill-worshipers.html', 'snippet': 'Three car bombs struck outside Shiite mosques on Tuesday, killing at least 20 people and wounding more than 50.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Three car bombs struck outside Shiite mosques in Baghdad on Tuesday, killing at least 20 people and wounding more than 50, the Interior Ministry said. The attacks, during the Shiite holy period of Ashura, occurred in three neighborhoods as worshipers were completing evening prayers. There was no immediate claim of responsibility, but the attacks were similar to others carried out by Al Qaeda in Iraq, a Sunni insurgent group.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Baghdad Bombings Kill Worshipers', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Middle East', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iraq: Bombings Kill Worshipers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T04:41:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Duraid Adnan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Duraid', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adnan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/16d936ab-7036-5f32-9c1f-b0f78835d4fe', 'word_count': 122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16d936ab-7036-5f32-9c1f-b0f78835d4fe'}, {'abstract': 'The mayor of London invites Indian talent to the United Kingdom.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/27/boris-johnson-woos-delhi/', 'snippet': 'The mayor of London invites Indian talent to the United Kingdom.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You know Mayor Boris Johnson of London really wants to win over the crowds here when he compliments India on its air quality.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Boris Johnson Woos Delhi', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 