'nyt://article/f23d2225-9186-52c1-8a25-df4066c90e23', 'word_count': 487, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f23d2225-9186-52c1-8a25-df4066c90e23'}, {'abstract': 'Kareem McKenzie, the offensive lineman known as Big Bear, is applying to postgraduate clinical psychology programs, with the goal of having his own practice to help counsel current and former N.F.L. players.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/a-former-giant-looks-to-share-what-he-learned-on-and-off-the-field/', 'snippet': 'Kareem McKenzie, the offensive lineman known as Big Bear, is applying to postgraduate clinical psychology programs, with the goal of having his own practice to help counsel current and former N.F.L. players.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Many former N.F.L. players find themselves stationed on the couch every Sunday, watching the games on TV and reminiscing about their glory. For the former Giants offensive lineman Kareem McKenzie, however, the weekends are generally used for an altogether different pursuit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Former Giant Looks to Share What He Learned On and Off the Field', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKenzie, Kareem', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T11:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e089a2be-6d09-57be-92d0-cc63c6b6ae26', 'word_count': 599, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e089a2be-6d09-57be-92d0-cc63c6b6ae26'}, {'abstract': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at the latest happenings in Washington.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/the-early-word-no-satisfaction/', 'snippet': 'Political news from today’s Times and around the Web, plus a look at the latest happenings in Washington.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today’s Times', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Early Word: No Satisfaction', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T11:00:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b9fc53f0-01f6-58ec-9908-4f14b37293d4', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b9fc53f0-01f6-58ec-9908-4f14b37293d4'}, {'abstract': 'When your children become adults it becomes impossible to protect them from setbacks, and that feels awful.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/booming/when-theyre-grown-the-real-pain-begins.html', 'snippet': 'When your children become adults it becomes impossible to protect them from setbacks, and that feels awful.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was 24 years old, I brought my firstborn son, 3-week-old Jacob, to my childhood home on the Eastern End of Long Island to meet his grandparents. When I arrived, an old family friend and neighbor, Cora Stevens, happened to be sitting in my parents’ kitchen. Cora, a mother to five grown children and grandmother to seven, grabbed tiny Jake, put her face right up to his and started speaking loud baby talk to him. Then, as she bounced him on her knee, she turned to me and said, “When they’re little they sit on your lap; when they’re big they sit on your heart.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/28/style/28mystory-booming-olderkids/28mystory-booming-olderkids-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 374, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/28/style/28mystory-booming-olderkids/28mystory-booming-olderkids-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 374}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/28/style/28mystory-booming-olderkids/28mystory-booming-olderkids-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 639, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/28/style/28mystory-booming-olderkids/28mystory-booming-olderkids-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1277, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/28/style/28mystory-booming-olderkids/28mystory-booming-olderkids-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/28/style/28mystory-booming-olderkids/28mystory-booming-olderkids-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/28/style/28mystory-booming-olderkids/28mystory-booming-olderkids-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When They’re Grown, the Real Pain Begins', 'kicker': 'My Story', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T11:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Engel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Engel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6b22fab-e8ff-5af2-bc01-699274e60948', 'word_count': 1042, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6b22fab-e8ff-5af2-bc01-699274e60948'}, {'abstract': 'A movie about the bloody overthrow of the despotic Qin dynasty 2,200 years ago was delayed until after the November party congress. But a film noir about the life of a present-day Beijing cop had a smoother passage.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/the-chinese-censors-peculiar-movie-reviews/', 'snippet': 'A movie about the bloody overthrow of the despotic Qin dynasty 2,200 years ago was delayed until after the November party congress. But a film noir about the life of a present-day Beijing cop had a smoother passage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — China’s censorship of films, books and the Internet is far-reaching and persnickety. It can also be puzzling. Why do some movies pass and others don’t? Sometimes, it may merely be a question of timing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/28/blogs/ihtrdv1128-china-film/ihtrdv1128-china-film-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 721, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/28/blogs/ihtrdv1128-china-film/ihtrdv1128-china-film-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1442, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/28/blogs/ihtrdv1128-china-film/ihtrdv1128-china-film-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/28/blogs/ihtrdv1128-china-film/ihtrdv1128-china-film-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/28/blogs/ihtrdv1128-china-film/ihtrdv1128-china-film-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Chinese Censors’ Peculiar Movie Reviews', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lu Chuan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Last Supper (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T11:43:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Didi Kirsten Tatlow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Didi', 'middlename': 'Kirsten', 'lastname': 'Tatlow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2615dbf0-430a-5c84-bf97-ac4528bb2bb6', 'word_count': 547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2615dbf0-430a-5c84-bf97-ac4528bb2bb6'}, {'abstract': 'A new study suggests older patients are undergoing too many diagnostic tests — the kind that help doctors figure out what is wrong.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/doctors-orders-another-test/', 'snippet': 'A new study suggests older patients are undergoing too many diagnostic tests — the kind that help doctors figure out what is wrong.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is no longer news that Americans, and older Americans in particular, get more routine screening tests than they need, more than are useful. Prostate tests for men over 75, annual Pap smears for women over 65 and colonoscopies for anyone over 75 — all are overused, large-scale studies have shown.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Doctor’s Orders? Another Test', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T11:48:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paula Span', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paula', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Span', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7836144-1ee8-5c2e-ad82-717ef1884088', 'word_count': 869, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7836144-1ee8-5c2e-ad82-717ef1884088'}, {'abstract': 'The little creature of the sea that appears to debunk the most fundamental law of the natural world: you are born, and then you die.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/magazine/can-a-jellyfish-unlock-the-secret-of-immortality.html', 'snippet': 'The little creature of the sea that appears to debunk the most fundamental law of the natural world: you are born, and then you die.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After more than 4,000 years — almost since the dawn of recorded time, when Utnapishtim told Gilgamesh that the secret to immortality lay in a coral found on the ocean floor — man finally discovered eternal life in 1988. He found it, in fact, on the ocean floor. The discovery was made unwittingly by Christian Sommer, a German marine-biology student in his early 20s. He was spending the summer in Rapallo, a small city on the Italian Riviera, where exactly one century earlier Friedrich Nietzsche conceived “Thus Spoke Zarathustra”: “Everything goes, everything comes back; eternally rolls the wheel of being. Everything dies, everything blossoms again. . . .”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '32', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02jellyfish1/02jellyfish1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 487, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02jellyfish1/02jellyfish1-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 487}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02jellyfish1/02jellyfish1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 985, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02jellyfish1/02jellyfish1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1443, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02jellyfish1/02jellyfish1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02jellyfish1/02jellyfish1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02jellyfish1/02jellyfish1-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can a Jellyfish Unlock the Secret of Immortality?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Forever and Ever', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jellyfish', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kubota, Shin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T12:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nathaniel Rich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nathaniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/819d2b7b-f544-59f4-b1cf-9d4ab8e3e760', 'word_count': 6570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/819d2b7b-f544-59f4-b1cf-9d4ab8e3e760'}, {'abstract': 'Taking a cue from Silicon Valley, nonprofits are learning to use their failures as an integral part of the process of innovation and, ultimately, progress.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/the-power-of-failure-2/', 'snippet': 'Taking a cue from Silicon Valley, nonprofits are learning to use their failures as an integral part of the process of innovation and, ultimately, progress.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seven years ago, the consulting group Bridgespan presented details on the performance of several prestigious nonprofits. Nearly all of them had one thing in common — failure.\xa0 These organizations had a point at which they struggled financially, stalled on a project or experienced high rates of attrition.\xa0 “Everyone in the room had the same response, which was relief,” said Paul Schmitz, the chief executive of the nonprofit Public Allies.\xa0 “It was good to see that I wasn’t the only one struggling with these things.”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Power of Failure', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Power Of Failure', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nonprofit Organizations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Playworks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'World Bank', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T12:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarika Bansal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarika', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bansal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a69c09bb-b6b7-5118-9cfb-07cbf3c8182b', 'word_count': 1446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a69c09bb-b6b7-5118-9cfb-07cbf3c8182b'}, {'abstract': 'Dealflicks, says one of its founders, wants to do for movie tickets what Priceline.com and Hotwire.com do for travel bookings.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/an-unsexy-company-tries-to-fill-movie-seats/', 'snippet': 'Dealflicks, says one of its founders, wants to do for movie tickets what Priceline.com and Hotwire.com do for travel bookings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sean Wycliffe knew “The King’s Speech” had gotten rave reviews. So when he arrived at the theater about two years ago, he was surprised by what he saw. “It was a great movie, but the place was just empty,” he said. “It had 230 or 240 seats, but there was me and a couple and that was it.”', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An ‘Unsexy’ Start-Up Tries to Fill Movie Seats', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Looking to Fill\r\nMovie Seats', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T12:00:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Bruder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46606b1c-65ce-5194-a33a-6b2019c1e153', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46606b1c-65ce-5194-a33a-6b2019c1e153'}, {'abstract': 'Screen saver programs can serve up information on the computer’s monitor when you are not using the machine.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/tip-of-the-week-idle-time-learning/', 'snippet': 'Screen saver programs can serve up information on the computer’s monitor when you are not using the machine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Screen saver programs, originally intended to help prevent damage to older computer monitors, are often used now for security or entertainment purposes when the computer is idle. Most operating systems include a few built-in screen savers that display animated geometric patterns or photos, but you do not have to go too far to find screen savers that educate as well.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip of the Week: Screen Savers That Educate', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Monitors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desktop Computers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T12:03:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e739a68-991d-52ba-89a3-c09704e137a9', 'word_count': 271, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e739a68-991d-52ba-89a3-c09704e137a9'}, {'abstract': 'Bill George, a Harvard business professor, contends that after a decade of turmoil at the company, it is time to split Hewlett-Packard into two businesses: enterprise systems and computer hardware.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/to-save-hewlett-packard-break-it-in-two/', 'snippet': 'Bill George, a Harvard business professor, contends that after a decade of turmoil at the company, it is time to split Hewlett-Packard into two businesses: enterprise systems and computer hardware.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bill George is a professor of management practice at Harvard Business School and a former chairman and chief executive of Medtronic.Hewlett-Packard’s $8.8 billion write-down of its Autonomy acquisition is just the latest evidence of the steady decline of one of the world’s great companies. Don’t blame Meg Whitman, the chief executive. She is just cleaning up the messes created since 1999 by her three predecessors and H.P.’s strategically challenged board of directors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To Save H.P., Break It in Two', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Enterprise Computing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Apotheker, Leo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fiorina, Carleton S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'George, Bill', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hurd, Mark V', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Packard, David (1912-96)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Whitman, Margaret C', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hewlett-Packard Company', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T12:24:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill George', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'George', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a0c45f29-9607-5e8c-9b13-ce93fa46e6f5', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a0c45f29-9607-5e8c-9b13-ce93fa46e6f5'}, {'abstract': 'During the N.F.L.’s Week 12, the Jets’ biggest cheerleader quit, and two Colts cheerleaders shaved their heads for a cause.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/jets-and-colts-jeers-and-cheerleaders/', 'snippet': 'During the N.F.L.’s Week 12, the Jets’ biggest cheerleader quit, and two Colts cheerleaders shaved their heads for a cause.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s be kind and call these two videos a contrast in outlooks. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jets and Colts: Jeers and Cheerleaders', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ANZALONE, EDWIN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indianapolis Colts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T12:28:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bea74816-550d-5669-8071-88b78077d139', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bea74816-550d-5669-8071-88b78077d139'}, {'abstract': 'The expedition is over. Now comes the analysis. If scientists can use the record of past mountain glaciation to determine how past climate changes have influenced mountain snowline altitudes, it will be easier to anticipate how rapidly the snowpack will diminish with future warming.', 'web_url': 'https://scientistatwork.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/bidding-farewell-to-the-jewel-of-the-lotus/', 'snippet': 'The expedition is over. Now comes the analysis. If scientists can use the record of past mountain glaciation to determine how past climate changes have influenced mountain snowline altitudes, it will be easier to anticipate how rapidly the snowpac...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Aaron Putnam, a postdoctoral researcher at the Lamont-Doherty Earth Observatory of Columbia University, led an expedition to Bhutan to examine links among climate, glaciers and water resources in the Himalaya. This is his final post.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bidding Farewell to the Jewel of the Lotus', 'kicker': 'Scientist at Work', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Geology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Glaciers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Putnam, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bhutan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T12:35:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aaron Putnam', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aaron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Putnam', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c382169c-751c-52e0-8921-bad562ccd7f6', 'word_count': 1362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c382169c-751c-52e0-8921-bad562ccd7f6'}, {'abstract': 'The initial offering of the Russian cellphone operator MegaFon raised $1.7 billion, making it Europe’s second-largest offering this year, but the market seem unimpressed on the first day of trading.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/shares-in-russias-megafon-fall-after-i-p-o/', 'snippet': 'The initial offering of the Russian cellphone operator MegaFon raised $1.7 billion, making it Europe’s second-largest offering this year, but the market seem unimpressed on the first day of trading.', 'lead_paragraph': '12:08 p.m. | Updated ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'MegaFon Shares Fall After I.P.O.', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'MegaFon Shares Decline After a $1.7 Billion I.P.O.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MegaFon', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T12:37:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer and Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e4cdc849-e153-5b7f-8160-249619594afe', 'word_count': 827, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e4cdc849-e153-5b7f-8160-249619594afe'}, {'abstract': 'Bill Murray, the free-form actor who’s starring as Franklin D. Roosevelt in the coming film “Hyde Park on Hudson,” takes an interviewer on an adventure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/movies/bill-murray-star-of-hyde-park-on-hudson.html', 'snippet': 'Bill Murray, the free-form actor who’s starring as Franklin D. Roosevelt in the coming film “Hyde Park on Hudson,” takes an interviewer on an adventure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHAT do we still want from Bill Murray? His unpremeditated film career — in which he has parlayed performances as the happy-go-lucky heroes of 1980s-era slapstick into the existentially uncertain leading men of thoughtful comedies like “Groundhog Day,” “Rushmore” and “Lost in Translation” — would seem to be sufficient. Yet we demand more from this 62-year-old actor, on whose rugged face a playful smirk and a contemplative gaze look equally at home, and he appears happy to give it to us in his life beyond the screen. Tracking his movements in the wild, as he crashes karaoke parties and kickball games, has become an online pastime; Mr. Murray himself has become the folkloric equivalent of a leprechaun or fairy godparent, popping up at unpredictable yet opportune moments.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02MURRY_SPAN/02MURRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 391, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02MURRY_SPAN/02MURRY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 391}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02MURRY_SPAN/02MURRY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 634, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02MURRY_SPAN/02MURRY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1267, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02MURRY_SPAN/02MURRY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thum