ts way to its most profitable year, but in the United States it suffers from poor ratings, layers of management and little sense of direction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: CNN’s Identity Crisis and ‘Downton Abbey’ in New York', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fellowes, Julian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kim Jong-un', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lorre, Chuck', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zucker, Jeff', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CNN', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Times', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Broadcasting Co', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "People's Daily", 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Politico, The', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Thomson Reuters Corporation', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'TMZ.com', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T14:32:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dadd8d85-8fcd-5487-8a78-ccfea710b6ee', 'word_count': 397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dadd8d85-8fcd-5487-8a78-ccfea710b6ee'}, {'abstract': 'The year-long festival of 20th-century music is inspired by Alex Ross’s 2007 book.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/music-critic-alex-ross-inspires-a-festival/', 'snippet': 'The year-long festival of 20th-century music is inspired by Alex Ross’s 2007 book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — First there was the book. Now there is the festival. The Southbank Center announced the details Tuesday morning of the Rest Is Noise, a yearlong festival of 20th-century music inspired by Alex Ross’s 2007 book of the same name. Mr. Ross, a music critic for The New Yorker, won considerable acclaim for “The Rest Is Noise,” a sweeping survey of 20th-century classical music.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From a Book Springs a Music Festival', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ross, Alex', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Yorker', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T14:35:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roslyn Suclas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roslyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Suclas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dffae6d0-d291-569b-9078-69e3ce6e9a10', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dffae6d0-d291-569b-9078-69e3ce6e9a10'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/what-were-reading-575/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dinner A Love Story: Hellmann’s mayonnaise. Goya black beans. Maldon sea salt. Domino sugar. Why you ought to have these items in your pantry. (The folks at Bon Appétit approve.) — Jeff Gordinier ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T14:41:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/54403201-340e-5204-8412-0e1722ea9e4d', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54403201-340e-5204-8412-0e1722ea9e4d'}, {'abstract': 'Adolescents from households headed by a low-income worker are more likely to drop out of school, to be obese, and to take on adult roles too young.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/how-children-subsidize-low-low-prices/', 'snippet': 'Adolescents from households headed by a low-income worker are more likely to drop out of school, to be obese, and to take on adult roles too young.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A significant part of our national antipoverty strategy over the past few decades has been increasing employment — moving people, particularly mothers, off “welfare rolls” and into jobs. But the types of jobs available to most lower-income parents, mothers and fathers alike, are low-wage jobs that present their own problems to those trying to\xa0 support and raise a family. The lack of benefits, the inflexible hours and the often nonstandard shifts exacerbate the low pay and create a situation in which parents don’t have the time they want and need to spend with their children or the money to find high-quality substitutes (like activities and child care) for that time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Children Subsidize ‘Low, Low Prices’', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Workplace Environment', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T14:50:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f35d9e4-783d-5225-a709-38b71c6b9005', 'word_count': 733, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f35d9e4-783d-5225-a709-38b71c6b9005'}, {'abstract': 'The American, Alan Gross, has spent three years in prison for taking part in a State Department program that Cuba considers illegal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/29/world/americas/for-alan-gross-imprisoned-in-cuba-suit-against-us-is-part-of-new-strategy.html', 'snippet': 'The American, Alan Gross, has spent three years in prison for taking part in a State Department program that Cuba considers illegal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — The Cuban government says he is perfectly fine. His lawyers say he could soon die of cancer. After nearly three years, the drama of Alan Gross, an American contractor imprisoned in Cuba nearly three years ago, has come down to this: a battle over a bulge of tissue on his shoulder.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'American Held in Cuba Takes Aim at Impasse', 'kicker': None, 'cont