ven Madison Park Drops Its Minimalist Grid Menu', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guidara, Will', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Humm, Daniel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Eleven Madison Park (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T18:08:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/258e0840-1ba4-5db8-b81f-adfa4cf1e543', 'word_count': 270, 'uri': 'nyt://article/258e0840-1ba4-5db8-b81f-adfa4cf1e543'}, {'abstract': 'After a rocky start on social media, The Times’s Jerusalem bureau chief will work closely with an editor on her posts.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/problems-with-a-reporters-facebook-posts-and-a-possible-solution/', 'snippet': 'After a rocky start on social media, The Times’s Jerusalem bureau chief will work closely with an editor on her posts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Start with a reporter who likes to be responsive to readers, is spontaneous and impressionistic in her personal writing style, and not especially attuned to how casual comments may be received in a highly politicized setting.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Problems With a Reporter’s Facebook Posts, and a Possible Solution', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T18:11:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebc24c69-52d3-57da-85ed-84775af1f9c8', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebc24c69-52d3-57da-85ed-84775af1f9c8'}, {'abstract': 'A German painter’s Civil War vision for the Gateway City as the heart of a reunited nation.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/28/meet-america-in-st-louis/', 'snippet': 'A German painter’s Civil War vision for the Gateway City as the heart of a reunited nation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though the sesquicentennial of his death will go largely unremarked, the German-American artist Carl Wimar died on Nov. 28, 1862, succumbing to the ravages of tuberculosis. Hardly recognized today, Wimar, like his mentor Emanuel Leutze, was an immigrant painter who found the promise and character of the American nation the perfect subject, even as that nation was being torn apart by war. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Meet America in St. Louis', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leutze, Emanuel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jefferson National Expansion Memorial', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'St Louis (Mo)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T18:20:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', '