oceanfront home at the end of Beach 137th Street in the Rockaways, the house became a repository of his half-century musical career as a singer, songwriter and producer.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/nyregion/28cityroom-vance/28cityroom-vance-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/nyregion/28cityroom-vance/28cityroom-vance-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/nyregion/28cityroom-vance/28cityroom-vance-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/29/nyregion/28cityroom-vance/28cityroom-vance-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/nyregion/28cityroom-vance/28cityroom-vance-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Artifacts of a Music Career, Gone With the Storm’s Roar', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Music Career’s Artifacts, Gathered Over Decades, Are Lost in One Storm', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rockaway Peninsula (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-28T22:27:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3dbc53ac-6ae4-582b-947b-d5d00608df3c', 'word_count': 781, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3dbc53ac-6ae4-582b-947b-d5d00608df3c'}, {'abstract': 'The work of the artist Agnes Denes, in all its variety, is being introduced to new audiences in shows on both coasts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/arts/design/agnes-denes-stretches-the-canvas-as-far-as-can-go.html', 'snippet': 'The work of the artist Agnes Denes, in all its variety, is being introduced to new audiences in shows on both coasts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AS a child in Budapest in the 1930s Agnes Denes decided she would be a poet, but history got in the way. She and her parents survived the Nazi occupation of Hungary, moved to Sweden after the war and then to the United States a few years later, when she was in her teens. Along the way “I lost my language because we traveled so much,” Ms. Denes, 81, said in a recent interview. So she became a visual artist instead. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02DENES_SPAN/02DENES_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02DENES_SPAN/02DENES_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02DENES_SPAN/02DENES1-jumbo.jpg', 'he