d by Georg Jensen is finally realized in silver.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1926, the Danish silversmith Georg Jensen designed a fruit and flower bowl decorated with acorns and oak leaves. The piece, which was never produced, was known as a jardinière. It embodied an Art Nouveau style that was giving way to the geometric abstractions of Art Deco, speculated Anne Mette Müller-Krogstrup, the head of the silver department at Georg Jensen Inc. And besides, Mr. Jensen was busy hobnobbing in Jazz Age Paris as well as attending to his many commissions.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/garden/29GOODS/29CURR1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 869, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/garden/29GOODS/29CURR1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1738, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/garden/29GOODS/29CURR1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/29/garden/29GOODS/29CURR1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/garden/29GOODS/29CURR1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Jensen Bowl Gets a Third Dimension', 'kicker': 'Currents | Goods', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jensen Bowl Gets a 3rd Dimension', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Silver',