': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T02:52:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dddabc8a-52a9-5355-8d5e-03723c8fcf21', 'word_count': 669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dddabc8a-52a9-5355-8d5e-03723c8fcf21'}, {'abstract': 'An unfocused argument from a Louisiana lawyer put the Supreme Court justices in a lighthearted mood, as they tried to puzzle out whether her client’s sentence must stand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/29/us/justices-consider-lawyers-misstep-and-find-more.html', 'snippet': 'An unfocused argument from a Louisiana lawyer put the Supreme Court justices in a lighthearted mood, as they tried to puzzle out whether her client’s sentence must stand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Supreme Court heard an unfocused argument on Wednesday from a Louisiana lawyer whose fumble at her client’s sentencing hearing was at issue in the case. The lawyer’s halting presentation put the justices in a collaborative and lighthearted mood, as they tried to puzzle out for themselves whether the sentence must stand. Unicorns figured in their analysis.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/us/SCOTUS/SCOTUS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 696, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/us/SCOTUS/SCOTUS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1602, 'width': 1089, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'cap