one, 'lastname': 'Werschkul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Channon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'Vijai', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Singh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/cb6a00a0-1eff-5e8c-bd76-f3823daacf03', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/cb6a00a0-1eff-5e8c-bd76-f3823daacf03'}, {'abstract': 'Audi hopes that American buyers will let go of the decades-old perception that diesels are smoky beasts.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/29/audi-diesels-changing-perceptions/', 'snippet': 'Audi hopes that American buyers will let go of the decades-old perception that diesels are smoky beasts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Introduced on Wednesday: 2014 Audi A6, A7, A8, Q5 and 2013 Q7 TDI models', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Audi Diesels: Changing Perceptions', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Los Angeles Auto Show', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Audi Division of Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T16:54:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tori Tellem', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tori', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tellem', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e164ba6c-d0fa-5c3b-a37d-fa47d260f3a4', 'word_count': 212, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e164ba6c-d0fa-5c3b-a37d-fa47d260f3a4'}, {'abstract': 'Is this fall’s big historical film unfair to radicalism?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/29/spielbergs-lincoln-and-its-critics/', 'snippet': 'Is this fall’s big historical film unfair to radicalism?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the wide-ranging online conversation about Steven Spielberg and Tony Kushner’s “Lincoln,” it’s been interesting to watch the movie be praised and criticized for the same artistic choice: Its determinedly narrow focus on the month or so of politicking and debate that pushed the 13th Amendment through a reluctant House of Representatives. To the film’s fans — my op-ed colleague David Brooks and A.O. Scott are in-house examples, but there are many others — this narrowing brings the democratic process to life in a way that few political movies ever have, capturing “the squalor and vigor, the glory and corruption of the Republic in action,” as Scott puts it, and throwing Lincoln’s distinctive balance of provisional cynicism and ultimate idealism into sharp relief. To its\xa0various\xa0detractors,\xa0this narrowing is a betrayal of the true story of emancipation, which was actually settled on the ground more than in the halls of Congress, through the agency of slaves as much as by the votes of politicians, and which failed to deliver fully on its promises (thanks to Reconstruction’s failures, and Jim Crow’s ascendance) precisely because of the kind of compromises that “Lincoln” the film treats as the better part of political valor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spielberg’s “Lincoln” and Its Critics', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T16:59:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4ea8c3a-bfb0-592b-ada6-120b0dd4377f', 'word_count': 1602, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4ea8c3a-bfb0-592b-ada6-120b0dd4377f'}, {'abstract': 'As with medicine, rigorous studies of social programs can produce important, credible evidence about what works, illuminating a path to major progress, a policy expert says.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/29/applying-evidence-to-social-programs/', 'snippet': 'As with medicine, rigorous studies of social programs can produce important, credible evidence about what works, illuminating a path to major progress, a policy expert says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In response to our recent series of posts on stagnant incomes for the middle class and poor, Jon Baron — president of the Coalition for Evidence-Based Policy, an unaligned research group in Washington — argues that better functioning government programs could help.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Applying Evidence to Social Programs', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T17:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Baron', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Baron', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01c525fb-0fd3-5c12-a438-fa1de65aa12c', 'word_count': 852, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01c525fb-0fd3-5c12-a438-fa1de65aa12c'}, {'abstract': 'My parents are housekeepers. I never expected to date the son of one of their clients.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/29/a-messy-relationship/', 'snippet': 'My parents are housekeepers. I never expected to date the son of one of their clients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One November morning, four years ago, I received a call from my mother. Before I could say hello, she blurted her news: she’d just been at a client’s house. Their son, Douglas, was visiting, and was even more handsome than when she’d seen him last. And she had his e-mail address. Did I have a pen ready?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Messy Relationship', 'kicker': 'Townies', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Messy Relationship', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T17:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Titi Nguyen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Titi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nguyen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/725a3613-a029-5823-b678-493ece375b7b', 'word_count': 1266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/725a3613-a029-5823-b678-493ece375b7b'}, {'abstract': 'A photographer documents London’s most familiar landmarks to look as if they have been freshly rendered by an architect.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/29/london-underground-olivo-barbieri-gets-site-specific/', 'snippet': 'A photographer documents London’s most familiar landmarks to look as if they have been freshly rendered by an architect.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Italian photographer Olivo Barbieri is on a mission. For the last decade he has documented dozens of cities around the world, from Rome to Las Vegas, as part of an continuing project to define the shape of the contemporary city. Barbieri has now turned his attention to London in “site specific_LONDON 12,” an exhibition of new work that opens tomorrow at the Ronchini Gallery in Mayfair.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'London Underground | Olivo Barbieri Gets Site Specific', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barbieri, Olivo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APERTURE FOUNDATION', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T17:04:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elias Redstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elias', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Redstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c030539-9527-5c21-8aaf-b402916da50a', 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c030539-9527-5c21-8aaf-b402916da50a'}, {'abstract': 'A friendship between two young women spawned laughter, a reality television spin-off idea and a plan to reach out to young adult cancer patients.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/29/life-interrupted-real-housewives-of-chemotherapy/', 'snippet': 'A friendship between two young women spawned laughter, a reality television spin-off idea and a plan to reach out to young adult cancer patients.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am never happy to be in the oncology waiting room. But when I first saw Kristen Howard sitting across from me at Mount Sinai Hospital, I could barely contain my excitement. Since my diagnosis with leukemia two years ago at age 22, I’ve learned that it is a rare sight to see young people in the hallways of an adult oncology unit. Kristen’s bald head confirmed that she, too, was a cancer patient. I beamed at her from beneath my face mask, with a strange sense of relief to see another young woman who looked like me. I knew we had to meet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/health/29suleika/29suleika-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 534, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/health/29suleika/29suleika-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1001, 'width': 1920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/health/29suleika/29suleika-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/29/health/29suleika/29suleika-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/health/29suleika/29suleika-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life, Interrupted: Real Housewives of Chemotherapy', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chemotherapy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Leukemia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T17:05:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suleika Jaouad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suleika', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jaouad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e06eaa46-b298-569e-9ba7-94e2a00ff39a', 'word_count': 547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e06eaa46-b298-569e-9ba7-94e2a00ff39a'}, {'abstract': 'Internet access disappeared across Syria on Thursday, and airports were closed, prompting antigovernment activists to warn that the authorities might be planning to escalate their crackdown.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/29/internet-outage-reported-across-syria/', 'snippet': 'Internet access disappeared across Syria on Thursday, and airports were closed, prompting antigovernment activists to warn that the authorities might be planning to escalate their crackdown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last Updated, 5:37 p.m. Internet access disappeared across Syria on Thursday, and commercial air traffic was halted, prompting antigovernment activists to warn that the authorities might be planning to escalate their crackdown against the country’s raging uprising. Only residents with their own satellite connections to the Internet could access the Web, activists said. Disruptions to phone service were also reported. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Internet Shutdown Reported Across Syria', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T17:09:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Barnard and Robert Mackey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mackey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd45c91c-4ec3-5f8f-b6e9-42fa863ca897', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd45c91c-4ec3-5f8f-b6e9-42fa863ca897'}, {'abstract': 'R. J. Dill started 33 games at Maryland before using an N.C.A.A. rule that permits graduate transfers to be eligible to play sports immediately under certain conditions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/sports/ncaafootball/rj-dills-transfer-to-rutgers-was-more-than-a-football-move.html', 'snippet': 'R. J. Dill started 33 games at Maryland before using an N.C.A.A. rule that permits graduate transfers to be eligible to play sports immediately under certain conditions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PISCATAWAY, N.J. — R. J. Dill, the 310-pound right tackle who added heft and savvy to the Rutgers offensive line, continues to say that football was only the second most important reason he transferred from Maryland a year ago.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/sports/30scarlet1/30scarlet1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 378, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/30/sports/30scarlet1/30scarlet1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 378}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',