b_date': '2012-11-29T17:55:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jerry Garrett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jerry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garrett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75259dd4-f070-5556-9a56-fb40bcb2c426', 'word_count': 253, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75259dd4-f070-5556-9a56-fb40bcb2c426'}, {'abstract': 'With clear skies, a Himalayan horizon, culture and history, this Yunnan city has emerged as a new favorite of foreign visitors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/travel/36-hours-in-lijiang-china.html', 'snippet': 'With clear skies, a Himalayan horizon, culture and history, this Yunnan city has emerged as a new favorite of foreign visitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE word “breathtaking” when applied to a Chinese city too often refers to respiratory-attack-inducing smog. But in the case of Lijiang, population 1.2 million, in the southwestern province of Yunnan, the word takes on its slack-jaw-in-awe meaning. The horizon here is Himalayan, and the blue sky above the city’s stone streets, willow-lined canals and black-tiled roofs is reliably visible. This unusual (for China) troika of culture, history and natural beauty is why Lijiang’s Old Town — a Unesco World Heritage site and a center of the Naxi people, one of China’s most vibrant ethnic minorities — is a top destination for Chinese tourists and a new favorite of the passport-carrying crowd. So giddy-making are Lijiang’s offerings that the Old Town is filled with such vaguely fortune-cookie-like signs as “Mountains and rivers will be your friends and you will be with good reputation as a civilized tourist.” Rough translation: Behave!', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/travel/02HOURS1_SPAN/02HOURS1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 455, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/12/02/travel/02HOURS1_SPAN/02HOURS1_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 455}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/travel/02HOURS1_SPAN/02HOURS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 777, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/travel/02HOURS1_SPAN/02HOURS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1553, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/travel/02HOURS1_SPAN/02HOURS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/travel/02HOURS1_SPAN/02HOURS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/travel/02HOURS1_SPAN/02HOURS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Lijiang, China', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lijiang, China', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T17:57:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeannie Ralston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeannie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ralston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fc8209a-08ec-54ec-be70-ceb914061069', 'word_count': 1458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fc8209a-08ec-54ec-be70-ceb914061069'}, {'abstract': 'Munch on deep-fried dragonflies and browse for bells in this historic Chinese city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/11/29/travel/02-lijiang-slide-show.html', 'snippet': 'Munch on deep-fried dragonflies and browse for bells in this historic Chinese city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Munch on deep-fried dragonflies and browse for bells in this historic Chinese city.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/travel/02-lijiang-slide-show-slide-3UMV/02-lijiang-slide-show-slide-3UMV-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/travel/02-lijiang-slide-show-slide-3UMV/02-lijiang-slide-show-slide-3UMV-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/travel/02-lijiang-slide-show-slide-3UMV/02-lijiang-slide-show-slide-3UMV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/29/travel/02-lijiang-slide-show-slide-3UMV/02-lijiang-slide-show-slide-3UMV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/29/travel/02-lijiang-slide-show-slide-3UMV/02-lijiang-slide-show-slide-3UMV-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Snacks and Shops in Lijiang', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Yunnan Province (China)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T17:57:33+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/bae661c6-d6d6-5ab0-a6cf-a960e2a5a529', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/bae661c6-d6d6-5ab0-a6cf-a960e2a5a529'}, {'abstract': 'The National Park Service has waived admission for 11 days in 2013, 6 fewer than this year.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/29/national-parks-free-days-will-come-less-often/', 'snippet': 'The National Park Service has waived admission for 11 days in 2013, 6 fewer than this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The National Park Service has waived admission for several days each year over the last decade, helping to drive up attendance at its 398 parks and offering a boon to private concessionaires like hotels and bookshops in the parks, which see an uptick in reservations and purchases on free days. At the same time, the service itself has lost revenue — about $700,000 to $1 million per free day — according to David Barna, chief spokesman for the National Park Service.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'National Parks’ Free Days Will Come Less Often', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Parks, Monuments and Seashores', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Park Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-29T18:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Brennan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brennan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3cbf662-03b9-5e53-96c6-53f3b5a021ac', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3cbf662-03b9-5e53-96c6-53f3b5a021ac'}, {'abstract': 'Hurricane Sandy crippled treatment plants in New York and New Jersey, revealing problems in the region’s wastewater infrastructure that could take several years and billions of dollars to fix.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/nyregion/sewage-flows-after-hurricane-sandy-exposing-flaws-in-system.html', 'snippet': 'Hurricane Sandy crippled treatment plants in New York and New Jersey, revealing problems in the region’s wastewater infrastructure that could take several years and billions of dollars to fix.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EAST ROCKAWAY, N.Y. — The water flowing out of the Bay Park sewage plant here in Nassau County is a greenish-gray soup of partially treated human waste, a sign of an environmental and public health disaster that officials say will be one of the most enduring and expensive effects of Hurricane Sandy. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/nyregion/30sewage-span/30sewage-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/30/nyregion/30sewage-span/30sewage-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 372}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption':