planning for any and all contingencies, do not want to give Congress any ideas about programs they see as expendable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gen. Dwight D. Eisenhower famously said that plans are nothing, but planning is everything because no war plan survives first contact with the enemy. Therefore, the military should hone its ability for continuous planning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No ‘Plan B’ for Pentagon in Case of Big Budget Cut', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'National Debt (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T05:43:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thom Shanker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thom', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shanker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5908a54b-9c6e-59cf-bbe1-610ec9237b2f', 'word_count': 568, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5908a54b-9c6e-59cf-bbe1-610ec9237b2f'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/arts/television/whats-on-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '2:20 P.M. (Starz) ORANGES AND SUNSHINE (2011) Emily Watson, above with Hugo Weaving, portrays Margaret Humphreys, a social worker in Nottingham, England, who, starting in 1986, exposed the deportation of as many as 130,000 children from orphanages and group homes in Britain to Australia between the 1940s and 1967. One deportee, now an adult, recalls being told at 10 that he was being sent to a sunny paradise where he could pick oranges off trees. Instead, many of the youngsters found themselves forced into hard labor and enduring physical cruelty and sexual abuse at places like the Bindoon Boys Town, presided over by the Christian Brothers, a Roman Catholic order. The victims were informed that their parents were dead, while their parents — mainly unwed mothers deemed unfit — were told that their children had been placed for adoption in better homes. “This film presents a chilling portrait of bureaucratic stonewalling and denial as Margaret presents her evidence to politicians who meet her accusations with indifference and skepticism, express only a vague regret and assume no responsibility,” Stephen Holden wrote in The New York Times about this drama, directed by Jim Loach from Ms. Humphreys’s 1994 book, “Empty Cradles.” “When she eventually faces a group of clergymen at Bindoon, she is met with absolute silence.” He added: “Rona Munro’s screenplay for ‘Oranges and Sunshine’ is unnecessarily flighty. As the story ricochets between Britain and Australia, the film often loses track of time and becomes fragmented as it struggles to integrate too many subplots. What holds it together is Ms. Watson’s calm, sturdy performance.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T06:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/61a95b24-4176-586a-9e46-1dc5a4576fce', 'word_count': 738, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61a95b24-4176-586a-9e46-1dc5a4576fce'}, {'abstract': 'A Moscow court ruled that videos of performances by the punk group were extremist, falling under a law intended to rein in hate speech.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/world/europe/russian-court-seeks-to-ban-pussy-riots-videos.html', 'snippet': 'A Moscow court ruled that videos of performances by the punk group were extremist, falling under a law intended to rein in hate speech.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A Moscow court on Thursday ruled that videos of performances by the punk group Pussy Riot were extremist, falling under a law intended to rein in hate speech. The court called for limiting public access to Web sites and blogs displaying the videos. But the ruling is unlikely to cut off access to them, since it applies only to servers in Russia. One video from February shows band members in the Cathedral of Christ the Savior beseeching the Virgin Mary to rid Russia of Vladimir V. Putin, who was returned to the presidency in March. Last summer, three of the performers were sentenced to two years in a penal colony. An appeals court released one of them on bail in October. Thursday’s ruling cited “psycho-linguistic research” proving that the videos “humiliate various social groups based on their religious beliefs” and contain “hidden calls to rebellion and nonsubmission to authority.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russian Court Ruling Seeks to Ban Pussy Riot’s Videos', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: Court Ruling Seeks to Ban Punk Band’s Videos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pussy Riot', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T06:06:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ellen Barry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa3566b1-ce95-53a5-a190-aa9534a6e7d1', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa3566b1-ce95-53a5-a190-aa9534a6e7d1'}, {'abstract': 'Chinese naval patrols have been authorized to search and repel ships that “illegally enter” an area in the South China Sea through which much of the region’s commercial vessels pass.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/world/asia/naval-patrols-to-expel-ships-in-south-china-sea.html', 'snippet': 'Chinese naval patrols have been authorized to search and repel ships that “illegally enter” an area in the South China Sea through which much of the region’s commercial vessels pass.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Chinese naval patrols have been authorized to search and repel ships that “illegally enter” an area in the South China Sea through which much of the region’s commercial vessels pass, the state news media reported Thursday. Beginning Jan. 1, said the official China Daily, the authorities in Hainan Province can board ships that “trespass” through the area, which has become the focus of a territorial dispute involving China, the Philippines, Vietnam and other countries. The patrols will be authorized to force foreign ships to change course, China Daily said, citing a law passed Tuesday by provincial lawmakers in Hainan, which China says has jurisdiction over the area. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Naval Patrols to Expel Ships in South China Sea', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Naval Patrols to Expel Ships in Disputed Sea', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South China Sea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philippines', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T06:10:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec42b54a-3467-5a95-b2c4-c7f7a19f839f', 'word_count': 107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec42b54a-3467-5a95-b2c4-c7f7a19f839f'}, {'abstract': 'The federal police said a scheme involved the sale of falsified public documents needed for transportation projects and extended into several government agencies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/world/americas/brazil-faces-a-new-corruption-scandal.html', 'snippet': 'The federal police said a scheme involved the sale of falsified public documents needed for transportation projects and extended into several government agencies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SÃO PAULO, Brazil — Even as President Dilma Rousseff of Brazil has moved energetically to stamp out corruption in her government, a new scandal is surging to the fore, centered on charges of influence peddling by an aide to the popular former president, Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Brazil Faces a New Corruption Scandal', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Brazil Faces a New Corruption Scandal', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brazil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Da Silva, Luiz Inacio Lula', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T06:13:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Simon Romero', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Simon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romero', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9295a382-84a5-526c-a3ab-3288f559720a', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9295a382-84a5-526c-a3ab-3288f559720a'}, {'abstract': 'President Juan Manuel Santos said that Colombia would no longer recognize the jurisdiction of the International Court of Justice in cases involving disputes with other countries in the region.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/world/americas/international-court-rebuffed-over-colombian-territorial-dispute.html', 'snippet': 'President Juan Manuel Santos said that Colombia would no longer recognize the jurisdiction of the International Court of Justice in cases involving disputes with other countries in the region.', 'lead_paragraph': ' President Juan Manuel Santos on Wednesday said that Colombia would no longer recognize the jurisdiction of the International Court of Justice in cases involving disputes with other countries in the region. The move followed a ruling by the court that gave Nicaragua control of a large area of the Caribbean that was formerly considered part of Colombian territorial waters. Mr. Santos said that Colombia would withdraw from a 1948 treaty that allowed countries in the region to settle their disagreements in the court, which is in The Hague. In its ruling this month, the court reaffirmed Colombia’s ownership of a group of small islands but gave Nicaragua control of a portion of the surrounding waters. Legal experts said that the court’s ruling remained in effect despite Mr. Santos’s decision to withdraw from the treaty. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'International Court Rebuffed Over Colombian Territorial Dispute', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Colombia: International Court Rebuffed Over Territorial Dispute', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colombia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nicaragua', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santos, Juan Manuel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Court of Justice (UN)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T06:16:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Neuman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Neuman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/69455e10-eee3-5d34-a1f7-dc9df3f8383e', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69455e10-eee3-5d34-a1f7-dc9df3f8383e'}, {'abstract': 'This almost-Picasso exemplifies the phenomenon of the trapper — a fake by implication. Examine the tiny details that gave it away.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/11/30/opinion/global/ganov30mould-zoomer.html', 'snippet': 'This almost-Picasso exemplifies the phenomenon of the trapper — a fake by implication. Examine the tiny details that gave it away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This almost-Picasso exemplifies the phenomenon of the trapper — a fake by implication. Examine the tiny details that gave it away.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Spotting a Fake', 'kicker': 'Global Agenda | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forgery', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T06:26:08+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Global Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/6009380d-e33e-55f8-ad15-4c8a4d312802', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/6009380d-e33e-55f8-ad15-4c8a4d312802'}, {'abstract': 'What does it mean? Is it achievable?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/opinion/global/gender-equality.html', 'snippet': 'What does it mean? Is it achievable?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In each issue, the editors of Global Agenda invite contributors to explore one of the big questions of the year. In this issue we asked: What does Gender Equality mean? Is it achievable? Below are responses from Naomi Wolf, Ronan Farrow, Chloe Breyer, Naina Lal Kidwai, Caitlin Moran, Ellen MacArthur, Nicholas D. Kristof and Leta Hong Fincher.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gender Equality', 'kicker': 'Global Agenda | The Big Question', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T06:26:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Global Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Special Report', '_id': 'nyt://article/070e682c-c47d-54e6-b300-cdc0940b3e11', 'word_count': 2474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/070e682c-c47d-54e6-b300-cdc0940b3e11'}, {'abstract': 'Susan Greenfield, Evgeny Morozov and Maria Popova on what technology and the Internet mean for our brains, our relationships and our future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/opinion/global/maria-popova-evgeny-morozov-susan-greenfield-are-we-becoming-cyborgs.html', 'snippet': 'Susan Greenfield, Evgeny Morozov and Maria Popova on what technology and the Internet mean for our brains, our relationships and our future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SINCE broadband began its inexorable spread at the start of this millennium, Internet use has expanded at a cosmic rate. Last year, the number of Internet users topped 2.4 billion — more than a third of all humans on the planet. The time spent on the screen was 16 hours per week globally — double that in high-use countries, and much of that on social media. We have changed how we interact. Are we also changing what we are?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/opinion/ganov30cyborgs/ganov30cyborgs-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/11/16/opinion/ganov30cyborgs/ganov30cyborgs-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/opinion/ganov30cyborgs/ganov30cyborgs-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 558, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/16/opinion/ganov30cyborgs/ga