ddlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonzalez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d883007c-da61-5068-8f66-13173a40670e', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d883007c-da61-5068-8f66-13173a40670e'}, {'abstract': 'John Isner has hired Michael Sell as his new coach, replacing Craig Boynton.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/isner-parts-ways-with-coach/', 'snippet': 'John Isner has hired Michael Sell as his new coach, replacing Craig Boynton.', 'lead_paragraph': 'John Isner, who at No. 14 is the top-ranked American in the A.T.P. rankings, has parted ways which Craig Boynton, who had served as his coach since March 2009. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/sports/tennis/30isner/30isner-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 744, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/sports/tennis/30isner/30isner-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1489, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/sports/tennis/30isner/30isner-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/30/sports/tennis/30isner/30isner-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/sports/tennis/30isner/30isner-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Isner Parts Ways With Coach', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T15:38:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By BEN ROTHENBERG', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'ROTHENBERG', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36b29b4d-093f-5d8b-9e57-592c21ee2ef8', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36b29b4d-093f-5d8b-9e57-592c21ee2ef8'}, {'abstract': 'While the prime minister rails against a soap opera for desecrating Turkey’s Ottoman past, he and his party are parodying that history with grandiose construction projects.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/erdogan-the-not-so-magnificent/', 'snippet': 'While the prime minister rails against a soap opera for desecrating Turkey’s Ottoman past, he and his party are parodying that history with grandiose construction projects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Istanbul — “The Magnificent Century,” a Turkish-made soap opera depicting harum-scarum adventures in the imperial household of Suleyman, the eponymous Magnificent, reaches farther than the Ottoman Empire did in the mid-16th century. Its domains extend from France to the Ukraine, the Czech Republic to Japan, with 150 million viewers in its thrall. In Turkey, the series brings Wednesday nights to a halt, claiming one third of the country’s entire television audience.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Erdogan, the Not-So Magnificent', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mosques', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ottoman Empire', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erdogan, Recep Tayyip', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Istanbul (Turkey)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T15:39:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Finkel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b161325-8568-50bf-b81f-4af9084f2547', 'word_count': 589, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b161325-8568-50bf-b81f-4af9084f2547'}, {'abstract': 'Talk Dutch to me.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/magazine/so-sue-me.html', 'snippet': 'Talk Dutch to me.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m applying to law school. I’m sure there are many schools that could provide me with a decent education; I’m less confident that a degree from some institutions will get me a job. In fact, some schools, while charging outrageously high tuition, place fewer than half of their recent graduates in long-term, full-time legal positions. Is it moral for schools like these to keep enrolling students and collecting tuition dollars knowing that their product is a risky (or outright bad) investment? MATTHEW DREILING, NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02ethicist/02ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/magazine/02ethicist/02ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'heig