 insurance team are betting that at least one Spanish firm is in good financial shape, even as its home country remains on shakier economic ground.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Warren E. Buffett has generally shied away from dipping his toe into southern Europe. But Mr. Buffett is nothing if not full of surprises. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Berkshire Moves Into Spain With CaixaBank Reinsurance Deal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Berkshire Acquires Spanish Insurer’s Cash Flow', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buffett, Warren E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Berkshire Hathaway Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:41:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f6ceb8b-3247-535c-b99e-af46438d6a4f', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f6ceb8b-3247-535c-b99e-af46438d6a4f'}, {'abstract': 'Two men asked a 70-year-old man in Queens if he was Muslim or Hindu, then beat him, the police said.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/police-seek-two-suspects-in-possible-bias-attack/', 'snippet': 'Two men asked a 70-year-old man in Queens if he was Muslim or Hindu, then beat him, the police said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The police are looking for two men they say asked a 70-year-old man in Queens if he was Muslim or Hindu, then beat him.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/nyregion/30assault-cityroom/30assault-cityroom-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 207, 'width': 297, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/nyregion/30assault-cityroom/30assault-cityroom-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 207, 'width': 297, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/nyregion/30assault-cityroom/30assault-cityroom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/30/nyregion/30assault-cityroom/30assault-cityroom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/nyregion/30assault-cityroom/30assault-cityroom-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 148, 'width': 148, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Police Seek Two Suspects in Possible Bias Attack', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Assaults', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Corona (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:41:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad3338eb-08c9-5855-ac89-85a2bf450aa9', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad3338eb-08c9-5855-ac89-85a2bf450aa9'}, {'abstract': 'This season’s gardening books include several that honor the tree’s noble status.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/the-man-who-planted-trees-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'This season’s gardening books include several that honor the tree’s noble status.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Several years ago, I visited a friend at her home in the South of France, which was surrounded by an olive grove. She had just come through a grueling battle with breast cancer, and the peaceful countryside was a consolation. As we set out for a walk, she encouraged me to wrap my arms around a gnarled olive trunk: “Go ahead, I do it all the time.” I hadn’t sized up this elegant woman as a tree hugger, and even though I’m one by profession I’d never actually done it. Well, not that anyone could see. . . . I sidled up awkwardly. “Do you feel it?” my friend asked. “Do you feel that . . . energy?” I did.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Gardening/1202-Gardening-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1019, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Gardening/1202-Gardening-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2038, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Gardening/1202-Gardening-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Gardening/1202-Gardening-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Gardening/1202-Gardening-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Gardening', 'kicker': 'Holiday Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gardening', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trees and Shrubs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Flowers and Plants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Daily, Gretchen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harrison, Lorraine', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gardens and Gardening', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robbins, Jim', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:42:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dominique Browning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dominique', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f5d5f88-edd8-5ab7-93e3-6f6fe34fff7e', 'word_count': 2579, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f5d5f88-edd8-5ab7-93e3-6f6fe34fff7e'}, {'abstract': 'This season’s travel books abound with journeys inspired by literary lions, including Epicurus, Virginia Woolf and the Nigerian writer Ken Saro-Wiwa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/looking-for-transwonderland-route-66-still-kicks-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'This season’s travel books abound with journeys inspired by literary lions, including Epicurus, Virginia Woolf and the Nigerian writer Ken Saro-Wiwa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This season’s travel books abound with journeys inspired by literary lions — a trip to a Greek island in pursuit of the teachings of Epicurus, a hike along the river where Virginia Woolf died, an excursion to the birthplace of the Nigerian writer Ken Saro-Wiwa. And not every excursion is highbrow: one of the best books of the bunch is partly a homage to Bobby Troup, the lyricist who wrote the 1946 hit “(Get Your Kicks on) Route 66.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Travel/1202-Travel-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 961, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Travel/1202-Travel-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1922, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Travel/1202-Travel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Travel/1202-Travel-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Travel/1202-Travel-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Travel', 'kicker': 'Holiday Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Travel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiener, Jon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klein, Daniel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Antonson, Rick', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Laing, Olivia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saro-Wiwa, Noo', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:42:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Hammer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hammer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b6492a1-d672-51f4-bbac-9c049b79eb8d', 'word_count': 2517, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b6492a1-d672-51f4-bbac-9c049b79eb8d'}, {'abstract': 'Some academics and child development experts say that boredom forces the brain to go on interesting tangents, perhaps fostering creativity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/01/your-money/why-its-not-all-bad-to-be-bored.html', 'snippet': 'Some academics and child development experts say that boredom forces the brain to go on interesting tangents, perhaps fostering creativity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I SPENT five unexpected hours in an airport this Thanksgiving holiday when our plane had mechanical difficulties and we had to wait for another plane to arrive. So I had plenty of time to think about the subject of boredom.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Constantly Plugged-In World, It’s Not All Bad to Be Bored', 'kicker': 'Shortcuts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why, in a Constantly Plugged-In World, It’s Not All Bad to Be Bored', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Creativity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boredom', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:43:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alina Tugend', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tugend', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e25a6f7-3094-5087-826a-6ad6f5c6696f', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e25a6f7-3094-5087-826a-6ad6f5c6696f'}, {'abstract': 'The quest to raise taxes isn’t a threat to Obama’s second-term agenda. It is his second-term agenda.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/a-test-for-liberalism/', 'snippet': 'The quest to raise taxes isn’t a threat to Obama’s second-term agenda. It is his second-term agenda.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s National Journal’s Major Garrett, explaining why the Obama White House, for all its shows of intransigence this week, ultimately wants and needs a fiscal deal:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Test For Liberalism', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:43:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19641925-3aa1-5829-8b2d-6e1599cb9496', 'word_count': 703, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19641925-3aa1-5829-8b2d-6e1599cb9496'}, {'abstract': 'Most of the current season’s cookbooks are heavily influenced, in visual style and in content, by the relentless advance of food television.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/bouchon-bakery-salt-sugar-smoke-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Most of the current season’s cookbooks are heavily influenced, in visual style and in content, by the relentless advance of food television.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The star chefs continue to twinkle, street food trumps haute cuisine, and no one ever gets tired of Southern cooking. These are the big messages of the current season’s cookbooks, most of them heavily influenced, in visual style and in content, by the relentless advance of food television. In days of yore, cookbooks had a few line drawings and the big authors weren’t chefs — because chefs were, for the most part, anonymous. The world has spun on its axis quite a few times since then. Today a front-burner cookbook has Busby Berkeley production values, a marquee name above the title and a heavy investment in fantasy, promising to take readers to exotic ports of call or inside the mind of a culinary genius.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Cooking/1202-Cooking-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 992, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Cooking/1202-Cooking-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1985, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Cooking/1202-Cooking-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Cooking/1202-Cooking-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Cooking/1202-Cooking-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cooking', 'kicker': 'Holiday Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cooking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oliver, Jamie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:43:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cff9d8d1-e32d-5322-ae67-3142d55dc7e0', 'word_count': 2239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cff9d8d1-e32d-5322-ae67-3142d55dc7e0'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Ben Ratliff and Jon Caramanica discuss “Unapologetic,” the new album by Rihanna, and the ongoing intrigue between the singer and Chris Brown. They also hear from the music journalists Mary H.K. Choi and Julianne Escobedo Shepherd, who endured a recent Rihanna junket-cum-kidnapping.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2012/11/30/arts/music/30popcast_pod.html', 'snippet': 'This week, Ben Ratliff and Jon Caramanica discuss “Unapologetic,” the new album by Rihanna, and the ongoing intrigue between the singer and Chris Brown. They also hear from the music journalists Mary H.K. Choi and Julianne Escobedo Shepherd, who e...', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Ben Ratliff and Jon Caramanica discuss “Unapologetic,” the new album by Rihanna, and the ongoing intrigue between the singer and Chris Brown. They also hear from the music journalists Mary H.K. Choi and Julianne Escobedo Shepherd, who endured a recent Rihanna junket-cum-kidnapping.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rihanna: The Music, the Attitude, the Psychodrama', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:43:54+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/d52d995c-fa10-5b2c-ba8a-2e62b0802a53', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/d52d995c-fa10-5b2c-ba8a-2e62b0802a53'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Landau’s coffee-table book documents a time when images that originated on album covers or concert posters became oversize roadside attractions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/rock-n-roll-billboards-of-the-sunset-strip-by-robert-landau.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Landau’s coffee-table book documents a time when images that originated on album covers or concert posters became oversize roadside attractions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sunset Boulevard, no traffic flow is gradual enough for the sensory occasion. The driver or his passenger, craning to take in a billboard’s extent through the insufficient frame of a windshield, is seduced into variant environs: an idyllic pond in which a band wades fully dressed (Paul McCartney and Wings), a Sergio Leone western (the Eagles), a soft-edged surrealist fantasy\xadscape (Yes) or the hypnotized front row at a festival show (Neil Young). Or they’re enticed onto some other street altogether: say, Abbey Road, once the 20-foot-high Beatles have crossed it — but wait, someone stole Paul’s head!', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Lethem/1202-Lethem-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 657, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Lethem/1202-Lethem-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1315, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Lethem/1202-Lethem-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Lethem/1202-Lethem-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Lethem/1202-Lethem-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Billboards', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Billboards', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Outdoor Advertising', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:44:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Lethem', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lethem', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/26381dec-cce5-5528-ae26-2ccfd77b2ae4', 'word_count': 695, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26381dec-cce5-5528-ae26-2ccfd77b2ae4'}, {'abstract': 'The second volume of essays adapted from Stella Adler’s legendary theater classes analyzes the works of Tennessee Williams, Eugene O’Neill and others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/stella-adler-on-americas-master-playwrights.html', 'snippet': 'The second volume of essays adapted from Stella Adler’s legendary theater classes analyzes the works of Tennessee Williams, Eugene O’Neill and others.', 'lead_paragraph': '”Life is boring. The weather is boring,” Stella Adler used to say. “Actors must not be boring. Life beats down and crushes the soul, and art reminds you that you have one.” When I first started taking classes with the already legendary Adler in 1955, I was only 16, having lied by two years to get accepted. I studied with her for four years, and took every class she offered: from Beginnings, through Scene Class, finally to Script Analysis, which was both the most daunting and the most electrifying. The excitement came because of Stella’s delivery, which was very theatrical but real, because Stella was nothing if not theatrical and real. Theater was in her blood, her parents having been major stars in New York’s Yiddish theater.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bogdanovich/1202-Bogdanovich-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bogdanovich/1202-Bogdanovich-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1605, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bogdanovich/1202-Bogdanovich-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bogdanovich/1202-Bogdanovich-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bogdanovich/1202-Bogdanovich-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'American Theater', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American Theater', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Arthur', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Tennessee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ALBEE, EDWARD', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paris, Barry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Neill, Eugene", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Odets, Clifford', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilder, Thornton', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ADLER, STELLA', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:44:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Bogdanovich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bogdanovich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/540746da-02fb-5058-af0a-f8fb8e69a6b6', 'word_count': 1646, 'uri': 'nyt://article/540746da-02fb-5058-af0a-f8fb8e69a6b6'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Gray, the editor in chief of Variety, traces the magazine’s evolution decade by decade, along with the trajectory of American culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/tim-grays-history-of-variety-magazine.html', 'snippet': 'Tim Gray, the editor in chief of Variety, traces the magazine’s evolution decade by decade, along with the trajectory of American culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Variety, the show-business trade magazine, has bestowed a lasting “slanguage” on the popular lexicon. “Cliffhanger,” “kudos,” “hoofers,” “niteries,” “Will it play in Peoria?” — all are terms and expressions made famous in its \xadpages. Such Runyonesque phraseology reflects an affectionate irreverence toward Hollywood that keeps the firmament’s Brobdingnagian egos vitally in check. In the coffee-table book “Variety,” Tim Gray, Variety’s editor in chief, traces the magazine’s evolution decade by decade, along with the trajectory of American culture.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Webster/1202-Webster-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Webster/1202-Webster-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Webster/1202-Webster-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 811, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Webster/1202-Webster-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1622, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Webster/1202-Webster-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Webster/1202-Webster-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Webster/1202-Webster-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Showbiz Press', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Showbiz Press', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Variety', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:44:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/24578dd0-54d4-540b-bd39-2cedf094a5a8', 'word_count': 391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24578dd0-54d4-540b-bd39-2cedf094a5a8'}, {'abstract': 'Marlo Thomas discusses the book, album and TV show that challenged gender stereotypes.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/the-40th-anniversary-of-free-to-be-you-and-me/', 'snippet': 'Marlo Thomas discusses the book, album and TV show that challenged gender stereotypes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you grew up in the ’70s or ’80s, chances are you not only remember the album “Free to Be … You and Me,” you also remember which role you played in the requisite school play, rampant in elementary schools across the nation. (I was Pamela Purse, the impudent little minx who cried “Ladies First!” in Shel Silverstein’s mischievous skit.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 40th Anniversary of ‘Free to Be . . . You and Me’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gender', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thomas, Marlo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:45:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0f56e93-a7f2-510b-aa0f-114e689b910e', 'word_count': 411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0f56e93-a7f2-510b-aa0f-114e689b910e'}, {'abstract': 'Orhan Pamuk’s guidebook to the museum he created to accompany the novel “The Museum of Innocence.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/the-innocence-of-objects-by-orhan-pamuk.html', 'snippet': 'Orhan Pamuk’s guidebook to the museum he created to accompany the novel “The Museum of Innocence.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Toward the end of Orhan Pamuk’s 2008 novel “The Museum of Innocence,” there are a dozen pages of exhilarated writing on what the act of collecting can mean. After a brief, passionate love affair with a distant cousin named Fusun, the narrator, Kemal, obsessively amasses objects that evoke their relationship in 1970s Istanbul. He has become, he says, “the anthropologist of my own experience.” As Kemal grows older he takes to visiting unlikely museums in the cities he passes through, from those that dramatize the lives of artists, writers and architects — Gustave Moreau, John Soane, Cézanne, Mario Praz — to such particular institutions as the Museo del Mare in Trieste and the Musée du Temps, a clock museum in Besançon, France. In visiting these \xadplaces, Kemal’s sense of what is possible in the disposition of objects grows ever deeper. As he views “the playing cards, rings, necklaces, chess sets and oil paintings of the State Museum of Württemberg,” he becomes convinced that Fusun’s possessions “deserved display in comparable splendor.” He realizes that objects beget narrative, just as stories need objects. (In a twist, it becomes clear in the closing pages that the novel is actually narrated by Pamuk — a novelist hired to tell the story.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-deWaal/1202-deWaal-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 936, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-deWaal/1202-deWaal-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1873, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-deWaal/1202-deWaal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-deWaal/1202-deWaal-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-deWaal/1202-deWaal-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cultural Artifacts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cultural Artifacts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pamuk, Orhan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:45:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edmund de Waal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edmund', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de Waal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/57065efe-55b9-5235-ac7d-aa33aab484ee', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57065efe-55b9-5235-ac7d-aa33aab484ee'}, {'abstract': 'Jason C. Anthony’s “Hoosh” is a paean to the lousy food available to Antarctic explorers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/hoosh-by-jason-c-anthony.html', 'snippet': 'Jason C. Anthony’s “Hoosh” is a paean to the lousy food available to Antarctic explorers.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I am not dead,” exclaimed an astonished Capt. Georges Lecointe on awaking one morning circa 1897. Or perhaps we should say “complained.” In the throes of scurvy, his ship trapped in the ice in the black Antarctic winter, one of the leaders of the first major scientific expedition of the heroic age had been saved from death by a “last meal”: penguin that tasted like “beef, odiferous cod fish and a canvas-backed duck, roasted together in a pot with blood and cod-liver oil for sauce.” The poor man might have welcomed death.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Sinkler/1202-Sinkler-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 986, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Sinkler/1202-Sinkler-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1064, 'width': 1025, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Sinkler/1202-Sinkler-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Sinkler/1202-Sinkler-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Sinkler/1202-Sinkler-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Antarctic Eats', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Antarctic Eats', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anthony, Jason C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:45:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rebecca Pepper Sinkler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rebecca', 'middlename': 'Pepper', 'lastname': 'Sinkler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfe2eb3b-9eb5-58f7-8489-5944bbc853ba', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfe2eb3b-9eb5-58f7-8489-5944bbc853ba'}, {'abstract': 'Record unemployment in the euro zone. Significant gains for Britain’s most anti-Europe political party. And E.U. leaders unable to agree on a budget. But Germany approves the latest Greek bailout. Where is Europe, the euro and the E.U. headed? James Kanter and Andrew Higgins explain.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/our-brussels-correspondents-answer-readers-questions-about-the-euro-europe-and-the-union/', 'snippet': 'Record unemployment in the euro zone. Significant gains for Britain’s most anti-Europe political party. And E.U. leaders unable to agree on a budget. But Germany approves the latest Greek bailout. Where is Europe, the euro and the E.U. headed? Jam...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The news this week from besieged Europe was — relatively — good, sort of. Yields on Italian government bonds — the amount Italy has to pay investors to assume a piece of their debt — fell to the lowest levels in more than a year. The German Parliament, the Bundestag, approved Germany’s latest contribution to bailout-broke Greece. And though unemployment in the euro zone reached record heights, the head of the European Central Bank, Mario Draghi, predicted that the euro zone’s economy would begin to recover in the latter part of next year. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/01/business/global/01eurozone/01eurozone-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 259, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'cr