er to the composer Richard Rodgers and perhaps the most popular songsmith-poet in America.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/lorenz-hart-biography-by-gary-marmorstein.html', 'snippet': 'A biography of Lorenz Hart, the lyric-writing partner to the composer Richard Rodgers and perhaps the most popular songsmith-poet in America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Biographies of theater people often work best in counterpoint to the lives they examine. \xadTypically, the life in question is headlong and harum-scarum, a rush of pressing deadlines and juggled schedules, of collapsed funding and hastily struck stage sets. There’s no time for poised reflection — which arrives only in retrospect, when the biographer converts a life-on-the-road to a life-on-the-page.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-LeithauserSub/1202-LeithauserSub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1002, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-LeithauserSub/1202-LeithauserSub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2005, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-LeithauserSub/1202-LeithauserSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-LeithauserSub/1202-LeithauserSub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-LeithauserSub/1202-LeithauserSub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'American Songsmith', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'American Songsmith', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marmorstein, Gary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hart, Lorenz', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:55:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brad Leithauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leithauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2b9f9e0-8d1b-5e18-881b-8cc45cc6b9bc', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2b9f9e0-8d1b-5e18-881b-8cc45cc6b9bc'}, {'abstract': 'The singer-\xadpianist Michael Feinstein’s illustrated take on the Gershwins and their work.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/the-gershwins-and-me-by-michael-feinstein-with-ian-jackman.html', 'snippet': 'The singer-\xadpianist Michael Feinstein’s illustrated take on the Gershwins and their work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Michael Feinstein, celebrated as a singer-\xadpianist, a cabaret entrepreneur and a major spokesman for the preservation of what has come to be known as the American Songbook, has now published a large (8 1/2 inches by 11 \xadinches), lavishly illustrated coffee-table book entitled “The Gershwins and Me: A Personal History in Twelve Songs.” Interestingly, Feinstein’s hefty volume resembles, in some respects, the gaudy 1920s Broadway musicals through which the composer George Gershwin (1898-1937) and his lyricist brother, Ira (1896-1983), began to make their marks as major figures in American popular culture.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '51', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Feingold-SUB/1202-Feingold-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 822, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Feingold-SUB/1202-Feingold-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1175, 'width': 943, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Feingold-SUB/1202-Feingold-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Feingold-SUB/1202-Feingold-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Feingold-SUB/1202-Feingold-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gershwins', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Gershwins', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackman, Ian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feinstein, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gershwin, Ira', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gershwin, George', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:55:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Feingold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feingold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d283bb2-4754-5af2-9a0a-6a825c1fec6c', 'word_count': 1249, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d283bb2-4754-5af2-9a0a-6a825c1fec6c'}, {'abstract': '“Joseph Cornell’s Manual of Marvels” is a new boxed-set based on a little-known work by the artist that was created by altering and remaking a French agricultural yearbook from 1911.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/joseph-cornells-manual-of-marvels.html', 'snippet': '“Joseph Cornell’s Manual of Marvels” is a new boxed-set based on a little-known work by the artist that was created by altering and remaking a French agricultural yearbook from 1911.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before there was Pinterist or Mixel or any of the many online applications devoted to the art of virtual collage, Joseph Cornell was busy creating his own real assemblage works by hand. With snippets of magazines, pasted photographs and found objects, the fantastical universe of his imagination was realized in meticulously composed masterworks born out of everyday material.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '56', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bloom/1202-Bloom-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bloom/1202-Bloom-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bloom/1202-Bloom-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bloom/1202-Bloom-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1327, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bloom/1202-Bloom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bloom/1202-Bloom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Bloom/1202-Bloom-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cornell’s Almanac', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Cornell's Almanac", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tashjian, Dickran', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cornell, Joseph', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leppanen-Guerra, Analisa', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:57:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bloom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bloom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/655e629b-bbda-5162-b7a3-22951542a340', 'word_count': 1031, 'uri': 'nyt://article/655e629b-bbda-5162-b7a3-22951542a340'}, {'abstract': 'This oral history traces the rise of women in comedy through the voices of comics, club owners, producers, writers, agents and network executives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/we-killed-by-yael-kohen.html', 'snippet': 'This oral history traces the rise of women in comedy through the voices of comics, club owners, producers, writers, agents and network executives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The good news and bad news in writing about women fighting their way into mostly male professions is that we know the drill: Women pushed like hell for a break; men were clueless and obstructionist; women forced their way into the boys’ club and brought something fresh to the table. It’s good news because it’s true. It’s bad news because it’s no longer surprising and can now sound (cue Helen Reddy’s “I Am Woman,” awful even back in 1972) cloyingly self-righteous.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '52', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202WELLER/1202WELLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202WELLER/1202WELLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1952, 'width': 1952, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202WELLER/1202WELLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202WELLER/1202WELLER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202WELLER/1202WELLER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Funny Women', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Funny Women', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kohen, Yael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:57:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sheila Weller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sheila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c09483d0-d041-5a77-a24d-777b60d12c35', 'word_count': 1185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c09483d0-d041-5a77-a24d-777b60d12c35'}, {'abstract': '“Glittering Images” takes its reader on a tour of 29 of Camille Paglia’s favorite artworks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/glittering-images-by-camille-paglia.html', 'snippet': '“Glittering Images” takes its reader on a tour of 29 of Camille Paglia’s favorite artworks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Camille Paglia’s new personalized “journey” through Western art history was born of her deep-\xadseated concern that Americans today are so overwhelmed by the constant onslaught of visual images that we are in danger of losing altogether our capacity for contemplation. “Culture,” she writes in the introduction to “Glittering Images: A Journey Through Art From Egypt to ‘Star Wars,’ ” “is now largely defined by all-pervasive mass media and slavishly monitored personal electronic devices.” The digital revolution has heated up our social interaction to the point where it has severely damaged our ability for sustained analytical thought, making the aesthetic encounter fraught with jarring interruptions and the chatter of competing visual and aural stimuli. Looking at art, then, is a discipline Paglia believes will help us retrieve the necessary stillness that can “realign our senses and produce a magical tranquillity.” Hence “Glittering Images,” a primer of sorts that takes its reader on a tour of 29 of Paglia’s favorite artworks, a list that includes the “Laocoön”; Donatello’s “Mary Magdalene”; paintings by Jacques-Louis David, Monet and Picasso; as well as examples of more recent trends like Pop, Conceptual, Land and Performance Art. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '55', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202ADAMS/1202ADAMS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 650, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202ADAMS/1202ADAMS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1301, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202ADAMS/1202ADAMS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202ADAMS/1202ADAMS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202ADAMS/1202ADAMS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Paglia on Art', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paglia on Art', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paglia, Camille', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:57:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Adams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cd55cb9-e64c-56d7-a69c-d40ffec88776', 'word_count': 1753, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cd55cb9-e64c-56d7-a69c-d40ffec88776'}, {'abstract': 'A diverse sample of comics depicting the political struggles of representation for lesbians and gay men and the personal struggles of coming out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/no-straight-lines-edited-by-justin-hall.html', 'snippet': 'A diverse sample of comics depicting the political struggles of representation for lesbians and gay men and the personal struggles of coming out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A comics creator who knows how to milk the fusion of, and tension between, word and image can place us deep inside a character’s head with an immediacy and sensuousness prose writers envy. In comics, style informs substance — physically shapes it to alter our perceptions in intimate ways. Mood and tone don’t emerge over the course of paragraphs and chapters; they’re evident at first glance, infused into the arrangement of panels, thickness of lines and density of detail. We read books; we feel comics.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '61', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-WELDON/1202-WELDON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 932, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-WELDON/1202-WELDON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1864, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-WELDON/1202-WELDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-WELDON/1202-WELDON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-WELDON/1202-WELDON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Comics', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Comics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comic Books and Strips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hall, Justin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:59:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glen Weldon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weldon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a578e8cc-b89c-5014-ba61-ba9e70bca691', 'word_count': 564, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a578e8cc-b89c-5014-ba61-ba9e70bca691'}, {'abstract': 'A chatty, slim memoir by the blues giant Buddy Guy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/when-i-left-home-buddy-guys-memoir.html', 'snippet': 'A chatty, slim memoir by the blues giant Buddy Guy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most memoirs by musicians are written later in life, when a subject finally wants to look back and tell his or her story. But since they are usually compiled years after an artist’s best work, there’s a struggle to make the final chapters as interesting as the ones about glory days.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '59', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202LIGHT/1202LIGHT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202LIGHT/1202LIGHT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1394, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202LIGHT/1202LIGHT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202LIGHT/1202LIGHT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202LIGHT/1202LIGHT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Blues', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Blues', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guy, Buddy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ritz, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blues Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:59:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Light', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Light', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f84db427-839f-5831-88ba-9c8cbf56681e', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f84db427-839f-5831-88ba-9c8cbf56681e'}, {'abstract': '“The Richard Burton Diaries” covers the actor’s seesawing yet intense marriages with Elizabeth Taylor, his intense reading habits and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/the-richard-burton-diaries-edited-by-chris-williams.html', 'snippet': '“The Richard Burton Diaries” covers the actor’s seesawing yet intense marriages with Elizabeth Taylor, his intense reading habits and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rarely have there been acting couples with both spouses equal stars. Even rarer is it that one partner is also a gifted writer. Yet just that is the case of Richard Burton and Elizabeth Taylor, twice married, twice divorced and always cynosures of hero worship, envy and obloquy. This, and their hobnobbing with everyone from rulers to fellow celebrities, from aristocrats to fascinating arrivistes, gives “The Richard Burton Diaries” considerable interest. Though Burton died at 58 a full 28 years ago, publication probably awaited the death of Taylor, who might have raised objections.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202BOGDANOVICH-1353010021368/1202BOGDANOVICH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202BOGDANOVICH-1353010021368/1202BOGDANOVICH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1604, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202BOGDANOVICH-1353010021368/1202BOGDANOVICH-1353010021368-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202BOGDANOVICH-1353010021368/1202BOGDANOVICH-1353010021368-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202BOGDANOVICH-1353010021368/1202BOGDANOVICH-1353010021368-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Burton on Burton', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Burton on Burton', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Taylor, Elizabeth (1932-2011)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Chris', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burton, Richard', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T16:59:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Simon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Simon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/19154018-02eb-5d5a-97c8-5c1c58341eb5', 'word_count': 1573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19154018-02eb-5d5a-97c8-5c1c58341eb5'}, {'abstract': 'A boy’s fantasies, and the gifts that keep him in character.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/christmas-in-costume/', 'snippet': 'A boy’s fantasies, and the gifts that keep him in character.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Christmas in Costume', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T17:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ada Calhoun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ada', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calhoun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/715fdacd-6d3f-5cc4-9227-fdf73514cacd', 'word_count': 943, 'uri': 'nyt://article/715fdacd-6d3f-5cc4-9227-fdf73514cacd'}, {'abstract': 'Cyndi Lauper’s memoir of her unglamorous outer-borough upbringing and not entirely glamorous life since MTV made her a pop star in the 1980s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/cyndi-lauper-a-memoir.html', 'snippet': 'Cyndi Lauper’s memoir of her unglamorous outer-borough upbringing and not entirely glamorous life since MTV made her a pop star in the 1980s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cyndi Lauper’s memory speaks with a Queens accent. The crudely endearing charms of Lauper’s book, a memoir of her unglamorous outer-borough upbringing and not entirely glamorous life since MTV made her a pop star in the 1980s, are its unpretentious attitude and its blunt, brassy voice. “Cyndi Lauper: A Memoir” is boisterous, erratic, sometimes goofy and — very much like its author’s music, in all this — not quite as dumb as it may seem.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '64', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Hajdu/1202-Hajdu-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 633, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Hajdu/1202-Hajdu-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1267, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Hajdu/1202-Hajdu-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Hajdu/1202-Hajdu-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Hajdu/1202-Hajdu-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '’80s Pop', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '’80s Pop', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lauper, Cyndi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T17:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Hajdu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hajdu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/353e6422-f5c7-5673-8e0e-81ad2c4fbe1e', 'word_count': 1037, 'uri': 'nyt://article/353e6422-f5c7-5673-8e0e-81ad2c4fbe1e'}, {'abstract': '“360 Sound” recounts the history of Columbia Records, from its modest beginnings 125 years ago to its current status as one of the last remaining giants of an industry in turmoil.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/books/review/360-sound-the-columbia-records-story-by-sean-wilentz.html', 'snippet': '“360 Sound” recounts the history of Columbia Records, from its modest beginnings 125 years ago to its current status as one of the last remaining giants of an industry in turmoil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The history of Columbia Rec\xadords is inseparable from the history of the rec\xadord business itself. “360 Sound” recounts that entire history, from the label’s modest beginnings 125 years ago as the American Graphophone Company to its current status as one of the last remaining giants of an industry in turmoil. The scope of this story explains why “360 Sound” is so fascinating. It also explains why it’s so frustrating.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '63', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Keepnews/1202-Keepnews-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 846, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Keepnews/1202-Keepnews-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1693, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Keepnews/1202-Keepnews-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Keepnews/1202-Keepnews-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/books/review/1202-Keepnews/1202-Keepnews-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Records', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Records', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilentz, Sean', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia Records', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T17:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Keepnews', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastn