 'value': 'Chen Kegui', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T18:20:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Jacobs', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9cd548fe-7cf4-5ae5-8970-ef6c8aa703ba', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9cd548fe-7cf4-5ae5-8970-ef6c8aa703ba'}, {'abstract': 'In the designer Julia Muggenburg’s London town house, there is a method to the brilliant madness.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/a-fine-mess/', 'snippet': 'In the designer Julia Muggenburg’s London town house, there is a method to the brilliant madness.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Julia Muggenburg doesn’t keep anything in drawers. In the downstairs library of the house on Thurloe Square in London that she shares with her husband and two children, a guest may find it hard to concentrate, surrounded as he is by a pleasant assault of objects and furnishings: deer antler sculptures by the Austrian artist Christian Eisenberger in the fireplace; walls of meticulously stacked art and design books; and hundreds of colorful glass objects, opalescent shells and fine pottery displayed on antique tables. This room is where Muggenburg, the jewelry designer behind Belmacz, receives guests, on sky blue leather Le Corbusier chairs, for tea served in tiny Japanese clay cups on an inlaid ivory and wood chess table from Syria. The parquet floors are hidden beneath lashings of bright Persian carpets, and ceiling space is shared between a translucent Flos fan and a clear Murano glass chandelier by Barovier & Toso. “It’s always been terribly important to be constantly surrounded by things that inspire me,” she says.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '143', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fine Mess', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Fine Mess', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T18:26:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rocky Casale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rocky', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Casale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/132cb22f-5a7d-5811-954d-7dd1db2c005b', 'word_count': 874, 'uri': 'nyt://article/132cb22f-5a7d-5811-954d-7dd1db2c005b'}, {'abstract': 'Picture books for the season, including the story of three cowboys who are waiting for “Santy Claus.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/11/30/books/review/02bookshelf-img.html', 'snippet': 'Picture books for the season, including the story of three cowboys who are waiting for “Santy Claus.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Picture books for the season, including the story of three cowboys who are waiting for “Santy Claus.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/books/review/02bookshelf-img-slide-WJTA/02bookshelf-img-slide-WJTA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 446, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/books/review/02bookshelf-img-slide-WJTA/02bookshelf-img-slide-WJTA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 653, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bookshelf: Holidays in America', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T18:29:12+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/081e370f-619c-5f5c-bb22-4216cd7d351e', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/081e370f-619c-5f5c-bb22-4216cd7d351e'}, {'abstract': 'A solid gold sculpture by the British artist Douglas Gordon, a winner of the Turner Prize, was stolen from a Christie’s warehouse in London.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/british-artist-angered-by-theft-of-his-work-from-christies/', 'snippet': 'A solid gold sculpture by the British artist Douglas Gordon, a winner of the Turner Prize, was stolen from a Christie’s warehouse in London.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A solid gold sculpture by the British artist Douglas Gordon, a winner of the Turner Prize, was stolen from a Christie’s warehouse in London, the British newspaper The Guardian  reported. “I don’t think this is an art theft,” Mr. Gordon said. “I’m pretty sure it has been melted down.” The gold used in his work, “Left Hand and Right Hand Have Left One Another,” was worth about 250,000 pounds ($400,000), he said. The piece was insured for twice that amount.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'British Artist Angered by Theft of His Work From Christie’s', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Artist Is Unhappy;  Christie’s Is Red-Faced', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gold', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Robberies and Thefts', 'ra