': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a97f6b3-6551-55a1-81fc-fd85bcb0d6b4', 'word_count': 882, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a97f6b3-6551-55a1-81fc-fd85bcb0d6b4'}, {'abstract': 'Volume 10 of the series written by Jason Aaron is No. 3 on the paperback list this week.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/graphic-books-best-sellers-the-end-of-scalped/', 'snippet': 'Volume 10 of the series written by Jason Aaron is No. 3 on the paperback list this week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At No. 3 on our paperback graphic books best-seller list this week is Volume 10 of “Scalped,” written by Jason Aaron and illustrated by R.M. Guera. It is the final volume of the series that chronicled trials and tribulations at the fictional Prairie Rose Indian Reservation in South Dakota. It was part of the Vertigo imprint from DC Comics. At the heart of the story are Dashiell Bad Horse, who grew up on the reservation and became an undercover agent for the F.B.I., and Lincoln Red Crow, the corrupt casino owner. The series has been on our paperback list several times before, including back in March and August this year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/books/30scalped/30scalped-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 464, 'width': 300, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/books/30scalped/30scalped-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 464, 'width': 300, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/books/30scalped/30scalped-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/30/books/30scalped/30scalped-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/books/30scalped/30scalped-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Graphic Books Best Sellers: The End of ‘Scalped’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Graphic Novels', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aaron, Jason', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T20:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By George Gene Gustines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'George', 'middlename': 'Gene', 'lastname': 'Gustines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d1e02b6b-2ea7-5b24-a43b-567f11d2e1ba', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d1e02b6b-2ea7-5b24-a43b-567f11d2e1ba'}, {'abstract': 'Most Americans in 2010 paid far less in total taxes than they would have 30 years earlier, but how much of the decline happened during or after the Reagan presidency depends on the income group.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/a-note-on-the-reagan-80s/', 'snippet': 'Most Americans in 2010 paid far less in total taxes than they would have 30 years earlier, but how much of the decline happened during or after the Reagan presidency depends on the income group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some readers of The New York Times – the version printed on trees – have noted that a headline on the front page of Friday’s paper emphasizes a different aspect of our analysis of tax trends than the accompanying chart. The headline says, “Complaints Aside, Most Face Lower Tax Burden Than in the Reagan ‘80s,” while the chart compares tax rates in 1980 and 2010. And as Greg Mankiw notes, President Ronald Reagan took office in 1981.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Note on the ‘Reagan ’80s’', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nineteen Hundred Eighties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reagan, Ronald Wilson', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T20:01:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b36d8a6d-207b-5c19-a44c-0871feb91dba', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b36d8a6d-207b-5c19-a44c-0871feb91dba'}, {'abstract': 'The singer Graham Parker and his old band the Rumour are getting back together for a film, album and tour.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/arts/music/graham-parker-and-the-rumour-reunite.html', 'snippet': 'The singer Graham Parker and his old band the Rumour are getting back together for a film, album and tour.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tarrytown, N.Y.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02PARKER1_SPAN/02PARKER1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02PARKER1_SPAN/02PARKER1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02PARKER1_SPAN/02PARKER1_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02PARKER1_SPAN/02PARKER1_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02PARKER1_SPAN/02PARKER1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02PARKER1_SPAN/02PARKER1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/12/02/arts/02PARKER1_SPAN/02PARKER1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'His Perfect Role: Rocker of a Certain Age', 'kicker': 'Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'His Perfect Role: Rocker of a Certain Age', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Parker, Graham', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T20:04:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Headlam', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Headlam', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbfef855-78c3-55b7-86d8-cac6b44815fe', 'word_count': 1832, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbfef855-78c3-55b7-86d8-cac6b44815fe'}, {'abstract': 'All I really know about 1960s Esquire are George Lois’s covers, which haven’t dimmed a bit over the years. They still feel exciting and audacious. But what about the inside?', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/16-things-i-learned-from-reading-every-last-word-of-the-october-1965-esquire/', 'snippet': 'All I really know about 1960s Esquire are George Lois’s covers, which haven’t dimmed a bit over the years. They still feel exciting and audacious. But what about the inside?', 'lead_paragraph': '“Esquire in the Sixties” — if you’re a magazine editor, you say those words in a hushed, reverential voice. That magazine in that era represents the best it has ever been for us, the apex of the craft. Or so the legend goes. Aside from a bunch of stories I’ve read in collections, all I really know about 1960s Esquire are George Lois’s covers, which still feel exciting and audacious. But what about the inside?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/magazine/esquire-pic-1/esquire-pic-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 852, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/magazine/esquire-pic-1/esquire-pic-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1704, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/magazine/esquire-pic-1/esquire-pic-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/11/30/magazine/esquire-pic-1/esquire-pic-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/11/30/magazine/esquire-pic-1/esquire-pic-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '16 Things I Learned From Reading Every Last Word of the October 1965 Esquire', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lois, George', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ESQUIRE', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T20:05:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hugo Lindgren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hugo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lindgren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a93a1cc-1a56-5a0f-856b-40d91269d032', 'word_count': 1211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a93a1cc-1a56-5a0f-856b-40d91269d032'}, {'abstract': 'Looking for a festive, dressy-but-easy first-course salad for the holiday season? You can’t go wrong with sea scallops.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/05/dining/take-scallops-out-of-the-shell-and-put-them-into-the-fire.html', 'snippet': 'Looking for a festive, dressy-but-easy first-course salad for the holiday season? You can’t go wrong with sea scallops.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR a festive, dressy-but-easy first-course salad for the holiday season, you can’t go wrong with sea scallops. Yes, it’s true, wild Atlantic sea scallops (the ones you want) are a bit pricey, but that’s exactly why I recommend them as a first