okbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T20:14:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Grimes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grimes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce2df9fe-b8bf-5b15-9b42-caa593194dae', 'word_count': 807, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce2df9fe-b8bf-5b15-9b42-caa593194dae'}, {'abstract': 'Dedicated to performance art, Relâche, the Party, presented by the arts foundation Performa, was a giddy recreation of 1924 surrealist performance by Francis Picabia and Erik Satie.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/life-as-a-runway-dinner-and-decadence/', 'snippet': 'Dedicated to performance art, Relâche, the Party, presented by the arts foundation Performa, was a giddy recreation of 1924 surrealist performance by Francis Picabia and Erik Satie.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Oud Immortel scented the air, lending a bitingly decadent touch to a darkly theatrical evening. Dedicated to performance art, Relâche – The Party, presented by the arts organization Performa, was a giddy recreation of a 1924 surrealist performance by Francis Picabia and Erik Satie. The affair last night at Stage 37 on Midtown’s western rim, drew a chicly eccentric crowd that included the model Arizona Muse, the mixed media artist Liz Magic Laser, Milly Glimcher, the art historian and evening’s honoree, and the art photographer Cindy Sherman. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Life as a Runway: Dinner and Decadence', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Performance Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cornejo, Maria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sherman, Cindy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T20:22:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth LaFerla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LaFerla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5127fe47-251f-57d1-a26c-6176dde3fab8', 'word_count': 197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5127fe47-251f-57d1-a26c-6176dde3fab8'}, {'abstract': '“Reasons to Be Happy” will open at MCC Theater in May, replacing “Small Engine Repair” which has been delayed because of scheduling conflicts.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/11/30/neil-labute-will-write-and-direct-reasons-to-be-pretty-follow-up/', 'snippet': '“Reasons to Be Happy” will open at MCC Theater in May, replacing “Small Engine Repair” which has been delayed because of scheduling conflicts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MCC Theater has a reason to be happy: Its playwright-in-residence, Neil LaBute, plans to direct the premiere of his play “Reasons to Be Happy” for the theater next year. The new play is described as “companion piece” to Mr. LaBute’s savage comedy “Reasons to be Pretty” and features the same four characters – Greg, Steph, Carly and Kent – though in different romantic pairings.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Neil LaBute Will Write and Direct ‘Reasons to Be Pretty’ Follow-Up', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A LaBute Sequel', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LaBute, Neil', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MCC Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T20:26:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93001a15-c6b1-5349-8dd7-7f672c1fd0c2', 'word_count': 181, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93001a15-c6b1-5349-8dd7-7f672c1fd0c2'}, {'abstract': 'A house call in Nanping Village.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/magazine/folk-medicine.html', 'snippet': 'A house call in Nanping Village.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dr. Ye Jiangsheng’s home in Nanping Village, in Yixian county, is also his office, where, like nearly everyone else in the ancient town, he cultivates silkworms. A sign outside says “No. 2 Clinic” in Chinese, with “The Doctor’s” written in English. The town is a warren of narrow alleys, stone and carved wood, ideal for kung fu heroes to jump from rooftop to rooftop or for tragic characters in Chinese costume dramas to wander from one darkened room to another. It’s no surprise that the director Zhang Yimou is one of many to use Nanping Village as a film set.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '47', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Folk Medicine', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Folk Medicine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baan, Iwan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kimmelman, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-11-30T20:31:10+0000